VBI({"OBJ-00600":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the wedding feast","d":"A king's invited guests would not come to his son's wedding but killed his servants, and he destroyed them and filled the hall from the highways; a guest without a wedding garment was cast into outer darkness, for many are called, but few are chosen (Matthew 22:1–14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 22:6; NKJV notes, note on Matthew 22:13; Matthew 22:1–14","v":"A king's invited guests would not come to his son's wedding but killed his servants, and he destroyed them and filled the hall from the highways; a guest without a wedding garment was cast into outer darkness, for many are called, but few are chosen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","v":"The kingdom of heaven is like a certain king who arranged a marriage for his son"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 22:1–14","e":"EVT-02861"}],"nm":{"EVT-02861":"The chief priests and elders question Jesus's authority","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00601":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the fig tree","d":"When the fig tree's branch is tender and puts forth leaves, summer is near; so when they see all these things, they are to know that it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near, at the doors (Matthew 24:32–33)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 24:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:29; Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28–29","v":"When the fig tree's branch is tender and puts forth leaves, summer is near; so when they see all these things, they are to know that it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near, at the doors"},{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 21:29–31","v":"As Luke tells it: when the fig tree and all the trees are already budding, they know that summer is near; so when they see these things happening, they are to know that the kingdom of God is near"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28","v":"learn this parable from the fig tree"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28; Luke 21:29","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 24:32–33; Mark 13:28–29; Luke 21:29–31","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00602":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the wise and foolish virgins","d":"Ten virgins went out with their lamps to meet the bridegroom; the five wise took oil and went in with him to the wedding, but the five foolish had none and were shut out, so they are to watch, not knowing the day or the hour (Matthew 25:1–13)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","v":"Ten virgins went out with their lamps to meet the bridegroom; the five wise took oil and went in with him to the wedding, but the five foolish had none and were shut out, so they are to watch, not knowing the day or the hour"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","v":"the kingdom of heaven shall be likened to ten virgins"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 25:1–13","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00603":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the talents","d":"A man going on a journey gave his servants five, two and one talents; those who traded doubled theirs and entered the joy of their lord, but the servant who hid his talent in the ground had it taken from him and was cast into the outer darkness (Matthew 25:14–30)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","v":"A man going on a journey gave his servants five, two and one talents; those who traded doubled theirs and entered the joy of their lord, but the servant who hid his talent in the ground had it taken from him and was cast into the outer darkness"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","v":"the kingdom of heaven is like a man traveling to a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Matthew 25:14–30","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00604":{"k":"object","n":"The alabaster flask of fragrant oil","d":"The alabaster flask of very costly fragrant oil that a woman poured on Jesus's head at Bethany (Matthew 26:7–9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 26:7–9; Mark 14:3–5","v":"Very costly fragrant oil, which the disciples said might have been sold for much and given to the poor"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 26:7","v":"an alabaster flask of very costly fragrant oil"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:3; Mark 14:3","v":"an alabaster flask of very costly oil of spikenard"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 14:3–5","v":"She broke the flask and poured the oil on His head; it might have been sold for more than three hundred denarii","at":"EVT-02873"},{"p":"owner","r":"Matthew 26:7; Mark 14:3","e":"PER-03060"}],"nm":{"EVT-02873":"A woman anoints Jesus at Bethany","PER-03060":"The woman who anointed Jesus at Bethany"}},"OBJ-00605":{"k":"object","n":"The thirty pieces of silver paid to Judas","d":"The thirty pieces of silver the chief priests counted out to Judas Iscariot to deliver Jesus to them, which he later threw down in the temple (Matthew 26:15; 27:3–10)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 26:15; 27:3, 9","v":"thirty pieces of silver"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Matthew 26:15","v":"The price for which Judas would deliver Jesus to the chief priests"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00606":{"k":"object","n":"The scarlet robe put on Jesus","d":"The scarlet robe the soldiers put on Jesus to mock Him, and took off again (Matthew 27:28, 31)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:28","v":"a scarlet robe"},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:28; Mark 15:17, 20","v":"purple","s":"alternative","w":"Mark says they clothed Him with purple (Mark 15:17, 20), where Matthew says they put a scarlet robe on Him (Matthew 27:28); Matthew's telling, entered first, stands as the preferred."}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00607":{"k":"object","n":"The crown of thorns","d":"The crown the soldiers twisted of thorns and put on Jesus's head to mock Him (Matthew 27:29)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 27:29; Matthew 27:29; Mark 15:17","v":"a crown of thorns"},{"p":"material","r":"Matthew 27:29; Mark 15:17","v":"Thorns"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00608":{"k":"object","n":"The accusation over the cross","d":"The accusation written against Jesus and put up over His head on the cross (Matthew 27:37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37","v":"That this is Jesus, the King of the Jews"},{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37; Mark 15:26","v":"The King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"Mark gives the inscription as THE KING OF THE JEWS (Mark 15:26), where Matthew gives it as THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS (Matthew 27:37); Matthew's telling, entered first, stands as the preferred."},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; Matthew 27:37; Luke 23:38","v":"This is the King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"Luke gives the inscription as This is the King of the Jews (Luke 23:38), where Matthew gives This is Jesus, the King of the Jews (Matthew 27:37); the telling entered first stands as the preferred."},{"p":"contents","r":"Matthew 27:37; John 19:19","v":"Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews","s":"alternative","w":"John gives the title as Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews (John 19:19), where Matthew gives This is Jesus the King of the Jews (Matthew 27:37); the telling entered first stands as the preferred."},{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:37","v":"the accusation written against Him"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:26; Mark 15:26","v":"the inscription of His accusation"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 23:38","v":"an inscription"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:19–20","v":"a title"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; Luke 23:38; John 19:20","v":"It was written over Him in letters of Greek, Latin and Hebrew (NU omits written and in letters of Greek, Latin, and Hebrew, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:19–20","v":"As John tells it: Pilate wrote it and put it on the cross, and many of the Jews read it, for the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 19:19–22","e":"PER-03065"}],"nm":{"PER-03065":"Pilate"}},"OBJ-00609":{"k":"object","n":"The stone at the door of the tomb","d":"The large stone Joseph rolled against the door of the tomb, which was sealed and guarded (Matthew 27:60, 66; 28:2)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 27:60","v":"a large stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Matthew 28:2; Matthew 28:2","v":"The angel of the Lord rolled it back from the door (NU omits from the door, by the NKJV note) and sat on it","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 16:3–4","v":"The women asked who would roll it away from the door, and looking up they saw it rolled away, for it was very large","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 24:2","v":"As Luke tells it, the women found it rolled away from the tomb","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 20:1","v":"As John tells it, Mary Magdalene saw that it had been taken away from the tomb","at":"EVT-02898"},{"p":"placed at","r":"Matthew 27:60; Mark 15:46","e":"PLC-01482"}],"nm":{"EVT-02898":"The women find the tomb empty and the angel tells them Jesus is risen","PLC-01482":"The tomb of Joseph of Arimathea"}},"OBJ-00610":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to Mark","d":"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God: the preaching of John the Baptist, the works and teaching of Jesus in Galilee and on the way to Jerusalem, His death and His rising again (Mark 1–16)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:12; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:25; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 1:35; Isaiah 40:3; Malachi 3:1; Mark 1:1–45","v":"The gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God, begins as written in the Prophets (NU Isaiah the prophet, by the NKJV note), with the messenger sent before Him and a voice in the wilderness (Malachi 3:1; Isaiah 40:3): John came baptizing in the wilderness, preaching a baptism of repentance for sins to be forgiven (or because of forgiveness, by the NKJV note); all Judea and Jerusalem were baptized by him, and he told of One mightier, who would baptize with the Holy Spirit. Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan; coming up from (NU out of, by the NKJV note) the water He saw the heavens parting (torn open, by the NKJV note) and the Spirit descending on Him like a dove, and a voice from heaven called Him My beloved Son. The Spirit drove (sent, by the NKJV note) Him into the wilderness forty days, tempted by Satan, with the wild beasts, and angels ministered to Him. After John was put in prison Jesus came to Galilee preaching the gospel of the kingdom (NU omits of the kingdom, by the NKJV note) of God: the time was fulfilled and the kingdom at hand (has drawn near, by the NKJV note). He called Simon and Andrew, and James and John the sons of Zebedee, to follow Him. In the synagogue at Capernaum on the Sabbath He taught with authority and silenced (lit. muzzled, by the NKJV note) an unclean spirit and cast it out, and all were amazed at His new doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note) and His authority over unclean spirits (the NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note); He raised up Simon's wife's mother from her fever, and at evening healed many sick and cast out many demons; in the morning He prayed in a solitary (deserted, by the NKJV note) place, then preached in the synagogues throughout all Galilee. He cleansed a leper, who spread the matter so that Jesus stayed outside in deserted places"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 2:19; Mark 2:1–28","v":"After some days Jesus was in the house at Capernaum (NU omits Immediately, by the NKJV note), preaching the word to a crowd that filled the doorway, and four men let a paralytic down on his bed through the roof; seeing their faith He forgave his sins, and when scribes reasoned that He blasphemed He showed that the Son of Man has power (authority, by the NKJV note) on earth to forgive sins, and the man rose, took up his bed and went out before them all. By the sea He called Levi the son of Alphaeus from the tax office and dined in his house with many tax collectors and sinners, answering the scribes and Pharisees (NU the scribes of the Pharisees, by the NKJV note) that He came to call sinners (NU omits to repentance, by the NKJV note). Asked why His disciples did not fast, He spoke of the bridegroom's friends (lit. sons of the bridechamber, by the NKJV note), of unshrunk cloth and new wine; when His disciples plucked grain on the Sabbath He answered the Pharisees with what David did in the days of Abiathar the high priest, and said the Sabbath was made for man, and the Son of Man is Lord also of the Sabbath"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 3:32; Mark 3:1–35","v":"In the synagogue, watched by those who would accuse (bring charges against, by the NKJV note) Him, Jesus bade the man with the withered hand step forward (lit. arise into the midst, by the NKJV note), asked whether it was lawful on the Sabbath to save life or to kill, and, grieved at their hardness of heart, restored the hand (NU omits as whole as the other, by the NKJV note); the Pharisees plotted with the Herodians to destroy Him. He withdrew to the sea, and a great multitude came from Galilee, Judea, Jerusalem, Idumea, beyond the Jordan, and Tyre and Sidon; He healed many, and warned the unclean spirits who called Him the Son of God not to make Him known. On the mountain He appointed twelve (NU adds whom He also named apostles, by the NKJV note; NU and He appointed the twelve, by the NKJV note) to be with Him, to preach and to have power (authority, by the NKJV note) to heal sicknesses (NU omits to heal sicknesses and, by the NKJV note) and to cast out demons: Simon, whom He named Peter, James and John, whom He named Boanerges, Sons of Thunder, Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Cananite and Judas Iscariot. His own people said He was out of His mind, and scribes from Jerusalem said He cast out demons by Beelzebub; He answered that Satan cannot cast out Satan, that a strong man must first be bound, and that blasphemy against the Holy Spirit is never forgiven. When His mother and brothers (NU, M add and Your sisters, by the NKJV note) sent for Him, He said that whoever does the will of God is His brother and sister and mother"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:39; NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:40; Isaiah 6:9–10; Mark 4:1–41","v":"By the sea, teaching from a boat, Jesus told the multitude the parable of the sower, whose seed fell by the wayside, where the birds (NU, M omit of the air, by the NKJV note) devoured it, on stony ground, among thorns, where it yielded no crop (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note), and on good ground. Alone with the twelve and those around Him He said the mystery (secret or hidden truths, by the NKJV note) of the kingdom of God was given to them, while to those outside all came in parables, as Isaiah wrote (Isaiah 6:9–10), and explained the sower: the word sown, taken away by Satan, received without root, choked by cares and riches, or accepted (received, by the NKJV note) and bearing fruit. A lamp is set on a lampstand, and with the measure one uses it is measured back. The kingdom of God is like seed a man scatters (sows, by the NKJV note), which grows he knows not how until the harvest, and like a mustard seed, the smallest of seeds, which grows greater than all herbs. That evening, as they crossed the sea, a great windstorm arose while He slept on a pillow in the stern; He rebuked the wind and said to the sea, Peace, be still (lit. Be quiet, by the NKJV note), and asked how they had no faith (NU Have you still no faith?, by the NKJV note); they feared exceedingly, asking who He was, that the wind and the sea obeyed Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:13; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 5:38; Mark 5:1–43","v":"In the country of the Gadarenes (NU Gerasenes, by the NKJV note) a man with an unclean spirit, living among the tombs, whom no chains could bind (NU adds anymore, by the NKJV note), ran and worshiped Jesus and cried out, adjuring (by the NKJV note) the Son of the Most High God not to torment him; his name was Legion, for they were many, and the unclean spirits, given leave (NU He gave, by the NKJV note), entered about two thousand swine, which rushed into the sea and drowned. The people found the man clothed and in his right mind and begged Jesus to leave; the man, sent home to tell what the Lord had done for him, proclaimed it in Decapolis (lit. Ten Cities, by the NKJV note). Back across the sea Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, begged Jesus to heal his little daughter, who lay at the point of death; on the way a woman with a flow of blood twelve years, who had suffered much from many physicians, touched His garment and was healed of her affliction (suffering, by the NKJV note), and Jesus, knowing that power had gone out of Him, told her that her faith had made her well. Told that the girl was dead, He bade Jairus only believe; with Peter, James and John and the child's parents He went in past the tumult (an uproar, by the NKJV note) of mourners, took her by the hand and said, Talitha, cumi, Little girl, arise, and the girl of twelve rose and walked; He charged them that no one should know it"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:20; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:36; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:44; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:45; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:50; NKJV notes, note on Mark 6:54; Mark 6:1–56","v":"In His own country, on the Sabbath, Jesus taught in the synagogue, and those who heard Him asked whether this was not the carpenter, the Son of Mary, the brother of James, Joses, Judas and Simon, with His sisters among them, and were offended; a prophet has honor except in his own country, and He could do no mighty work there but heal a few sick, and marveled at their unbelief. Going about the villages teaching, He sent the twelve out two by two with power over unclean spirits, taking only a staff, wearing sandals, and shaking off the dust where they were not received (NU whatever place, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 11, by the NKJV note); they preached repentance, cast out demons and anointed the sick with oil. King Herod said John, whom he had beheaded, was raised, while others said Jesus was Elijah or the Prophet (NU, M a prophet, by the NKJV note): Herod had imprisoned John for the sake of Herodias, his brother Philip's wife, whom he had married; Herodias held it against (held a grudge, by the NKJV note) John, but Herod feared him as a just and holy man and heard him gladly (NU was very perplexed, yet, by the NKJV note), until at his birthday feast her daughter danced and, prompted by her mother, asked for John's head on a platter, and the king, sorry but bound by his oaths, had him beheaded in prison; John's disciples laid his corpse in a tomb. The apostles returned and told Jesus all they had done; in a deserted place, where the multitudes (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) ran before them, He had compassion on them as sheep without a shepherd, taught them, and fed about (NU, M omit about, by the NKJV note) five thousand men with five loaves and two fish, sitting in ranks on the green grass (the disciples' bread, NU something to eat, by the NKJV note), and twelve baskets of fragments were taken up. He made (invited, strongly urged, by the NKJV note) the disciples go before Him to Bethsaida, prayed on the mountain, and in the fourth watch came to them walking on the sea, bidding them be of good cheer (take courage, by the NKJV note); the wind ceased, and they were amazed, for their heart was hardened. At Gennesaret the people (lit. they, by the NKJV note) brought the sick to Him from the whole region, and as many as touched the hem of His garment were made well"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:2; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 7:35; Exodus 20:12; 21:17; Isaiah 29:13; Mark 7:1–37","v":"The Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem, who with all the Jews wash their hands in a special way (lit. with the fist, by the NKJV note) by the tradition of the elders, found fault (NU omits when and they found fault, by the NKJV note) that His disciples ate with unwashed hands; Jesus answered with Isaiah's word of lip worship (Isaiah 29:13) and showed how they set aside (by the NKJV note) the commandment to honor father and mother (Exodus 20:12; 21:17) by calling a parent's due Corban, a gift to God (NU omits the rest of v. 8, by the NKJV note). He told the multitude that nothing entering a man defiles him, but what comes out of him (NU omits v. 16, by the NKJV note), and in the house explained that food enters the stomach, not the heart, thus purifying all foods (NU sets this off as Mark's comment that Jesus declared all foods clean, by the NKJV note), while evil thoughts and every wickedness come from within. In the region of Tyre and Sidon (NU omits and Sidon, by the NKJV note) a Greek (Gentile, by the NKJV note) woman, a Syro-Phoenician (a Syrian of Phoenicia, by the NKJV note) by birth, begged (by the NKJV note) Him to cast a demon out of her daughter, and answered that even the little dogs eat the children's crumbs; for that saying the demon left her daughter. Coming through Decapolis to the Sea of Galilee, He took aside a deaf man with an impediment in his speech, touched his ears and tongue and said, Ephphatha, Be opened, and his tongue was loosed (lit. bond, by the NKJV note); charged to tell no one, they proclaimed it the more"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 8:33; Mark 8:1–38","v":"When a great multitude had been with Him three days with nothing to eat, Jesus had compassion on them and fed about four thousand with seven loaves and a few small fish, and seven large baskets were left; by boat He came to the region of Dalmanutha. The Pharisees sought a sign from heaven, testing Him, and He sighed and said no sign would be given to this generation. In the boat, when the disciples (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) had only one loaf, He warned them of the leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) of the Pharisees and of Herod, and asked whether their heart was still (NU omits still, by the NKJV note) hardened, reminding them of the baskets left from the five thousand and the four thousand. At Bethsaida He led a blind man out of the town and laid His hands on his eyes twice, until he saw everyone clearly, and sent him home, not into the town (NU Do not even go into the town, by the NKJV note). On the way to the towns of Caesarea Philippi Peter answered that He was the Christ, and Jesus charged them to tell no one; He began to teach openly that the Son of Man must suffer, be rejected by the elders, chief priests and scribes, be killed and rise after three days, and rebuked Peter, who was not mindful (setting his mind, by the NKJV note) of the things of God. Whoever would come after Him must deny himself, take up his cross and follow Him, for whoever loses his life for His sake and the gospel's will save it, and the Son of Man will be ashamed of those ashamed of Him in this adulterous and sinful generation"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:23; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:33; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:40; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:42; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:43; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:44; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:45; NKJV notes, note on Mark 9:49; Isaiah 66:24; Mark 9:1–50","v":"Some standing there would see the kingdom of God present (having come, by the NKJV note) with power. After six days, on a high mountain, Jesus was transfigured before Peter, James and John, His clothes whiter than any launderer could make them; Elijah and Moses talked with Him, Peter in his fear spoke of three tabernacles, and a voice from the cloud said, This is My beloved Son. Hear Him. Coming down He charged them to tell no one until the Son of Man had risen, and said Elijah had come. A father whose son had a mute spirit, which the disciples could not cast out, was told that all things are possible to him who believes (NU If You can!, by the NKJV note) and cried, Lord, I believe; help my unbelief; Jesus, who called them an unbelieving (by the NKJV note) generation, cast out the deaf and dumb spirit, saying this kind comes out only by prayer and fasting (NU omits and fasting, by the NKJV note). Passing through Galilee He foretold His death and rising the third day, and they did not understand. At Capernaum, when they had disputed (discussed, by the NKJV note) who was greatest, He said the first must be last and servant of all and took a child in His arms; He forbade them to hinder one who cast out demons in His name, for whoever is not against us (M you, by the NKJV note) is on our side, and promised reward for a cup of water. Whoever causes a little one to stumble (to fall into sin, by the NKJV note) were better drowned; better to enter life maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note) than go to hell (Gr. Gehenna, by the NKJV note), where their worm does not die (Isaiah 66:24; NU omits vv. 44, 46 and the rest of v. 45, by the NKJV note); everyone will be seasoned with fire (NU omits the rest of v. 49, by the NKJV note), and they were to have salt in themselves and peace with one another"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:34; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:51; NKJV notes, note on Mark 10:52; Genesis 1:27; 2:24; Mark 10:1–52","v":"In Judea beyond the Jordan the Pharisees asked about divorce; Jesus answered that Moses wrote that precept (command, by the NKJV note) for the hardness of their heart, but God made them male and female and joined the two as one flesh (Genesis 1:27; 2:24), and that whoever divorces and marries another commits adultery. He was greatly displeased when the disciples rebuked those who brought little children, and took them in His arms and blessed them. A man who ran and knelt to ask how to inherit eternal life, and had kept the commandments from his youth, He loved and bade sell all and follow Him, and he went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions; it is hard for those who trust in riches (NU omits for those who trust in riches, by the NKJV note) to enter the kingdom of God, but with God all things are possible, and those who have left all (NU omits or wife, by the NKJV note; lit. fields, by the NKJV note) will receive a hundredfold now, with persecutions, and eternal life. Going up to Jerusalem, while they followed amazed and afraid, He told the twelve He would be mocked, scourged (flogged with a Roman scourge, by the NKJV note), spat upon and killed, and rise the third day. James and John asked to sit at His right and left in His glory; He said they would drink His cup, and that the Son of Man came to serve and give His life a ransom for many. Leaving Jericho He gave sight to blind Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, who cried, Son of David, have mercy on me, and called Him Rabboni (lit. My Great One, by the NKJV note); his faith had made him well (lit. saved, by the NKJV note), and he followed Jesus on the road"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:10; NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:26; Psalms 118:26; Isaiah 56:7; Jeremiah 7:11; Mark 11:1–33","v":"Drawing near Jerusalem at Bethphage (M Bethsphage, by the NKJV note) and Bethany, at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples for a colt on which no one had sat, found tied by the door (NU, M a door, by the NKJV note), and rode into Jerusalem as many spread clothes and branches and cried Hosanna, blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord (Psalm 118:26), and blessed the kingdom of our father David (NU omits in the name of the Lord, by the NKJV note). He looked around in the temple and went out to Bethany with the twelve. The next day, hungry, He found only leaves on a fig tree, for it was not the season for figs, and said no one would eat fruit from it again; in the temple He drove out the buyers and sellers, let no one carry wares through it, and taught that His house is a house of prayer for all nations, but they had made it a den of thieves (Isaiah 56:7; Jeremiah 7:11), and the scribes and chief priests sought to destroy Him. The next morning the fig tree was dried up from the roots, and He taught them to have faith in God, to believe when they pray, and to forgive, that their Father may forgive them (NU omits v. 26, by the NKJV note). In the temple the chief priests, scribes and elders asked by what authority He acted; He asked whether John's baptism was from heaven or from men, and when they would not answer, He would not tell them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:28; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:30; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:40; NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Exodus 3:6; Leviticus 19:18; Deuteronomy 6:4–5; Psalms 110:1; 118:22–23; Mark 12:1–44","v":"In parables He told of a vineyard leased to vinedressers (tenant farmers, by the NKJV note) who beat, stoned (NU omits, by the NKJV note) and killed the servants sent for its fruit and at last killed the owner's beloved son, so that the owner would destroy them and give the vineyard to others, and of the stone the builders rejected (Psalm 118:22–23); the rulers knew He spoke against them. To the Pharisees and Herodians, who said He courted no man's favor (by the NKJV note; lit. look at the face of men, by the NKJV note), He answered over a denarius, Render (pay, by the NKJV note) to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's. To the Sadducees He said they were mistaken (or deceived, by the NKJV note), for the risen neither marry nor are given in marriage, and God is the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, of the living (Exodus 3:6). To a scribe who asked the first (foremost, by the NKJV note) commandment He answered, Hear, O Israel, the LORD our God, the LORD is one, and to love God with all the heart, soul, mind and strength (NU omits the rest of v. 30, by the NKJV note), and one's neighbor as oneself (Deuteronomy 6:4–5; Leviticus 19:18), and told him he was not far from the kingdom of God. He asked how the Christ could be David's Son when David called Him Lord (Psalm 110:1), warned against the scribes who devour widows' houses and pray long for a pretense (for appearance' sake, by the NKJV note), and said a poor widow who threw two mites (Gr. lepta, very small copper coins, making a quadrans, a Roman coin, by the NKJV notes) into the treasury had given more than all, for she gave all she had"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:11; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:13; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:14; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:22; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 13:29; Mark 13:1–37","v":"As Jesus went out of the temple a disciple pointed to its stones and buildings, and He said not one stone would be left upon another. On the Mount of Olives Peter, James, John and Andrew asked when these things would be: He warned them that many would come in His name, with wars, earthquakes and famines (NU omits and troubles, by the NKJV note), the beginnings of sorrows (lit. birth pangs, by the NKJV note); that they would be delivered up and brought (NU, M stand, by the NKJV note) before rulers and kings, the gospel first preached to all the nations, and the Holy Spirit speaking for them (NU omits or premeditate, by the NKJV note), and that he who endures (bears patiently, by the NKJV note) to the end will be saved. At the abomination of desolation (NU omits spoken of by Daniel the prophet, by the NKJV note) those in Judea are to flee, for the tribulation will be unlike any since creation, and the days will be shortened for the elect (chosen ones, by the NKJV note); after it the sun and moon will be darkened, the stars fall, and they will see the Son of Man coming in the clouds with great power and glory, gathering His elect (chosen ones, by the NKJV note) from the four winds. As the fig tree's leaves tell that summer is near, so they will know it (or He, by the NKJV note) is near; this generation will not pass till all takes place, and His words will not pass away. Of that day and hour no one knows, not the angels nor the Son, but only the Father; like the doorkeeper of a man gone to a far country, they are to watch"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:1; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:3; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:12; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:19; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:22; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:24; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:27; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:29; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:56; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:65; NKJV notes, note on Mark 14:70; Zechariah 13:7; Mark 14:1–72","v":"Two days before the Passover the chief priests and scribes sought to take Jesus by trickery (deception, by the NKJV note). At Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper, a woman broke an alabaster flask of very costly spikenard (perfume of pure nard, by the NKJV note) and poured it on His head; to those who scolded (by the NKJV note) her, saying it might have been sold for more than three hundred denarii, He said she had anointed His body beforehand for burial and would be remembered wherever the gospel is preached. Judas Iscariot went to the chief priests, who promised him money. On the first day of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover lamb was killed (sacrificed, by the NKJV note), two disciples followed a man carrying a pitcher of water to a large upper room, furnished and prepared; at evening, eating with the twelve, Jesus said one who dipped with Him in the dish would betray Him (NU omits the rest of v. 19, by the NKJV note), and He gave them the bread, His body (NU omits eat, by the NKJV note), and the cup, His blood of the new (NU omits new, by the NKJV note) covenant, shed for many. When they had sung a hymn (or hymns, by the NKJV note) He foretold that all would stumble (NU omits because of Me this night, by the NKJV note), as the Shepherd struck (Zechariah 13:7), and that Peter, who said he would not fall away (by the NKJV note), would deny Him three times before the rooster crowed twice. In Gethsemane He prayed, Abba, Father, take this cup away from Me, yet not what I will, and three times found Peter, James and John sleeping. Judas came with a multitude from the chief priests, scribes and elders, said Rabbi and kissed Him; one cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, all forsook Him and fled, and a young man left his linen cloth and fled naked. Before the high priest the false witnesses did not agree (were not consistent, by the NKJV note); asked if He was the Christ, the Son of the Blessed, He said, I am, and that they would see the Son of Man at the right hand of the Power, and all condemned Him; they spat on Him and struck Him (NU received Him with slaps, by the NKJV note). Below in the courtyard Peter denied Him three times, the last with curses, when told his speech showed him a Galilean (accent, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 70, by the NKJV note); the rooster crowed a second time, and he wept"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:4; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:26; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:28; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:32; NKJV notes, note on Mark 15:39; Psalms 22:1; Isaiah 53:12; Mark 15:1–47","v":"In the morning the chief priests, with the elders, scribes and whole council, bound Jesus and delivered Him to Pilate; asked if He was the King of the Jews He said, It is as you say, and to their many accusations (NU of which they accuse You, by the NKJV note) answered nothing. At the feast the crowd, crying aloud (NU going up, by the NKJV note) and stirred up by the chief priests, asked for Barabbas, who was chained with rebels who had murdered in the rebellion, and cried Crucify Him; Pilate, knowing it was envy, gratified them, scourged Jesus and delivered Him up. The soldiers in the Praetorium (the governor's headquarters, by the NKJV note) clothed Him in purple, crowned Him with thorns and hailed Him King of the Jews. Simon a Cyrenian, the father of Alexander and Rufus, was compelled to bear His cross to Golgotha, the Place of a Skull; refusing wine mingled with myrrh, He was crucified at the third hour between two robbers, under the inscription of His accusation (crime, by the NKJV note), THE KING OF THE JEWS, and the Scripture was fulfilled that He was numbered with the transgressors (Isaiah 53:12; NU omits v. 28, by the NKJV note); passers-by and the chief priests mocked Him (M believe Him, by the NKJV note). At the ninth hour He cried, Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani, My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me (Psalm 22:1), and breathed His last; the veil of the temple was torn, and the centurion, seeing how He breathed His last (NU He thus breathed His last, by the NKJV note), said He was truly the Son of God. Women looked on from afar, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James the Less and of Joses, and Salome, who had followed Him in Galilee. On the Preparation Day Joseph of Arimathea, a prominent council member, asked Pilate for the body, wrapped it in fine linen and laid it in a tomb hewn out of the rock"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:8; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:9; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:17; NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:18; Mark 16:1–20","v":"When the Sabbath was past Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Salome brought spices, and early on the first day of the week found the very large stone rolled away; a young man in a long white robe, sitting within the tomb, told them that Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified, was risen, and that they were to tell His disciples, and Peter, that He was going before (ahead of, by the NKJV note) them into Galilee; they fled trembling (NU, M omit quickly, by the NKJV note) and said nothing to anyone. Risen early on the first day of the week, He appeared first to Mary Magdalene, out of whom He had cast seven demons, then in another form to two of them in the country, and the rest did not believe; then to the eleven at the table, rebuking their unbelief, and sent them into all the world to preach the gospel to every creature: whoever believes and is baptized will be saved, and signs will follow those who believe (have believed, by the NKJV note; NU and in their hands they will, by the NKJV note). The Lord was received up into heaven and sat at the right hand of God, and they preached everywhere, the Lord working with them. Verses 9–20 are bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, lacking in Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, though nearly all other manuscripts of Mark contain them (by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 1:1","v":"The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:1–11","v":"It opens with John the Baptist preparing the way, as written in the prophets, and begins the story of Jesus at His baptism"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:10, 12, 18, 20–21, 28, 31, 42; 2:8, 12; 3:6; 4:5, 15–17, 29; 5:2, 29–30, 42; 6:25, 27, 45, 50, 54; 7:35; 8:10; 9:15, 20, 24; 10:52; 11:2–3; 14:43, 45; 15:1","v":"It moves quickly from one work of Jesus to the next, again and again with the word immediately"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 1:25, 34, 43–44; 3:12; 5:43; 7:36; 8:26, 30; 9:9","v":"Again and again Jesus charges the unclean spirits, and those He heals, to keep silent about Him"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 3:17; 5:41; 7:11, 34; 14:36; 15:34","v":"It gives some words of Jesus in His own tongue and translates them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 16:14–20","v":"It ends with the risen Jesus sending the eleven into all the world to preach the gospel to every creature, His receiving up into heaven, and their preaching everywhere, the Lord working with them"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 16:9; Mark 16:9–20","v":"Its last verses, 16:9–20, are bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text; they are lacking in Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, though nearly all other manuscripts of Mark contain them","b":"NKJV note"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00611":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the growing seed","d":"The kingdom of God is as a man who scatters seed on the ground; it sprouts and grows he does not know how, and when the grain ripens he puts in the sickle (Mark 4:26–29)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 4:26; Mark 4:26–29","v":"The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter (sow, by the NKJV note) seed on the ground and sleep and rise night and day, while the seed sprouts and grows, he does not know how; the earth bears of itself, first the blade, then the head, then the full grain, and when the grain ripens he puts in the sickle, for the harvest has come"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 4:26","v":"The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter seed"},{"p":"made by","r":"Mark 4:26","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Mark 4:26–29","e":"EVT-02825"}],"nm":{"EVT-02825":"Jesus teaches the multitude in parables by the sea","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00612":{"k":"object","n":"The herd of swine of the Gadarenes","d":"The large herd of swine feeding near the mountains in the country of the Gadarenes, about two thousand, into which the unclean spirits went, and which ran down the steep place into the sea and drowned (Mark 5:11–13; Matthew 8:30–32)","f":[{"p":"count","r":"Mark 5:13","v":"2000"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 5:11","v":"a large herd of swine"},{"p":"feature","r":"Matthew 8:32; Mark 5:13; Luke 8:32–33","v":"At Jesus's leave the unclean spirits entered them, and the herd ran violently down the steep place into the sea and drowned","at":"EVT-02802"}],"nm":{"EVT-02802":"Jesus sends the demons out of two men into a herd of swine"}},"OBJ-00613":{"k":"object","n":"The colt on which Jesus rode into Jerusalem","d":"The colt the disciples found tied in the village opposite them, on which no one had sat, and on which Jesus rode into Jerusalem (Mark 11:2–7; Matthew 21:2–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Matthew 21:2; Mark 11:2; Luke 19:30, 33, 35; John 12:15","v":"a colt"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:14","v":"a young donkey"},{"p":"feature","r":"Mark 11:2; Luke 19:30","v":"No one had ever sat on it"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 11:4; Mark 11:4–6","v":"It was found tied by the door (NU, M a door, by the NKJV note) outside on the street, and those who stood there let the disciples take it when they said the Lord had need of it","at":"EVT-02857"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 19:33","e":"GRP-01105"}],"nm":{"EVT-02857":"Jesus rides into Jerusalem on a donkey","GRP-01105":"The owners of the colt"}},"OBJ-00614":{"k":"object","n":"The widow's two mites","d":"The two mites, very small copper coins making a quadrans, which the poor widow threw into the treasury (Mark 12:42)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"two mites"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"They were very small copper coins (Gr. lepta, by the NKJV note), and two make a quadrans, a Roman coin (by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"material","r":"NKJV notes, note on Mark 12:42; Mark 12:42","v":"Copper","b":"NKJV note"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00615":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the man gone to a far country","d":"A man going to a far country leaves his house, gives his servants authority and each his work, and commands the doorkeeper to watch; so they are to watch, for they do not know when the master is coming (Mark 13:34–37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Mark 13:34–36","v":"It is like a man going to a far country, who left his house, gave his servants authority and each his work, and commanded the doorkeeper to watch: they are to watch, for they do not know whether the master of the house is coming in the evening, at midnight, at the crowing of the rooster or in the morning, lest coming suddenly he find them sleeping"},{"p":"designation","r":"Mark 13:34","v":"a man going to a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Mark 13:34","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Mark 13:34–37","e":"EVT-02869"}],"nm":{"EVT-02869":"Jesus teaches His disciples on the Mount of Olives about the end of the age","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00616":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to Luke","d":"An orderly account, written to most excellent Theophilus, of the things delivered by those who were eyewitnesses from the beginning: the births of John the Baptist and of Jesus, the works and teaching of Jesus, His death, His rising and His being carried up into heaven (Luke 1–24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:78; Malachi 4:6; Luke 1:1–80","v":"Writing an orderly account to most excellent Theophilus of the things fulfilled (or most surely believed, by the NKJV note) among them, the writer, having perfect understanding (lit. accurately followed, by the NKJV note) from the first, tells that in the days of Herod, the king of Judea, the priest Zacharias of the division of Abijah and his wife Elizabeth, of the daughters of Aaron, were righteous but childless and old. As Zacharias burned incense in the temple, chosen by lot (by the NKJV note), Gabriel told him Elizabeth would bear a son, John, who would go before the Lord in the spirit and power of Elijah (Malachi 4:6); for not believing these glad tidings (this good news, by the NKJV note) he was mute. Elizabeth conceived, and in the sixth month Gabriel told the virgin Mary at Nazareth, betrothed to Joseph of the house of David, that by the Holy Spirit she would bear a Son, Jesus, the Son of the Highest, who would reign over the house of Jacob forever (NU omits blessed are you among women, by the NKJV note; NU omits when she saw him, by the NKJV note; am a virgin, by the NKJV note). Mary visited Elizabeth in the hill country, the babe leaped in Elizabeth's womb, and Elizabeth blessed her who believed (or believed that there, by the NKJV note); Mary sang that her soul magnifies (declares the greatness of, by the NKJV note) the Lord. John was born; on the eighth day, when they would have named him Zacharias, his father wrote, His name is John, and his mouth was opened; filled with the Holy Spirit, Zacharias blessed the Lord God of Israel for a horn of salvation in David's house and the Dayspring (lit. Dawn; the Messiah, by the NKJV note) that has visited (NU shall visit, by the NKJV note) them, and said his son would be called the prophet of the Highest; the child grew in the deserts till his manifestation to Israel"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:37; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:40; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:51; Exodus 13:2; Leviticus 12:8; Luke 2:1–52","v":"In the days of a decree of Caesar Augustus that all the world be registered, while Quirinius governed Syria, Joseph went up from Nazareth to Bethlehem, the city of David, being of David's house, with Mary his betrothed (NU omits wife, by the NKJV note), who was with child; there she brought forth her firstborn Son and laid Him in a manger (feed trough, by the NKJV note), for there was no room in the inn. An angel of the Lord (NU omits behold, by the NKJV note) told shepherds keeping watch by night that a Savior, Christ the Lord, was born in the city of David, and a multitude of the heavenly host praised God, on earth peace, goodwill toward men (NU toward men of goodwill, by the NKJV note); the shepherds found the Babe and made the saying (NU omits widely, by the NKJV note) known, and Mary kept these things in her heart. On the eighth day (NU for His circumcision, by the NKJV note) He was named Jesus. When the days of purification were completed they presented Him to the Lord in the temple with the offering of the law (Exodus 13:2; Leviticus 12:8); Simeon, waiting for the Consolation of Israel, took Him in his arms and blessed God that he had seen His salvation, a light to the Gentiles, and told Mary of the fall and rising of many and the sword through her soul (NU And His father and mother, by the NKJV note); Anna, a prophetess of great age (NU until she was eighty-four, by the NKJV note), gave thanks (NU God, by the NKJV note) and spoke of Him. They returned to Nazareth, and the Child grew strong (NU omits in spirit, by the NKJV note) and wise. At twelve, at the Passover, He stayed behind in Jerusalem, and after three days His parents (NU, by the NKJV note) found Him in the temple among the teachers, about His Father's business; He went down with them to Nazareth, subject (obedient, by the NKJV note) to them, and increased in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and men"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 3:19; Isaiah 40:3–5; Luke 3:1–38","v":"In the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, Pilate governing Judea, Herod tetrarch of Galilee, his brother Philip of Iturea and Trachonitis, Lysanias of Abilene, and Annas and Caiaphas high priests (NU, M in their high priesthood, by the NKJV note), the word of God came to John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness, and he preached a baptism of repentance through all the region of the Jordan, as Isaiah wrote of the voice in the wilderness (Isaiah 40:3–5). He called the multitudes a brood (offspring, by the NKJV note) of vipers, bade those with two tunics share, tax collectors take no more than appointed and soldiers not intimidate (lit. shake down for money, by the NKJV note) anyone, and told of One mightier who would baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire. Herod the tetrarch, rebuked for Herodias, his brother Philip's wife (NU brother's wife, by the NKJV note), shut John up in prison. When all the people were baptized, Jesus was baptized too, and as He prayed the heaven opened and the Holy Spirit descended on Him in bodily form like a dove, and a voice from heaven called Him My beloved Son. Jesus began His ministry at about thirty, being, as was supposed, the son of Joseph, the son of Heli, and so back through Zerubbabel and Nathan the son of David, through Abraham, Shem and Noah, to Adam, the son of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 4:44; Deuteronomy 6:13, 16; 8:3; Isaiah 61:1–2; Luke 4:1–44","v":"Filled with the Holy Spirit, Jesus returned from the Jordan and was led by the Spirit into (NU in, by the NKJV note) the wilderness, tempted (tested, by the NKJV note) forty days by the devil, and answered him from Scripture (NU omits but by every word of God, by the NKJV note; NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note; NU omits Get behind Me, Satan, by the NKJV note; test, by the NKJV note; Deuteronomy 8:3; 6:13; 6:16), when shown the kingdoms of the world and set on the pinnacle of the temple; the devil departed until an opportune time (testing, by the NKJV note). In the power of the Spirit He taught in the synagogues of Galilee. At Nazareth, where He was brought up, He read in the synagogue from the book of Isaiah, The Spirit of the Lord is upon Me to preach the gospel to the poor (NU omits to heal the brokenhearted, by the NKJV note; downtrodden, by the NKJV note; Isaiah 61:1–2), and said it was fulfilled that day; asked whether He was not Joseph's son, He said no prophet is accepted in his own country (NU Capharnaum, by the NKJV note), and spoke of Elijah sent only to a widow of Zarephath (Gr. Sarepta, by the NKJV note) and of Naaman the Syrian, so that in wrath (rage, by the NKJV note) they thrust Him out to throw Him down from the brow of the hill, but He passed through them. At Capernaum He cast out the spirit of an unclean demon (lit. Be muzzled, by the NKJV note), rebuked the high fever (afflicted with, by the NKJV note) of Simon's wife's mother, and at sunset laid His hands on the sick and healed them, not letting the demons who called Him the Son of God (NU omits the Christ, by the NKJV note) speak (or say that they knew, by the NKJV note). At day He went to a deserted place, and when the crowd would keep Him He said He must preach the kingdom of God to the other cities, and He preached in the synagogues of Galilee (NU Judea, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:36; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 5:39; Luke 5:1–39","v":"Teaching the multitude from Simon's boat by the Lake of Gennesaret, Jesus bade him let down the nets after a fruitless night, and the catch filled both boats; Simon fell at His knees, saying he was a sinful man, and Jesus said that from then on he would catch men, and with James and John he left behind (by the NKJV note) all and followed Him. In a certain city a man full of leprosy begged (by the NKJV note) to be made clean, and Jesus touched him; the report spread, and He often withdrew into the wilderness to pray. Before Pharisees and teachers of the law from Galilee, Judea and Jerusalem, the power of the Lord present to heal (NU with Him to heal, by the NKJV note), men let a paralyzed man down through the tiling, and Jesus forgave his sins and bade him rise and go home. Levi the tax collector left all and gave Him a great feast, and to the Pharisees and their scribes who grumbled (NU But the Pharisees and their scribes, by the NKJV note; grumbled, by the NKJV note) He said He came to call sinners to repentance; asked why His disciples did not fast (NU makes it a statement, by the NKJV note), He spoke of the bridegroom, of a piece from a new garment (NU tears a piece from a new garment, by the NKJV note), of new wine in new wineskins (NU omits and both are preserved, by the NKJV note), and of those who find the old wine better (NU omits immediately, by the NKJV note; NU good, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:48; NKJV notes, note on Luke 6:49; 1 Samuel 21:6; Luke 6:1–49","v":"On a Sabbath (NU on a Sabbath, by the NKJV note) His disciples rubbed grain in their hands, and He answered the Pharisees with what David did (1 Samuel 21:6), for the Son of Man is Lord also of the Sabbath; on another Sabbath He healed a man whose right hand was withered (NU, M him, by the NKJV note; NU omits as whole as the other, by the NKJV note), asking whether it was lawful to save life or to destroy (M to kill, by the NKJV note), and the scribes and Pharisees were filled with rage. After a night of prayer on the mountain He chose twelve, whom He named apostles, among them Simon called the Zealot and Judas the son of James. On a level place He healed a great multitude from Judea, Jerusalem and the coast of Tyre and Sidon, and taught His disciples: blessed are the poor, the hungry who will be filled (satisfied, by the NKJV note) and those who weep, and woe to the rich, the full and those of whom all (M omits all, by the NKJV note; NU, M omit to you, by the NKJV note) speak well; love your enemies, give, lend hoping (expecting, by the NKJV note) for nothing, and be merciful as the Father; judge not, for with the measure one uses it is measured back; the blind cannot lead the blind; a tree is known by its fruit, and out of the heart's treasure (NU omits treasure of his heart, by the NKJV note) the mouth speaks; and he who hears and does His sayings is like a house founded (NU well built, by the NKJV note) on the rock, while the house without a foundation fell (NU collapsed, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 7:34; Malachi 3:1; Luke 7:1–50","v":"At Capernaum a centurion, who loved the nation and built the Jews a synagogue, sent elders and friends to ask Jesus to heal his dying servant, not thinking himself worthy to come, and Jesus marveled at his faith; the servant was found well (NU omits who had been sick, by the NKJV note). The next day at the gate of Nain Jesus had compassion on a widow, touched the coffin of her only son and raised him, and fear seized (by the NKJV note) all, who said a great prophet had risen. John sent two disciples to ask whether He was the Coming One (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note; should we expect, by the NKJV note); He cured many of illnesses (by the NKJV note) and bade them tell John what they saw, blessed is he who is not offended (caused to stumble, by the NKJV note), and He told the multitudes that John was the messenger of whom Malachi wrote (Malachi 3:1), none greater (NU none greater than John, by the NKJV note); the tax collectors justified (declared the righteousness of, by the NKJV note) God, but the Pharisees and lawyers (the experts in the law, by the NKJV note) rejected His will, like children in the marketplace (NU, M omit And the Lord said, by the NKJV note) calling John demon-possessed and the Son of Man a glutton and winebibber (an excessive drinker, by the NKJV note). In the house of Simon the Pharisee a sinful woman washed His feet with her tears and anointed them; He told Simon of two debtors forgiven, the one who owed more loving more, and said her many sins were forgiven, for she loved much, and her faith had saved her"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:36; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:48; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:49; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:51; NKJV notes, note on Luke 8:54; Luke 8:1–56","v":"He went through every city and village proclaiming the good news (by the NKJV note) with the twelve and certain women healed of evil spirits and sicknesses (by the NKJV note), Mary called Magdalene, Joanna the wife of Chuza, Herod's steward, Susanna and many others, who provided for Him (NU, M them, by the NKJV note) from their possessions (by the NKJV note). He told the parable of the sower, whose seed on good ground yielded a hundredfold (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note), and explained that the seed is the word of God, the mysteries (secret or hidden truths, by the NKJV note) given to them; those on the rock fall away in testing (by the NKJV note), those on good ground bear fruit with patience (endurance, by the NKJV note); one lights a lamp to set it on a lampstand, and from him who has not, even what he seems (thinks that he has, by the NKJV note) to have will be taken. His mother and brothers are those who hear and do the word. In the storm on the lake, the boat in danger (by the NKJV note), He rebuked the wind and asked where their faith was. In the country of the Gadarenes (NU Gerasenes, by the NKJV note) He cast the demons named Legion out of a man who long had worn no clothes (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note) and lived in the tombs, and they went into the swine; the man, healed (delivered, by the NKJV note), proclaimed what Jesus had done. A woman with a flow of blood twelve years touched the border of His garment and was healed, and Peter (NU omits and those with him, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 45, by the NKJV note) wondered that He asked who touched Him (NU omits be of good cheer, by the NKJV note); Jairus's only daughter, about twelve, died, but Jesus (NU adds anymore, by the NKJV note; NU adds with Him, by the NKJV note; NU, M Peter, John, and James, by the NKJV note; NU omits put them all outside, by the NKJV note) took her by the hand, and her spirit returned"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:33; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:50; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:54; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:55; NKJV notes, note on Luke 9:56; Luke 9:1–62","v":"Jesus gave the twelve power over all demons and sent them to preach the kingdom of God and heal, taking nothing for the journey. Herod the tetrarch, perplexed that some said John had risen, said he had beheaded John and sought to see Jesus. The apostles returned, and in a deserted place belonging to Bethsaida He fed about five thousand men with five loaves and two fish, all filled (satisfied, by the NKJV note), and twelve baskets left. As He prayed alone Peter confessed Him the Christ of God, and He foretold that the Son of Man would suffer, be rejected and killed and be raised the third day, and bade them take up their cross daily (M omits daily, by the NKJV note). About eight days later, as He prayed on the mountain, His face was altered and His robe glistened, and Moses and Elijah spoke of His decease (death, lit. departure, by the NKJV note) at Jerusalem; Peter spoke of three tabernacles (tents, by the NKJV note), and a voice from the cloud said, This is My beloved Son (NU My Son, the Chosen One, by the NKJV note), Hear Him. The next day He healed a boy, an only child, whose spirit the disciples could not cast out, calling them a faithless (unbelieving, by the NKJV note) generation that He must bear (put up, by the NKJV note) with, and foretold His betrayal; to their dispute who was greatest He set a child by Him, and forbade them to hinder one who cast out demons in His name (NU you, your, by the NKJV note). When the time came for Him to be received up He set His face to go to Jerusalem; a Samaritan village would not receive Him, and He rebuked James and John, who would call down fire (NU omits just as Elijah did, by the NKJV note; NU omits the rest of v. 55, by the NKJV note; NU omits v. 56, by the NKJV note); on the road He told three that the Son of Man has nowhere to lay His head, that the dead bury their own dead, and that no one who looks back from the plow is fit for the kingdom of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:20; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:22; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:41; Luke 10:1–42","v":"After these things the Lord appointed seventy (NU seventy-two, by the NKJV note) others and sent them two by two before Him, as lambs among wolves, carrying nothing, to heal and say the kingdom of God had come near, and to wipe off the dust (NU our feet, by the NKJV note) where they were not received, for it would be more tolerable (NU, M omit But, by the NKJV note) for Sodom; woe to Chorazin and Bethsaida, and Capernaum will be brought down to Hades (NU reads it as a question, by the NKJV note). The seventy returned with joy that the demons were subject to them, and He said He saw Satan fall like lightning, but they were to rejoice (NU, M omit rather, by the NKJV note) that their names were written in heaven. He rejoiced in the Spirit and thanked the Father, Lord of heaven and earth (M And turning to the disciples He said, by the NKJV note), and blessed the eyes that saw what they saw. To a lawyer (expert in the law, by the NKJV note) who asked how to inherit eternal life He told of a man who fell among thieves (robbers, by the NKJV note) on the road from Jerusalem to Jericho, passed by a priest and a Levite, and cared for by a Samaritan (NU omits when he departed, by the NKJV note), and bade him go and do likewise. In a village Martha welcomed Him, and her sister Mary sat at His (NU the Lord's, by the NKJV note) feet; Jesus (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note) told Martha, troubled with much serving, that Mary had chosen the good part"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:22; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:41; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:44; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:53; NKJV notes, note on Luke 11:54; Genesis 4:8; 2 Chronicles 24:20–22; Luke 11:1–54","v":"Asked to teach them to pray, as John taught his disciples, He gave them a prayer to the Father (NU omits Our and in heaven and the rest of v. 2 and But deliver us from the evil one, by the NKJV notes), told of a friend at midnight who gives for persistence, and bade them ask, seek and knock, for the Father gives the Holy Spirit to those who ask (NU reads v. 11 otherwise, by the NKJV note). When He cast out a mute demon some said He did it by Beelzebub (NU, M Beelzebul, by the NKJV note) and others sought a sign: a kingdom divided falls, He casts out demons by the finger of God, a stronger one overcomes the strong man and divides his spoils (plunder, by the NKJV note), and an unclean spirit returns with seven worse; blessed are those who hear the word of God and keep it; no sign will be given but the sign of Jonah (NU omits the prophet, by the NKJV note), for the queen of the South and the men of Nineveh will condemn this generation, and the lamp of the body is the eye, good (clear, or healthy, by the NKJV note) or bad (evil, or unhealthy, by the NKJV note). At a Pharisee's table He said the Pharisees cleanse the outside of the cup while within is greed (lit. eager grasping or robbery, by the NKJV note), and should give alms of what they have (or what is inside, by the NKJV note); He pronounced woes on the Pharisees, who love the best seats (or places of honor, by the NKJV note), and hidden graves (NU omits scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites, by the NKJV note), and on the lawyers, who load burdens, build the prophets' tombs and take away the key of knowledge, so that the blood of the prophets from Abel to Zechariah would be required of this generation; the scribes and Pharisees began to assail Him (NU And when He left there, by the NKJV note; NU omits and seeking and that they might accuse Him, by the NKJV notes)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:26; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 12:42; Luke 12:1–59","v":"In the meantime, before an innumerable multitude, He warned His disciples against the leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy, and bade them fear Him who can cast into hell, for five sparrows sold for two copper coins (Gr. assarion, a coin worth about a sixteenth of a denarius, by the NKJV note) are not forgotten before God, and the Holy Spirit would teach them what to say. To one who asked Him to bid his brother divide the inheritance He said to beware of covetousness (NU all covetousness, by the NKJV note) and told of a rich fool whose soul was required of him that night. He bade His disciples not be anxious (worry, by the NKJV note), for God feeds the ravens and clothes the lilies more finely than Solomon was arrayed (clothed, by the NKJV note), but to seek the kingdom of God (NU His kingdom, and these things, by the NKJV note), and to be ready like servants waiting for their master, for the Son of Man comes at an hour not expected, as a thief comes to a house whose master would have watched (NU not have allowed it to be broken into, by the NKJV note); to Peter's question He told of the faithful steward who gives the household its food in due season (at the right time, by the NKJV note). He came to send fire on the earth and division, and He bade the multitudes discern this time and settle with their adversary on the way"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:32; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:35; Luke 13:1–35","v":"Told of the Galileans whose blood Pilate had mingled (mixed, by the NKJV note) with their sacrifices, He said that neither they nor the eighteen on whom the tower in Siloam fell were worse sinners than others, but unless they repented all would likewise perish, and told of a fig tree that bore no fruit for three years and was spared one year more, though it used up (wasted, by the NKJV note) the ground (NU reads v. 9 otherwise, by the NKJV note). In a synagogue on the Sabbath He loosed a woman bent over eighteen years by a spirit of infirmity, who could not raise (straighten, by the NKJV note) herself up, and answered the indignant ruler of the synagogue, Hypocrite (NU, M Hypocrites, by the NKJV note); He likened the kingdom of God to a mustard seed that grew into a large (NU omits large, by the NKJV note) tree and to leaven (yeast, by the NKJV note) hidden in three measures (Gr. sata, about 2 pecks in all, by the NKJV note) of meal. Journeying toward Jerusalem He bade them strive to enter through the narrow gate. On that very day (NU In that very hour, by the NKJV note) some Pharisees warned Him that Herod wanted to kill Him; He bade them tell that fox that the third day He would be perfected (Resurrected, by the NKJV note), and lamented over Jerusalem, whose house is left to her desolate (NU, M omit assuredly, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:35; Luke 14:1–35","v":"On a Sabbath at the house of one of the rulers of the Pharisees, watched closely, He asked the lawyers and Pharisees whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath (NU adds or not, by the NKJV note), healed a man with dropsy, and asked which of them would not pull out a donkey (NU, M son, by the NKJV note) or an ox fallen into a pit. To the guests who chose the best places He told a parable, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note); He bade His host invite the poor, the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note), the lame and the blind, to be repaid at the resurrection of the just; and to one who blessed him who eats bread (M dinner, by the NKJV note) in the kingdom of God He told of a great supper whose guests made excuses, so that the master brought in the poor and the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note) and compelled others from the highways. To the great multitudes He said that whoever does not hate his own family and life, bear his cross and forsake all cannot be His disciple, and that salt that has lost its flavor is fit neither for the land nor for the dunghill (rubbish heap, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:22; Luke 15:1–32","v":"When the tax collectors and sinners drew near to hear Him and the Pharisees and scribes complained that He received (welcomed, by the NKJV note) sinners and ate with them, He told of a man who left the ninety-nine to seek one lost sheep, for there is more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine just (upright, by the NKJV note) persons; of a woman who swept the house for one lost silver coin (Gr. drachma, a valuable coin often worn in a ten-piece garland by married women, by the NKJV note); and of a younger son who wasted his share in prodigal (wasteful, by the NKJV note) living, longed for the pods (carob pods, by the NKJV note) the swine ate, and came home to a father who ran to meet him and bade his servants (NU Quickly bring, by the NKJV note) bring the best robe, while the angry older son was told that his brother had been dead and lived again"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:21; Luke 16:1–31","v":"He told His disciples of a steward accused of wasting (squandering, by the NKJV note) his master's goods, who lowered the debtors' bills, a hundred measures of oil (Gr. batos, the same as the Hebrew bath, 8 or 9 gallons each, by the NKJV note) to fifty and a hundred measures of wheat (Gr. koros, the same as the Hebrew kor, 10 or 12 bushels each, by the NKJV note) to eighty, and was commended for his shrewdness; they were to make friends by unrighteous mammon (lit., in Aramaic, wealth, by the NKJV note), that when they fail (NU it fails, by the NKJV note) they may be received into an everlasting home, and they cannot serve God and mammon. The Pharisees, lovers of money, derided (lit. turned up their nose at, by the NKJV note) Him; He said God knows their hearts, the law and the prophets were until John, and not one tittle (the smallest stroke in a Hebrew letter, by the NKJV note) of the law will fail, and He told of a rich man who fared sumptuously (lived in luxury, by the NKJV note) and a beggar named Lazarus who longed for the crumbs (NU what fell, by the NKJV note) from his table: Lazarus was carried by the angels to Abraham's bosom, the rich man was tormented in Hades, and Abraham said the rich man's brothers had Moses and the prophets"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 17:36; Luke 17:1–37","v":"He told the disciples that offenses (stumbling blocks, by the NKJV note) must come, but woe to him who offends (causes to stumble, by the NKJV note) one of these little ones, and that a brother who sins (NU omits against you, by the NKJV note) and repents, even seven times in a day (M omits to you, by the NKJV note), is to be forgiven; to the apostles, who asked Him to increase their faith, He said that faith as a mustard seed could uproot a mulberry tree, and that servants who have done all they were commanded (NU omits the rest of v. 9, M omits him, by the NKJV note) are unprofitable servants who have done their duty. Passing through the midst of Samaria and Galilee on the way to Jerusalem, He cleansed ten lepers, and only one, a Samaritan, returned to give thanks. He told the Pharisees that the kingdom of God does not come with observation, for it is within (in your midst, by the NKJV note) them (NU reverses here and there, by the NKJV note); He told the disciples that the Son of Man would come like the lightning (NU reverses here and there, by the NKJV note) but must first suffer, and that His day would be as the days of Noah and of Lot: the one on the housetop is not to come down for his goods (possessions, by the NKJV note), and of two men (or people, by the NKJV note) in one bed, two women grinding and two men in the field (NU, M omit v. 36, by the NKJV note), one will be taken and the other left"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:31; Luke 18:1–43","v":"He told a parable, that men always ought to pray and not lose heart, of an unjust judge who did not regard (respect, by the NKJV note) man but avenged (vindicated, by the NKJV note) a widow who kept asking him to get her justice from (avenge her on, by the NKJV note) her adversary; and to some who trusted in their own righteousness, of a Pharisee and a tax collector who went up to the temple to pray, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note). He bade the disciples let the infants come to Him; to a ruler who asked how to inherit eternal life He said to sell all and follow Him, for it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God; and to Peter, who said they had left all (NU our own, by the NKJV note), He promised many times more and eternal life. He told the twelve that all the prophets wrote of the Son of Man would be accomplished (fulfilled, by the NKJV note), His death and His rising the third day, but they understood none of it; and as He came near Jericho He gave sight to a blind man who cried out to Him as the Son of David"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:21; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:29; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:46; Luke 19:1–48","v":"Passing through Jericho, He looked up (NU omits and saw him, by the NKJV note) at Zacchaeus, a rich chief tax collector who had climbed a sycamore tree to see Him, and bade him make haste (hurry, by the NKJV note) and come down; he made haste (hurried, by the NKJV note) and received Him joyfully, while all complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note), and gave half his goods to the poor, for the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost. Near Jerusalem He told of a nobleman who gave his servants minas (Gr. mna, the same as the Hebrew minah, each worth about three months' salary, by the NKJV note) to trade with, and judged the one who called him an austere (a severe, by the NKJV note) man and hid his mina. Drawing near to Bethphage (M Bethsphage, by the NKJV note) and Bethany at Olivet, He sent two disciples for a colt and rode it, as the multitude of the disciples praised God; seeing the city He wept over it, foretelling its fall. In the temple He drove out those who bought and sold (NU were selling, saying, by the NKJV note), saying His house is (NU shall be, by the NKJV note) a house of prayer, and He taught there daily"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:9; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:10; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 20:31; Luke 20:1–47","v":"Asked by the chief priests, scribes and elders by what authority He acted, He asked whether John's baptism was from heaven, and they, reasoning why then (NU, M omit then, by the NKJV note) they had not believed him, said they did not know. He told the people of vinedressers (tenant farmers, by the NKJV note) who at vintage-time (lit. the season, by the NKJV note) beat the owner's servants and killed his beloved son, and the chief priests and scribes sought to seize Him but feared (M were afraid, by the NKJV note) the people. To spies who asked whether it was lawful to pay taxes to Caesar He said (NU omits Why do you test Me, by the NKJV note) to render (pay, by the NKJV note) to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's; to the Sadducees, who asked whose wife a woman married to seven brothers (NU omits the rest of v. 30, NU, M also left no children, by the NKJV notes) would be, that the sons of the resurrection neither marry nor die, and that God is the God of the living. He asked how the Christ is David's Son when David calls Him Lord, and warned His disciples against the scribes"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:5; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:8; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 21:36; Luke 21:1–38","v":"He saw the rich putting their gifts into the treasury and a poor widow putting in two mites (Gr. lepta, very small copper coins, by the NKJV note), and said she had put in more than all, for they gave of their abundance to the offerings for God (NU omits for God, by the NKJV note), but she all her livelihood. When some spoke of the temple, adorned (decorated, by the NKJV note) with beautiful stones, He said not one stone would be left upon another; asked when, He bade them not be deceived nor (NU omits Therefore, by the NKJV note) go after (follow, by the NKJV note) those who come in His name, and foretold wars, earthquakes, famines and signs from heaven, and before them persecution, when they were not to meditate beforehand what to answer (say in defense, by the NKJV note), for He would give them wisdom none could resist (withstand, by the NKJV note); Jerusalem surrounded by armies and trampled by Gentiles until the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled; signs in the sun, moon and stars, and the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory. He spoke of the fig tree and all the trees, and bade them take heed lest their hearts be weighed down with carousing (dissipation, by the NKJV note), and watch and pray to be counted worthy (NU have strength, by the NKJV note) to escape these things. By day He taught in the temple, and at night He stayed on Olivet"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:14; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:31; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:37; Luke 22:1–38","v":"As the Feast of Unleavened Bread drew near, the chief priests and scribes sought how to kill Him, and Satan entered Judas Iscariot, who agreed with the chief priests and captains to betray Him for money. On the Day of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover must be killed (sacrificed, by the NKJV note), He sent Peter and John to prepare it in a large furnished upper room. Sitting down with the twelve (NU omits twelve, by the NKJV note) apostles, He said He had desired to eat this Passover with them before He suffered, and gave them the cup, saying He would not drink of the fruit of the vine (NU adds from now on, by the NKJV note) until the kingdom of God comes; He gave them the bread as His body and, after supper, the cup as the new covenant in His blood, and said the hand of His betrayer was with Him on the table. When they disputed which of them was greatest, He said He was among them as One who serves, and bestowed on them a kingdom; He told Simon (NU omits And the Lord said, by the NKJV note) that Satan had asked for him and that he would deny Him three times before the rooster crowed that day; and He bade them now take money bag and sword, for He must be numbered with the transgressors, as was written, accomplished (fulfilled, by the NKJV note) in Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:43; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:57; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:60; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:61; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:64; NKJV notes, note on Luke 22:68; Luke 22:39–71","v":"On the Mount of Olives He bade them pray not to enter into temptation, and, kneeling a stone's throw away, prayed that the cup be taken from Him if it were the Father's will; an angel strengthened Him, and in agony His sweat became like drops of blood (NU brackets vv. 43 and 44 as not in the original text, by the NKJV note). Judas came with a multitude to kiss Him; one of them cut off the right ear of the high priest's servant, and Jesus healed it. At the high priest's house Peter denied Him (NU it, by the NKJV note) three times, and as the rooster (NU, M a rooster, by the NKJV note) crowed the Lord looked at him, and he remembered His word (NU adds today, by the NKJV note) and wept bitterly. The men who held Jesus blindfolded and struck Him (NU And having blindfolded Him, they asked Him, by the NKJV note); at daybreak the council asked whether He was the Christ, and He said they would not answer (NU omits also, and the rest of v. 68, by the NKJV notes), but that the Son of Man would sit at the right hand of the power of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:15; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:23; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:25; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:34; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:38; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:39; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:42; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:44; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:45; NKJV notes, note on Luke 23:51; Luke 23:1–56","v":"Before Pilate they accused Him of perverting the (NU our, by the NKJV note) nation and forbidding taxes to Caesar; Pilate found no fault in Him and, hearing of Galilee (NU omits of Galilee, by the NKJV note), sent Him to Herod, who with his men of war (troops, by the NKJV note) mocked Him and sent Him back. Pilate said neither he nor Herod (NU he sent Him back to us, by the NKJV note) had found Him deserving of death, and would have released Him at the feast (NU omits v. 17, by the NKJV note), but the voices of the people and of the chief priests (NU omits and of the chief priests, by the NKJV note) prevailed, and he released to them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note) Barabbas and delivered Jesus to their will. Simon a Cyrenian bore the cross; Jesus told the women who mourned Him to weep for themselves; at Calvary He was crucified between two criminals, asked the Father to forgive them (NU brackets the first sentence as a later addition, by the NKJV note), and over Him was written (NU omits written and in letters of Greek, Latin, and Hebrew, by the NKJV note) This is the King of the Jews; one criminal blasphemed Him (NU Are You not the Christ, by the NKJV note), but the other asked (NU Jesus, remember me, by the NKJV note) to be remembered and was promised Paradise. From about the sixth hour (NU adds already, by the NKJV note) darkness covered the earth, the sun was darkened (NU obscured, by the NKJV note) and the veil of the temple was torn in two (the middle, by the NKJV note); He committed His spirit to the Father and breathed His last. Joseph of Arimathea, who had not consented to the council's deed and was waiting (NU who was waiting, by the NKJV note) for the kingdom of God, laid Him in a new tomb, and the women prepared spices and rested on the Sabbath"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:4; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:11; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:12; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:17; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:27; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:28; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:35; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:40; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:42; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:46; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:49; NKJV notes, note on Luke 24:53; Luke 24:1–53","v":"Very early on the first day of the week the women (NU omits and certain other women with them, by the NKJV note) came with spices and found the tomb empty; as they were greatly (NU omits greatly, by the NKJV note) perplexed, two men in shining garments told them He was risen, as He had said in Galilee, but the apostles took their words for idle tales (nonsense, by the NKJV note); Peter ran to the tomb and saw the linen cloths lying (NU omits lying, by the NKJV note) by themselves. That day two going to Emmaus, seven miles (lit. 60 stadia, by the NKJV note) from Jerusalem, walked sad (NU they stood still, looking sad, by the NKJV note) with Jesus unrecognized; He expounded (explained, by the NKJV note) the Scriptures concerning Himself, made as if (acted as if, by the NKJV note) to go farther, and was known (recognized, by the NKJV note) to them in the breaking of bread; returning, they found the eleven saying He had appeared to Simon. Jesus stood among them, showed them His hands and His feet (some printed New Testaments omit v. 40, though it is found in nearly all Greek manuscripts, by the NKJV note), ate broiled fish and honeycomb (NU omits and some honeycomb, by the NKJV note), and said it was written that the Christ should suffer and rise (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note) and repentance be preached to all nations, and bade them tarry in the city (NU omits of Jerusalem, by the NKJV note) for the Promise of His Father. At Bethany He blessed them and was carried up into heaven, and they were continually in the temple praising (NU omits praising and, by the NKJV note) and blessing God (NU omits Amen, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 1:3","v":"an orderly account"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:3; Luke 1:1–4","v":"It is written as an orderly account to most excellent Theophilus, by one who had perfect understanding (lit. accurately followed, by the NKJV note) of all things from the first, as those who were eyewitnesses and ministers of the word delivered the things fulfilled (or most surely believed, by the NKJV note) among them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 1:5–80; 2:1–52","v":"It tells first of the births of John the Baptist and of Jesus, side by side, with the songs and prophecies spoken at them"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 3:23–38","v":"It gives Jesus's genealogy, as was supposed, from Joseph back through Nathan the son of David, Abraham and Adam to God"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Luke 1:4","v":"That Theophilus might know the certainty of the things in which he had been instructed"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00617":{"k":"object","n":"The song of Mary","d":"Mary's song at Elizabeth's greeting, that her soul magnifies the Lord, who has regarded her lowly state, scattered the proud, exalted the lowly and helped Israel, as He spoke to Abraham (Luke 1:46–55)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; Luke 1:46–55","v":"Her soul magnifies (declares the greatness of, by the NKJV note) the Lord and her spirit rejoices in God her Savior, who regarded her lowly state, so that all generations will call her blessed; He who is mighty has done great things, His mercy is on those who fear Him, He has scattered the proud, put down the mighty and exalted the lowly, filled the hungry and sent the rich away empty, and helped His servant Israel, as He spoke to Abraham and his seed forever"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:46; Luke 1:46","v":"My soul magnifies the Lord"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 1:46","e":"PER-03009"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 1:46–55","e":"EVT-02921"}],"nm":{"EVT-02921":"Mary visits Elizabeth","PER-03009":"Mary"}},"OBJ-00618":{"k":"object","n":"The prophecy of Zacharias","d":"Zacharias's prophecy, when he was filled with the Holy Spirit, blessing the Lord God of Israel for raising up a horn of salvation in the house of David, and telling of his son, the prophet of the Highest (Luke 1:67–79)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 1:78; Luke 1:68–79","v":"The Lord God of Israel has visited and redeemed His people and raised up a horn of salvation in the house of His servant David, as the prophets spoke, to save them from their enemies and remember His covenant and His oath to Abraham, that they might serve Him without fear; and the child will be called the prophet of the Highest, to go before the Lord and prepare His ways, through God's tender mercy, by which the Dayspring (lit. Dawn; the Messiah, by the NKJV note) from on high has visited (NU shall visit, by the NKJV note) them, to give light to those in darkness and guide their feet into the way of peace"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 1:68","v":"Blessed is the Lord God of Israel"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 1:67","e":"PER-03092"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 1:64, 67","e":"EVT-02923","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"When his mouth was opened at the naming he spoke, praising God (Luke 1:64), and, filled with the Holy Spirit, he prophesied (1:67)."}],"nm":{"EVT-02923":"John is circumcised and named","PER-03092":"Zacharias"}},"OBJ-00619":{"k":"object","n":"The manger in which Jesus was laid","d":"The manger in Bethlehem in which Mary laid her firstborn Son, wrapped in swaddling cloths, and in which the shepherds found Him (Luke 2:7; 2:12; 2:16)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 2:7; Luke 2:7, 12, 16","v":"a manger"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 2:7","v":"Mary wrapped Him in swaddling cloths and laid Him in it, because there was no room for them in the inn","at":"EVT-02770"}],"nm":{"EVT-02770":"Birth of Jesus"}},"OBJ-00620":{"k":"object","n":"The song of Simeon","d":"Simeon's blessing of God in the temple, that he could depart in peace, for his eyes had seen God's salvation, a light to the Gentiles and the glory of Israel (Luke 2:29–32)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 2:29–32","v":"The Lord lets His servant depart in peace, according to His word, for his eyes have seen His salvation, prepared before all peoples, a light to bring revelation to the Gentiles and the glory of His people Israel"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 2:29","v":"Lord, now You are letting Your servant depart in peace"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 2:28–29","e":"PER-03096"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 2:28–32","e":"EVT-02928"}],"nm":{"EVT-02928":"Jesus is presented to the Lord in the temple","PER-03096":"Simeon"}},"OBJ-00621":{"k":"object","n":"Simon's boat","d":"The boat of Simon from which Jesus taught the multitudes by the Lake of Gennesaret, and which with its partners' boat was filled with fish (Luke 5:2–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 5:3","v":"one of the boats, which was Simon's"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 5:3–7","v":"From it Jesus taught the multitudes; at His word the nets let down from it took so great a number of fish that the net was breaking, and it and its partners' boat were filled and began to sink","at":"EVT-02934"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 5:3","e":"PER-03015"}],"nm":{"EVT-02934":"Jesus teaches from Simon's boat and the disciples take a great catch of fish","PER-03015":"Simon"}},"OBJ-00622":{"k":"object","n":"The flask of the woman in Simon's house","d":"The alabaster flask of fragrant oil with which the sinful woman anointed Jesus's feet in the Pharisee's house (Luke 7:37–38; 7:46)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 7:37","v":"an alabaster flask of fragrant oil"},{"p":"feature","r":"Luke 7:38, 46","v":"She anointed His feet with its fragrant oil","at":"EVT-02938"},{"p":"owner","r":"Luke 7:37","e":"PER-03144"}],"nm":{"EVT-02938":"A sinful woman anoints Jesus's feet in Simon the Pharisee's house","PER-03144":"The sinful woman who anointed Jesus's feet"}},"OBJ-00623":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the two debtors","d":"A creditor freely forgave two debtors, one of five hundred denarii and one of fifty; the one forgiven more will love him more (Luke 7:41–43)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 7:41–43","v":"A creditor had two debtors, one owing five hundred denarii and the other fifty, and when they could not pay he forgave them both; Simon judged rightly that the one forgiven more would love him more"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 7:41","v":"a certain creditor who had two debtors"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 7:40–41","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 7:40–43","e":"EVT-02938"}],"nm":{"EVT-02938":"A sinful woman anoints Jesus's feet in Simon the Pharisee's house","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00624":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the good Samaritan","d":"A man going down from Jerusalem to Jericho fell among thieves; a priest and a Levite passed by, but a Samaritan had compassion, cared for him and paid for him at an inn (Luke 10:30–37)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Luke 10:35; Luke 10:30–37","v":"A man going down from Jerusalem to Jericho fell among thieves (robbers, by the NKJV note), who stripped and wounded him and left him half dead; a priest and a Levite came by and passed on the other side, but a Samaritan had compassion, bound up his wounds with oil and wine, set him on his own animal, brought him to an inn, and on the next day (NU omits when he departed, by the NKJV note) gave the innkeeper two denarii to care for him, promising to repay more; the neighbor was he who showed mercy"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 10:30","v":"A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 10:30","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 10:29–37","e":"EVT-02946"}],"nm":{"EVT-02946":"A lawyer asks Jesus what to do to inherit eternal life","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00625":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the friend at midnight","d":"A man who goes to his friend at midnight to borrow three loaves for a traveler is given what he needs because of his persistence (Luke 11:5–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 11:5–8","v":"A man goes to his friend at midnight to borrow three loaves for a friend come on a journey; though the friend in bed with his children will not rise because he is his friend, he rises and gives him all he needs because of his persistence"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 11:5","v":"Which of you shall have a friend, and go to him at midnight"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 11:5","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 11:5–8","e":"EVT-02948"}],"nm":{"EVT-02948":"Jesus teaches His disciples to pray","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00626":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the rich fool","d":"A rich man whose ground yielded plentifully planned greater barns and many years of ease, but God called him a fool, for that night his soul would be required of him (Luke 12:16–21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Luke 12:16–21","v":"A rich man whose ground yielded plentifully, having no room to store his crops, resolved to pull down his barns and build greater, and to tell his soul to take its ease, eat, drink and be merry for many years; but God called him a fool, for that night his soul would be required of him, and whose then would his goods be; so is he who lays up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 12:16","v":"The ground of a certain rich man yielded plentifully"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 12:16","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 12:15–21","e":"EVT-02952"}],"nm":{"EVT-02952":"One from the crowd asks Jesus to bid his brother divide the inheritance","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00627":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the barren fig tree","d":"A man who for three years found no fruit on the fig tree in his vineyard bade it be cut down, but the keeper asked that it be let alone one more year (Luke 13:6–9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Luke 13:9; Luke 13:6–9","v":"A man came seeking fruit on the fig tree planted in his vineyard and found none, and told the keeper of the vineyard that for three years he had found none, so it should be cut down, for why should it use up (waste, by the NKJV note) the ground; the keeper asked him to let it alone this year also while he dug around it and fertilized it, and if it bore fruit, well, but if not, after that he could cut it down (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 13:6","v":"A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 13:6","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 13:6–9","e":"EVT-02955"}],"nm":{"EVT-02955":"Jesus is told of the Galileans whose blood Pilate mingled with their sacrifices","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00628":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lowest place","d":"One invited to a wedding feast should not sit in the best place, lest a more honorable guest come and he be moved down with shame, but sit in the lowest place and be bidden go up higher (Luke 14:7–11)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:11; Luke 14:8–11","v":"One invited to a wedding feast should not sit down in the best place, lest one more honorable be invited, and the host ask him to give place, and he begin with shame to take the lowest place; he should sit in the lowest place, so that the host may bid him go up higher and he have glory before those at the table; for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note), and he who humbles himself will be exalted"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 14:8","v":"When you are invited by anyone to a wedding feast"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 14:7","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 14:7–11","e":"EVT-02962"}],"nm":{"EVT-02962":"Jesus teaches the guests and His host at the ruler's table","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00629":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the great supper","d":"A man who gave a great supper sent his servant to call the invited, who all made excuses, so he brought in the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind, and compelled others from the highways, and none of those invited would taste his supper (Luke 14:16–24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 14:21; Luke 14:16–24","v":"A certain man gave a great supper and invited many, and at supper time sent his servant to say that all was ready; but they all began to make excuses: one had bought a piece of ground and must see it, another five yoke of oxen to test, another had married a wife; the angry master sent the servant into the streets and lanes of the city to bring in the poor, the maimed (crippled, by the NKJV note), the lame and the blind, and, there being still room, into the highways and hedges to compel them to come in, that his house might be filled, for none of those invited would taste his supper"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 14:16","v":"A certain man gave a great supper and invited many"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 14:16","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 14:15–24","e":"EVT-02962"}],"nm":{"EVT-02962":"Jesus teaches the guests and His host at the ruler's table","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00630":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lost coin","d":"A woman with ten silver coins who loses one lights a lamp, sweeps the house and searches until she finds it, and calls her friends to rejoice; so there is joy before the angels of God over one sinner who repents (Luke 15:8–10)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:8; Luke 15:8–10","v":"A woman having ten silver coins (Gr. drachma, a valuable coin often worn in a ten-piece garland by married women, by the NKJV note) who loses one lights a lamp, sweeps the house and searches carefully until she finds it, and calls her friends and neighbors to rejoice with her; likewise there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner who repents"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 15:8","v":"what woman, having ten silver coins"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 15:8","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 15:8–10","e":"EVT-02964"}],"nm":{"EVT-02964":"Jesus tells the Pharisees and scribes of the lost sheep, the lost coin and the lost son","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00631":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the lost son","d":"A younger son who took his share and wasted it in a far country came home in want and was welcomed by his father with a feast, while the angry older son was told that his brother had been dead and lived again (Luke 15:11–32)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:16; NKJV notes, note on Luke 15:22; Luke 15:11–32","v":"A man had two sons, and the younger asked for the portion of goods that fell to him, and the father divided his livelihood between them; not many days after, the younger journeyed to a far country and wasted his possessions in prodigal (wasteful, by the NKJV note) living, and when he had spent all and a severe famine arose, he was sent to feed a citizen's swine and would gladly have eaten the pods (carob pods, by the NKJV note) they ate. Coming to himself, he went home to confess that he had sinned against heaven and his father and was no longer worthy to be called his son; while he was still a great way off his father had compassion, ran, fell on his neck and kissed him, and bade the servants (NU Quickly bring, by the NKJV note) bring the best robe, a ring and sandals and kill the fatted calf, for this son had been dead and lived again, had been lost and was found. The older son, coming from the field, was angry and would not go in, saying he had served many years and was never given a young goat, but his father said that all he had was his, and it was right to make merry, for his brother had been dead and lived again"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 15:11","v":"A certain man had two sons"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 15:11","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 15:11–32","e":"EVT-02964"}],"nm":{"EVT-02964":"Jesus tells the Pharisees and scribes of the lost sheep, the lost coin and the lost son","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00632":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the unjust steward","d":"A steward accused of wasting his master's goods lowered the debts owed to his master, so that the debtors would receive him, and the master commended him for his shrewdness (Luke 16:1–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:1; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:7; Luke 16:1–8","v":"A rich man's steward, accused of wasting (squandering, by the NKJV note) his goods, was told to give an account, for he could be steward no longer; unable to dig and ashamed to beg, he called his master's debtors and had one who owed a hundred measures of oil (Gr. batos, the same as the Hebrew bath, 8 or 9 gallons each, by the NKJV note) write fifty, and one who owed a hundred measures of wheat (Gr. koros, the same as the Hebrew kor, 10 or 12 bushels each, by the NKJV note) write eighty, that they might receive him into their houses; the master commended the unjust steward because he had dealt shrewdly, for the sons of this world deal more shrewdly with their own than the sons of light"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 16:1","v":"There was a certain rich man who had a steward"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 16:1","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 16:1–8","e":"EVT-02965"}],"nm":{"EVT-02965":"Jesus tells His disciples of the unjust steward","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00633":{"k":"object","n":"The rich man and Lazarus","d":"Jesus's account of a rich man and a beggar named Lazarus laid at his gate: at death Lazarus was carried to Abraham's bosom and the rich man was tormented in Hades, where Abraham told him that his brothers had Moses and the prophets (Luke 16:19–31)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:19; NKJV notes, note on Luke 16:21; Luke 16:19–31","v":"A rich man clothed in purple and fine linen fared sumptuously (lived in luxury, by the NKJV note) every day, and at his gate lay a beggar named Lazarus, full of sores, longing to be fed with the crumbs (NU what fell, by the NKJV note) from his table, while the dogs licked his sores. The beggar died and was carried by the angels to Abraham's bosom; the rich man died and was buried, and in torments in Hades saw Abraham afar off and Lazarus in his bosom, and begged that Lazarus might cool his tongue. Abraham answered that he had received his good things in his lifetime and Lazarus evil things, and that a great gulf was fixed between them; asked to send Lazarus to warn the rich man's five brothers, he said they had Moses and the prophets, and if they would not hear them, neither would they be persuaded though one rose from the dead"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 16:20","v":"a certain beggar named Lazarus"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 16:19","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 16:19–31","e":"EVT-02966"}],"nm":{"EVT-02966":"The Pharisees deride Jesus, and He tells of the rich man and Lazarus","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00634":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the persistent widow","d":"An unjust judge who did not fear God nor regard man avenged a widow because her continual coming troubled him; so God will avenge His elect who cry out to Him (Luke 18:1–8)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:2; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:3; NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:5; Luke 18:2–5","v":"In a certain city was a judge who did not fear God nor regard (respect, by the NKJV note) man, and a widow of that city kept coming to him, asking him to get her justice from (avenge me on, by the NKJV note) her adversary; for a while he would not, but afterward he said within himself that because she troubled him he would avenge (vindicate, by the NKJV note) her, lest her continual coming weary him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 18:2","v":"There was in a certain city a judge"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 18:1–2","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 18:1–8","e":"EVT-02972"}],"nm":{"EVT-02972":"Jesus tells the parable of the persistent widow","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00635":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector","d":"A Pharisee who thanked God he was not like other men, and a tax collector who would not raise his eyes but asked God's mercy, prayed in the temple, and the tax collector went down justified (Luke 18:9–14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 18:14; Luke 18:10–14","v":"Two men went up to the temple to pray, a Pharisee and a tax collector; the Pharisee stood and thanked God that he was not like other men, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or this tax collector, for he fasted twice a week and gave tithes of all he possessed; the tax collector, standing afar off, would not raise his eyes to heaven but beat his breast and asked God to be merciful to him, a sinner; he went down to his house justified rather than the other, for whoever exalts himself will be humbled (put down, by the NKJV note), and he who humbles himself will be exalted"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 18:10","v":"Two men went up to the temple to pray"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 18:9","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 18:9–14","e":"EVT-02973"}],"nm":{"EVT-02973":"Jesus tells the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00636":{"k":"object","n":"The parable of the minas","d":"A nobleman who went to receive a kingdom gave his servants a mina each to trade with, rewarded those who gained with cities, took the mina from the one who hid it, and slew the citizens who would not have him reign (Luke 19:11–27)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Luke 19:21; Luke 19:12–27","v":"A nobleman went into a far country to receive a kingdom and return, and gave ten of his servants ten minas (Gr. mna, the same as the Hebrew minah, each worth about three months' salary, by the NKJV note), bidding them do business till he came; but his citizens hated him and sent a delegation to say they would not have him reign over them. Returning with the kingdom, he called the servants: the one whose mina had earned ten minas was given authority over ten cities, and the one whose mina had earned five, over five; but one who had kept his mina in a handkerchief, fearing him as an austere (a severe, by the NKJV note) man, was judged out of his own mouth, and his mina was given to him who had ten, for to everyone who has more will be given, and from him who has not even what he has will be taken; and the enemies who would not have him reign were slain before him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Luke 19:12","v":"A certain nobleman went into a far country"},{"p":"made by","r":"Luke 19:11–12","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Luke 19:11–27","e":"EVT-02976"}],"nm":{"EVT-02976":"Jesus tells the parable of the minas","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00637":{"k":"object","n":"The Gospel according to John","d":"The book that begins, In the beginning was the Word: the Word made flesh, John the Baptist's witness to Him, the signs, teaching and works of Jesus, His death, His rising and His appearing to His disciples (John 1–21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 1:5; NKJV notes, note on John 1:9; NKJV notes, note on John 1:11; NKJV notes, note on John 1:12; NKJV notes, note on John 1:15; NKJV notes, note on John 1:16; NKJV notes, note on John 1:18; NKJV notes, note on John 1:27; NKJV notes, note on John 1:28; NKJV notes, note on John 1:30; NKJV notes, note on John 1:41; NKJV notes, note on John 1:42; NKJV notes, note on John 1:51; Isaiah 40:3; John 1:1–51","v":"In the beginning the Word was with God and was God; all things were made through Him, and in Him was life, the light of men, shining in the darkness, which did not comprehend (or overcome, by the NKJV note) it. John, a man sent from God, came to bear witness of the true Light, giving light to every man coming into the world (or, coming into the world, giving light to every man, by the NKJV note); the world did not know Him, and His own (His own things or domain, and His own people, by the NKJV notes) did not receive Him, but to those who believe in His name He gave the right (authority, by the NKJV note) to become children of God. The Word became flesh and dwelt among us, the only begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth; John cried that He who came after him was preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), for He was before him; of His fullness (NU For of His fullness, by the NKJV note) we have all received grace for grace, for whereas the law came through Moses, grace and truth came through Jesus Christ, and the only begotten Son (NU God, by the NKJV note), in the bosom of the Father, has declared God, whom no one has seen. To priests and Levites sent by the Jews from Jerusalem, John confessed that he was not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet, but the voice crying in the wilderness (Isaiah 40:3), and that One stood among them, coming after him and preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), whose sandal strap he was not worthy to loose; this was in Bethabara (NU, M Bethany, by the NKJV note) beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing. The next day he called Jesus the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world, preferred before him (ranks higher than I, by the NKJV note), and bore witness that he saw the Spirit descend from heaven like a dove and remain on Him, and that He is the Son of God. The next day two of John's disciples followed Jesus and remained with Him that day; one of them, Andrew, told his brother Simon that they had found the Messiah (lit. Anointed One, by the NKJV note), and Jesus said that Simon the son of Jonah (NU John, by the NKJV note) would be called Cephas, A Stone (Gr. Petros, usually translated Peter, by the NKJV note). The following day Jesus found Philip of Bethsaida and bade him follow Him, and Philip brought Nathanael, who, when Jesus said He had seen him under the fig tree, called Him the Son of God and the King of Israel, and was told that hereafter (NU omits hereafter, by the NKJV note) he would see heaven open and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of Man"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 2:11; NKJV notes, note on John 2:14; NKJV notes, note on John 2:17; NKJV notes, note on John 2:22; Psalms 69:9; John 2:1–25","v":"On the third day there was a wedding in Cana of Galilee; when the wine ran out the mother of Jesus told Him, and He answered that His hour had not yet come, but at His word the servants filled with water six waterpots of stone, set there for the purification of the Jews, and took to the master of the feast water made wine, the good wine kept until now: this beginning of signs Jesus did in Cana, and manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) His glory, and His disciples believed in Him. He went down to Capernaum with His mother, brothers and disciples, staying not many days, and at the Passover of the Jews went up to Jerusalem; finding in the temple those who sold oxen, sheep and doves and the money changers doing business (lit. sitting, by the NKJV note), He made a whip of cords and drove them all out, and bade those who sold doves not make His Father's house a house of merchandise, and His disciples remembered that it was written, Zeal for Your house has eaten (NU, M will eat, by the NKJV note) Me up (Psalm 69:9). Asked for a sign, He bade them destroy this temple, saying He would raise it up in three days; He spoke of the temple of His body, and when He had risen His disciples remembered that He had said this to them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note). At the feast many believed in His name, seeing His signs, but He did not commit Himself to them, for He knew what was in man"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 3:3; NKJV notes, note on John 3:13; NKJV notes, note on John 3:15; Numbers 21:8–9; John 3:1–36","v":"Nicodemus, a Pharisee and a ruler of the Jews, came to Jesus by night, owning Him a teacher come from God; Jesus told him that unless one is born again (or from above, by the NKJV note), of water and the Spirit, he cannot see or enter the kingdom of God; that no one has ascended to heaven but He who came down from heaven, the Son of Man who is in heaven (NU omits who is in heaven, by the NKJV note), who must be lifted up as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, that whoever believes in Him should not perish but (NU omits not perish but, by the NKJV note) have eternal life; that God, loving the world, gave His only begotten Son, sent not to condemn the world but that the world through Him might be saved; and that men loved darkness rather than light because their deeds were evil. After these things Jesus remained with His disciples in the land of Judea and baptized, while John, not yet thrown into prison, baptized in Aenon near Salim, where there was much water. When a dispute arose about purification and John's disciples told him that all were coming to Jesus, John said he was not the Christ but sent before Him; the friend of the bridegroom rejoices greatly at the bridegroom's voice, and so his joy was fulfilled: He must increase and John decrease; He who comes from above is above all, the Father has given all things into the Son's hand, and he who believes in the Son has everlasting life, while the wrath of God abides on him who does not"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 4:42; NKJV notes, note on John 4:46; John 4:1–54","v":"Knowing that the Pharisees had heard He made and baptized more disciples than John (though His disciples baptized, not He Himself), the Lord left Judea and departed again to Galilee, needing to go through Samaria. At Jacob's well by Sychar, a city of Samaria near the plot of ground Jacob gave to his son Joseph, He asked a woman of Samaria for a drink and told her of living water springing up into everlasting life; He told her of her five husbands, said the hour was coming when the true worshipers would worship the Father in spirit and truth, and told her that He was the Messiah. She left her waterpot and called the men of the city to come and see. In the meantime He told His disciples that His food was to do the will of Him who sent Him, and that the fields were already white for harvest. Many Samaritans of that city believed, because of her word and then of His own, and He stayed with them two days; they knew that He was indeed the Christ (NU omits the Christ, by the NKJV note), the Savior of the world. In Galilee, though a prophet has no honor in his own country, the Galileans who had been at the feast received Him. At Cana a nobleman (royal official, by the NKJV note) whose son lay at the point of death at Capernaum believed His word that his son lived, and found that the fever had left him at that same hour: the second sign Jesus did when He had come out of Judea into Galilee"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 5:2; NKJV notes, note on John 5:3; NKJV notes, note on John 5:16; NKJV notes, note on John 5:31; John 5:1–47","v":"After this there was a feast of the Jews, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem. By the Sheep Gate was a pool called in Hebrew Bethesda (NU Bethzatha, by the NKJV note), with five porches, in which a great multitude of sick people lay, blind, lame, paralyzed (withered, by the NKJV note), waiting for the moving of the water, for an angel went down at a certain time and stirred it, and whoever stepped in first was made well (NU omits the rest of v. 3 and all of v. 4, by the NKJV note). On the Sabbath Jesus bade a man who had an infirmity thirty-eight years rise, take up his bed and walk, and he was made well, and the Jews told him it was not lawful to carry his bed. Afterward Jesus found him in the temple and bade him sin no more, and he told the Jews it was Jesus who had made him well. For this the Jews persecuted Jesus and sought to kill Him (NU omits and sought to kill Him, by the NKJV note), because He had done these things on the Sabbath, and sought all the more to kill Him because He said God was His Father, making Himself equal with God. Jesus answered that the Son does what He sees the Father do; the Father has committed all judgment to the Son, that all should honor the Son as they honor the Father; he who hears His word and believes in Him who sent Him has everlasting life; the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God and live, and all in the graves will come forth, to the resurrection of life or of condemnation. If He bore witness of Himself His witness was not true (valid as testimony, by the NKJV note), but John, His works, the Father and the Scriptures bear witness of Him; Moses, in whom they trust, accuses them, for he wrote about Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 6:2; NKJV notes, note on John 6:11; NKJV notes, note on John 6:19; NKJV notes, note on John 6:22; NKJV notes, note on John 6:37; NKJV notes, note on John 6:41; NKJV notes, note on John 6:43; NKJV notes, note on John 6:45; NKJV notes, note on John 6:47; NKJV notes, note on John 6:55; NKJV notes, note on John 6:60; NKJV notes, note on John 6:61; NKJV notes, note on John 6:66; NKJV notes, note on John 6:69; John 6:1–71","v":"After these things Jesus went over the Sea of Galilee, which is the Sea of Tiberias, and a great multitude followed Him for the signs He did on the diseased (sick, by the NKJV note); the Passover, a feast of the Jews, was near. On the mountain He tested Philip, asking where they might buy bread; Philip said two hundred denarii worth would not suffice, and Andrew told of a lad with five barley loaves and two small fish. About five thousand men sat down where there was much grass, and Jesus gave thanks and distributed the loaves to the disciples, and they to those sitting down (NU omits to the disciples, and the disciples, by the NKJV note), and likewise the fish, as much as they wanted; the fragments filled twelve baskets, and the men said He was truly the Prophet who is to come into the world. Perceiving that they would take Him by force to make Him king, He departed to the mountain alone. His disciples went over the sea toward Capernaum in the dark, with a great wind blowing, and had rowed about three or four miles (lit. 25 or 30 stadia, by the NKJV note) when they saw Him walking on the sea; when they received Him, the boat was at once at the land. The next day the people saw that only one boat had been there, that one (NU omits that, by the NKJV note) which His disciples had entered (NU omits which His disciples had entered, by the NKJV note), and that Jesus had not gone in it; other boats came from Tiberias, and they came in boats to Capernaum seeking Him. He told them to labor not for the food which perishes but for that which endures to everlasting life, which the Son of Man would give. He said He is the bread of life come down from heaven, and will by no means (certainly not, by the NKJV note) cast out the one who comes to Him but will raise him up at the last day. The Jews complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note) that He said He came down from heaven, since they knew His father and mother. He bade them not murmur (Stop grumbling, by the NKJV note), and said that all would be taught by God, that everyone who has heard and learned (M hears and has learned, by the NKJV note) from the Father comes to Him, that he who believes in Him (NU omits in Me, by the NKJV note) has everlasting life, and that the bread He would give is His flesh, given for the life of the world. Unless they eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood they have no life in them, for His flesh is food indeed (NU true food, by the NKJV note) and His blood drink indeed (NU true drink, by the NKJV note). He taught this in the synagogue at Capernaum. Many of His disciples called it a hard (difficult, by the NKJV note) saying and complained (grumbled, by the NKJV note). He asked whether it offended them (make you stumble, by the NKJV note) and said it is the Spirit who gives life, and from that time many went back (or away, lit. to the back, by the NKJV note) and walked with Him no more. When He asked the twelve whether they also would go away, Simon Peter answered that He had the words of eternal life and was the Christ, the Son of the living God (NU Holy One of God, by the NKJV note). Jesus said one of the twelve He had chosen was a devil, speaking of Judas Iscariot the son of Simon, who would betray Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:1; NKJV notes, note on John 7:8; NKJV notes, note on John 7:16; NKJV notes, note on John 7:26; NKJV notes, note on John 7:29; NKJV notes, note on John 7:33; NKJV notes, note on John 7:39; NKJV notes, note on John 7:40; NKJV notes, note on John 7:50; NKJV notes, note on John 7:52; NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; John 7:1–53","v":"After these things Jesus walked in Galilee, not wanting to walk in Judea, because the Jews (the ruling authorities, by the NKJV note) sought to kill Him. With the Jews' Feast of Tabernacles at hand, His brothers, who did not believe in Him, bade Him go into Judea and show Himself to the world; He said His time had not yet come and that He was not yet (NU omits yet, by the NKJV note) going up to the feast, and He remained in Galilee. When they had gone up, He also went up, as it were in secret. While the people murmured, some saying He was good and others that He deceived the people, about the middle of the feast He taught in the temple, and to the Jews who marveled at His learning He answered (NU, M So Jesus, by the NKJV note) that His doctrine was His who sent Him; none of them kept the law Moses gave, and they circumcised on the Sabbath yet were angry that He made a man completely well on the Sabbath, so they should judge with righteous judgment and not by appearance. Some from Jerusalem asked whether the rulers knew He was truly (NU omits truly, by the NKJV note) the Christ; He cried out that He had not come of Himself, but (NU, M omit But, by the NKJV note) knew Him who sent Him; no one laid a hand on Him, for His hour had not yet come, and many believed in Him. The Pharisees and chief priests sent officers to take Him, and He told them (NU, M omit to them, by the NKJV note) He would be with them a little while longer and then go where they could not come. On the last day, that great day of the feast, He cried out that anyone who thirsts should come to Him and drink, speaking of the Spirit whom those believing (NU who believed, by the NKJV note) in Him would receive, for the Holy (NU omits Holy, by the NKJV note) Spirit was not yet given. Many (NU some, by the NKJV note) said He was the Prophet, others the Christ, and others asked whether the Christ would come out of Galilee and not from Bethlehem, and the people were divided. The officers came back saying no man ever spoke like this Man; the Pharisees called the crowd that does not know the law accursed, and when Nicodemus, who came to Jesus (lit. Him, by the NKJV note) by night (NU before, by the NKJV note), asked whether their law judges a man before it hears him, they answered that no prophet has arisen (NU is to rise, by the NKJV note) out of Galilee. And everyone went to his own house (NU brackets 7:53–8:11 as not in the original text, though they are present in over 900 manuscripts of John, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; NKJV notes, note on John 8:2; NKJV notes, note on John 8:4; NKJV notes, note on John 8:5; NKJV notes, note on John 8:6; NKJV notes, note on John 8:7; NKJV notes, note on John 8:9; NKJV notes, note on John 8:10; NKJV notes, note on John 8:11; NKJV notes, note on John 8:13; NKJV notes, note on John 8:28; NKJV notes, note on John 8:38; NKJV notes, note on John 8:54; NKJV notes, note on John 8:59; John 8:1–59","v":"Jesus went to the Mount of Olives, and early (M very early, by the NKJV note) in the morning He came again into the temple and taught all the people. The scribes and Pharisees set before Him a woman, saying she was caught (M we found this woman, by the NKJV note) in adultery in the very act and that Moses in the law (M in our law Moses, by the NKJV note) commanded that such should be stoned (NU, M to stone such, by the NKJV note), and asked what He said (M adds about her, by the NKJV note), testing Him; He stooped and wrote on the ground with His finger, as though He did not hear (NU, M omit as though He did not hear, by the NKJV note), raised Himself up (M He looked up, by the NKJV note) and bade the one among them without sin throw a stone at her first. Being convicted by their conscience (NU, M omit being convicted by their conscience, by the NKJV note), they went out one by one, beginning with the oldest; seeing no one but the woman (NU omits and saw no one but the woman; M He saw her and said, by the NKJV note), He asked where her accusers (NU, M omit of yours, by the NKJV note) were, did not condemn her, and bade her go and sin no more (NU, M add from now on, by the NKJV note); the passage 7:53–8:11 is bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, though present in over 900 manuscripts of John (by the NKJV note on 7:53). Jesus said He was the light of the world; when the Pharisees said His witness of Himself was not true (valid as testimony, by the NKJV note), He said the Father who sent Him bore witness of Him. He spoke in the treasury while teaching in the temple, and no one seized Him, because His hour had not yet come. He said that where He was going they could not come, and that when they lifted up (Crucify, by the NKJV note) the Son of Man they would know that He is He; and many believed in Him. To those Jews who believed Him He said the truth would make them free; when they said they were Abraham's descendants He said they did what they had seen (NU heard from, by the NKJV note) with their father, and were of their father the devil, a murderer from the beginning and a liar. When the Jews said He had a demon, He said it was His Father who honored Him, of whom they said that He was their (NU, M our, by the NKJV note) God, that Abraham rejoiced to see His day, and that before Abraham was, I AM; they took up stones to throw at Him, but He hid Himself and went out of the temple, going through the midst of them (NU omits the rest of v. 59, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 9:4; NKJV notes, note on John 9:8; NKJV notes, note on John 9:9; NKJV notes, note on John 9:11; NKJV notes, note on John 9:16; NKJV notes, note on John 9:34; NKJV notes, note on John 9:35; John 9:1–41","v":"As Jesus passed by He saw a man blind from birth. His disciples asked whether this man or his parents had sinned, that he was born blind; He answered that neither had, but that the works of God should be revealed in him: He must (NU We, by the NKJV note) work the works of Him who sent Him while it is day, and as long as He is in the world He is the light of the world. He made clay with His saliva, anointed the man's eyes and sent him to wash in the pool of Siloam (which is translated, Sent), and he came back seeing. The neighbors and those who had seen that he was blind (NU a beggar, by the NKJV note) asked whether this was he who sat and begged; some said it was, others that he was like him (NU No, but he is like him, by the NKJV note), and he said, I am he, and told how a Man called Jesus had sent him to the pool of Siloam (NU omits the pool of, by the NKJV note) to wash, and he received sight. They brought him to the Pharisees; it was a Sabbath when Jesus made the clay. The Pharisees were divided, some saying this Man was not from God because He did not keep (observe, by the NKJV note) the Sabbath; asked again, the man told them, He is a prophet. The Jews did not believe he had been blind until they called his parents, who, fearing the Jews, said only that he was their son, born blind, and of age to be asked; for the Jews had agreed already that whoever confessed Him as Christ would be put out of the synagogue. Called again and bidden to give God the glory, the man said that though he was blind, now he saw, and that had this Man not been from God He could have done nothing; they reviled him and cast him out (excommunicated him, by the NKJV note). Jesus found him and asked whether he believed in the Son of God (NU Man, by the NKJV note); he said, Lord, I believe, and worshiped Him. Jesus said He came into this world for judgment, to give sight to those who do not see and to make blind those who see, and to the Pharisees who asked whether they were blind also He said that because they claim to see, their sin remains"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 10:8; NKJV notes, note on John 10:12; NKJV notes, note on John 10:20; NKJV notes, note on John 10:24; NKJV notes, note on John 10:26; NKJV notes, note on John 10:38; Psalms 82:6; John 10:1–42","v":"Jesus told of the sheepfold: whoever climbs in some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber, but the shepherd comes in by the door, calls his own by name and leads them out, and they follow, knowing his voice; they did not understand this illustration. He said He is the door of the sheep, and all who ever came before Him (M omits before Me, by the NKJV note) are thieves and robbers; whoever enters by Him will be saved, and He came that they may have life more abundantly. He is the good shepherd, who gives His life for the sheep, as the hireling (hired man, by the NKJV note) who flees from the wolf does not; He has other sheep not of this fold, and there will be one flock and one shepherd; He lays down His life of Himself, that He may take it again, and this command He received from His Father. The Jews were divided again, many saying He had a demon and was mad (insane, by the NKJV note), others asking whether a demon can open the eyes of the blind. At the Feast of Dedication in Jerusalem, in winter, as He walked in the temple in Solomon's porch, the Jews surrounded Him, asking how long He would keep them in doubt (suspense, by the NKJV note) and bidding Him say plainly whether He was the Christ. He answered that the works He did in His Father's name bore witness of Him, but they did not believe because they were not of His sheep, as He had said to them (NU omits as I said to you, by the NKJV note); His sheep hear His voice, no one can snatch them out of His Father's hand, and I and My Father are one. They took up stones again to stone Him for blasphemy, saying that He, being a Man, made Himself God; He answered from their law, I said, You are gods, and that the Scripture cannot be broken, and bade them believe the works, that they may know and believe (NU understand, by the NKJV note) that the Father is in Him and He in the Father. They sought again to seize Him, but He escaped out of their hand and withdrew once more across the Jordan to where John had baptized at first, and remained there; many came to Him, saying that John did no sign yet all he had said of this Man was true, and there many believed in Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 11:18; NKJV notes, note on John 11:30; NKJV notes, note on John 11:31; NKJV notes, note on John 11:41; NKJV notes, note on John 11:50; John 11:1–57","v":"Lazarus of Bethany, the town of Mary and her sister Martha, was sick; this Mary is the one who, with fragrant oil, anointed the Lord and used her hair to wipe His feet. The sisters sent word to Jesus, who said the sickness was 'not unto death' but for God's glory, remained two more days where He was, and then set out for Judea again, though the Jews had lately sought to stone Him; He said Lazarus slept, and then plainly that he was dead, and Thomas, called the Twin, said they should go and die with Him. When Jesus came, Lazarus had been four days in the tomb; Bethany was about two miles (lit. 15 stadia, by the NKJV note) from Jerusalem, and many Jews had come to comfort the sisters. Martha met Jesus, who said He was the resurrection and the life, and she believed He was the Christ, the Son of God. Jesus had not entered the town but was (NU was still, by the NKJV note) at the spot where Martha had met Him, and Mary came to Him there, the Jews following and saying she was on her way to weep at the tomb (NU supposing that she was going, by the NKJV note); seeing her weep, Jesus 'groaned in the spirit', and He wept. At the tomb, a cave with a stone against it, the stone was removed from where the dead man lay (NU omits from the place where the dead man was lying, by the NKJV note); Jesus thanked the Father for hearing Him and called aloud, Lazarus, come forth, and Lazarus came out bound with graveclothes. Many of the Jews believed in Him, while some told the Pharisees; the chief priests and Pharisees gathered a council, fearing the Romans would take away their place and nation, and Caiaphas, that year's high priest, said it was expedient for us (NU you, by the NKJV note) for one man to die for the people rather than have the whole nation perish, in this prophesying Jesus's death for the nation and the gathering into one of God's scattered children; from that day they plotted His death. Jesus went to the city called Ephraim near the wilderness and remained there with His disciples; as the Jews' Passover drew near, many who went up to Jerusalem to purify themselves sought Him in the temple, and the chief priests and Pharisees had commanded that anyone who knew where He was should report it"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:1; NKJV notes, note on John 12:5; NKJV notes, note on John 12:7; NKJV notes, note on John 12:24; NKJV notes, note on John 12:32; NKJV notes, note on John 12:41; NKJV notes, note on John 12:47; Isaiah 6:10; 53:1; Zechariah 9:9; John 12:1–50","v":"Six days before the Passover Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was, who had been dead (NU omits who had been dead, by the NKJV note), whom He had raised. At a supper there Martha served and Lazarus sat at the table with Him, and Mary anointed His feet with a pound of very costly oil of spikenard and wiped them with her hair. Judas Iscariot, Simon's son, a thief who had the money box, asked why it was not sold for three hundred denarii (about one year's wages for a worker, by the NKJV note) and given to the poor; Jesus bade him let her alone, for she had kept it for the day of His burial (NU that she may keep it, by the NKJV note). Many of the Jews came to see Lazarus and believed in Jesus, and the chief priests plotted to kill Lazarus also. The next day a great multitude come to the feast went out with palm branches to meet Him, crying Hosanna and the King of Israel, as He sat on a young donkey, as it is written; those who had been with Him when He raised Lazarus bore witness, and the Pharisees said the world had gone after Him. Certain Greeks asked Philip to see Jesus, and He said the hour had come for the Son of Man to be glorified, and that a grain of wheat that dies produces much grain (lit. fruit, by the NKJV note). His soul troubled, He said, Father, glorify Your name, and a voice from heaven answered, which some took for thunder; He said the ruler of this world would be cast out, and that if lifted up (Crucified, by the NKJV note) from the earth He would draw all peoples to Himself; bidding the people walk while they had the light, He departed and was hidden from them. Though He had done so many signs they did not believe, that Isaiah's words might be fulfilled, spoken when (NU because, by the NKJV note) he saw His glory; many even among the rulers believed but did not confess Him for fear of the Pharisees. Jesus cried out that he who believes in Him believes in Him who sent Him, that He came as a light into the world, that He does not judge whoever hears His words and does not believe (NU keep them, by the NKJV note), having come not to judge the world but to save it, and that the word He has spoken will judge in the last day whoever rejects Him and does not receive His words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 13:2; NKJV notes, note on John 13:12; NKJV notes, note on John 13:18; NKJV notes, note on John 13:23; NKJV notes, note on John 13:25; Psalms 41:9; John 13:1–38","v":"Before the Feast of the Passover, knowing His hour had come to depart from this world to the Father, Jesus loved His own to the end. When supper was ended (NU during supper, by the NKJV note), the devil having already put it into the heart of Judas Iscariot, Simon's son, to betray Him, He rose, girded Himself with a towel and washed the disciples' feet; when Peter would not let Him, He said that unless He washed him he had no part with Him, and that they were clean, but not all. Seated again, He asked whether they knew (understand, by the NKJV note) what He had done: if their Lord and Teacher had washed their feet, they ought to wash one another's, for a servant is not greater than his master. He said the Scripture must be fulfilled, that he who eats bread with Him (NU My bread, by the NKJV note) has lifted up his heel against Him (Psalm 41:9), and, troubled in spirit, that one of them would betray Him; the disciple whom He loved, leaning (reclining, by the NKJV note) on His bosom, leaned back (NU, M add thus, by the NKJV note) at Peter's sign and asked who it was; Jesus gave the dipped piece of bread to Judas, Satan entered him, and he went out at once, and it was night. Then Jesus said the Son of Man was now glorified, that He would be with them a little while longer, and gave them a new commandment, to love one another as He had loved them; when Peter said he would lay down his life for Him, He told him the rooster would not crow till he had denied Him three times"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 14:2; NKJV notes, note on John 14:14; NKJV notes, note on John 14:15; NKJV notes, note on John 14:16; NKJV notes, note on John 14:21; NKJV notes, note on John 14:26; NKJV notes, note on John 14:28; John 14:1–31","v":"He bade them not let their heart be troubled but believe in God and in Him: in His Father's house are many mansions (lit. dwellings, by the NKJV note); if it were not so He would have told them (NU reads it otherwise, by the NKJV note); He goes to prepare a place for them and will come again to receive them to Himself. To Thomas He said He is the way, the truth, and the life, and no one comes to the Father except through Him; to Philip, that he who has seen Him has seen the Father, for He is in the Father and the Father in Him. Whoever believes in Him will do greater works, because He goes to His Father, and what they ask (NU ask Me, by the NKJV note) in His name He will do. If they love Him they are to keep (NU you will keep, by the NKJV note) His commandments, and the Father will give them another Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Spirit of truth, to abide with them forever; He will not leave them orphans. He will manifest (reveal, by the NKJV note) Himself to him who loves Him, and to Judas, not Iscariot, He said that whoever loves Him will keep His word, and His Father and He will make Their home with him. The Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in His name, will teach them all things and bring to their remembrance all He said. He left them His peace; if they loved Him they would rejoice because He said (NU omits I said, by the NKJV note) He was going to the Father, for His Father is greater than He; the ruler of this world is coming and has nothing in Him; and He said, Arise, let us go from here"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 15:2; NKJV notes, note on John 15:7; NKJV notes, note on John 15:26; Psalms 35:19; 69:4; John 15:1–27","v":"Jesus said He is the true vine and His Father the vinedresser, who takes away (or lifts up, by the NKJV note) every branch in Him that bears no fruit and prunes the fruitful; the disciples, the branches, already clean by His word, were to abide in Him, for without Him they could do nothing, and abiding in Him they would (NU omits you will, by the NKJV note) ask what they desired and it would be done. As the Father loved Him He loved them; they were to keep His commandments and love one another as He loved them, for no one has greater love than to lay down his life for his friends, and He called them friends, not servants, having chosen them to bear fruit that remains. The world would hate and persecute them as it had hated Him without a cause, as written in their law; the Helper (Comforter, Gr. Parakletos, by the NKJV note), the Spirit of truth whom He would send from the Father, would testify of Him, and they also would bear witness"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 16:3; NKJV notes, note on John 16:4; NKJV notes, note on John 16:15; NKJV notes, note on John 16:18; NKJV notes, note on John 16:32; NKJV notes, note on John 16:33; John 16:1–33","v":"He told them these things that they should not stumble: they would be put out of the synagogues, and whoever killed them would think he served God, doing this (NU, M omit to you, by the NKJV note) because they had not known the Father nor Him; He told them so that when the time (NU their time, by the NKJV note) came they would remember. Now He was going to Him who sent Him, and it was to their advantage, for then He would send the Helper, who would convict the world of sin, of righteousness and of judgment, and the Spirit of truth would guide them into all truth and glorify Him, taking of what is His (NU, M takes of Mine and will declare, by the NKJV note). When He said that in a little while they would not see Him, and again a little while they would see Him, some of the disciples did not know (understand, by the NKJV note) what He meant; He told them their sorrow would be turned to joy, as a woman's when her child is born, and that the Father would give whatever they asked in His name. When they said they believed He came forth from God, He told them they would be scattered, each to his own (own things or place, by the NKJV note), and leave Him alone, yet the Father was with Him; in the world they would (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) have tribulation, but He had overcome the world"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:2; NKJV notes, note on John 17:5; NKJV notes, note on John 17:6; NKJV notes, note on John 17:11; NKJV notes, note on John 17:12; NKJV notes, note on John 17:17; NKJV notes, note on John 17:20; John 17:1–26","v":"Lifting up His eyes to heaven, Jesus prayed that, the hour having come, the Father glorify His Son, to whom He had given authority over all flesh to give (M shall give, by the NKJV note) eternal life to those the Father gave Him, eternal life being to know the only true God and Jesus Christ whom He sent; having finished His work, He asked to be glorified together with (lit. alongside, by the NKJV note) the Father with the glory He had before the world was. He had manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) the Father's name to the men given Him out of the world, and prayed for them, not for the world: that the Holy Father keep them through His name (NU, M keep them through Your name which You have given Me, by the NKJV note), that they be one; He had kept them (NU omits in the world, and reads Your name which You gave Me. And I guarded them, by the NKJV notes), and none was lost (destroyed, by the NKJV note) but the son of perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note), that the Scripture might be fulfilled; that they be kept from the evil one and sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) by the truth, sent into the world as He was sent. He prayed also for those who will (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) believe in Him through their word, that all may be one and the world believe that the Father sent Him, and that those given Him may be with Him to behold His glory, for the Father loved Him before the foundation of the world"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 18:5; NKJV notes, note on John 18:14; NKJV notes, note on John 18:15; NKJV notes, note on John 18:20; NKJV notes, note on John 18:22; NKJV notes, note on John 18:28; NKJV notes, note on John 18:30; NKJV notes, note on John 18:33; John 18:1–40","v":"When He had spoken these words Jesus went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron into a garden where He often met with them, and Judas came there with a detachment of troops and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees, with lanterns, torches and weapons. When those who sought Jesus of Nazareth (lit. the Nazarene, by the NKJV note) heard Him say, I am He, they drew back and fell to the ground; He bade them let His disciples go, that His saying might be fulfilled that of those given Him He had lost none. Simon Peter cut off the right ear of Malchus, the high priest's servant, and Jesus bade him sheathe his sword, for He would drink the cup His Father had given Him. They bound Jesus and led Him first to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas, the high priest that year, who had advised the Jews that it was expedient (advantageous, by the NKJV note) that one man should die for the people. Peter followed with another (M the other, by the NKJV note) disciple, known to the high priest, who brought him into the courtyard, and Peter told the servant girl who kept the door that he was not one of His disciples. Asked by the high priest about His disciples and His doctrine, Jesus answered that He had taught openly in synagogues and in the temple, where the Jews always meet (NU all the Jews meet, by the NKJV note); an officer struck Him with the palm of his hand (lit. gave Jesus a slap, by the NKJV note), and Annas sent Him bound to Caiaphas. Warming himself at the fire, Peter denied Him twice more, and a rooster crowed. Early in the morning they led Jesus from Caiaphas to the Praetorium (the governor's headquarters, by the NKJV notes) but did not go in, lest they be defiled, that they might eat the Passover; they said they would not have delivered Him up had He not been an evildoer (a criminal, by the NKJV note), and that it was not lawful for them to put anyone to death, fulfilling His saying of what death He would die. Jesus told Pilate that His kingdom was not of this world and that He was a king, born to bear witness to the truth; Pilate asked, What is truth, and found no fault in Him. When he offered by their custom to release to them the King of the Jews at the Passover, they cried out for Barabbas, a robber"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 19:3; NKJV notes, note on John 19:7; NKJV notes, note on John 19:10; NKJV notes, note on John 19:16; NKJV notes, note on John 19:28; Psalms 22:18; Zechariah 12:10; John 19:1–42","v":"Pilate took Jesus and scourged Him, and the soldiers put a crown of thorns on His head and a purple robe on Him, hailed Him King of the Jews (NU And they came up to Him and said, by the NKJV note) and struck Him. Pilate brought Him out wearing them, that they might know he found no fault in Him, and said, Behold the Man; the chief priests and officers cried, Crucify Him, and the Jews said that by their (NU the, by the NKJV note) law He ought to die, for He made Himself the Son of God. The more afraid, Pilate asked Jesus where He was from; when He gave no answer Pilate spoke of his power (authority, by the NKJV note) to crucify or release Him, and Jesus said he could have none unless it were given him from above, and the one who delivered Him had the greater sin. When the Jews cried that in letting Him go he would be no friend of Caesar, Pilate sat in the judgment seat at The Pavement, in Hebrew Gabbatha, on the Preparation Day of the Passover about the sixth hour, and said, Behold your King; they cried, Crucify Him, the chief priests saying they had no king but Caesar, and he delivered Him to be crucified, and they led Him away (NU omits and led Him away, by the NKJV note). Bearing His cross He went out to the Place of a Skull, in Hebrew Golgotha, and was crucified between two others; Pilate's title on the cross, in Hebrew, Greek and Latin, read Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews, and he would not change it for the chief priests. The soldiers parted His garments in four and cast lots for the seamless tunic, that the Scripture might be fulfilled (Psalm 22:18). By the cross stood His mother, her sister, Mary the wife of Clopas and Mary Magdalene, and He gave His mother to the disciple whom He loved, who took her to his own home. Knowing (M seeing, by the NKJV note) that all was accomplished, He said, I thirst, received sour wine on hyssop, said, It is finished, and gave up His spirit. Because it was the Preparation Day and that Sabbath a high day, the Jews asked that the legs be broken; the soldiers broke the two others' legs, but finding Jesus dead, one of them pierced His side with a spear, and blood and water came out, as he who saw it has testified, that the Scripture might be fulfilled, Not one of His bones shall be broken, and another, They shall look on Him whom they pierced (Zechariah 12:10). Joseph of Arimathea, a disciple in secret for fear of the Jews, took the body with Pilate's leave, and with Nicodemus, who brought about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes, bound it in strips of linen with the spices, as the Jews bury, and laid it in a new tomb in a garden at the place of the crucifixion, because of the Jews' Preparation Day, for the tomb was nearby"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 20:7; NKJV notes, note on John 20:9; NKJV notes, note on John 20:16; NKJV notes, note on John 20:18; NKJV notes, note on John 20:19; NKJV notes, note on John 20:29; John 20:1–31","v":"On the first day of the week Mary Magdalene came to the tomb early, while it was still dark, and finding the stone taken away ran to Simon Peter and the other disciple, whom Jesus loved, saying the Lord had been taken out of the tomb. They ran to the tomb; the other disciple came first and, stooping, saw the linen cloths lying there, and Peter went in and saw them, and the handkerchief (face cloth, by the NKJV note) that had been around His head folded together in a place by itself; the other disciple went in also and saw and believed, for as yet they did not know (understand, by the NKJV note) the Scripture that He must rise again from the dead. Mary, weeping by the tomb, saw two angels in white where the body had lain, and then Jesus, whom she took for the gardener until He called her by name and she turned and said to Him (NU adds in Hebrew, by the NKJV note), Rabboni. He bade her not cling to Him, for He had not yet ascended to His Father, but go to His brethren, and she told the disciples that she had seen the Lord (NU I have seen the Lord, by the NKJV note). That evening, the doors shut where the disciples were assembled (NU omits assembled, by the NKJV note) for fear of the Jews, He stood in their midst and showed them His hands and His side; He sent them as the Father had sent Him, breathed on them and said, Receive the Holy Spirit, and spoke of the sins they would forgive and retain. Thomas, absent, would not believe without seeing and touching the print of the nails; after eight days Jesus came again, the doors being shut, and bade him reach his finger and his hand, and Thomas answered, My Lord and my God. Jesus told him, Thomas (NU, M omit Thomas, by the NKJV note), that he had believed because he had seen, and called blessed those who have not seen and yet have believed. Jesus did many other signs before His disciples that are not written in this book; these are written that the reader may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and believing have life in His name"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 21:3; NKJV notes, note on John 21:15; NKJV notes, note on John 21:16; NKJV notes, note on John 21:17; NKJV notes, note on John 21:22; John 21:1–25","v":"After these things Jesus showed Himself again to the disciples at the Sea of Tiberias. Simon Peter, Thomas called the Twin, Nathanael of Cana in Galilee, the sons of Zebedee and two others of His disciples were together; Peter went fishing and the others with him, and they went out and immediately (NU omits immediately, by the NKJV note) got into the boat, but caught nothing that night. In the morning Jesus stood on the shore, unknown to them, and bade them cast the net on the right side of the boat, and they could not draw it in for the multitude of fish. The disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, It is the Lord, and Peter put on his outer garment and plunged into the sea; the others came in the little boat dragging the net, and on land found a fire of coals with fish laid on it, and bread. Peter dragged the net to land, full of one hundred and fifty-three large fish, and it was not broken; Jesus bade them eat breakfast and gave them the bread and the fish, the third time He showed Himself to His disciples after He was raised from the dead. After breakfast He asked Simon, son of Jonah (NU John, by the NKJV note), three times whether he loved Him, the third time whether he loved (had affection for, by the NKJV note) Him; the first two times Simon answered that He knew that he loved (had affection for, by the NKJV note) Him, and grieved at the third asking he said, Lord, You know all things; You know that I love You. Jesus bade him feed His lambs, tend His sheep and feed His sheep, told him that when he was old another would gird him and carry him where he did not wish, signifying by what death he would glorify God, and said, Follow Me. Peter, turning, saw the disciple whom Jesus loved following, who had leaned on His breast at the supper, and asked what of this man; Jesus answered that it was nothing to Peter whether He willed (desired, by the NKJV note) that he remain till He came, and that Peter was to follow Him. So the saying went out among the brethren that this disciple would not die, though Jesus had not said so. This is the disciple who testifies of these things and wrote them, and his testimony is true; and Jesus did so many other things that the world itself, the writer supposes, could not contain the books that would be written of them one by one"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 1:1","v":"In the beginning was the Word"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:1–14","v":"It opens in the beginning, with the Word who was with God and was God, through whom all things were made, and who became flesh and dwelt among us"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:6–8, 15, 19–36","v":"It calls John the Baptist a man sent from God to bear witness of the Light, and gives his witness to Jesus as the Lamb of God and the Son of God"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:38, 41–42","v":"It gives some words as they were spoken and translates them: Rabbi, Teacher; Messiah, the Christ; Cephas, A Stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 1:29, 35, 43","v":"It marks the days one after another: the next day, again the next day, the following day"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:11, 23; 3:2","v":"It calls the works of Jesus signs, the water made wine at Cana being the beginning of them"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 7:53; John 7:53–8:11","v":"Its passage 7:53–8:11 is bracketed in the NU text as not in the original text, though it is present in over 900 manuscripts of John","b":"NKJV note"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:35","v":"Of the piercing it gives the true witness of one who saw it and knows he speaks the truth, so that those it addresses may believe"},{"p":"feature","r":"Psalms 22:18; Zechariah 12:10; John 19:24, 28, 36–37","v":"At the cross it tells of Scripture fulfilled in the garments divided by lot, in His thirst and in no bone of His being broken, and gives another Scripture, of the One they pierced"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 20:30; 21:25","v":"It says that Jesus did many other signs in the presence of His disciples that are not written in it, and so many other things that the world itself, the writer supposes, could not contain the books of them"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:20–24","v":"It closes by saying that the disciple whom Jesus loved, who had leaned on His breast at the supper, testifies of these things and wrote them, and that his testimony is true"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 21:20–24","e":"PER-03184"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 20:30–31","v":"That its readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing they may have life in His name"}],"nm":{"PER-03184":"The disciple whom Jesus loved"}},"OBJ-00638":{"k":"object","n":"The six waterpots of stone at Cana","d":"Six waterpots of stone set at the wedding in Cana for the purification of the Jews, which the servants filled with water at Jesus's word (John 2:6–9)","f":[{"p":"count","r":"John 2:6","v":"6"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 2:6","v":"six waterpots of stone"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:6","v":"Each held twenty or thirty gallons"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 2:7–9","v":"At Jesus's word they were filled with water up to the brim, and what was drawn from them and taken to the master of the feast was water made wine","at":"EVT-02996"},{"p":"material","r":"John 2:6","v":"Stone"},{"p":"placed at","r":"John 2:1, 6","e":"PLC-01514"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 2:6","v":"Set there for the manner of purification of the Jews"}],"nm":{"EVT-02996":"Jesus turns water into wine at a wedding in Cana","PLC-01514":"Cana"}},"OBJ-00639":{"k":"object","n":"The whip of cords Jesus made","d":"A whip Jesus made of cords, with which He drove the sellers and money changers out of the temple (John 2:15)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 2:15","v":"a whip of cords"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 2:15","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"material","r":"John 2:15","v":"Cords"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 2:15","e":"EVT-02999"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 2:15","v":"With it Jesus drove them all out of the temple, with the sheep and the oxen","b":"inferred","w":"When He had made a whip of cords, He drove them all out of the temple (John 2:15); the text does not say in words that He drove them with it."}],"nm":{"EVT-02999":"Jesus drives the sellers out of the temple with a whip of cords","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00640":{"k":"object","n":"The illustration of the sheepfold","d":"One who climbs into the sheepfold some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber, but the shepherd enters by the door, calls his own sheep by name and leads them out, and they follow him, knowing his voice (John 10:1–6)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 10:1–5","v":"Whoever climbs into the sheepfold some other way than by the door is a thief and a robber; the shepherd comes in by the door, the doorkeeper opens to him, and the sheep hear his voice. He calls his own by name and leads them out, walking ahead of them, and they follow because they know his voice; a stranger they will never follow but will run from, since they do not know his voice"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 10:6","v":"this illustration"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 10:1–6","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 10:1–6","e":"EVT-03040"}],"nm":{"EVT-03040":"Jesus says He is the door of the sheep and the good shepherd","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00641":{"k":"object","n":"The oil of spikenard with which Mary anointed Jesus","d":"The pound of very costly oil of spikenard with which Mary anointed the feet of Jesus at Bethany six days before the Passover (John 12:3–7)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:3","v":"a pound of very costly oil of spikenard"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:5; John 12:3–5","v":"Its fragrance filled the house; Judas said it might have been sold for three hundred denarii (about one year's wages for a worker, by the NKJV note)","at":"EVT-03054"},{"p":"material","r":"John 12:3","v":"oil of spikenard"},{"p":"owner","r":"John 12:3, 7","e":"PER-03150"},{"p":"purpose","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 12:7; John 12:7","v":"She had kept it for the day of His burial (NU that she may keep it, by the NKJV note)"}],"nm":{"EVT-03054":"Mary anoints the feet of Jesus at Bethany","PER-03150":"Mary, the sister of Martha"}},"OBJ-00642":{"k":"object","n":"The money box Judas Iscariot kept","d":"The money box Judas Iscariot had, from which he used to take what was put in it, for he was a thief (John 12:6)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 12:6","v":"What was put in it, which Judas used to take"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 12:6; 13:29","v":"the money box"},{"p":"owner","r":"John 12:6","e":"PER-03039"}],"nm":{"PER-03039":"Judas"}},"OBJ-00643":{"k":"object","n":"Jesus's prayer for His disciples and all who will believe","d":"The prayer Jesus prayed to the Father, lifting up His eyes to heaven, for Himself, for His disciples and for those who will believe in Him through their word (John 17:1–26)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:2; NKJV notes, note on John 17:5; John 17:1–5","v":"Jesus asked the Father, the hour having come, to glorify His Son, that the Son might glorify Him, as He had given Him authority over all flesh to give (M shall give, by the NKJV note) eternal life to as many as He had given Him; eternal life is to know the only true God and Jesus Christ whom He sent; He had glorified the Father on the earth and finished His work, and asked to be glorified together with (lit. alongside, by the NKJV note) the Father with the glory He had with Him before the world was"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:6; NKJV notes, note on John 17:11; NKJV notes, note on John 17:12; NKJV notes, note on John 17:17; John 17:6–19","v":"He had manifested (revealed, by the NKJV note) the Father's name to the men given Him out of the world, who had kept the Father's word and believed that the Father sent Him; He prayed for them, not for the world: that the Holy Father keep through His name those given Him (NU, M keep them through Your name which You have given Me, by the NKJV note), that they be one as They are one; while with them He had kept them (NU omits in the world, and reads Your name which You gave Me. And I guarded them, by the NKJV notes), and none was lost (destroyed, by the NKJV note) but the son of perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note), that the Scripture might be fulfilled; not that they be taken out of the world, but kept from the evil one; that the Father sanctify (set apart, by the NKJV note) them by His truth, for His word is truth; as the Father sent Him into the world He has sent them, and for their sakes He sanctifies Himself"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on John 17:20; John 17:20–26","v":"He prayed also for those who will (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) believe in Him through their word, that all may be one, as the Father is in Him and He in the Father, that the world may believe and know that the Father sent Him and loved them as He loved Him; He had given them the glory the Father gave Him, and desired that those given Him be with Him where He is to behold His glory, for the Father loved Him before the foundation of the world; the world has not known the righteous Father, but He has, and these have known that the Father sent Him; He has declared the Father's name and will declare it, that the Father's love may be in them, and He in them"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 17:1","v":"Father, the hour has come"},{"p":"made by","r":"John 17:1","e":"PER-03010"},{"p":"occasion","r":"John 17:1","e":"EVT-03068"}],"nm":{"EVT-03068":"Jesus prays for Himself, His disciples and all who will believe","PER-03010":"Jesus"}},"OBJ-00644":{"k":"object","n":"The myrrh and aloes Nicodemus brought","d":"A mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds, that Nicodemus brought and that was bound with the body of Jesus in strips of linen (John 19:39–40)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:39","v":"a mixture of myrrh and aloes"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 19:40","v":"the spices"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 19:39","v":"About a hundred pounds"},{"p":"material","r":"John 19:39","v":"Myrrh and aloes"},{"p":"purpose","r":"John 19:40","v":"To be bound with the body of Jesus in strips of linen, as the Jews bury"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00645":{"k":"object","n":"The net full of fish at the Sea of Tiberias","d":"The net the disciples cast on the right side of the boat at Jesus's word, which Simon Peter dragged to land full of one hundred and fifty-three large fish, and which was not broken (John 21:6–11)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"John 21:11","v":"Large fish, one hundred and fifty-three"},{"p":"designation","r":"John 21:6, 8, 11","v":"the net"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:11","v":"Though there were so many fish, it was not broken","at":"EVT-03077"},{"p":"feature","r":"John 21:6, 8","v":"Cast on the right side of the boat at Jesus's word, it could not be drawn in for the multitude of fish, and the other disciples dragged it after the little boat","at":"EVT-03077"}],"nm":{"EVT-03077":"Jesus shows Himself to seven disciples at the Sea of Tiberias"}},"OBJ-00646":{"k":"object","n":"The Acts of the Apostles","d":"The account of what the apostles did after Jesus was taken up, from Jerusalem to Rome, written to Theophilus after a former account (Acts 1:1–28:31)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:20; Acts 1:1–26","v":"The writer's former account to Theophilus ran until the day Jesus was taken up. He showed Himself alive to the apostles by many infallible (unmistakable, by the NKJV note) proofs during forty days, bade them wait in Jerusalem for the Promise of the Father, the baptism with the Holy Spirit, and said they would be witnesses (NU My witnesses, by the NKJV note) to Him to the end of the earth; while they watched He was taken up, and two men in white said He would come again in like manner. Returning from Olivet to the upper room, the apostles continued with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note) in prayer (NU omits and supplication, by the NKJV note) with the women, Mary and His brothers. Peter told the disciples (NU brethren, by the NKJV note), about a hundred and twenty, that Scripture had to be fulfilled concerning Judas, who bought a field with the wages of iniquity (reward of unrighteousness, by the NKJV note) and, falling headlong, burst open so that his entrails (intestines, by the NKJV note) gushed out, and that his dwelling should be desolate (deserted, by the NKJV note) and another take his office (Gr. episkopen, position of overseer, by the NKJV note); the lot fell on Matthias, who was numbered with the eleven apostles"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:40; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:41; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:42; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:45; NKJV notes, note on Acts 2:47; Acts 2:1–47","v":"On the Day of Pentecost, as they were all with one accord (NU together, by the NKJV note) in one place, a sound as of a rushing mighty wind filled the house, divided tongues as of fire (or tongues as of fire, distributed and resting on each, by the NKJV note) sat on each of them, and filled with the Holy Spirit they spoke with other tongues, so that devout men from every nation heard them each in his own language (dialect, by the NKJV note), Parthians to Arabs (Arabians, by the NKJV note). Peter said they were not drunk at the third hour (9 a.m., by the NKJV note), but this was Joel's word; Jesus, attested by God and crucified by lawless hands (NU omits have taken, by the NKJV note), God raised up, having loosed (destroyed or abolished, by the NKJV note) the pains (lit. birth pangs, by the NKJV note) of death, as David foresaw of the One to sit on his throne (NU He would seat one on his throne, by the NKJV note); exalted to (possibly by, by the NKJV note) the right hand of God, He poured out the Spirit. He bade them repent and be baptized for the remission (forgiveness, by the NKJV note) of sins and be saved from this perverse (crooked, by the NKJV note) generation; those who gladly (NU omits gladly, by the NKJV note) received his word were baptized, about three thousand, and continued in the apostles' doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note), sold (would sell, by the NKJV note) their goods and divided (distributed, by the NKJV note) them as any had need, and the Lord added to the church (NU omits to the church, by the NKJV note) daily those who were being saved"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 3:24; Acts 3:1–26","v":"At the ninth hour Peter and John, going up to the temple, healed in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth a man lame from birth who was laid daily at the Beautiful Gate to ask (beg, by the NKJV note) alms. In Solomon's porch Peter told the people that the God of their fathers had glorified His Servant Jesus, whom they denied before Pilate, asking for a murderer and killing the Prince (or Originator, by the NKJV note) of life, whom God raised; they were to repent, that He might send Jesus Christ (NU, M Christ Jesus, by the NKJV note), preached (NU, M ordained, by the NKJV note) to them before, whom heaven must receive until the restoration God spoke of since the world (or time, by the NKJV note) began, of whom Moses spoke as a Prophet like him, and all the prophets foretold (NU, M proclaimed, by the NKJV note) these days"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 4:36; Acts 4:1–37","v":"The priests, the captain of the temple and the Sadducees put Peter and John in custody, and about five thousand men believed; the next day, before Annas, Caiaphas and the rulers, Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said the man stood whole by the name of Jesus, the stone the builders rejected, and there is salvation in no other name. Unable to deny the notable miracle (remarkable sign, by the NKJV note), evident (well known, by the NKJV note) to all in Jerusalem, the council forbade them to teach in that name, but they answered that they must speak what they had seen and heard. Let go, they prayed with their companions, in the words God spoke by the mouth of David (NU through the Holy Spirit, by the mouth of our father David, by the NKJV note), for boldness, and the place was shaken; the believers had all things in common, and Joses (NU Joseph, by the NKJV note), named Barnabas, Son of Encouragement (or Consolation, by the NKJV note), a Levite of Cyprus, sold his land and laid the money at the apostles' feet"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:35; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 5:41; Acts 5:1–42","v":"Ananias and his wife Sapphira kept back part of the price of their land and lied to the Holy Spirit, and each fell dead at Peter's word, and great fear came on the church. Many signs were done through the apostles, and the sick were laid in the streets for Peter's shadow to fall on them. The high priest and the Sadducees, filled with indignation (jealousy, by the NKJV note), put the apostles in prison, but an angel opened the doors; the officers found the prison shut and the guards (NU, M omit outside, by the NKJV note) at the doors, and the high priest (NU omits the high priest, by the NKJV note) and the chief priests wondered, until one told them (NU, M omit saying, by the NKJV note) the men were teaching in the temple. Before the council (Sanhedrin, by the NKJV note) and the elders (council of elders or senate, by the NKJV note) Peter said they must obey God rather than men; the council, furious (cut to the quick, by the NKJV note), heeded Gamaliel, who bade them take heed (be careful, by the NKJV note), recalling Theudas, whom four hundred joined (followed, by the NKJV note), and Judas of Galilee; beaten and let go, the apostles rejoiced to suffer shame for His name (NU the name; M the name of Jesus, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 6:13; Acts 6:1–15","v":"When the Hellenists (Greek-speaking Jews, by the NKJV note) complained that their widows were neglected, the twelve had the disciples choose seven, Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas and Nicolas, and laid hands on them; the word spread and many priests obeyed the faith. Stephen, full of faith (NU grace, by the NKJV note) and power, did great wonders; men of the Synagogue of the Freedmen, unable to resist his wisdom, brought him before the council with false witnesses who said he spoke blasphemous (NU omits blasphemous, by the NKJV note) words against the holy place and the law, and his face was as the face of an angel"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:51; NKJV notes, note on Acts 7:54; Acts 7:1–60","v":"Stephen told of God's appearing to Abraham in Mesopotamia before he dwelt in Haran, the promise and the covenant of circumcision; of Joseph sold into Egypt, the famine and great trouble (affliction, by the NKJV note), and Jacob's going down with seventy-five (or seventy, Exodus 1:5, by the NKJV note) souls; of Moses, born in the oppression, rejected by his brethren, sent by the Angel (NU omits of the Lord, by the NKJV note) in the bush to deliver them, who spoke of the Prophet like him (NU, M omit Him you shall hear, by the NKJV note) and was in the congregation (Gr. ekklesia, assembly or church, by the NKJV note) in the wilderness and received the living oracles (sayings, by the NKJV note), whom the fathers would not obey but made a calf; of the tabernacle of witness and the house Solomon built, though the Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands. He called them stiff-necked (stubborn, by the NKJV note), resisting the Holy Spirit; furious (cut to the heart, by the NKJV note), they stoned him outside the city as he saw Jesus standing at the right hand of God, and the witnesses laid their clothes at the feet of a young man named Saul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 8:40; Acts 8:1–40","v":"Saul consented to Stephen's death; a great persecution scattered the church through Judea and Samaria except the apostles, devout men buried Stephen, and Saul made havoc of the church. Philip preached Christ in the city (or a city, by the NKJV note) of Samaria, where Simon had astonished the people (or nation, by the NKJV note) with sorcery (magic, by the NKJV note; magic arts, by the NKJV note on 8:11), and they believed and were baptized, Simon too; Peter and John laid hands on them and they received the Holy Spirit, and Simon, offering money for that power, was rebuked. An angel sent Philip to the road from Jerusalem to Gaza, which is desert (or a deserted place, by the NKJV note), where he preached Jesus from Isaiah to a eunuch of Ethiopia, Candace's treasurer, who confessed Jesus Christ the Son of God (NU, M omit v. 37, found in Western texts, including the Latin tradition, by the NKJV note) and was baptized; the Spirit caught Philip away to Azotus (the same as the Hebrew Ashdod, by the NKJV note), and he preached in all the cities to Caesarea"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 9:36; Acts 9:1–43","v":"Saul, with letters from the high priest, was struck near Damascus by a light from heaven and heard Jesus, whom he persecuted (NU, M omit the rest of v. 5, by the NKJV note), bid him go into the city; blind three days, he was found by Ananias, whom the Lord sent though he had heard of Saul's harm (bad things, by the NKJV note) to the saints, for Saul was a chosen vessel to bear His name before Gentiles, kings and the children (lit. sons, by the NKJV note) of Israel; the Lord (M omits Jesus, by the NKJV note) restored his sight and he was baptized. He preached the Christ (NU Jesus, by the NKJV note) in the synagogues, escaped the Jews' plot over the wall in a basket, was brought to the apostles by Barnabas, disputed with the Hellenists (Greek-speaking Jews, by the NKJV note) and was sent to Tarsus. The churches (NU church, by the NKJV note) had peace and were edified (built up, by the NKJV note). Peter healed Aeneas at Lydda, and at Joppa raised Tabitha, which is Dorcas (lit., in Aramaic and in Greek, Gazelle, by the NKJV notes), and stayed with Simon a tanner"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:3; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:32; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:39; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:43; NKJV notes, note on Acts 10:45; Acts 10:1–48","v":"At the ninth hour (3 p.m., by the NKJV note) an angel bade Cornelius, a centurion of the Italian Regiment (Cohort, by the NKJV note) who gave alms (charitable gifts, by the NKJV note), send to Joppa for Peter, who lodged with Simon a tanner by the sea (NU, M omit the rest of v. 6, by the NKJV note). At the sixth hour (noon, by the NKJV note) Peter saw in a trance a sheet let down with all kinds of beasts and heard that what God had cleansed (declared clean, by the NKJV note) he must not call common; while he wondered (was perplexed, by the NKJV note) the men came (NU, M omit who had been sent to him from Cornelius, by the NKJV note), and he went with them to Caesarea. Cornelius told of his vision four days before (NU four days ago to this hour, at the ninth hour, by the NKJV note), and his alms (charitable gifts, by the NKJV note) remembered (NU omits the rest of v. 32, by the NKJV note); Peter preached the word sent to the children (lit. sons, by the NKJV note) of Israel, Jesus whom they (NU, M they also, by the NKJV note) killed and God raised, through whose name is remission (forgiveness, by the NKJV note) of sins; the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard, to the astonishment of those of the circumcision (the Jews, by the NKJV note), and they were baptized"},{"p":"contents","r":"Acts 11:1–30","v":"At Jerusalem those of the circumcision contended with Peter for eating with uncircumcised men, but when he told them in order of his vision and of the Holy Spirit falling on the Gentiles, they glorified God, who had granted the Gentiles repentance to life. Those scattered after Stephen preached as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch, and at Antioch men of Cyprus and Cyrene preached the Lord Jesus to the Hellenists and many believed; Barnabas, sent by the church in Jerusalem, brought Saul from Tarsus, they taught there a whole year, and the disciples were first called Christians at Antioch. Agabus foretold a great famine, which came in the days of Claudius Caesar, and the disciples sent relief to the elders in Judea by Barnabas and Saul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 12:25; Acts 12:1–25","v":"Herod the king killed James the brother of John with the sword and, during the Days of Unleavened Bread, put Peter in prison under four squads (Gr. tetrads, squads of four, by the NKJV note) of soldiers, while the church prayed constantly (NU constantly or earnestly, by the NKJV note). The night before he was to be brought out, with the guards keeping (guarding, by the NKJV note) the prison, an angel freed Peter, and he came to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where Rhoda left him knocking at the gate; he bade them tell James and the brethren, and went to another place. At day there was no small stir (disturbance, by the NKJV note) among the soldiers; Herod had the guards put to death and went down to Caesarea, where the people of Tyre and Sidon, having made Blastus, the king's personal aide (who was in charge of the king's bedchamber, by the NKJV note), their friend, asked for peace, for their country was supplied with food (lit. nourished, by the NKJV note) by the king's; hailed as a god, Herod was struck by an angel of the Lord, eaten by worms, and died (breathed his last, by the NKJV note). Barnabas and Saul returned from (NU, M to, by the NKJV note) Jerusalem with John Mark"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:23; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:36; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 13:42; Acts 13:1–52","v":"At Antioch the Holy Spirit bade the church set apart Barnabas and Saul; they sailed from Seleucia to Cyprus and went through the island (NU the whole island, by the NKJV note) to Paphos, where Elymas the sorcerer withstood (opposed, by the NKJV note) them and Saul, also called Paul, struck him blind, and the proconsul believed. From Perga, where John left them, they came to Antioch in Pisidia; bidden to give a word of exhortation (encouragement, by the NKJV note), Paul told how God chose Israel (M omits Israel, by the NKJV note) and brought them out with an uplifted arm (mighty power, by the NKJV note), gave them judges and kings, and of David's seed raised up a Savior (M salvation, by the NKJV note), Jesus, the word of salvation (message, by the NKJV note) sent to the family (stock, by the NKJV note) of Abraham, whom God raised, no more to see corruption (the state of decay, by the NKJV note), giving the sure mercies (blessings, by the NKJV note) of David, while David, having served his (in his, by the NKJV note) generation, saw corruption (underwent decay, by the NKJV note) and He did not (underwent no decay, by the NKJV note); when they went out (NU And when they went out, they begged, by the NKJV note), the Gentiles begged to hear more, and when the Jews opposed, Paul and Barnabas turned to the Gentiles and were expelled"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 14:12; Acts 14:1–28","v":"At Iconium many believed, but the unbelieving Jews poisoned (embittered, by the NKJV note) the Gentiles' minds (lit. souls, by the NKJV note), and they fled to Lystra and Derbe; at Lystra Paul healed (lit. who, by the NKJV note) a man lame from birth, and the people called Barnabas Zeus (Jupiter, by the NKJV note) and Paul Hermes (Mercury, by the NKJV note) and would have sacrificed to them; Jews from Antioch and Iconium stoned Paul, but he rose and went to Derbe. Returning through the cities they appointed elders, and sailed from Attalia to Antioch, where they told how God had opened the door of faith to the Gentiles"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:33; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:40; Acts 15:1–41","v":"Men from Judea taught at Antioch that the Gentiles must be circumcised, and Paul and Barnabas went up to Jerusalem. Peter said God acknowledged (bore witness to, by the NKJV note) the Gentiles by the Holy Spirit and that all are saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ (NU, M omit Christ, by the NKJV note); when they stopped speaking (by the NKJV note), James judged by the prophets' words of the tabernacle of David rebuilt, by the Lord who does these things (NU who makes these things, by the NKJV note), known from eternity (NU known from eternity of old, by the NKJV note), that the Gentiles should only abstain from things polluted by idols, sexual immorality (or fornication, by the NKJV notes), things strangled and blood. The apostles and elders, with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note), wrote so to the Gentile brethren (NU omits saying, You must be circumcised and keep the law, by the NKJV note) and sent Judas called Barsabas (NU, M Barsabbas, by the NKJV note) and Silas with Paul and Barnabas; at Antioch the letter brought joy, and Judas was sent back to the apostles (NU those who had sent them, by the NKJV note), but Silas stayed (NU, M omit v. 34, by the NKJV note). When Barnabas was determined (resolved, by the NKJV note) to take Mark, they parted; Barnabas sailed with Mark to Cyprus, and Paul, commended (committed, by the NKJV note) by the brethren, went with Silas through Syria and Cilicia"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:12; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 16:35; Acts 16:1–40","v":"At Lystra Paul took Timothy, son of a believing Jewess and a Greek father, and they delivered the decrees through the cities. Forbidden by the Holy Spirit to preach in Asia (the Roman province of Asia, by the NKJV note) and not permitted by the Spirit (NU adds of Jesus, by the NKJV note) to enter Bithynia, they came to Troas, where Paul saw a man of Macedonia pleading for help; by Samothrace and Neapolis they came to Philippi, the foremost (lit. first, by the NKJV note) city of that part of Macedonia, a colony, where Lydia was baptized. Paul, annoyed (distressed, by the NKJV note), cast a spirit of divination out of a slave girl; her masters had Paul and Silas beaten and imprisoned, but at midnight an earthquake opened the doors and the jailer believed; at day the magistrates sent officers (lictors, lit. rod bearers, by the NKJV note) to release them, and learning they were Romans pleaded with them to depart"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:19; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 17:26; Acts 17:1–34","v":"At Thessalonica Paul reasoned three Sabbaths that the Christ had to suffer and rise, and many Greeks believed; the Jews who were not persuaded (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), envious (M omits becoming envious, by the NKJV note), set the city in an uproar and dragged Jason, who had harbored (welcomed, by the NKJV note) them, before the rulers. The Bereans were more fair-minded (lit. noble, by the NKJV note) and searched the Scriptures daily. At Athens, a city given over to (full of, by the NKJV note) idols, Epicurean and (NU, M add also, by the NKJV note) Stoic philosophers called Paul a babbler (lit. seed picker, an idler who makes a living picking up scraps, by the NKJV note) and brought him to the Areopagus (lit. Hill of Ares, or Mars' Hill, by the NKJV notes), where he proclaimed the unknown God, who made from one blood (NU omits blood, by the NKJV note) every nation and will judge the world by the Man He raised from the dead; some mocked, but Dionysius and Damaris believed"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:5; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:12; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:21; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 18:23; Acts 18:1–28","v":"At Corinth Paul worked with Aquila and Priscilla, tentmakers come from Italy because Claudius had commanded the Jews to leave Rome; when Silas and Timothy came, Paul, compelled by the Spirit (or in his spirit or in the Spirit, by the NKJV note), testified that Jesus is the Christ, and when the Jews blasphemed he went to the house of Justus (NU Titius Justus, by the NKJV note); Crispus believed, and Paul stayed a year and six months. The Jews brought him to the judgment seat (Gr. bema, by the NKJV note) of Gallio, who drove them away, and the Greeks (NU they all, by the NKJV note) beat Sosthenes. After a good while (lit. many days, by the NKJV note) Paul sailed for Syria, cut his hair at Cenchrea for a vow, reasoned at Ephesus, promising to keep the feast at Jerusalem (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), landed at Caesarea, went up (to Jerusalem, by the NKJV note) and greeted the church, and went down to Antioch; then he went over Galatia and Phrygia in order (successively, by the NKJV note). Apollos of Alexandria, knowing only John's baptism, taught at Ephesus, was taught the way of God more accurately by Aquila and Priscilla, and in Achaia refuted the Jews"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:27; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:35; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:37; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:38; NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:40; Acts 19:1–41","v":"At Ephesus Paul baptized about twelve disciples who knew only John's baptism, and the Holy Spirit came on them; he reasoned three months in the synagogue and two years in the school of Tyrannus, and God worked unusual miracles by his hands. Itinerant Jewish exorcists said they exorcised (NU I exorcise, by the NKJV note; adjure, solemnly command, by the NKJV note) by the Jesus whom Paul preached, but the evil spirit overpowered (M and they overpowered, by the NKJV note) the seven sons of Sceva (NU both of them, by the NKJV note), and many burned their books of magic. Demetrius, who made silver shrines of Diana (Gr. Artemis, by the NKJV note), said her magnificence might be destroyed (NU she be deposed from her magnificence, by the NKJV note), and the city rushed into the theater; the officials of Asia (Asiarchs, rulers of Asia, the province, by the NKJV note) begged Paul not to go in, and the city clerk said Ephesus was temple guardian of Diana (Gr. Artemis, by the NKJV note) and of the image that fell from Zeus (heaven, by the NKJV note), these men were no blasphemers of their (NU our, by the NKJV note) goddess, a case (lit. matter, by the NKJV note) belonged to the courts, and they were in danger of being called in question (or charged with rebellion concerning today, by the NKJV note) for the uproar"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:13; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:27; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:28; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:34; NKJV notes, note on Acts 20:37; Acts 20:1–38","v":"Paul went through Macedonia to Greece and stayed three months, returned through Macedonia because of a plot, and sailed from Philippi after the Days of Unleavened Bread to Troas, where in the upper room where they (NU, M we, by the NKJV note) were gathered Eutychus fell from the third story and was taken up alive. Having arranged (by the NKJV note) to go on foot to Assos, he sailed by Mitylene, Chios and Samos to Miletus, hurrying to be at Jerusalem for Pentecost, and told the elders of Ephesus that, though he did not count his life dear (NU But I do not count my life of any value or dear to myself, by the NKJV note), finishing his race (course, by the NKJV note), he was innocent (lit. clean, by the NKJV note) of the blood of all, had not shunned (avoided declaring, by the NKJV note) the whole counsel of God, and bade them shepherd the church of God (M of the Lord and God, by the NKJV note), for wolves and men speaking perverse (misleading, by the NKJV note) things would come; they wept freely (lit. much, by the NKJV note) at his words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:8; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:26; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:31; NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:39; Acts 21:1–40","v":"By Cos, Rhodes and Patara Paul's company sailed to Tyre, where the disciples (NU the disciples, by the NKJV note) told him through the Spirit not to go up to Jerusalem; at Caesarea they (NU omits who were Paul's companions, by the NKJV note) stayed with Philip the evangelist, and Agabus bound himself with Paul's belt; they packed (made preparations, by the NKJV note) and went up to Jerusalem. James and the elders said the assembly must meet (NU What then is to be done? They will certainly hear, by the NKJV note) and bade Paul be purified with four men, reminding him of what they wrote to the Gentiles (NU omits that they should observe no such thing, except, by the NKJV note; or fornication, by the NKJV note); at the completion (by the NKJV note) of the days the Jews from Asia, who had previously (M omits previously, by the NKJV note) seen Trophimus with him, seized him in the temple, and the commander of the garrison (cohort, by the NKJV note) took him; asked whether he was the Egyptian, Paul said he was a Jew of Tarsus, a citizen of no mean (insignificant, by the NKJV note) city"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 22:23; Acts 22:1–30","v":"In Hebrew Paul told the people of his upbringing at the feet of Gamaliel, his persecuting of the Way, the light near Damascus, which those with him saw (NU omits and were afraid, by the NKJV note), Ananias's word, and the Lord's word in a trance in the temple sending him to the Gentiles, for he had stood by consenting to Stephen's death (NU omits to his death, by the NKJV note); at that word they cried out and tore off (lit. threw, by the NKJV note) their clothes, and the commander would have him examined under scourging until Paul said he was a Roman, born a citizen"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:30; NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:35; Acts 23:1–35","v":"Struck at the high priest Ananias's command, Paul called him a whitewashed wall; crying that he was a Pharisee judged for the resurrection, he divided the council, and the scribes of the Pharisees found no evil in him (NU what if a spirit or an angel has spoken to him, by the NKJV note). The Lord bade him be of good cheer (take courage, by the NKJV note), for he must witness at Rome. More than forty bound themselves to kill him when he was brought down tomorrow (NU omits tomorrow, by the NKJV note), but his sister's son told the commander, who sent him by night to Felix, writing that the Jews lay in wait (NU there would be a plot, by the NKJV note), and Felix kept him in Herod's Praetorium (headquarters, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:4; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:6; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:20; NKJV notes, note on Acts 24:26; Acts 24:1–27","v":"After five days Tertullus praised Felix for peace and prosperity (or reforms, by the NKJV note), begged his courtesy (graciousness, by the NKJV note), and accused Paul of dissension and of profaning the temple (NU ends the sentence there, omitting the rest of v. 6, all of v. 7 and the first clause of v. 8, by the NKJV note), and the Jews assented (NU, M joined the attack, by the NKJV note). Paul answered that he worshiped the God of his fathers by the Way, hoping in the resurrection of the dead (NU omits of the dead, by the NKJV note), and asked what wrong they found (NU, M what wrongdoing they found, by the NKJV note); Felix put them off, heard Paul with Drusilla, hoped for money to release him (NU omits that he might release him, by the NKJV note), and after two years left him bound"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 25:23; Acts 25:1–27","v":"Three days after coming to the province Festus went up to Jerusalem, where the high priest (NU chief priests, by the NKJV note) and the chief men asked him to summon Paul, meaning to ambush him; at Caesarea the Jews laid complaints they could not prove, and Paul, asked to be judged at Jerusalem, appealed to Caesar. King Agrippa and Bernice came to greet Festus, who told the king that the Romans deliver no man to destruction (NU omits to destruction, although it is implied, by the NKJV note) unheard, and that the accusers brought no charge such as he supposed (suspected, by the NKJV note), but questions about a certain Jesus whom Paul affirmed to be alive; the next day Agrippa and Bernice came with great pomp (pageantry, by the NKJV note), and Festus said he had nothing certain to write of Paul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:18; NKJV notes, note on Acts 26:25; Acts 26:1–32","v":"Permitted to speak, Paul told Agrippa he had lived a Pharisee and was judged for the hope of the promise to the fathers; he had done many things contrary to (against, by the NKJV note) the name of Jesus of Nazareth, until on the road to Damascus a light brighter than the sun shone around him and Jesus spoke to him in Hebrew, sending him to the Gentiles, from whom and from the Jewish people He would deliver (rescue, by the NKJV note) him, to whom He now sent him (NU, M omit now, by the NKJV note), that they might be sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) by faith in Him; he had preached repentance to Jew and Gentile, saying only what the prophets and Moses foretold. Festus said he was mad, and Paul answered that he was not (out of my mind, by the NKJV note); Agrippa said Paul almost persuaded him to become a Christian, and might have been set free had he not appealed to Caesar"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:9; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:14; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:15; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:16; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:17; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:22; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:29; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:40; NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:41; Acts 27:1–44","v":"Delivered with other prisoners to Julius, Paul sailed by Sidon and Myra to Fair Havens on Crete, where, the Fast being over (the Day of Atonement, late September or early October, by the NKJV note), he warned of disaster; making for Phoenix they were caught by Euroclydon (a southeast wind that stirs up broad waves; NU Euraquilon, a northeaster, by the NKJV note) and let the ship drive (be driven, by the NKJV note), and under Clauda (NU Cauda, by the NKJV note), fearing the Syrtis Sands (M Syrtes, by the NKJV note), they struck sail. When hope was gone Paul bade them take heart (courage, by the NKJV note), for an angel had told him none would be lost; on the fourteenth night they dropped anchors and prayed (or wished, by the NKJV note) for day, and after Paul had them eat, they let go (cast off, by the NKJV note) the anchors and ran aground where two seas met (a reef, by the NKJV note), and all two hundred and seventy-six escaped to land"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:2; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:7; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:11; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:19; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:29; Acts 28:1–31","v":"On Malta the natives (lit. barbarians, by the NKJV note) showed kindness, a viper fastened on Paul's hand without harm, and Publius, the leading citizen (magistrate, by the NKJV note), received them, and Paul healed his father and the sick of the island. After three months they sailed in an Alexandrian ship whose figurehead was the Twin Brothers (Gr. Dioskouroi, Zeus's sons Castor and Pollux, by the NKJV note) by Syracuse, Rhegium and Puteoli to Rome, where Paul dwelt with a soldier; he told the leaders of the Jews that when the Jews (the ruling authorities, by the NKJV note) spoke against his release he appealed to Caesar, and when some disbelieved he said the Holy Spirit spoke rightly through Isaiah to their fathers (NU your fathers, by the NKJV note), and they departed disputing (NU omits v. 29, by the NKJV note); he dwelt two whole years in his rented house, preaching the kingdom of God with no one forbidding him"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 1:1","v":"The former account I made"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 1:2; Acts 1:1–2","v":"It is written to Theophilus, after a former account of all that Jesus began both to do and to teach until the day He was taken up (He ascended into heaven, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"feature","r":"Acts 16:10–17; 20:5–21:18; 27:1–28:16","v":"From Troas on its writer tells part of the journeys as one of Paul's company, saying we"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00647":{"k":"object","n":"The letter of the apostles and elders to the Gentile brethren","d":"The letter the apostles, elders and brethren at Jerusalem wrote to the Gentile brethren in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia, laying on them no greater burden than four necessary things (Acts 15:23–29)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:25; NKJV notes, note on Acts 15:29; Acts 15:23–29","v":"The apostles, elders and brethren greet the Gentile brethren in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia: some who went out from them troubled them, saying they must be circumcised and keep the law (NU omits it, by the NKJV note), without commandment; with one accord (purpose or mind, by the NKJV note) they send chosen men with Barnabas and Paul, Judas and Silas, for it seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to them to lay on them no greater burden than to abstain from things offered to idols, from blood, from things strangled and from sexual immorality (or fornication, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 15:23","v":"this letter"},{"p":"feature","r":"Acts 16:4","v":"Its decrees were delivered to the churches through the cities"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 15:22–23","e":"GRP-01049"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 15:22–23","e":"GRP-01199"},{"p":"occasion","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 21:25; Acts 15:22–29; 21:25","e":"EVT-03154"}],"nm":{"EVT-03154":"The apostles and elders send their letter with Judas and Silas","GRP-01049":"The twelve apostles","GRP-01199":"The elders of the church at Jerusalem"}},"OBJ-00648":{"k":"object","n":"The altar to the unknown God at Athens","d":"An altar at Athens with the inscription TO THE UNKNOWN GOD, found by Paul among the objects of their worship (Acts 17:23)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Acts 17:23","v":"The inscription: To the unknown God"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 17:23","v":"an altar"},{"p":"placed at","r":"Acts 17:23","e":"PLC-01586"}],"nm":{"PLC-01586":"Athens"}},"OBJ-00649":{"k":"object","n":"The books of magic burned at Ephesus","d":"The books of those who had practiced magic at Ephesus, brought together and burned in the sight of all (Acts 19:19)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 19:19","v":"their books"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Acts 19:19","e":"EVT-03192"}],"nm":{"EVT-03192":"Those who practiced magic burn their books at Ephesus"}},"OBJ-00650":{"k":"object","n":"The image of Diana that fell down from Zeus","d":"The image which fell down from Zeus, of which the city of the Ephesians was guardian (Acts 19:35)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 19:35; Acts 19:35","v":"the image which fell down from Zeus"},{"p":"owner","r":"Acts 19:35","e":"DEI-00034","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"The city clerk named the image with the temple of Diana, of which Ephesus was guardian (Acts 19:35)."},{"p":"placed at","r":"Acts 19:35","e":"PLC-01594"}],"nm":{"DEI-00034":"Diana","PLC-01594":"Ephesus"}},"OBJ-00651":{"k":"object","n":"The letter of Claudius Lysias to Felix","d":"The letter the commander wrote to Felix the governor when he sent Paul to Caesarea (Acts 23:25–30)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 23:30; Acts 23:26–30","v":"Claudius Lysias greets the most excellent governor Felix: this man, seized by the Jews and about to be killed, he rescued with the troops, having learned he was a Roman; brought before their council, he was accused of questions of their law but of nothing deserving death or chains; told that the Jews lay in wait for him (NU there would be a plot against the man, by the NKJV note), he sent him at once and bade his accusers state their charges before Felix"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 23:25","v":"a letter"},{"p":"made by","r":"Acts 23:25–26","e":"PER-03273"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Acts 23:25–33","e":"EVT-03217"}],"nm":{"EVT-03217":"Paul is sent by night to Felix at Caesarea","PER-03273":"The commander of the garrison at Jerusalem"}},"OBJ-00652":{"k":"object","n":"The ship of Adramyttium","d":"The ship of Adramyttium in which Paul put to sea for Italy, meaning to sail along the coasts of Asia, as far as Myra (Acts 27:2–6)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:2","v":"a ship of Adramyttium"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00653":{"k":"object","n":"The Alexandrian ship that was wrecked at Malta","d":"The Alexandrian ship sailing to Italy which the centurion found at Myra, driven by the tempest and broken up off Malta (Acts 27:6–44)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:6","v":"an Alexandrian ship"},{"p":"owner","r":"Acts 27:11","e":"PER-03290"}],"nm":{"PER-03290":"The owner of the Alexandrian ship"}},"OBJ-00654":{"k":"object","n":"The skiff of the Alexandrian ship","d":"The ship's skiff, secured under Clauda, by which the sailors sought to escape until the soldiers cut it away (Acts 27:16–32)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 27:16, 30, 32","v":"the skiff"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00655":{"k":"object","n":"The wind Euroclydon","d":"The tempestuous head wind that caught the ship off Crete and drove it (Acts 27:14–15)","f":[{"p":"name","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 27:14; Acts 27:14","v":"Euroclydon"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00656":{"k":"object","n":"The viper that fastened on Paul's hand","d":"The viper that came out of the sticks Paul laid on the fire on Malta and fastened on his hand (Acts 28:3–5)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:3","v":"a viper"},{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:4–5","v":"the creature"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00657":{"k":"object","n":"The Alexandrian ship of the Twin Brothers","d":"The Alexandrian ship which had wintered at Malta, whose figurehead was the Twin Brothers, in which Paul sailed from Malta (Acts 28:11)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Acts 28:11","v":"an Alexandrian ship"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Acts 28:11; Acts 28:11","v":"Its figurehead was the Twin Brothers (Gr. Dioskouroi, Zeus's sons Castor and Pollux, by the NKJV note), and it had wintered at the island"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00658":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Romans","d":"The letter Paul wrote to all in Rome beloved of God and called to be saints, of the gospel of God's righteousness through faith for Jew and Gentile, and of his wish to come to them (Romans 1:1–16:27)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:16; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:29; NKJV notes, note on Romans 1:31; Romans 1:1–32","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle and set apart to the gospel God promised through His prophets, greeted all in Rome called to be saints; he thanked God for their faith and longed to come to them, having often been hindered, for he was not ashamed of the gospel (NU omits of Christ, by the NKJV note), God's power to save all who believe, in which His righteousness is revealed from faith to faith. God's wrath is shown against men who suppress (hold down, by the NKJV note) the truth: knowing God, they did not glorify Him or give thanks, and He gave them up to uncleanness, vile passions and a debased mind, full of every wrong (NU omits sexual immorality and unforgiving, by the NKJV notes)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:5; NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 2:29; Romans 2:1–29","v":"Whoever judges another while doing the same has no excuse and stores up (by the NKJV note) wrath for the day of God's righteous judgment, when He will repay each by his works without partiality, the Jew first and also the Greek (Gentile, by the NKJV note). Gentiles who by nature do what the law asks show it written on their hearts; a Jew who boasts in the law and breaks it dishonors God, and the true Jew is one inwardly, circumcised in heart, whose praise is from God (Jew being literally praise, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:22; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:25; NKJV notes, note on Romans 3:28; Romans 3:1–31","v":"The Jews' advantage is that God's oracles (sayings, Scriptures, by the NKJV note) were entrusted to them, and their unbelief cannot undo His faithfulness; yet Jews and Greeks alike are under sin, as the Scriptures say, and no one is justified by the works of the law. Now God's righteousness is shown apart from the law, through faith in Jesus Christ, to all who believe (NU omits and on all, by the NKJV note), all being justified freely (without any cost, by the NKJV note) by His grace through the redemption in Christ Jesus, whom God set forth as a propitiation (mercy seat, by the NKJV note); boasting is shut out, a man is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) by faith apart from the deeds of the law, and the law is upheld"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 4:3; Romans 4:1–25","v":"Abraham was not justified by works: he believed God, and it was counted (imputed, credited, reckoned, counted, by the NKJV note) to him for righteousness, as David too speaks of the blessedness of the man whose sins are forgiven. It was counted while he was uncircumcised, and circumcision was the seal of that faith, so that he is the father of all who believe; the promise came through faith and not through the law, and at about a hundred years old he did not waver but was sure God could do what He had promised, as righteousness will be counted to those who believe in Him who raised Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Romans 5:6; Romans 5:1–21","v":"Justified by faith, we have (some ancient manuscripts let us have, by the NKJV note) peace with God through Jesus Christ and rejoice in hope, tribulation working perseverance (endurance, by the NKJV note), character and hope; Christ died for the ungodly at the right time (by the NKJV note), and God shows His love in that Christ died for us while we were sinners. As sin and death came into the world through one man, and death reigned from Adam to Moses, so the gift of grace and righteousness abounds through the one Man, Jesus Christ: one man's disobedience made many sinners, one Man's obedience will make many righteous, and where sin abounded grace abounded much more"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 6:23; Romans 6:1–23","v":"Those baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death and buried with Him, to walk in newness of life as He was raised; their old man was crucified with Him so that they need serve sin no longer, and they are to count themselves (consider, by the NKJV note) dead to sin and alive to God, under grace and not under law. Set free from sin, they have become slaves of righteousness and of God; sin pays its wage in death, while God's free gift (by the NKJV note) is eternal life in Christ Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 7:1; Romans 7:1–25","v":"The law rules (by the NKJV note) a man only while he lives, and as a wife is free when her husband dies, so believers have died to the law through the body of Christ, to belong to Him who was raised and to serve in the newness of the Spirit. The law is not sin but holy, and the commandment holy, just and good; yet sin seized its chance in the commandment, deceived and killed, and Paul found another law in his members at war with the law of his mind, cried out for one to rescue him from this deadly body, and gave thanks to God through Jesus Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:6; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:7; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:15; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:21; NKJV notes, note on Romans 8:26; Romans 8:1–39","v":"There is now no condemnation for those in Christ Jesus (NU omits the rest of the verse, by the NKJV note), for the law of the Spirit of life has freed them from the law of sin and death; God sent His Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and condemned sin in the flesh. The fleshly (by the NKJV notes) mind is at enmity with God, but those led by the Spirit of God are His sons, crying Abba (Father, in Aramaic, by the NKJV note), heirs of God with Christ. The creation, made subject to futility, waits to be freed from its bondage to corruption (decay, by the NKJV note); the Spirit intercedes for the saints (NU omits for us, by the NKJV note); everything turns to good for those who love God and are called by His purpose, and nothing can part them from His love in Christ Jesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:3; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:28; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:29; NKJV notes, note on Romans 9:32; Romans 9:1–33","v":"Paul grieves for his countrymen (or relatives, by the NKJV note), the Israelites, to whom the covenants and promises belong and from whom Christ came; yet God's word has not failed, for not all who are of Israel are Israel: the children of the promise are counted as the seed, through Isaac, and before the twins were born it was said that the older would serve the younger. God has mercy on whom He wills, as He told Moses, and raised up Pharaoh to show His power; as the potter has power over the clay, He has called vessels of mercy from Jews and Gentiles, as Hosea and Isaiah said, a remnant to be saved (NU the Lord will finish the work and cut it short upon the earth, by the NKJV note), left by the Lord of Sabaoth (Hosts, in Hebrew, by the NKJV note). Gentiles gained the righteousness of faith, while Israel, seeking it by works (NU by works, omitting of the law, by the NKJV note), stumbled at the stone laid in Zion"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 10:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 10:15; Romans 10:1–21","v":"Paul prays that Israel (NU them, by the NKJV note) may be saved: zealous for God but without knowledge, they sought a righteousness of their own, but Christ ends the law as a way to righteousness for all who believe. The word of faith is near: whoever confesses Jesus as Lord and believes that God raised Him from the dead will be saved, with no distinction between Jew and Greek. Yet how can they call without hearing, or hear without a preacher who is sent (NU omits part of the words on the feet of those who preach, by the NKJV note)? Faith comes by hearing the word of God, and Moses and Isaiah said that God would be found by a nation that did not seek Him, while He held out His hands all day to a disobedient people"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:6; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:26; NKJV notes, note on Romans 11:32; Romans 11:1–36","v":"God has not cast away His people: Paul himself is an Israelite of the tribe of Benjamin, and as in Elijah's day God kept seven thousand, so now there is a remnant chosen by grace (NU omits the rest of v. 6, by the NKJV note), the rest being blinded. Through Israel's fall (trespass, by the NKJV note) salvation came to the Gentiles; the wild branches grafted into the olive tree are not to boast, for God can graft the natural branches in again. Blindness in part has come on Israel until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and so all Israel will be saved (or delivered, by the NKJV note), for God's gifts and calling cannot be revoked, and He has shut all up in (by the NKJV note) disobedience to have mercy on all; to Him be glory forever"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 12:1; Romans 12:1–21","v":"Paul urges (by the NKJV note) the brethren to offer their bodies to God as a living sacrifice, their rational (by the NKJV note) service, and to be changed by the renewing of their mind; each is to think soberly of himself and use the gift he has been given, for they are many members of one body in Christ. Love is to be without hypocrisy, warm and humble; they are to bless those who persecute them, repay no one evil for evil, leave vengeance to the Lord, feed a hungry enemy and overcome evil with good"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 13:13; Romans 13:1–14","v":"Every soul is to be subject to the governing authorities, for all authority is from God, and rulers are His ministers for good who bear the sword against evil; so they pay taxes and give each his due. They are to owe nothing but love, for the commandments (NU omits the one against false witness, by the NKJV note) are summed up in loving one's neighbor as oneself; knowing the time, they are to wake from sleep, cast off the works of darkness, walk decently (by the NKJV note) as in the day and put on the Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:9; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:10; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:19; NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:23; Romans 14:1–23","v":"They are to receive the one weak in faith without disputes: the one who eats all things and the one who eats only vegetables, the one who keeps a day and the one who does not, each lives and dies to the Lord, for Christ died and rose (NU omits and rose, by the NKJV note) to be Lord of the dead and the living, and all will stand before His judgment seat (NU God's, by the NKJV note). They are to put no stumbling block in a brother's way but pursue peace and what builds up (by the NKJV note), for the kingdom of God is righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit, and whatever is not from faith is sin (M places 16:25–27 here, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:1; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:8; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 15:31; Romans 15:1–33","v":"The strong are to bear with the weaknesses (by the NKJV note) of the weak and please their neighbor, as Christ did not please Himself; the Scriptures were written for our learning, and Paul prays the God of patience and comfort to make them of one mind. Christ became a servant (minister, by the NKJV note) to the circumcision to confirm the promises to the fathers, and that the Gentiles might glorify God, as the Scriptures and Isaiah say. Paul wrote boldly as a minister of Jesus Christ to the Gentiles, having fully preached the gospel from Jerusalem round about to Illyricum where Christ was not named; often hindered from coming to Rome, he hoped to see them on his way to Spain (NU omits I shall come to you, by the NKJV note), once he had taken the contribution of Macedonia and Achaia to the poor among the saints at Jerusalem, and asked their prayers that he be delivered from the unbelievers (the disobedient, by the NKJV note) in Judea"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:5; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:11; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:16; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:25; Romans 16:1–27","v":"Paul commended Phoebe, a servant of the church in Cenchrea, and greeted Priscilla and Aquila, who had risked their necks for him, and the church in their house; Epaenetus, the firstfruits of Achaia (NU Asia, by the NKJV note); Mary; Andronicus and Junia, his countrymen and fellow prisoners; Amplias, Urbanus, Stachys, Apelles, the households of Aristobulus and Narcissus, Herodion his countryman (or relative, by the NKJV note), Tryphena, Tryphosa, Persis, Rufus and his mother, Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermas, Patrobas, Hermes, Philologus, Julia, Nereus and his sister, Olympas and the saints with them, adding the greeting of the churches of Christ (NU all the churches, by the NKJV note). They were to avoid those who cause divisions, and the God of peace would soon crush Satan under their feet. Timothy, Lucius, Jason, Sosipater, Tertius who wrote the letter, Gaius his host, Erastus the city's treasurer and Quartus sent greetings (NU omits v. 24, by the NKJV note), and the letter closes in praise to God, alone wise, through Jesus Christ (M places 16:25–27 after 14:23, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Romans 16:22","v":"this epistle"},{"p":"feature","r":"Romans 1:1–7","v":"It is a letter from Paul to all in Rome beloved of God and called to be saints, opening with grace and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Romans 14:23; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:24; NKJV notes, note on Romans 16:25; Romans 14:23; 16:24–27","v":"By the NKJV notes, the NU text omits 16:24, and the M text places the closing praise (16:25–27) after 14:23"},{"p":"made by","r":"Romans 1:1; 15:15","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Romans 1:1–7; 15:15","e":"EVT-03254"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Romans 15:15–16","v":"Paul wrote more boldly on some points to remind them, by the grace given him to be a minister of Jesus Christ to the Gentiles"}],"nm":{"EVT-03254":"Paul writes to the saints at Rome","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00659":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul and Sosthenes wrote to the church of God at Corinth, of its contentions, the message of the cross, the matters the Corinthians had written about, the Lord's Supper, spiritual gifts, love and the resurrection of the dead (1 Corinthians 1:1–16:24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 1:23; 1 Corinthians 1:1–31","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle, and Sosthenes greeted the church of God at Corinth, sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) in Christ Jesus, and thanked God for their gifts; but Chloe's household had told him of contentions (quarrels, by the NKJV note), each saying he was of Paul, Apollos, Cephas or Christ. Paul had baptized only Crispus, Gaius and the household of Stephanas, for Christ sent him to preach; the message (word, by the NKJV note) of the cross is foolishness to those perishing but God's power to the saved, Christ crucified a stumbling block (by the NKJV note) to Jews and foolishness to Greeks (NU Gentiles, by the NKJV note), and God chose the foolish and weak things of the world so that none should glory but in the Lord"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 2:7; 1 Corinthians 2:1–16","v":"Paul came to them not with excellent speech but resolved to know nothing among them but Christ crucified, preaching in demonstration of the Spirit and of power; he speaks God's hidden wisdom, ordained (predetermined, by the NKJV note) before the ages, which the rulers of this age did not know, or they would not have crucified the Lord of glory, but which God has revealed by His Spirit, who searches His deep things; the natural man does not receive them, but we have the mind of Christ"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 3:3; 1 Corinthians 3:1–23","v":"Still carnal (walking according to man, by the NKJV note), they quarrel over Paul and Apollos, who are only ministers: Paul planted, Apollos watered, and God gave the increase; Paul laid the foundation, which is Jesus Christ, and each one's work on it will be tested by fire. They are God's temple, where His Spirit dwells; the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God, so none is to boast in men, for all things are theirs, and they are Christ's"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 4:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 4:9; 1 Corinthians 4:1–21","v":"Paul and Apollos are servants of Christ and stewards of God's mysteries, judged by the Lord, who will bring to light the counsels (motives, by the NKJV note) of the hearts; the apostles are made a spectacle (theater, by the NKJV note) to the world, hungry, beaten and homeless, working with their own hands. As their father in Christ Jesus through the gospel, Paul urges them to imitate him, has sent Timothy to remind them of his ways, and will come shortly, with a rod or in love"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:9; 1 Corinthians 5:1–13","v":"It is reported that a man among them has his father's wife, an immorality not even named (NU omits named, by the NKJV note) among the Gentiles, and they are puffed up (arrogant, by the NKJV note) instead of mourning; Paul, absent in body, has judged him, and bids them deliver him to Satan for the destruction of the flesh and purge out (clean out, by the NKJV note) the old leaven, for Christ our Passover was sacrificed. He had written before not to keep company (associate, by the NKJV note) with the sexually immoral, meaning not those of the world but anyone called a brother who is so"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 6:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 6:20; 1 Corinthians 6:1–20","v":"Brother is not to go to law against brother before unbelievers, for the saints will judge the world and angels; the unrighteous will not inherit the kingdom of God, and such were some of them, but they were washed, sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) and justified. The body is for the Lord, who will raise it, a member of Christ and the temple of the Holy Spirit; they are to flee sexual immorality, for they were bought at a price, and glorify God in their body (NU omits the rest of v. 20, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 7:17; 1 Corinthians 7:1–40","v":"Answering what they wrote to him, Paul says each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband, giving each other their due; he gives the Lord's command that a wife not depart from her husband nor a husband divorce his wife, and his own word that a believer not leave an unbelieving spouse willing to stay. Each is to remain in the calling in which he was called, as he directs (by the NKJV note) in all the churches; for the present distress it is good to stay as one is, though marrying is no sin, and a widow may marry whom she wishes, only in the Lord"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 8:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 8:9; 1 Corinthians 8:1–13","v":"Concerning things offered to idols: knowledge puffs up (makes arrogant, by the NKJV note) but love builds up (by the NKJV note); an idol is nothing, and for us there is one God, the Father, and one Lord Jesus Christ. Yet not everyone has that knowledge, and their liberty must not become a stumbling block (cause of offense, by the NKJV note) to the weak brother for whom Christ died; if food makes his brother stumble, Paul will never eat meat"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 9:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 9:5; 1 Corinthians 9:1–27","v":"Paul is an apostle who has seen Jesus Christ, and the Corinthians are the seal (certification, by the NKJV note) of it; he has the right the other apostles, the brothers of the Lord and Cephas have, to eat and drink and to take along a believing wife (a sister, a wife, by the NKJV note), as the law of Moses says the ox is not to be muzzled and the Lord commanded that those who preach the gospel live from it. But he has used none of it, preaching without charge; free from all, he became a servant to all, all things to all men, that he might save some, and he disciplines his body like a runner for an imperishable crown"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:9; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 10:16; 1 Corinthians 10:1–33","v":"The fathers were all under the cloud, passed through the sea, were baptized into Moses, and ate and drank spiritual food and drink from the Rock that followed them, which was Christ; yet with most of them God was not pleased, and they fell in the wilderness for their lust, idolatry, immorality, testing (by the NKJV note) and complaining, examples written for our instruction (by the NKJV note). God is faithful and makes the way of escape; they are to flee idolatry, for the cup of blessing is the communion (fellowship or sharing, by the NKJV note) of Christ's blood and they cannot share the Lord's table and the table of demons; they may eat what is sold in the market, but do all to the glory of God and give no offense"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 11:22; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 11:30; 1 Corinthians 11:1–34","v":"They are to imitate Paul as he imitates Christ and keep the traditions he delivered: the head of every man is Christ, the head of woman is man and the head of Christ is God, so a man prays with head uncovered and a woman covered. When they come together there are divisions, and it is not the Lord's Supper they eat, one hungry and another drunk, shaming the poor (by the NKJV note); Paul delivers what he received from the Lord of the bread and the cup on the night He was betrayed, and whoever eats unworthily eats judgment to himself, so that many among them are weak and sick and many sleep (are dead, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 12:31; 1 Corinthians 12:1–31","v":"No one says Jesus is Lord but by the Holy Spirit, and none speaking by the Spirit calls Him accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note); there are diversities (allotments or various kinds, by the NKJV note) of gifts, ministries and activities, but one Spirit, one Lord and one God, the Spirit giving to each as He wills. By one Spirit all were baptized into one body, many members needing one another, and they are the body of Christ, in which God appointed apostles, prophets, teachers and the rest; they are to desire the best (NU greater, by the NKJV note) gifts, and yet there is a more excellent way"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 13:10; 1 Corinthians 13:1–13","v":"Tongues, prophecy, knowledge, faith and giving are nothing without love; love is patient and kind, not puffed up (arrogant, by the NKJV note), keeping no account of evil (by the NKJV note), bearing and enduring all things, and it never fails, while prophecies and tongues will cease when that which is perfect (complete, by the NKJV note) has come; faith, hope and love abide, and the greatest is love"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:33; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 14:34; 1 Corinthians 14:1–40","v":"They are to pursue love and desire to prophesy, for prophecy builds up (by the NKJV note) the church, while a tongue without interpretation builds up only the speaker; Paul speaks with tongues more than all, yet in church would rather speak five words with understanding. Tongues are a sign to unbelievers and prophecy to believers; speakers in tongues are to be two or three, in turn, with one to interpret, and the prophets two or three, for God is not the author of confusion (disorder, by the NKJV note) but of peace; their (NU omits your, by the NKJV note) women are to keep silent in the churches, and all is to be done decently and in order"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:20; NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 15:51; 1 Corinthians 15:1–58","v":"Paul delivered what he received: Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, was buried and rose the third day, and was seen by Cephas (Peter, by the NKJV note), then by the twelve, by over five hundred brethren at once, by James, by all the apostles, and last by Paul, the least of the apostles. If the dead do not rise, Christ is not risen and their faith is futile; but He is risen, the firstfruits of those who have died (by the NKJV note), and as in Adam all die so in Christ all will be made alive; at the end He will deliver the kingdom to God the Father, death the last enemy destroyed. The body is sown perishable and raised incorruptible, and in a mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note) all will be changed at the last trumpet, death swallowed up in victory"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 16:22; 1 Corinthians 16:1–24","v":"On the first day of the week each is to lay aside for the collection for the saints, as Paul ordered the churches of Galatia, to be carried to Jerusalem by those they approve. Paul will come through Macedonia and perhaps winter with them, staying at Ephesus until Pentecost, where a great door has opened and there are many adversaries; Timothy is to be received without fear, and Apollos will come when he can. They are to submit to such as the household of Stephanas, whose coming with Fortunatus and Achaicus refreshed Paul; the churches of Asia, Aquila and Priscilla greet them, Paul greets them with his own hand, and whoever does not love the Lord is accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note); O Lord, come"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1","v":"Paul, called to be an apostle"},{"p":"feature","r":"1 Corinthians 16:21","v":"Paul writes its last greeting with his own hand"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1; 16:21","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 Corinthians 1:1–3","e":"EVT-03256"}],"nm":{"EVT-03256":"Paul writes his first letter to the church at Corinth","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00660":{"k":"object","n":"Paul's earlier letter to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul had written to the Corinthians before this one, not to keep company with the sexually immoral (1 Corinthians 5:9)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Corinthians 5:9; 1 Corinthians 5:9–11","v":"Not to keep company (associate, by the NKJV note) with the sexually immoral, by which Paul meant not those of the world but anyone called a brother who was so"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 5:9","v":"my epistle"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 5:9","e":"PER-03213"}],"nm":{"PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00661":{"k":"object","n":"The Corinthians' letter to Paul","d":"The letter in which the Corinthians wrote to Paul of matters he answers, marriage first (1 Corinthians 7:1)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","v":"Matters on which they asked Paul, of which he answers marriage first"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","v":"the things of which you wrote to me"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Corinthians 7:1","e":"GRP-01214"}],"nm":{"GRP-01214":"The Corinthians who believed"}},"OBJ-00662":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Corinthians","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to the church of God at Corinth and the saints in Achaia, of God's comfort in affliction, the ministry of the new covenant, reconciliation, the gift for the saints, and Paul's defense of his apostleship (2 Corinthians 1:1–13:14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 1:10; 2 Corinthians 1:1–24","v":"Paul and Timothy greeted the church of God at Corinth with all the saints in Achaia, and blessed the God of all comfort, who comforts them in all their tribulation that they may comfort others in any trouble (tribulation, by the NKJV note). In Asia they were burdened beyond strength and despaired of life, but God, who raises the dead, delivered them and does (NU shall, by the NKJV note) deliver them. Paul had meant to come to them on the way to Macedonia and again on his return, and was not fickle, for all God's promises are Yes in Christ; he did not come again to Corinth, to spare them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:12; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:17; 2 Corinthians 2:1–17","v":"Paul resolved not to come to them again in sorrow, and wrote instead out of much affliction (tribulation, by the NKJV note) and with many tears, to show his love and test their obedience; the one who caused grief has been punished enough by the majority, and they are to forgive and comfort him, lest Satan take advantage. At Troas, though the Lord opened a door (opportunity, by the NKJV note), Paul had no rest for not finding Titus, and went on to Macedonia; God leads them in triumph in Christ, a fragrance of life to some and of death to others, not peddling (adulterating for gain, by the NKJV note) the word of God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 3:6; 2 Corinthians 3:1–18","v":"The Corinthians are Christ's epistle, written by the Spirit of the living God on tablets of the heart, not of stone. God made Paul fit to serve the new covenant, which is of the Spirit (or spirit, by the NKJV note), who gives life, and not of the letter, which kills. If the ministry carved in stone came with such glory that Israel could not gaze at Moses' face, the ministry of the Spirit has far greater glory; a veil still lies on Israel's hearts when Moses is read, but in Christ it is taken away, and the Lord is the Spirit, who brings liberty"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 4:2; 2 Corinthians 4:1–18","v":"Having this ministry, Paul does not lose heart or handle the word of God deceitfully (adulterating it, by the NKJV note); the god of this age blinds unbelievers' minds, but God, who first called light out of darkness, has lit believers' hearts with the knowledge of His glory seen in Christ's face. They hold this treasure in earthen vessels, pressed on every side but never crushed or destroyed, carrying the dying of Jesus that His life may show in them; their outward man wastes away while the inward man is made new each day, and their brief trouble is gaining them lasting glory"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 5:19; 2 Corinthians 5:1–21","v":"If the earthly tent (the physical body, by the NKJV note) is taken down, believers have an eternal house from God in the heavens, and the Spirit is His guarantee (down payment, earnest, by the NKJV note) of it; they walk by faith, and all will stand before Christ's judgment seat. Christ's love drives them, for He died for all; whoever is in Christ is made new, and in Christ God was bringing the world back to Himself, not imputing (reckoning, by the NKJV note) its trespasses, and made the sinless One sin on our behalf; so Paul speaks as Christ's ambassador, urging them to be reconciled to God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 6:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 6:16; 2 Corinthians 6:1–18","v":"Now is the day of salvation, and they are not to receive God's grace in vain; Paul proves himself God's minister in much endurance (by the NKJV note), through beatings, prisons, riots, toil, sleepless nights and fasts, dying yet alive, poor yet enriching many. His heart is open to them, and they are to open theirs; they are not to be yoked unequally with unbelievers, for light has no communion with darkness nor Christ any accord with Belial, and they are God's living temple (NU we, by the NKJV note), to leave the unbelievers and keep apart"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 7:7; 2 Corinthians 7:1–16","v":"Having these promises, they are to make themselves clean and holy, and open their hearts to Paul, who has wronged no one. When he came to Macedonia he had no rest, with conflicts outside and fears within, but God, the comforter of the downcast, cheered him when Titus arrived, and by the comfort (by the NKJV note) Titus had found among them. Paul's letter made them sorry, and he does not regret it, for their godly sorrow led to repentance and zeal; Titus was refreshed by them, who received him with fear and trembling"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 8:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 8:23; 2 Corinthians 8:1–24","v":"The churches of Macedonia, sorely tried and in deep poverty, gave beyond their means, begging to share in the ministering to the saints (NU, M omit that we would receive, by the NKJV note); Paul urged Titus to complete the same gift among the Corinthians, for Christ made Himself poor for their sakes. He gives advice, not command, that they finish what they began a year before, by an equality, as it is written that he who gathered much had nothing over and he who gathered little had no lack; Titus goes to them of his own accord with a brother famed in all the churches and another often proved diligent, messengers (apostles, sent ones, by the NKJV note) of the churches"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 9:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 9:7; 2 Corinthians 9:1–15","v":"Paul has boasted of them to the Macedonians, saying Achaia has been prepared since last year, and sends the brethren ahead so the gift is ready as a willing gift and not one wrung from them (covetousness, by the NKJV note), lest Macedonians come with him and find them unready. Sparing sowing brings a sparing harvest; each is to give what he has resolved, not under pressure (compulsion, by the NKJV note), for God loves a cheerful giver; He can give them every grace in abundance, and the service meets the saints' needs and overflows in thanks to God for His gift beyond words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 10:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 10:8; 2 Corinthians 10:1–18","v":"Paul pleads with them by the meekness of Christ, lowly when present and bold when absent; the weapons he fights with are not of the flesh (by the NKJV note) but have God's power to tear down strongholds, and his authority from the Lord is for building up (by the NKJV note), not destruction. Some say his letters are weighty but his presence weak; he will be in deed what he is in his letters, and boasts only within the sphere God gave him, which reaches to them, hoping to preach in regions beyond, for the one approved is the one the Lord commends"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 11:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 11:7; 2 Corinthians 11:1–33","v":"Paul is jealous for them with godly jealousy, having betrothed them to Christ, and fears that as the serpent deceived Eve their minds may be led from the simplicity in Christ (NU adds and purity, by the NKJV note). He is not behind the most eminent apostles; he preached to them free of charge, putting himself down (by the NKJV note), while brethren from Macedonia met his needs. Such men are false apostles, as Satan disguises himself as an angel of light. Boasting as a fool, he names his labors, stripes, beatings, stoning, shipwrecks and perils, his care for all the churches, and his escape from Damascus in a basket when the governor under King Aretas held the city"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 12:7; 2 Corinthians 12:1–21","v":"Paul knows a man in Christ taken up fourteen years before to the third heaven, into Paradise, where he heard words no man may speak; to keep him from pride over the greatness of the revelations he was given a thorn in the flesh, Satan's messenger to beat (by the NKJV note) him, and when he asked three times for its removal the Lord answered that His grace was enough, for His power is perfected in weakness. The signs of an apostle were done among them; he is ready to come the third time and will not be a burden, though he fears he will find quarrels and unrepented sin"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 13:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 13:14; 2 Corinthians 13:1–14","v":"This will be his third coming, and every word is to be established by two or three witnesses; he will not spare those who sinned, since they seek proof of Christ speaking in him, who was crucified in weakness yet lives by God's power. They are to examine themselves whether they are in the faith, unless they fail the test (by the NKJV note); he writes while absent so that when present he need not use sharpness, and bids them farewell, wishing them Christ's grace, God's love and the communion (fellowship, by the NKJV note) of the Holy Spirit"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1; 10:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 Corinthians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03262"}],"nm":{"EVT-03262":"Paul writes his second letter to the church at Corinth","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00663":{"k":"object","n":"Paul's letter written with many tears","d":"The letter Paul wrote to the Corinthians out of much affliction and with many tears, to show his love and test their obedience (2 Corinthians 2:3–4, 9)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 2:4","v":"the letter written with many tears"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Corinthians 7:8","v":"my letter","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul writes that his letter made them sorry for a while and that he wrote to show his care for them (2 Corinthians 7:8–12), as he wrote the letter with many tears to show his love and test their obedience (2:3–4, 9)."},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Corinthians 2:4; 2 Corinthians 2:4","v":"Paul wrote it out of much affliction (tribulation, by the NKJV note) and anguish of heart, with many tears"},{"p":"feature","r":"2 Corinthians 7:8–12","v":"It made them sorry for a while, and their godly sorrow led to repentance; Paul had regretted sending it but no longer did, for he wrote not for the sake of the one who did wrong or the one wronged, but that his care for them might appear","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul writes of a letter that made them sorry (2 Corinthians 7:8–12), as he wrote earlier of the letter he sent with many tears (2:3–4, 9)."},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Corinthians 2:3–4, 9","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"purpose","r":"2 Corinthians 2:4, 9","v":"Not to grieve them but that they might know how much Paul loved them, and to test whether they were obedient in all things"}],"nm":{"PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00664":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Galatians","d":"The letter Paul, with all the brethren with him, wrote to the churches of Galatia, of the one gospel, which he received by the revelation of Jesus Christ, of justification by faith, not by works of the law, and of liberty in Christ and the walk in the Spirit (Galatians 1:1–6:18)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:7; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 1:18; Galatians 1:1–24","v":"Paul, an apostle sent not by men but by Jesus Christ and by God the Father, who raised Christ from the dead, with all the brethren with him, greeted the churches of Galatia. He marveled that they were turning so soon to a different gospel, though there is no other; some trouble them, wanting to pervert (distort, by the NKJV note) the gospel of Christ, and whoever preaches another gospel, even Paul or an angel from heaven, is to be accursed (Gr. anathema, by the NKJV note). The gospel Paul preached came not from man but through the revelation of Jesus Christ. In Judaism he had persecuted the church of God; but when God revealed His Son in him, he went to Arabia and back to Damascus, and after three years went up to Jerusalem to visit Peter (NU Cephas, by the NKJV note), staying fifteen days, and then into Syria and Cilicia; the churches of Judea, who did not know him by face, glorified God in him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:6; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:12; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 2:21; Galatians 2:1–21","v":"After fourteen years Paul went up to Jerusalem again by (because of, by the NKJV note) revelation, with Barnabas and Titus, and laid his gospel privately before those of reputation; Titus, a Greek, was not compelled to be circumcised, and the false brethren who crept in to spy on the liberty believers have in Christ were not yielded to for a single hour. God shows no partiality (lit. does not receive the face of a man, by the NKJV note); nothing was added to Paul by those who seemed to be something, but James, Cephas (Peter, by the NKJV note) and John gave the right hand of fellowship to him and Barnabas, the two to go to the Gentiles, the three to the circumcised, asking only that the two remember the poor. When Peter (NU Cephas, by the NKJV note) came to Antioch, Paul withstood (opposed, by the NKJV note) him to his face, for when men came from James he drew back from eating with Gentiles, fearing those of the circumcision (Jewish Christians, by the NKJV note), and the rest of the Jews and even Barnabas went with him. Paul asked him before them all why (NU how can you, by the NKJV note) he compelled Gentiles to live as Jews (some interpreters stop the quotation here, by the NKJV note): a man is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) by faith in Christ, not by the law's works; Paul has been crucified with Christ, who lives in him, and if righteousness came through the law, Christ died in vain (for nothing, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:4; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:11; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:17; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:23; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 3:24; Galatians 3:1–29","v":"Paul asked the foolish Galatians who had bewitched them (NU omits that you should not obey the truth, by the NKJV note), before whose eyes Christ crucified was clearly portrayed (NU omits among you, by the NKJV note): had they received the Spirit through the law's works or through the hearing of faith, and had they suffered so many (or great, by the NKJV note) things in vain? As Abraham believed God and was counted righteous, only those of faith are his sons, and they are blessed with him; all who rest on the law's works are under its curse, and by the law no one is justified (declared righteous, by the NKJV note) before God, since the just shall live by faith. Christ redeemed us from the law's curse, becoming a curse for us, that Abraham's blessing might come on the Gentiles. A man's covenant, once confirmed, is not annulled or added to; the law, coming four hundred and thirty years after, cannot set aside the covenant God confirmed before (NU omits in Christ, by the NKJV note), and the promises were made to Abraham and his Seed, who is Christ. The law was added on account of transgressions, to stand until the Seed came, appointed through angels by a mediator. The law is not against God's promises; Scripture confined all under sin, so that those who believe might receive the promise that rests on faith in Jesus Christ. The law kept us under guard, kept (lit. confined, by the NKJV note) for the faith that would later be revealed, and was our tutor (in a household, the guardian responsible for the care and discipline of the children, by the NKJV note) to lead us to Christ. Now the Galatians are all God's sons through faith in Christ Jesus; the baptized have put on Christ, and all are one in Him, neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female, and those who are Christ's are Abraham's seed and heirs by promise"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:6; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:7; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:14; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:15; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 4:24; Galatians 4:1–31","v":"An heir, while a child, is no different from a slave, though master of all, but is placed under guardians and stewards till the time his father appointed; so we, as children, were held in bondage under the world's elements. When the fullness of time came, God sent His Son, born (or made, by the NKJV note) of a woman and under the law, so as to redeem those under the law, and so that we might receive adoption as sons; and because the Galatians are sons, God sent into their hearts the Spirit of His Son, crying out, 'Abba (lit., in Aram., Father, by the NKJV note), Father!' So each of them is now a son and not a slave, and, being a son, an heir (NU through God, by the NKJV note) of God through Christ (NU omits through Christ, by the NKJV note). Once, not knowing God, the Galatians served what by nature are not gods; Paul asked how it was that now, knowing God, or rather known by Him, they were turning again to those weak and beggarly elements, wishing to be in bondage again; they were observing days, months, seasons and years. Paul feared he had labored for them in vain. He urged the brethren to become as he was, for he had become as they were; they had not wronged him. Because of a physical infirmity he first preached the gospel to them, and they did not despise or reject his trial in the flesh but received him as an angel (or messenger, by the NKJV note) of God, as they would Christ Jesus. Paul asked what (NU where, by the NKJV note) was the blessing they had enjoyed, for if possible they would have plucked out their own eyes to give to him; and he asked whether he had become their enemy by telling them the truth. Others were courting them eagerly, but not for good, wishing to shut them out so that they would be zealous for those others; zeal in a good thing is good at all times, not only when Paul was with them. He travailed again, as in birth, for his little children until Christ should take form in them, and wished he were with them now to change his tone, for he had doubts about them. Paul asked those who wished to be under the law whether they did not hear the law: Abraham had two sons, one by a bondwoman, born according to the flesh, the other by a freewoman, through promise; these things are symbolic, for the women are the (NU, M omit the, by the NKJV note) two covenants. The covenant from Mount Sinai, giving birth to bondage, is Hagar, who is Mount Sinai in Arabia, answering to the present Jerusalem, in bondage with her children; while the Jerusalem above is free, and she is mother to us all; it is written that the barren one is to rejoice, since the desolate one has many more children than the one with a husband. The brethren, like Isaac, are children of promise; and as the son born according to the flesh persecuted the one born according to the Spirit, so it is now. But Scripture says the bondwoman and her son are to be cast out, for her son is not to inherit with the freewoman's son; so the brethren are children of the free, not of the bondwoman"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:3; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:12; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:19; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 5:23; Galatians 5:1–26","v":"Paul bade the Galatians stand fast (NU For freedom Christ has made us free; stand fast therefore, and, by the NKJV note) in the liberty with which Christ set us free, and not be held again under a yoke of bondage. If they became circumcised, Christ would profit them nothing, and every man who becomes circumcised is a debtor (obligated, by the NKJV note) to keep all the law; those of them who tried to be justified by law were estranged from Christ and fallen from grace. Through the Spirit we eagerly await the hope of righteousness by faith; in Christ Jesus, circumcision and uncircumcision alike count for nothing, but faith working through love does. They had run well, and Paul asked who had hindered them from obeying the truth; the persuasion that hindered them was not from Him who calls them, and a little leaven works through the whole lump. In the Lord he was confident they would hold no other view, but the one troubling them would bear his judgment, whoever he was. If Paul still preached circumcision, he asked, why was he still persecuted? For then the cross would no longer give offense. He wished that those who troubled them would even cut themselves off (mutilate themselves, by the NKJV note). The brethren were called to liberty, yet were not to use it as an opening for the flesh, but to serve one another through love, since one word fulfills the whole law, to love one's neighbor as oneself; and if they bit and devoured one another, they were to beware lest they be consumed by one another. Paul bade them walk in the Spirit, and they would not carry out the flesh's lust, since the flesh and the Spirit are set against each other, so that the brethren do not do what they wish; but if led by the Spirit, they are not under the law. The flesh's works are plain: idolatry and sorcery; adultery (NU omits adultery, by the NKJV note), fornication (sexual immorality, by the NKJV note), lewdness and uncleanness; envy, jealousies and hatred; contentions, dissensions, heresies and selfish ambitions; outbursts of wrath and murders (NU omits murders, by the NKJV note); drunkenness, revelries and the like; as he had told them before, he told them again that those who practice such works will not inherit God's kingdom. But the Spirit's fruit is love, joy and peace, kindness and goodness, longsuffering and faithfulness, gentleness (meekness, by the NKJV note) and self-control; no law stands against such things. Christ's own have crucified the flesh, its desires and its passions. If we live in the Spirit, we are also to walk in the Spirit, not becoming conceited, provoking or envying one another"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Galatians 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Galatians 6:14; Galatians 6:1–18","v":"Paul bade the brethren who are spiritual restore gently any man overtaken (caught, by the NKJV note) in a trespass, each watching himself lest he too be tempted; they were to bear one another's burdens, so fulfilling Christ's law. Whoever thinks himself something when he is nothing deceives himself; each was to examine his own work, and then his rejoicing would rest in himself alone, not in anyone else, for each will bear his own load. The one taught the word was to share in every good thing with his teacher. God is not mocked: a man will reap whatever he sows, corruption from the flesh if he sows to his flesh, everlasting life from the Spirit if he sows to the Spirit. We are not to grow tired of doing good, since we will reap in due season, provided we do not lose heart; so, as we have opportunity, we are to do good to all, above all to the household of faith. Paul wrote to the Galatians in large letters with his own hand. Those wanting a good showing in the flesh would compel them to be circumcised, only to escape persecution for Christ's cross; even the circumcised did not keep the law, yet wanted the Galatians circumcised so as to boast in the Galatians' flesh. Paul would boast only in our Lord Jesus Christ's cross, by whom (Or which, the cross, by the NKJV note) the world and he have been crucified, each to the other, since in Christ Jesus circumcision and uncircumcision alike count for nothing, but a new creation does. He wished peace and mercy on all who walk by this rule, and on the Israel of God. From then on no one was to trouble him, for he bore the marks of the Lord Jesus in his body; and he wished that our Lord Jesus Christ's grace be with the brethren's spirit"},{"p":"designation","r":"Galatians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle"},{"p":"feature","r":"Galatians 6:11","v":"Paul writes to the Galatians in large letters with his own hand"},{"p":"made by","r":"Galatians 1:1; 6:11","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Galatians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03269"}],"nm":{"EVT-03269":"Paul writes to the churches of Galatia","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00665":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Ephesians","d":"The letter Paul wrote to the saints at Ephesus, faithful in Christ Jesus, of every spiritual blessing in Christ, of salvation by grace through faith, of Jew and Gentile made one body in Christ, of the mystery made known to him, of the walk worthy of their calling, and of God's armor (Ephesians 1:1–6:24)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:10; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 1:21; Ephesians 1:1–23","v":"Paul, by God's will an apostle of Jesus Christ, greeted the saints in Ephesus, faithful in Christ Jesus, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul blessed God, our Lord Jesus Christ's Father, for He has given us every spiritual blessing in Christ, in the heavenly places: before the world was founded He chose us in Him, to be holy and blameless before Him in love, and predestined us to be adopted as sons through Jesus Christ, as it pleased His will, to the praise of His glorious grace, through which He made us accepted (Lit. bestowed grace (favor) upon us, by the NKJV note) in the Beloved. In Him we are redeemed through His blood, our sins forgiven, by the riches of His grace, lavished on us in all wisdom and prudence (understanding, by the NKJV note); God has let us know the mystery of His will, by the good pleasure He purposed in Himself: in the dispensation when the times reach their fullness, to gather into one in Christ all things, both (NU, M omit both, by the NKJV note) in heaven and on earth. In Him we have also obtained an inheritance, predestined by the purpose of the One who works all things by the counsel of His will, so that we, the first to trust in Christ, might be for the praise of His glory. The saints at Ephesus too trusted in Him, when they heard the gospel of their salvation, the word of truth, and, having believed, were sealed, their seal the Holy Spirit of promise, who (NU which, by the NKJV note) is the guarantee (down payment, earnest, by the NKJV note) of our inheritance until the purchased possession is redeemed, for the praise of His glory. Having heard of their faith in the Lord Jesus and their love for all the saints, Paul never ceased giving thanks for them, remembering them in his prayers, asking that God, the Father of glory, would give them a spirit of wisdom and revelation in knowing Him, the eyes of their understanding (NU, M hearts, by the NKJV note) enlightened, to know the hope of His calling, the glorious riches of His inheritance in the saints, as well as how exceedingly great His power is toward us who believe, by the working of the mighty power He worked in Christ, raising Him from the dead and setting Him in the heavenly places at His right hand, far above all principality (rule, by the NKJV note) and might (power, by the NKJV note), dominion and power (authority, by the NKJV note), and every name named, in this age and in the age to come; God put all things under Christ's feet and made Him head over all things for the church, His body, the fullness of the One who fills all in all"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 2:2; Ephesians 2:1–22","v":"God made alive the saints at Ephesus, who had been dead in trespasses and sins, once walking in them by the course (Gr. aion, aeon, by the NKJV note) of this world and by the prince who holds the power of the air, that spirit now at work in the sons of disobedience; among them we all once lived in the lusts of the flesh, doing what the flesh and the mind desired, and by nature were children of wrath, like the rest. But God, rich in mercy, out of His great love for us made us alive with Christ even when we were dead in our trespasses (by grace the saints at Ephesus have been saved), raised us up with Him and seated us in Christ Jesus in the heavenly places, to show in the ages to come how exceedingly rich His grace is, in His kindness to us in Christ Jesus. By grace they have been saved through faith, and not of themselves, as God's gift, not of works, so that no one may boast; for we are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which God prepared beforehand for us to walk in. They were to remember that once, as Gentiles in the flesh, called Uncircumcision by those called the Circumcision (a circumcision made by hands in the flesh), they were without Christ, aliens from Israel's commonwealth and strangers to the covenants of promise, without God and without hope in the world; now, though, in Christ Jesus they who were once far away have been brought near by Christ's blood. For He Himself is our peace: He made Jew and Gentile one, broke down the dividing wall that separated them, abolishing in His flesh the enmity, the law of commandments in ordinances, to make of the two one new man in Himself, so making peace, and, in one body, to reconcile both to God through the cross, by which He put the enmity to death. He came preaching peace to the saints at Ephesus, once afar off, and to those near, since we both have access through Him to the Father by one Spirit. So they are strangers and foreigners no more, but fellow citizens with the saints, of God's household, built on the apostles and prophets as their foundation, with Jesus Christ Himself the chief corner stone; in Him the whole building, joined together, grows to be a temple holy in the Lord, and in Him they too are being built together into a place where God dwells in the Spirit"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:10; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 3:14; Ephesians 3:1–21","v":"Paul, the prisoner of Christ Jesus on behalf of the saints at Ephesus, whom he addressed as Gentiles, wrote of the dispensation (stewardship, by the NKJV note) of God's grace given him for them, if they had heard of it: by revelation God made known to him the mystery, as he had briefly written already, by which, in reading, they could understand his insight into the mystery of Christ, not made known to men in other ages as it has now been revealed to the holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit: that the Gentiles were to be fellow heirs, of one body, sharers in God's promise in Christ by the gospel. Of the gospel Paul became a minister, by the gift of God's grace given him by the working of His power. This grace was given to him, though he was less than the least of the saints, to preach Christ's unsearchable riches among the Gentiles and to make all see the fellowship (NU, M stewardship (dispensation), by the NKJV note) of the mystery, hidden from the beginning of the ages in God, the Creator of all things through Jesus Christ (NU omits through Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note), so that through the church the manifold (variegated or many-sided, by the NKJV note) wisdom of God might now be made known to the principalities (rulers, by the NKJV note) and powers in heavenly places, by the eternal purpose He fulfilled in Christ Jesus our Lord; in Him we have bold and confident access through faith in Him. Paul asked the saints at Ephesus not to lose heart at his tribulations for them, which are their glory. For this reason he bowed his knees to our Lord Jesus Christ's Father (NU omits of our Lord Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note), the whole family in heaven and on earth being named from Him, praying that by the riches of His glory He would strengthen them with might in the inner man through His Spirit, that Christ might dwell in their hearts through faith, and that they, rooted and grounded in love, might with all the saints grasp the length and width, the height and depth, and know Christ's love, which passes knowledge, and be filled with all God's fullness. Paul closed in praise, that glory be to Him who can do far more than all we ask or think, by the power at work in us, by Christ Jesus in the church, for all generations, forever"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:1; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:6; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:17; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:27; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:29; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 4:31; Ephesians 4:1–32","v":"Paul, the prisoner of the Lord (Lit. in, by the NKJV note), urged (exhort, encourage, by the NKJV note) the saints at Ephesus to walk in a way worthy of the calling to which they were called, in all lowliness and gentleness, longsuffering, bearing lovingly with one another, striving to keep the Spirit's unity in the bond of peace. Paul wrote of one Spirit and one body, as they were called in one hope; one faith and baptism, and one Lord; and one God and Father of all, over all, through all and in them (NU omits you; M us, by the NKJV note) all. To each of us grace was given by the measure of Christ's gift; and so it is said that He ascended on high, leading captivity captive, and gave men gifts. Paul asked what His ascending means, if not that He had also first (NU omits first, by the NKJV note) gone down into the earth's lower parts; the One who descended also ascended far above all heavens, to fill all things. It is He who gave some as evangelists, some as pastors and teachers, some as apostles, and some as prophets, to equip the saints to do the work of ministry, for the edification (building up, by the NKJV note) of Christ's body, until we all reach unity in the faith and in knowing the Son of God, and come to be a perfect man, attaining the measure of the stature of Christ's fullness; so that we should be children no longer, tossed to and fro, carried here and there by every wind of doctrine through men's trickery and their cunning craftiness in deceitful plotting, but rather, speaking the truth in love, might grow up in every way into Christ, who is the head. From Him the whole body, knit and joined together by what each joint supplies, by the effective working through which each part does its share, grows and builds itself up in love. Paul testified in the Lord that the saints at Ephesus were to walk no longer as the rest of the Gentiles do (NU omits the rest of, by the NKJV note); those Gentiles walk in their mind's futility, their understanding darkened, estranged from God's life through the ignorance in them and the blindness of their heart, and, past feeling, have given themselves up to lewdness, practicing every uncleanness greedily. But the saints at Ephesus had not learned Christ in that way, if indeed they had heard Him and were taught by Him, as truth is in Jesus: to put off the old man of their former conduct, which grows corrupt by deceitful lusts, to be made new in the spirit of their mind, and to put on the new man, created according to God in righteousness and holiness that are true. So, putting away lying, each was to speak the truth to his neighbor, since we are members of one another. They were to be angry without sinning; they were not to let the sun set on their wrath, nor give the devil place (an opportunity, by the NKJV note). The one who stole was to steal no more, but rather to labor, working with his hands at what is good, so as to have something to give the one in need. No corrupt word was to come out of their mouth, but only what is good for needed edification (building up, by the NKJV note), to give grace to those who hear. They were not to bring grief to the Holy Spirit of God; by Him they had been sealed for the day of redemption. All malice and bitterness, anger and wrath, clamor (loud quarreling, by the NKJV note) and evil speaking were to be put away from them; they were to be kind and tenderhearted to one another, forgiving each other as God in Christ forgave them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:5; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:11; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:13; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:15; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:21; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:26; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 5:30; Ephesians 5:1–33","v":"The saints at Ephesus were to imitate God as dear children, walking in love as Christ loved us and gave Himself for us as an offering and sacrifice to God, a sweet-smelling aroma. Fornication, every kind of uncleanness and covetousness were not even to be named among them, as befits saints, nor filthiness, foolish talk or coarse jesting, which are unfitting; rather there was to be thanksgiving, for, as they knew (NU know this, by the NKJV note), no fornicator, no unclean person and no covetous man, the covetous man being an idolater, has an inheritance in Christ's and God's kingdom. No one was to deceive them with empty words, for these things bring God's wrath on the sons of disobedience; so the saints at Ephesus were not to be partakers with them. They were once darkness, but now light in the Lord; they were to walk as children of light, finding out what pleases the Lord, since the fruit of the Spirit (NU light, by the NKJV note) lies in all goodness, righteousness and truth. They were to have no fellowship with the fruitless works of darkness, but were rather to expose (reprove, by the NKJV note) them, since what such men do in secret is shameful even to mention; but all that is exposed (reproved, by the NKJV note) is made manifest by the light, since what makes manifest is light. So it is said that the sleeper was to awake and rise from the dead, and Christ would give him light. They were to see that they walked circumspectly (carefully, by the NKJV note), as wise men and not as fools, redeeming the time, since the days are evil; they were not to be unwise, but to understand what the Lord's will is. They were not to be drunk with wine, for in it is dissipation, but to be filled with the Spirit, addressing one another in hymns, psalms and spiritual songs, and singing and making melody to the Lord in their heart, always giving thanks to God the Father for all things in our Lord Jesus Christ's name, and submitting to one another out of the fear of God (NU Christ, by the NKJV note). Wives were to submit to their own husbands as to the Lord, since the husband is the wife's head as Christ is the church's head, He being the body's Savior; and as the church submits to Christ, wives were likewise to be subject in everything to their own husbands. Husbands were to love their wives as Christ loved the church, giving Himself for her to sanctify (set it apart, by the NKJV note) and cleanse her by the word, washing her with water, and present to Himself a glorious church with no spot, wrinkle or such thing, holy and without blemish. Husbands were to love their wives as their own bodies; a husband who loves his wife loves himself, since no one hates his own flesh, but feeds and cherishes it, as the Lord does the church; and we, the members of His body, are of His bones and His flesh (NU omits the rest of v. 30, by the NKJV note). Therefore a man will leave his father and mother and will be joined to his wife, and both will become one flesh: Paul called this a great mystery, but spoke of Christ and the church. Each husband was to love his wife as himself, and each wife to respect her husband"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:9; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:11; NKJV notes, note on Ephesians 6:12; Ephesians 6:1–24","v":"Children were to obey their parents in the Lord, as is right, and honor father and mother, which Paul called the first commandment with promise, so that it would go well with them and they would have long life on the earth; fathers were not to provoke their children to wrath, but to raise them in the Lord's training and admonition. Bondservants were to obey their masters in the flesh in fear and trembling, with a sincere heart, as to Christ, not serving with eyeservice, to please men, but doing God's will from the heart as Christ's bondservants, serving with goodwill as to the Lord rather than to men, since the Lord will repay whatever good anyone does, slave or free; masters were, in turn, to do the same to their bondservants and give up threats, knowing that their own Master also is (NU He who is both their Master and yours is, by the NKJV note) in heaven, a Master who shows no partiality. Finally, the brethren were to be strong in the Lord, in His mighty power, and put on God's whole armor to stand against the devil's wiles (schemings, by the NKJV note), for our wrestling is not with flesh and blood, but with the rulers of this age's darkness (NU this darkness, by the NKJV note), with principalities and powers, and with the spiritual hosts of wickedness that are in heavenly places; so they were to take up God's whole armor, to hold their ground in the evil day and, when all is done, to stand, armed with salvation for a helmet, righteousness for a breastplate, truth girded about the waist, their feet shod with the preparation that is of the gospel of peace, the Spirit's sword, the word of God, and above all faith as a shield to quench every fiery dart of the wicked one. They were to pray always in the Spirit with all prayer and supplication, watchful for it and persevering in it, for all the saints and on Paul's behalf, that words might be given him to open his mouth boldly and make the gospel's mystery known, for which he was an ambassador in chains. Paul sent them Tychicus, a beloved brother and a faithful minister in the Lord, to make known to them how Paul was doing and all his affairs, and to comfort their hearts. Paul wished the brethren peace, and love joined to faith, from God the Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ, and grace on all who sincerely love our Lord Jesus Christ"},{"p":"designation","r":"Ephesians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"Ephesians 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Ephesians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03275"}],"nm":{"EVT-03275":"Paul writes to the saints at Ephesus","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00666":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Philippians","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to the saints in Christ Jesus at Philippi, together with the bishops and deacons, of Paul's chains and the furtherance of the gospel, of the mind of Christ, who humbled Himself and was exalted, of knowing Christ and pressing toward the goal, of rejoicing in the Lord, and of the gift the Philippians sent Paul (Philippians 1:1–4:23)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:13; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:16; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:22; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 1:28; Philippians 1:1–30","v":"Paul and Timothy, Jesus Christ's bondservants, greeted all the saints in Christ Jesus at Philippi, together with the bishops (Lit. overseers, by the NKJV note) and deacons, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul thanked his God whenever he remembered them, praying always for them all with joy for their fellowship in the gospel ever since the first day, confident that He who began a good work in them will bring it to completion until the day of Jesus Christ; it was right for him to think so, for he held them in his heart, since in his chains and in defending and confirming the gospel they all shared grace with him, and God was his witness how greatly he longed for them all with Jesus Christ's affection. Paul prayed that their love might abound still more in knowledge and all discernment, so that they might approve what is excellent and be sincere and without offense until the day of Christ, filled with the fruits of righteousness that come by Jesus Christ, to God's glory and praise. What had happened to Paul had turned out to further the gospel: it became plain to the whole palace guard (Or Praetorium, by the NKJV note) and everyone else that his chains are in Christ, while most brethren in the Lord, made confident by his chains, dared far more to speak the word fearlessly. Some preached Christ from envy and strife, some from goodwill; the former (NU reverses vv. 16 and 17, by the NKJV note) from selfish ambition, not sincerely, thinking to add affliction to his chains, the latter from love, knowing he is appointed to defend the gospel. What then? Only that Christ is preached in every way, in pretense or in truth, and in this Paul rejoiced and would rejoice. He knew this would turn out for his deliverance through the prayer of the saints at Philippi and the supply that is of the Spirit of Jesus Christ, as he eagerly expected and hoped that he would be ashamed in nothing, but that with all boldness Christ would now, as always, be magnified in his body, by life or by death. For him, to live is Christ, and dying is gain; if he lived on in the flesh, that would mean fruit from his labor, and which to choose he could not tell (do not know, by the NKJV note); he was hard-pressed between the two, desiring to depart and to be with Christ, which would be far better, yet remaining in the flesh was more needful for them. Confident of this, he knew he would remain and continue with them all for their progress and joy of faith, so that their rejoicing for him might abound in Jesus Christ by his coming to them again. Above all, their conduct was to be worthy of Christ's gospel, so that, whether he came and saw them or was absent, he might hear that they stand fast in one spirit, striving together with one mind for the faith of the gospel, in no way terrified by their adversaries, which is to the adversaries a proof of perdition, but to the saints at Philippi of salvation (NU of your salvation, by the NKJV note), and that from God, since to them it has been given, for Christ's sake, both to believe in Him and to suffer for Him, having the same conflict they had seen in Paul and now heard was in him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:6; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:15; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:19; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 2:30; Philippians 2:1–30","v":"If there was any affection and mercy, any fellowship of the Spirit, any comfort of love, any consolation (Or encouragement, by the NKJV note) in Christ, the saints at Philippi were to fulfill Paul's joy by being of one mind and one accord, like-minded and sharing the same love, doing nothing from selfish ambition or conceit, but each, in lowliness of mind, esteeming others better than himself and looking out for others' interests as well as his own. They were to have the mind that was in Christ Jesus. Though He was in the form of God, He did not count it robbery to be equal (Or something to be held onto to be equal, by the NKJV note) to God, but rather made Himself of no reputation (emptied Himself of His privileges, by the NKJV note), taking a bondservant's form and coming in men's likeness; and, found in appearance to be a man, He humbled Himself, obedient even to death, death on the cross. Therefore God has exalted Him highly and given Him the name above every name, so that at Jesus' name every knee should bow, in heaven, on earth and beneath it, and every tongue should confess Jesus Christ as Lord, to God the Father's glory. As they had always obeyed, not only in Paul's presence but much more now in his absence, they were to work out their own salvation with fear and trembling, since God is the one at work in them, both to will and to act, for His good pleasure. They were to do everything without complaining (grumbling, by the NKJV note) and disputing (arguing, by the NKJV note), so as to become blameless and harmless (innocent, by the NKJV note), God's children without fault amid a crooked and perverse generation, in which they shine as lights in the world as they hold fast to the word of life, so that Paul might rejoice on the day of Christ that he had not run or labored in vain. If he was being poured out like a drink offering upon the sacrifice and service of their faith, he was glad and rejoiced with them all, and so they too were to be glad and rejoice with him. Paul trusted in the Lord Jesus that he would soon send Timothy to them, to be encouraged himself on learning their state (condition, by the NKJV note), for he had no one else like-minded who would sincerely care for their state, since all seek their own, not what is Christ Jesus'; they knew Timothy's proven character, how he served with Paul in the gospel like a son with his father. Paul hoped to send him without delay, once he saw how his own affairs would go, and trusted in the Lord that he himself would also come shortly. He thought it needful to send back to them Epaphroditus, his fellow soldier, fellow worker and brother, their messenger, who ministered to his need, since Epaphroditus longed for them all and was distressed that they had heard he was sick, as he was, almost to death; yet God had mercy on him, and on Paul too, lest Paul have sorrow upon sorrow. So Paul sent him the more eagerly, that they might rejoice to see him again and Paul be less sorrowful. They were to welcome him gladly in the Lord, and to hold such men in esteem, since he came close to death for the work of Christ, not regarding (risking, by the NKJV note) his life, to make up what was lacking in their service to Paul"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 3:3; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 3:11; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 3:12; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 3:13; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 3:16; Philippians 3:1–21","v":"Finally, the saints at Philippi, Paul's brethren, were to rejoice in the Lord; writing the same things to them again was not tedious for Paul, and for them it was safe. They were to beware of the mutilation, of evil workers and of dogs, for it is we who are the circumcision, who put no confidence in the flesh, rejoice in Christ Jesus and worship God in the Spirit (NU, M in the Spirit of God, by the NKJV note). Yet Paul might have confidence in the flesh more than anyone: as to the law's righteousness, he was blameless; as to zeal, a persecutor of the church; as to the law, a Pharisee; and he was a Hebrew of the Hebrews, of Benjamin's tribe and Israel's stock, circumcised on the eighth day. But what had been gain to him he counted loss for Christ, counting all things as loss because of the excellence of knowing Christ Jesus his Lord, for whom he had suffered the loss of all things, counting them rubbish, so that Christ might be his gain and he be found in Him, not having his own righteousness, which comes from the law, but the one that is from God by faith, through faith in Christ; that he might know Him, His resurrection's power and fellowship in His sufferings, and be made like Him in His death, if by any means he might attain (Lit. arrive at, by the NKJV note) the resurrection from the dead. He had not already attained (obtained it, by the NKJV note) or been perfected; he pressed on to grasp that for which Christ Jesus had also laid hold of him. He did not count himself to have apprehended (laid hold of it, by the NKJV note), but one thing he did: forgetting what lay behind and reaching forward to what lay ahead, he pressed on toward the goal, to win the prize of God's upward call in Christ Jesus. We, as many as were mature, were to have this mind, and if the saints at Philippi thought otherwise in anything, God would reveal even this to them; only, to the degree we had already attained (arrived, by the NKJV note), we were to walk by the same rule (NU omits rule and the rest of v. 16, by the NKJV note) and be like-minded. They were to join in following Paul's example and note those who walk so, as they had a pattern in Paul and those with him; for many walk as enemies of the cross of Christ, of whom Paul had told them often and now told them even weeping: they set their mind on earthly things and glory in their shame; their belly is their god, and their end is destruction. Our citizenship is in heaven, and from there we eagerly await the Savior, the Lord Jesus Christ; He will transform our lowly body to be conformed to His glorious body, by the working by which He can even subdue all things to Himself"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:3; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:5; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:10; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:13; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:18; NKJV notes, note on Philippians 4:23; Philippians 4:1–23","v":"Therefore the saints at Philippi, Paul's beloved and longed-for brethren, his joy and crown, were to stand fast in the Lord. Paul implored Euodia and Syntyche to be of one mind in the Lord, and (NU, M Yes, by the NKJV note) urged his true companion to help these women, who had labored with Paul in the gospel, together with Clement and the rest of Paul's fellow workers, whose names are written in the Book of Life. The saints at Philippi were to rejoice in the Lord always, and again Paul said, rejoice. They were to let all men know their gentleness (graciousness or forbearance, by the NKJV note); the Lord is at hand. They were to be anxious for nothing, and in everything make their requests known to God by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving, and God's peace, which surpasses all understanding, would guard their hearts and minds through Christ Jesus. They were, finally, to meditate on whatever was of good report, lovely, pure, just, noble or true, if there was any virtue and anything praiseworthy, and to do what they had heard and seen in Paul, and learned and received, and the God of peace would be with them. Paul rejoiced greatly in the Lord that now at last their care for him had flourished again (you have revived your care, by the NKJV note); though they had cared, they had lacked opportunity. Not that he spoke from need, for he had learned to be content in whatever state he was: he knew how to abound (live in prosperity, by the NKJV note) and how to be abased (live humbly, by the NKJV note), and everywhere, in all things, he had learned to be full or hungry, to have plenty or to suffer need; he could do all things through Christ who (NU Him who, by the NKJV note) gives him strength. Still, they had done well to share in his distress. They knew that at the beginning of the gospel, as Paul departed from Macedonia, they alone of the churches shared with him in giving and receiving, for even in Thessalonica they had sent aid for his needs more than once. Not that he sought the gift; he sought the fruit abounding to their account. He had all (Or have received all, by the NKJV note) and abounded, full now that he had received from Epaphroditus what they sent, an acceptable sacrifice and a sweet-smelling aroma, well pleasing to God; his God would supply all their need in keeping with His riches in glory, through Christ Jesus. Paul gave praise, that glory be to our God and Father forever and ever. They were to greet every saint in Christ Jesus. Greetings came from the brethren with Paul and from all the saints, especially those of Caesar's household. Paul closed by wishing our Lord Jesus Christ's grace to be with them all (NU your spirit, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"designation","r":"Philippians 1:1","v":"Paul and Timothy, bondservants of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"Philippians 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Philippians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03277"}],"nm":{"EVT-03277":"Paul writes to the saints at Philippi","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00667":{"k":"object","n":"The Book of Life","d":"The book in which are the names of Paul's fellow workers (Philippians 4:3)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"Philippians 4:3","v":"As Paul wrote: the names of Paul's fellow workers"},{"p":"designation","r":"Philippians 4:3","v":"the Book of Life"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00668":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Colossians","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to the saints at Colosse, faithful brethren in Christ, of the preeminence of Christ, in whom all the fullness dwells, of the mystery made known among the Gentiles, Christ in the Colossians, the hope of glory, of being complete in Him and not cheated through philosophy or subject to men's regulations, of seeking the things above and putting on the new man, of wives and husbands, children and fathers, bondservants and masters, and of the greetings of Paul and his fellow workers (Colossians 1:1–4:18)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:13; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:14; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:16; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:25; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:26; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 1:27; Colossians 1:1–29","v":"Paul, by God's will an apostle of Jesus Christ, and Timothy our brother greeted the saints and faithful brethren in Christ who are in Colosse, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ (NU omits and the Lord Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note). Paul and Timothy gave thanks to God, our Lord Jesus Christ's Father, always praying for the Colossians, having heard of their faith in Christ Jesus and of their love for all the saints, on account of the hope laid up for them in heaven. Of this hope the Colossians had heard before in the gospel's word of truth; the gospel had come to them, as it had also in all the world, and was bearing fruit (NU, M add and growing, by the NKJV note), as it had been doing among them too since the day they heard and knew God's grace in truth, as they learned it from Epaphras, the dear fellow servant of Paul and Timothy, a faithful minister of Christ on the Colossians' behalf, who also declared to Paul and Timothy the Colossians' love in the Spirit. Therefore Paul and Timothy, since the day they heard it, had not ceased to pray for the Colossians, asking that they might be filled, in all wisdom and spiritual understanding, with the knowledge of God's will, so that they might walk worthy of the Lord, pleasing Him fully, bearing fruit in every good work and growing in the knowledge of God, being strengthened by His glorious power with all might, for all patience and joyful longsuffering, thanking the Father, who has qualified us for a share in the saints' inheritance in the light. The Father has delivered us out of the power of darkness and has conveyed (transferred, by the NKJV note) us into the kingdom of His Son, the Son of His love. In the Son we are redeemed through His blood (NU, M omit through His blood, by the NKJV note), our sins forgiven. The Son, the image of the invisible God, is the firstborn over all creation, since by Him were created all things in heaven and on earth, the visible and the invisible, whether thrones or dominions, powers (authorities, by the NKJV note) or principalities (rulers, by the NKJV note); through Him and for Him all things were created. The Son is before all things, and all things consist in Him. He is also the head of the body, the church; He is the beginning, and He is the firstborn from the dead, so that He may have the preeminence in all things. For it pleased the Father that all the fullness should dwell in Him, and to reconcile all things to Himself through Him, whether on earth or in heaven, having made peace by the blood of His cross. As for the Colossians, once alienated and enemies in their mind by wicked works, He has now reconciled them through death, in the body of His flesh, to present them in His sight holy, blameless and above reproach, if indeed they continue grounded and steadfast in the faith, not shifted from the hope of the gospel they heard, which was proclaimed to every creature under heaven, and of that gospel Paul became a minister. Paul rejoiced in his sufferings for the Colossians, and filled up in his flesh what was lacking in Christ's afflictions, for the sake of His body, the church, of which Paul became a minister in accordance with the stewardship (dispensation or administration, by the NKJV note) from God given him for the Colossians, to fulfill God's word, the mystery (secret or hidden truth, by the NKJV note) hidden from ages and generations but now revealed to His saints, to whom God willed to make known the riches of this mystery's glory among the Gentiles, the mystery which (M who, by the NKJV note) is Christ in the Colossians, the hope of glory. Christ is the one whom Paul and those with him preached, teaching and warning every man in all wisdom, so that they might present every man as perfect in Christ Jesus; toward this end Paul also labored, striving by His working, which works in him mightily"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:5; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:8; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:10; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:14; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:17; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:18; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:20; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 2:23; Colossians 2:1–23","v":"Paul wanted the saints at Colosse to know how great a conflict (struggle, by the NKJV note) he had for them, for those in Laodicea, and for all who had not seen his face in the flesh, so that their hearts might be encouraged, knit together in love, and attain all the riches of understanding's full assurance, and the knowledge of God's mystery, of both the Father and Christ (NU omits both of the Father and, by the NKJV note), in whom all the treasures of knowledge and wisdom are hidden. Paul said this lest anyone deceive the saints at Colosse with persuasive words; for though absent in the flesh, he was with them in spirit, rejoicing to see (Lit. and seeing, by the NKJV note) their good order and their faith's steadfastness in Christ. As they had received Christ Jesus the Lord, they were so to walk in Him, rooted in Him and built up in Him, firm in the faith as they had been taught, and to abound in it (NU omits in it, by the NKJV note) with thanksgiving. They were to beware lest anyone cheat them (Lit. plunder you or take you captive, by the NKJV note) through philosophy and empty deceit, after men's tradition and the world's basic principles, not after Christ; for all the Godhead's fullness dwells bodily (in bodily form, by the NKJV note) in Him, and they are complete in Him, the head of all principality and power (rule and authority, by the NKJV note). They were also circumcised in Him with a circumcision not made with hands, as the body of the flesh's sins (NU omits of the sins, by the NKJV note) was put off by Christ's circumcision; they were buried with Him in baptism and in it were also raised with Him through their faith in God's working, God who raised Christ from the dead. When they were dead in their trespasses and their flesh's uncircumcision, God made them alive together with Christ, having forgiven them all trespasses; He wiped away the handwriting of (certificate of debt with its, by the NKJV note) requirements, which stood against us, contrary to us, and took it out of the way and nailed it to the cross; He disarmed principalities and powers and made them a public spectacle, triumphing over them in it. Accordingly, the saints at Colosse were to let no one judge them over food or drink, or over sabbaths, a new moon or a festival (feast day, by the NKJV note): these are a shadow of the things to come, while the substance (Lit. body, by the NKJV note) belongs to Christ. They were to let no one cheat them of their reward, no one who delights in false humility and the worship of angels, intruding into things he has not (NU omits not, by the NKJV note) seen, puffed up in vain by a fleshly mind, not holding fast to Christ, the Head, from whom the whole body grows with the increase from God, nourished and knit together by its joints and ligaments. Therefore (NU, M omit Therefore, by the NKJV note), if they died with Christ from the world's basic principles, Paul asked why, as if they still lived in the world, they subjected themselves to regulations forbidding them to touch, taste or handle things that all perish with use, after men's commandments and doctrines. Such rules indeed look like wisdom, with false humility, self-imposed religion and the body's neglect (severe treatment, asceticism, by the NKJV note), but they are worth nothing against indulging the flesh"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Colossians 3:24; Colossians 3:1–25","v":"If then the saints at Colosse were raised with Christ, they were to seek the things above, where Christ is, seated at God's right hand, and to set their mind on what is above, not on earthly things; for they had died, and their life is hidden in God with Christ, and when Christ, our life, appears, they too will appear in glory with Him. So they were to put to death their members on the earth: fornication and uncleanness, evil desire and passion, and covetousness, which is idolatry. God's wrath is coming on the sons of disobedience because of these things, and the saints at Colosse once walked in these things too, when they lived in them. But now they themselves were to put off all these: malice and blasphemy, wrath and anger, and filthy language out of their mouth. They were not to lie to one another, since they had put off the old man and his deeds and put on the new man, renewed in knowledge after the image of the One who created him; in the new man there is neither Jew nor Greek, uncircumcised nor circumcised, Scythian, barbarian, free nor slave; rather, Christ is all and is in all. So, as God's elect, holy and beloved, they were to put on kindness and humility, longsuffering, meekness and tender mercies, bearing with one another and, if one had a complaint against another, forgiving one another; as Christ forgave them, so they too were to forgive. Above all, they were to put on love, the bond of perfection. They were to let God's peace, to which they were also called in one body, rule in their hearts, and to be thankful. They were to let Christ's word dwell in them richly, in all wisdom, as they taught and admonished one another with hymns, psalms and spiritual songs, singing to the Lord with grace in their hearts. Whatever they did in word or deed, they were to do all in the Lord Jesus' name, giving thanks through Him to God the Father. Wives were to submit to their own husbands, as is proper in the Lord. Husbands were to love their wives and not be bitter toward them. Children were to obey their parents in all things, since the Lord is well pleased with this; fathers were not to provoke their children, lest the children become discouraged. Bondservants were to obey their masters according to the flesh in all things, not with eyeservice, to please men, but with a sincere heart, fearing God; whatever they did, they were to do it heartily, as for the Lord rather than for men, knowing that they will receive from the Lord the inheritance as their reward, for (NU omits for, by the NKJV note) they serve the Lord Christ; but the wrongdoer will be repaid for his wrong, and there is no partiality"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Colossians 4:3; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 4:8; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 4:12; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 4:13; NKJV notes, note on Colossians 4:15; Colossians 4:1–18","v":"Masters were to give their bondservants what was just and fair, knowing that they too have a Master in heaven. The saints at Colosse were to continue earnestly in prayer, vigilant in it with thanksgiving, praying also for Paul and those with him, that God would open to Paul and those with him a door for the word, so as to speak Christ's mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note), for which Paul was also in chains, and that Paul might make it manifest, as he ought to speak. They were to walk in wisdom toward outsiders, redeeming the time; their speech was always to be gracious, seasoned with salt, that they might know how they ought to answer each one. Paul was sending them Tychicus, whom he called a beloved brother, faithful minister, and fellow servant in the Lord, who would tell them all the news about Paul; Paul was sending him for this very purpose, so that Tychicus might know their circumstances and comfort (NU you may know our circumstances and he may comfort, by the NKJV note) their hearts, and with Tychicus, Paul was sending Onesimus, a faithful and beloved brother and one of the saints at Colosse; the two would make known to them all that was happening where Paul was. Aristarchus, Paul's fellow prisoner, sent greetings, and so did Mark the cousin of Barnabas (the saints at Colosse had received instructions about Mark: if he came to them, they were to welcome him) and Jesus called Justus. These were the only ones of the circumcision who worked with Paul for God's kingdom, and they had proved a comfort to him. Epaphras, one of the saints at Colosse, a bondservant of Christ, sent greetings, always laboring fervently for them in prayers, that they might stand complete (NU fully assured, by the NKJV note) and perfect in all God's will; Paul bore him witness that he had great zeal (NU concern, by the NKJV note) for them, and for those in Hierapolis and in Laodicea. Greetings came too from Demas and from Luke the beloved physician. The saints at Colosse were to greet the brethren in Laodicea, and Nymphas (NU Nympha, by the NKJV note) with the church in his (NU her, by the NKJV note) house. Once this epistle had been read among them, they were to see that the church of the Laodiceans read it too, and the saints at Colosse in turn were to read the epistle from Laodicea. They were to say to Archippus that he was to take heed to the ministry he had received in the Lord, so that he might fulfill it. Paul wrote the salutation with his own hand, asked them to remember his chains, and closed by wishing grace to be with them"},{"p":"designation","r":"Colossians 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"Colossians 4:18","v":"Paul writes its last greeting with his own hand"},{"p":"made by","r":"Colossians 1:1, 23; 4:18","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Colossians 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03281"}],"nm":{"EVT-03281":"Paul writes to the saints at Colosse","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00669":{"k":"object","n":"The epistle from Laodicea","d":"The epistle from Laodicea, which the saints at Colosse were also to read (Colossians 4:16)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"Colossians 4:16","v":"the epistle from Laodicea"}],"nm":{}},"OBJ-00670":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Thessalonians","d":"The letter Paul, Silvanus and Timothy wrote to the church of the Thessalonians, of their faith and their example, of Paul's conduct among them, of Timothy sent to them from Athens and his good report, of a life of holiness and brotherly love, of the dead in Christ rising at the Lord's coming, of the day of the Lord coming as a thief, and of many short exhortations (1 Thessalonians 1:1–5:28)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 1:1; 1 Thessalonians 1:1–10","v":"Paul, Silvanus and Timothy greeted the church of the Thessalonians, who are in the Lord Jesus Christ and in God the Father, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ (NU omits from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note). Paul, Silvanus and Timothy gave thanks to God always for them all, mentioning them in prayer and remembering without ceasing, before our God and Father, the patience of their hope in our Lord Jesus Christ, their labor of love and their work of faith, and knowing that God had chosen them, whom Paul called beloved brethren. For the gospel of Paul and those with him had not come to them in word only but in power too, with much assurance, and in the Holy Spirit, as the believers at Thessalonica knew what kind of men Paul and those with him had been among them for their sake. The believers at Thessalonica became followers of Paul and those with him and of the Lord, receiving the word with joy of the Holy Spirit, though in much affliction, so that they became examples to all the believers in Macedonia and Achaia. From the believers at Thessalonica the word of the Lord had sounded out in every place, not only in Macedonia and Achaia; their faith toward God had gone out, so that Paul and those with him had no need to say anything. For those in every place themselves told of what manner of entry Paul and those with him had among the believers at Thessalonica, and how the believers turned from idols to God, to serve Him, the living and true God, and await from heaven His Son, whom God raised from the dead, Jesus, who delivers us from the coming wrath"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 2:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 2:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 2:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 2:15; 1 Thessalonians 2:1–20","v":"The believers at Thessalonica knew that the coming of Paul and those with him to them had not been in vain. Even (NU, M omit even, by the NKJV note) after Paul and those with him had suffered and been spitefully treated at Philippi, they were bold in their God to speak the gospel of God to the believers amid much conflict. Their exhortation came not from error or uncleanness, and was not made in deceit; as God had approved them and entrusted them with the gospel, they spoke to please not men but God, who tests their hearts. They never used flattering words or a cloak for covetousness (pretext for greed, by the NKJV note), with God as witness, nor sought glory from men, though as apostles of Christ they might have made demands. They were gentle among the believers at Thessalonica, like a nursing mother cherishing her own children, and in their longing for them were well pleased to share with them their own lives as well as the gospel of God, since the believers had become dear to them. Laboring night and day so as to be a burden to none of the believers, Paul and those with him preached the gospel of God to them, and behaved blamelessly, justly and devoutly among those who believed, as the believers and God were witnesses; as a father his own children, they comforted, exhorted and charged (NU, M implored, by the NKJV note) each one to walk worthy of God, who calls the believers into His own glory and kingdom. For this reason Paul and those with him thanked God without ceasing, that the believers at Thessalonica had welcomed the word they heard from them not as men's word but, as it truly is, God's word, which works effectively in them, who believe. The believers had become imitators of the churches of God in Judea in Christ Jesus, suffering from their own countrymen the same things those churches suffered from the Judeans, who killed their own prophets and the Lord Jesus and persecuted Paul and those with him; the Judeans did not please God and were contrary (hostile, by the NKJV note) to all men, forbidding Paul and those with him to speak to the Gentiles so that the Gentiles might be saved, so as to be always filling up the full measure of the Judeans' sins; but wrath had come to the uttermost upon the Judeans. Taken away from the believers at Thessalonica for a short time, in presence but not in heart, Paul and those with him endeavored the more eagerly to see the believers' face; they wanted to come to them, Paul himself time and again, but were hindered by Satan. What was the joy, hope or crown of rejoicing of Paul and those with him? Was it not the believers at Thessalonica themselves, before our Lord Jesus Christ when He comes? For the believers were the glory and joy of Paul and those with him"},{"p":"contents","r":"1 Thessalonians 3:1–13","v":"So, when Paul and those with him could bear it no longer, they chose to be left at Athens alone, and sent Timothy, their brother, God's minister and their fellow laborer in Christ's gospel, to establish the believers at Thessalonica and encourage them in their faith, so that none of them would be shaken by the afflictions of that time, since, as the believers knew, we are appointed to such afflictions; Paul and those with him, when with the believers, had told them beforehand that we would suffer tribulation, as had happened. When Paul could bear it no longer, he sent to learn of the believers' faith, lest the tempter had somehow tempted them and the labor of Paul and those with him be in vain. But now Timothy had come to Paul and those with him from the believers at Thessalonica, bringing good news of the believers' faith and love, and that the believers always remembered Paul and those with him kindly, longing to see them, as Paul and those with him longed to see the believers; so Paul and those with him, in all their own distress and affliction, were comforted by the believers' faith, since Paul and those with him now lived, if the believers stood fast in the Lord. What thanks could Paul and those with him render to God for the believers, for all their rejoicing before their God for the believers' sake, praying exceedingly night and day to see the believers' face and perfect what was lacking in the believers' faith? Paul prayed that God Himself, our God and Father, with our Lord Jesus Christ, would direct the way of Paul and those with him to the believers at Thessalonica, and that the Lord would make the believers' love for one another and for all increase and abound, as did the love of Paul and those with him for them, so that He might establish the believers' hearts, blameless and holy before our God and Father, when our Lord Jesus Christ comes with all His saints"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 4:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 4:13; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 4:14; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 4:15; 1 Thessalonians 4:1–18","v":"Finally, Paul and those with him urged the believers at Thessalonica, in the Lord Jesus, to abound more and more, walking and pleasing God as they had learned from Paul and those with him, for the believers knew the commandments Paul and those with him had given them through the Lord Jesus. God's will is their sanctification: they were to abstain from sexual immorality, each knowing how to keep his own vessel in honor and sanctification, not in lustful passion, as the Gentiles do, who do not know God; and no one was to wrong or defraud his brother by taking advantage of him in this matter, since the Lord avenges all such things, as Paul and those with him had warned them before and testified. For God called us not to uncleanness but in holiness, so he who rejects this rejects not man but God, who also has given (NU who also gives, by the NKJV note) His Holy Spirit to us. The believers at Thessalonica had no need for Paul to write to them of brotherly love, since God had taught them to love one another, and they did so toward all the brethren throughout Macedonia; yet Paul and those with him urged them to increase more and more, to work with their own hands, mind their own business and aim to lead a quiet life, as Paul and those with him had commanded them, so that they might walk properly toward outsiders and lack nothing. Paul would not have them ignorant about those who had fallen asleep (died, by the NKJV note), so that they might not sorrow like others who have no hope, since, if we believe Jesus died and rose again, God will bring those who sleep in Jesus (Or through Jesus sleep, by the NKJV note) with Him. By the word of the Lord, Paul and those with him told the believers at Thessalonica this: we who are alive and remain at the Lord's coming will not go ahead of those who are asleep (dead, by the NKJV note). The Lord Himself will come down from heaven with a shout, with an archangel's voice and God's trumpet, and first the dead in Christ will rise; afterward, we who are alive and remain will be caught up in the clouds with them to meet with the Lord in the air, and so we will be with the Lord always. So the believers at Thessalonica were to comfort one another with these words"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:6; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:14; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:23; NKJV notes, note on 1 Thessalonians 5:27; 1 Thessalonians 5:1–28","v":"The believers at Thessalonica had no need for Paul to write to them about times and seasons, for they knew well how the day of the Lord comes like a thief at night: when they are saying 'Peace and safety!', sudden ruin falls on them, like the pangs of a woman in labor, and they will not get away. But the believers at Thessalonica were not in darkness, and so that Day would not take them by surprise like a thief; each of them was a son of light and of the day, and we belong neither to night nor to darkness. So we are not to sleep like the rest, but to keep watch and stay sober (self-controlled, by the NKJV note): sleepers sleep by night and drunkards are drunk by night, but we who belong to the day are to stay sober, putting on faith and love as a breastplate and taking hope of salvation as our helmet. God destined us for salvation, not for wrath, a salvation our Lord Jesus Christ brings; He died on our behalf so that, awake or asleep, we should live with Him. So the believers at Thessalonica were to comfort (Or encourage, by the NKJV note) and to edify (build one another up, by the NKJV note) one another, as they were already doing. Paul and those with him urged them to acknowledge those who labored among them, who were over them in the Lord, who admonished (instruct or warn, by the NKJV note) them, and to hold them in very high esteem in love because of their work; and to keep peace with one another. Paul and those with him exhorted (encourage, by the NKJV note) the believers to warn the unruly (insubordinate or idle, by the NKJV note), cheer the fainthearted, help the weak and be patient toward all; to see that none of them paid anyone back evil for evil, but always to seek the good, for one another and for all; to rejoice at all times, pray constantly and give thanks in every circumstance, this being God's will for them in Christ Jesus; not to quench the Spirit or despise prophecies, but to test everything, holding on to what is good and keeping clear of evil in every form. Paul prayed that the God of peace would Himself sanctify (set you apart, by the NKJV note) them through and through, and that they be kept blameless in spirit, in soul and in body at our Lord Jesus Christ's coming; the One who calls them is faithful and will do it. Paul asked the believers at Thessalonica to pray for him and those with him, and to give all of the brethren a holy kiss in greeting; charging them by the Lord, he bade them have this epistle (letter, by the NKJV note) read to every one of the holy (NU omits holy, by the NKJV note) brethren, and he closed by wishing our Lord Jesus Christ's grace to be with them. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Thessalonians 1:1","v":"Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:15; 1 Thessalonians 1:1; 2 Thessalonians 2:15","v":"our epistle","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul, Silvanus and Timothy wrote the first letter to the church of the Thessalonians (1 Thessalonians 1:1); in the second, Paul bids them hold the traditions they were taught by word or by the epistle (letter, by the NKJV note) of Paul and those with him (2 Thessalonians 2:15), and that epistle is taken to be the first letter."},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Thessalonians 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 Thessalonians 1:1","e":"EVT-03283"}],"nm":{"EVT-03283":"Paul writes to the church of the Thessalonians","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00671":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of Paul the Apostle to the Thessalonians","d":"The second letter Paul, Silvanus and Timothy wrote to the church of the Thessalonians, of their growing faith and their patience in persecution, of God's righteous judgment at the revealing of the Lord Jesus from heaven, of the falling away and the man of sin (NU lawlessness, by the NKJV note) to be revealed before the day of Christ (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note), of standing fast in the traditions they were taught, of keeping away from any brother who walks disorderly and will not work, and of Paul's greeting in his own hand (2 Thessalonians 1:1–3:18)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 1:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 1:6; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 1:10; 2 Thessalonians 1:1–12","v":"Paul, Silvanus and Timothy greeted the church of the Thessalonians, who are in the Lord Jesus Christ and in God our Father, wishing them grace and peace both from God our Father and from the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul and those with him were bound, as was fitting, always to thank God for the believers at Thessalonica, whom Paul called brethren, since their faith was growing exceedingly and the love of each of them for one another abounded; so Paul and those with him boasted of them among the churches of God because of their patience and faith in all the persecutions and tribulations (afflictions, by the NKJV note) that they endured. Their patience and faith were manifest (plain, by the NKJV note) evidence of God's righteous judgment, that they might be counted worthy of God's kingdom, for which they were suffering too; for it is a righteous thing for God to repay with tribulation (affliction, by the NKJV note) the ones troubling them, and to give them, who are troubled, rest along with Paul and those with him, when the Lord Jesus, with His mighty angels, is revealed from heaven, taking vengeance in flaming fire on those not knowing God, as well as on those who do not obey our Lord Jesus Christ's gospel. These will be punished by everlasting destruction from the Lord's presence and the glory of His power, in that Day, when He comes to be glorified in His saints, admired also among all who believe (NU, M have believed, by the NKJV note), since the testimony that Paul and those with him gave among the believers at Thessalonica had been believed. So Paul and those with him also prayed always for the believers at Thessalonica that our God would count them worthy of this calling and, with power, fulfill every good pleasure of His goodness, as well as the work of faith, so that our Lord Jesus Christ's name might be glorified in them, and they in Him, in keeping with the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ and of our God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:2; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:13; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:15; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 2:17; 2 Thessalonians 2:1–17","v":"As to our Lord Jesus Christ's coming and our being gathered to Him, Paul and those with him asked the believers at Thessalonica not to be quickly shaken in mind or alarmed, whether by a spirit, by a message or by a letter, said to be from Paul and those with him, as if the day of Christ (NU the Lord, by the NKJV note) had already come. No one was to deceive them in any way, for that Day would not come before the falling away had come and the man of sin (NU lawlessness, by the NKJV note), the son of perdition, had been revealed; he would oppose and exalt himself above everything called God or worshiped, so that he would take his seat as God (NU omits as God, by the NKJV note) in God's temple, displaying himself as God. Paul asked whether the believers did not remember that he had told them these things when he was still with them. Now they knew what was restraining, so that the man of sin might be revealed at his own time, since the mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note) of lawlessness was already at work; but He (Or he, by the NKJV note) who now restrained would keep doing so only until He was taken away. Then the lawless one would be revealed, and the Lord would consume him by the breath of His mouth and would destroy him by the brightness of His coming. And the lawless one's coming would be by Satan's working, with all power, with signs and with false wonders, and with every unrighteous deception among those who are perishing, because they did not welcome the love of the truth, so as to be saved; God, for this reason, would send them a strong delusion, so that they would believe the lie, and so that all who had not believed the truth but had delighted in unrighteousness might be condemned. But Paul and those with him were bound (under obligation, by the NKJV note) always to thank God for the believers at Thessalonica, brethren beloved by the Lord, since from the beginning God had chosen them to be saved, through belief in the truth and through sanctification by (being set apart by, by the NKJV note) the Spirit; through the gospel of Paul and those with him, God had called them to this, so that they might obtain our Lord Jesus Christ's glory. So the believers were to stand fast and hold to the traditions they had been taught, whether by word or by the epistle (letter, by the NKJV note) of Paul and those with him. Paul prayed that our Lord Jesus Christ Himself, with our God and Father, who loved us and by grace gave us good hope and everlasting consolation, would comfort the believers' hearts and establish (strengthen, by the NKJV note) them in every good word and work"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:6; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:8; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:12; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:14; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:15; NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:17; 2 Thessalonians 3:1–18","v":"Finally, Paul asked the believers at Thessalonica to pray for him and those with him, that the Lord's word might spread swiftly and be glorified, as it was among the believers, and that Paul and those with him might be delivered from wicked and unreasonable men, since not everyone had faith. Yet the Lord is faithful, and He will establish the believers at Thessalonica and guard them from the evil one. Paul and those with him were confident in the Lord about the believers, that they were doing and would do what Paul and those with him commanded them. Paul prayed that the Lord would direct the hearts of the believers into the patience of Christ and the love of God. In our Lord Jesus Christ's name, Paul and those with him commanded the believers to withdraw from every brother who walked disorderly, contrary to the tradition he (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) had received from Paul and those with him. For the believers themselves knew how they ought to follow Paul and those with him, for Paul and those with him had not been disorderly among them, nor had eaten anyone's bread free of charge (Lit. for nothing, by the NKJV note); instead they had toiled and labored night and day so as to be a burden to none of the believers, not that they lacked the authority, but to make themselves an example for the believers to follow. Even when Paul and those with him were with the believers, they had commanded them that if anyone would not work, he was not to eat. For Paul and those with him heard that some among the believers walked in a disorderly way, not working at all, but being busybodies; such people Paul and those with him commanded and exhorted (encourage, by the NKJV note) through our Lord Jesus Christ to work quietly and eat their own bread. As for the believers, they were not to grow weary in doing good. If anyone did not obey the word of Paul and those with him in this epistle (letter, by the NKJV note), the believers were to note that person and keep away from him, so that he might be ashamed; yet they were to admonish (warn, by the NKJV note) him as a brother, not count him an enemy. Paul prayed that the Lord of peace would Himself give them peace always and in every way, and wished the Lord to be with them all. Paul wrote the salutation with his own hand, which he called a sign in every epistle (letter, by the NKJV note) of his; this was how he wrote. Paul closed by wishing our Lord Jesus Christ's grace to be with them all. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Thessalonians 1:1","v":"Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Thessalonians 3:17; 2 Thessalonians 3:17","v":"Paul writes its last greeting with his own hand, which he calls a sign in every epistle (letter, by the NKJV note) of his"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Thessalonians 1:1; 3:17","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 Thessalonians 1:1","e":"EVT-03286"}],"nm":{"EVT-03286":"Paul writes his second letter to the church of the Thessalonians","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00672":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of Paul the Apostle to Timothy","d":"The letter Paul wrote to Timothy, his true son in the faith, whom he had urged to remain in Ephesus, of charging some to teach no other doctrine, of prayer for all men, of how men are to pray and women to adorn themselves, of what bishops and deacons must be, of the mystery of godliness, of the departing from the faith in latter times, of widows, elders and bondservants, of godliness with contentment and the love of money, and of fighting the good fight of faith (1 Timothy 1:1–6:21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:10; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:13; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:17; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 1:18; 1 Timothy 1:1–20","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ at the command of God our Savior and of the Lord Jesus Christ, our hope, greeted Timothy, his true son in the faith, wishing him grace, mercy and peace both from God our Father and from Jesus Christ our Lord. As Paul had urged him when Paul went into Macedonia, Timothy was to remain in Ephesus to charge (command, by the NKJV note) some to teach no other doctrine and not to heed fables or endless genealogies, which lead to disputes rather than to godly edification in faith. The commandment aims at love that comes from a good conscience, a pure heart and sincere (Lit. unhypocritical, by the NKJV note) faith; some had strayed from these and turned aside to idle talk, wanting to be teachers of the law, though they understood neither what they said nor what they affirmed. The law is good if used lawfully, with the knowledge that it is not made for the righteous but for sinners of every kind, the ungodly and the lawless, liars, the sexually immoral and murderers, and for whatever else is contrary (opposed, by the NKJV note) to sound doctrine, in keeping with the blessed God's glorious gospel, which had been committed to Paul's trust. Paul thanked Christ Jesus our Lord, who had enabled him, since He counted him faithful, putting him into the ministry, though Paul had once blasphemed and persecuted and been insolent (violently arrogant, by the NKJV note); he had obtained mercy because he had acted ignorantly in unbelief, and our Lord's grace had been exceedingly abundant, with the faith and love in Christ Jesus. Worthy of all acceptance is the faithful saying that Christ Jesus entered the world to save sinners; among them Paul was chief. Yet he had obtained mercy so that Jesus Christ might show in him first all His longsuffering, as a pattern for those who would come to believe on Him and gain everlasting life. Paul gave praise, that honor and glory be to God who alone is wise (NU the only God, by the NKJV note), the King eternal, immortal, invisible, forever and ever. Paul committed this charge (command, by the NKJV note) to Timothy, his son, according to the prophecies made before concerning him, so that by them Timothy might wage the good warfare, holding to faith and a good conscience, that some had rejected and so suffered shipwreck concerning the faith; of these were Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom Paul had delivered to Satan so that they might learn not to blaspheme"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 2:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 2:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 2:9; 1 Timothy 2:1–15","v":"Paul exhorted (encourage, by the NKJV note) first of all that thanksgiving, intercession, prayer and supplication be made for all men, including all in authority (a prominent place, by the NKJV note) and kings, so that we might live quiet, peaceable lives in all godliness and reverence (dignity, by the NKJV note). For such prayer is good and acceptable before God our Savior, who desires that all men be saved and gain a knowledge of the truth. For God is one, and the Mediator between God and men is one, the Man Christ Jesus, He who gave Himself as a ransom for all, the testimony to be borne in due time; to this end Paul had been appointed an apostle and a preacher, and in faith and truth a teacher of the Gentiles, and he affirmed that he spoke the truth in Christ (NU omits in Christ, by the NKJV note) and did not lie. Paul desired, then, that the men pray in every place, lifting holy hands, free of wrath and doubting, and that the women likewise dress in modest apparel, adorning themselves with propriety and moderation (discretion, by the NKJV note), not with costly clothing, gold, pearls or braided hair, but with good works, as befits women who profess godliness. A woman was to learn in silence with all submission; Paul did not allow a woman authority over a man, nor did he allow her to teach; she was to be in silence, since Adam had been formed first, then Eve, and it was not Adam who had been deceived, but the woman, who, being deceived, had fallen into transgression. Yet she will be saved in childbearing, provided they abide in faith, love and holiness, and are self-controlled"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:6; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:9; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:15; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:16; 1 Timothy 3:1–16","v":"Paul wrote that it is a faithful saying that to seek the office of a bishop (Lit. overseer, by the NKJV note) is to desire a good work. A bishop, therefore, was to be blameless, hospitable, temperate, of good behavior, sober-minded, able to teach, the husband of one wife; not covetous (loving money, by the NKJV note), not quarrelsome, gentle, not greedy for money (NU omits not greedy for money, by the NKJV note), not violent, not given (addicted, by the NKJV note) to wine; ruling his own house well, keeping his children submissive with all reverence, for how would a man unable to manage his own household care for the church of God? A bishop was not to be a novice (new convert, by the NKJV note), lest pride puff him up and he fall into condemnation as the devil did; and he was to have, among those outside, a good testimony, so that he would not fall into reproach and be caught in the devil's snare. Likewise the deacons were to be reverent, not greedy for money, not given to much wine, not double-tongued, keeping the mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note) of the faith in a pure conscience; they were also first to be tested, and then, being found blameless, to serve as deacons. Likewise the deacons' wives were to be faithful in all things, temperate, reverent, and not slanderers (malicious gossips, by the NKJV note). The deacons were to be husbands of one wife, ruling their own houses and their children well, for those who serve well as deacons gain good standing for themselves, and much boldness in the faith that is in Christ Jesus. Paul wrote these things to Timothy, though he hoped to come to him shortly, so that, should Paul be delayed, Timothy might know how he ought to conduct himself in God's household, the church of the living God, which is the truth's pillar and ground (foundation, mainstay, by the NKJV note). Beyond controversy, the mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note) of godliness is great: God (NU Who, by the NKJV note) was received up in glory, believed on in the world, preached among the Gentiles, seen by angels, justified in the Spirit and manifested in the flesh"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 4:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 4:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 4:10; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 4:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 4:13; 1 Timothy 4:1–16","v":"Paul wrote that the Spirit says expressly (explicitly, by the NKJV note) that in latter times some will fall away from the faith, heeding deceiving spirits and the demons' doctrines; with their own conscience seared by a hot iron, they will speak lies in hypocrisy, and will forbid marriage and command abstinence from foods that God made for believers, who know the truth, to receive with thanksgiving. For all that God created is good, and nothing received with thanksgiving is to be rejected, for it is sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) by God's word and by prayer. If Timothy taught the brethren these things, he would prove a good minister of Jesus Christ, one nourished by good doctrine, which he had carefully followed, and by the words of faith. Timothy was to have nothing to do with profane and old wives' fables, but to train himself for godliness, for bodily training profits a little, while godliness is of profit in all things, with promise for the present life and for the life to come. The saying is faithful and worthy of all acceptance, because to this end we labored and suffered reproach (NU we labor and strive, by the NKJV note), since we trusted in the living God, and He is Savior of all men, of believers most of all. Timothy was to command and teach these things; to let no one despise his youth (look down on your youthfulness, by the NKJV note), but to set the believers an example in purity, in faith, in spirit (NU omits in spirit, by the NKJV note), in love, in conduct and in word; until Paul came, to give attention to doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note), to exhortation and to reading; not to neglect the gift in him, given him through prophecy when the eldership laid hands on him; to meditate on these things and give himself entirely to them, so that his progress might be evident to all; and to take heed to himself and to the doctrine and continue in them, for in doing so he would save both himself and those who heard him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:6; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:16; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:21; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 5:23; 1 Timothy 5:1–25","v":"Paul wrote that Timothy was not to rebuke an older man but to exhort him as he would a father, and to exhort the younger women as sisters, in all purity, the older women as mothers, and the younger men as brothers. Timothy was to honor the widows who were truly widows. A widow's children or grandchildren, if she had any, were first to learn to practice piety in their own home and to make some return to their parents, for that is good and (NU, M omit good and, by the NKJV note) acceptable in God's sight. She who is a widow indeed, left alone, puts her trust in God, continuing in prayers and supplications night and day, but she who gives herself to pleasure (indulgence, by the NKJV note) is dead even while she lives. Timothy was to command these things, so that they might be blameless; anyone who fails to provide for his own, most of all for his own household, has denied the faith; he is worse than an unbeliever. No widow under sixty years old was to be taken into the number, and none unless she had been the wife of one man, with a good report for good works: if she had diligently followed every good work, relieved the afflicted, washed the saints' feet, lodged strangers and brought up children. Timothy was to refuse (Refuse to enroll, by the NKJV note) the younger widows, because when they begin to grow wanton against Christ, they wish to marry, incurring condemnation for having cast off their first faith (Or solemn promise, by the NKJV note); besides, they learn idleness, going about from house to house, and become not only idle but busybodies and gossips too, saying what they ought not. So Paul desired that the younger widows marry, manage the house and bear children, and give the adversary no opportunity to speak reproachfully, for some had already turned aside after Satan. Any believing man or (NU omits man or, by the NKJV note) woman who had widows was to relieve (give aid to, by the NKJV note) them, and the church was not to be burdened, so that it might relieve those who were truly widows. The elders who ruled well were to be held worthy of double honor, most of all those whose labor was in the word and doctrine, since the Scripture says not to muzzle an ox treading out the grain, and that a laborer is worthy of his wages. Timothy was not to receive an accusation against an elder unless it came from two or three witnesses, and was to rebuke those who were sinning, doing so before all, so that the rest too might fear. Paul charged him, before the elect (chosen, by the NKJV note) angels and in the presence of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to observe these things without prejudice, and to do nothing by partiality. Timothy was to lay hands hastily on no one, nor to share in the sins of others, and was to keep himself pure; he was no longer to drink only water, but to use a little wine for the sake of his stomach and of his frequent infirmities (illnesses, by the NKJV note). The sins of some men are plain, going before them to judgment, while those of others follow after; so too some men's good works are plain, and those that are not cannot stay hidden"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 6:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 6:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 6:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 6:15; NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 6:20; 1 Timothy 6:1–21","v":"Paul wrote that bondservants under the yoke were to hold the masters over them deserving of full honor, lest God's name and His doctrine be blasphemed; those whose masters were believers were not to look down on them for being brethren, but rather to serve them, since those who receive the benefit are believers and beloved. Timothy was to teach and to exhort these things. Anyone who taught otherwise, not consenting to wholesome words, our Lord Jesus Christ's words, or to the doctrine (teaching, by the NKJV note) in keeping with godliness, knew nothing and was proud, obsessed with disputes and quarrels about words, which give rise to strife, envy, evil suspicions and reviling, and to the useless wranglings (NU, M constant friction, by the NKJV note) of men corrupt in mind, destitute of the truth, men who thought godliness a way to gain; from such Timothy was to withdraw (NU omits the rest of v. 5, by the NKJV note). Yet godliness joined with contentment is a great gain, since we came into the world with nothing, and it is certain (NU omits and it is certain, by the NKJV note) that nothing can be taken out of it; with food and clothing we shall be content. Those who want riches are caught by a snare and by temptation, and by many lusts, foolish and harmful, that sink men into destruction and perdition; for every kind of evil has a root in the love of money, and some, greedy for money, have wandered from the faith and pierced themselves with many sorrows. Timothy, a man of God, was to flee these things, pursuing instead faith, love, righteousness, gentleness, godliness and patience; he was to wage the good fight of faith and take hold of eternal life, to which he had been called; and he had made the good confession before many witnesses. Paul urged him, before God, who gives all things life, and in the presence of Christ Jesus, who before Pontius Pilate witnessed the good confession, to keep this commandment spotless and blameless till our Lord Jesus Christ appears, an appearing that He will show in His own time: the One whom no one has seen or is able to see, who dwells in light none can approach, who alone has immortality, the King of kings and Lord of lords, the blessed and only Potentate (Sovereign, by the NKJV note). Paul ascribed honor and everlasting power to Him. Amen. Timothy was to command the rich of this present age: they were not to be arrogant, nor to trust in riches, which are uncertain, but rather in the living God, the One who richly gives us all things to enjoy; they were to store up a good foundation for themselves for the time ahead, so that they might take hold of eternal life, and to that end were to be quick to give and glad to share, and to do good, so as to grow rich in good works. Paul called on Timothy by name to guard what had been committed to his trust, turning from profane and idle babblings (empty chatter, by the NKJV note), as well as the contradictions of what is wrongly called knowledge; some, professing it, have gone astray concerning the faith. Paul closed by wishing grace to be with Timothy. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Timothy 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Timothy 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 Timothy 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03287"},{"p":"purpose","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Timothy 3:15; 1 Timothy 3:14–15","v":"So that, should Paul be delayed in coming, Timothy might know how he ought to conduct himself in God's household, the church of the living God, which is the truth's pillar and ground (foundation, mainstay, by the NKJV note)"}],"nm":{"EVT-03287":"Paul writes his first letter to Timothy","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00673":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of Paul the Apostle to Timothy","d":"The letter Paul wrote to Timothy, his beloved son, of stirring up God's gift in him, of not being ashamed of our Lord's testimony or of Paul, of enduring hardship as Christ's good soldier, of the perilous times of the last days, of all Scripture as given by God's inspiration, of preaching the word, and of Paul's departure at hand (2 Timothy 1:1–4:22)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 1:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 1:5; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 1:11; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 1:18; 2 Timothy 1:1–18","v":"Paul, apostle of Jesus Christ (NU, M Christ Jesus, by the NKJV note) through God's will, in keeping with the promise of life in Christ Jesus, greeted Timothy, a beloved son, wishing him grace and peace and mercy from God the Father as well as from Christ Jesus our Lord. Paul thanked God, whom he served with a pure conscience, just as his forefathers had, as he remembered Timothy without ceasing in his prayers by night and by day, longing to see him and mindful of Timothy's tears, so as to be filled with joy, recalling the genuine (Lit. unhypocritical, by the NKJV note) faith in Timothy, which had dwelt first in Timothy's grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice, and which Paul was persuaded was in him as well. So Paul reminded him to stir up God's gift, which was in him by means of Paul's laying on of hands; for the spirit God has given us is not one of fear, but of a sound mind, of love and of power. Timothy was therefore not to be ashamed of our Lord's testimony, nor of Paul, His prisoner, but to share with Paul in suffering for the gospel, in keeping with God's power. God has saved us and, with a holy calling, has called us, not in keeping with our works but in keeping with His own grace and purpose, given us before time began, in Christ Jesus, and now made known in the appearing of Jesus Christ our Savior, who abolished death and through the gospel brought immortality and life to light. Paul was appointed to this gospel as a teacher of the Gentiles (NU omits of the Gentiles, by the NKJV note), an apostle and a preacher, and for this he suffered; yet he was not ashamed, for he knew whom he had believed and was persuaded that He was able, until that Day, to keep what Paul had committed to Him. Timothy was to hold fast the pattern he had heard from Paul, a pattern of words that are sound, in the faith and love that are in Christ Jesus, and to keep the good thing committed to him, through the Holy Spirit who lives in us. Timothy knew all those in Asia had turned away from Paul, Phygellus and Hermogenes among them. Paul wished that the Lord would grant mercy to Onesiphorus' household, for Onesiphorus had often refreshed him and had not been ashamed of Paul's chain; on coming to Rome he had sought Paul out with great zeal and found him. Paul wished that the Lord would grant Onesiphorus to find, in that Day, mercy from the Lord; and Timothy knew well in how many ways Onesiphorus had ministered to Paul (to me from Vg., a few Gr. mss., by the NKJV note) at Ephesus"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:10; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:14; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:16; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:19; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 2:21; 2 Timothy 2:1–26","v":"Timothy, Paul's son, was therefore to be strong in grace, the grace found in Christ Jesus, and to commit what he had heard from Paul among many witnesses to faithful men able to teach others as well. Timothy was to endure (NU You must share, by the NKJV note) hardship as Christ's good soldier: no one in warfare entangles himself in this life's affairs, so as to please the one who enlisted him; one who competes in athletics wins no crown unless he keeps the rules; and the farmer who works hard must be first to share in the crops. Timothy was to consider what Paul said, and Paul wished that the Lord might give (NU the Lord will give you, by the NKJV note) Timothy understanding in all things. Timothy was to remember that Jesus Christ had been raised from death, He who was of David's seed, as Paul's gospel told; for that gospel Paul suffered trouble, treated as an evildoer and even chained, but God's word is not chained. So, for the elect's (chosen ones, by the NKJV note) sake, Paul endured everything, so that they too might obtain, with eternal glory, the salvation found in Christ Jesus. The faithful saying runs that if we have died with Him, we too shall live with Him; enduring, we shall reign with Him; denying Him, we shall be denied by Him; and if we prove faithless, He remains faithful and is not able to deny Himself. Timothy was to remind them of all this, and to charge them before the Lord that they not strive (battle, by the NKJV note) over words, which profits nothing and ruins those who hear. Timothy was to be diligent to present himself to God as approved, a worker with no cause for shame who divides the word of truth rightly, and to shun the babblings that are profane and idle (empty chatter, by the NKJV note), since they will increase (lead, by the NKJV note) to further ungodliness, the message of such men spreading like cancer. Of this sort were Hymenaeus and Philetus, who had strayed concerning the truth, claiming the resurrection was already past, and were overthrowing some people's faith. Yet God's solid foundation stands, having this seal: that the Lord knows His own, and that all who name the name of Christ (NU, M the Lord, by the NKJV note) are to depart from iniquity. A great house holds vessels of wood and clay as well as of gold and silver, some meant for honor, others for dishonor; whoever cleanses himself of the latter will be ready for any good work and fit for the Master's use, sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) as a vessel of honor. Timothy was to flee youthful lusts, pursuing love, faith and righteousness, and peace, together with those who call upon the Lord from a pure heart; and he was to avoid the disputes that are foolish and ignorant, since they give rise to strife. One who serves the Lord must be patient, able to teach and gentle to all, and must not quarrel, in humility correcting those in opposition, in the hope that God may grant them repentance and they may come to know the truth, so that they escape the devil's snare, in which he had held them captive to carry out his will, and come back to their senses"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 3:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 3:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 3:16; 2 Timothy 3:1–17","v":"Timothy was to know that perilous times (times of stress, by the NKJV note) will arrive in the last days, for men will be keeping a form of godliness while denying its power, loving pleasure more than God, haughty, headstrong, treacherous, despising what is good, brutal, without self-control, slanderers, unforgiving (irreconcilable, by the NKJV note), unloving, unholy, unthankful, disobedient to parents, blasphemers, proud, boasters, lovers of money and of themselves; from such people Timothy was to turn away. Such men include the ones who creep into households, taking gullible women captive, women always learning yet never able to reach knowledge of the truth, led away by lusts of many kinds and weighed down by their sins. Just as Moses was resisted by Jannes and Jambres, these men too resist the truth, corrupt in mind and disapproved concerning the faith. Yet they will make no further progress, since their folly will be made manifest to all, as the folly of Jannes and Jambres was. Timothy, however, had carefully followed Paul's persecutions and afflictions, and Paul's perseverance and love, longsuffering and faith, purpose, manner of life and doctrine: the persecutions and afflictions that came on Paul at Antioch, at Lystra and at Iconium, such persecutions as he endured; and the Lord delivered Paul out of them all. Persecution will come to all who desire a godly life in Christ Jesus, while evil men and impostors alike will grow ever worse, deceiving others while being deceived. Timothy was to continue in what he had learned and was assured of, since he knew from whom he had learned it, and that from childhood he had known the Holy Scriptures, Scriptures able to make him wise for salvation through the faith found in Christ Jesus. All of Scripture comes by God's inspiration and is profitable for instruction (training, discipline, by the NKJV note) in righteousness, for correction, for reproof and for doctrine, so the man of God might be complete, equipped thoroughly for every good work"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 4:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 4:22; 2 Timothy 4:1–22","v":"Paul therefore (NU omits therefore, by the NKJV note) charged Timothy, in the presence of the Lord Jesus Christ, who is to judge the dead and the living at (NU and by, by the NKJV note) His kingdom and His appearing, and in the presence of God, to preach the word, ready whether in season or out of season, and to exhort, rebuke and convince with all teaching and longsuffering; for a time is coming when people will refuse to endure sound doctrine, but, because their ears itch, will heap up teachers for themselves to suit their own desires, and, turning their ears from the truth, will be turned aside to fables. Timothy was to fulfill his ministry, doing an evangelist's work, enduring afflictions, and being watchful in all things, for Paul was already being poured out like a drink offering, and his time of departure was at hand. He had kept the faith, finished the race and fought the good fight, and now the crown of righteousness was laid up for him, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, is to give him on that Day, and not to him alone but to everyone who has loved His appearing. Timothy was to be diligent to come to Paul quickly, for Demas had forsaken Paul, having loved this present world, and had gone to Thessalonica, Crescens to Galatia and Titus to Dalmatia; only Luke was with Paul. Timothy was to get Mark and bring him along, since Mark was useful to Paul for ministry. Paul sent Tychicus to Ephesus. When Timothy came, he was to bring the books, above all the parchments, and the cloak Paul had left with Carpus at Troas. Alexander the coppersmith had done Paul much harm; Paul wished that the Lord would repay Alexander for his works, and Timothy too was to beware of him, for he had greatly resisted our words. At Paul's first defense no one had stood with him, but all had forsaken him; Paul wished that it might not be charged against them. Yet the Lord had stood with him and strengthened him, so that through him the message might be fully preached and all the Gentiles might hear, and he had been delivered from the lion's mouth; the Lord will deliver him from every evil work and preserve him for His heavenly kingdom, and Paul gave praise, that glory be to Him forever and ever. Timothy was to greet the household of Onesiphorus, and Prisca and Aquila. Erastus had stayed in Corinth, and Paul had left Trophimus sick in Miletus. Timothy was to do his utmost to come before winter. Eubulus sent greetings, and so did all the brethren, with Claudia, Linus and Pudens. Paul closed by wishing that the Lord Jesus Christ (NU omits Jesus Christ, by the NKJV note) be with Timothy's spirit, and grace with him. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Timothy 1:1; 2 Timothy 1:1","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Timothy 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 Timothy 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03292"}],"nm":{"EVT-03292":"Paul writes his second letter to Timothy","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00674":{"k":"object","n":"The cloak Paul left with Carpus at Troas","d":"The cloak Paul had left with Carpus at Troas, which Timothy was to bring when he came (2 Timothy 4:13)","f":[{"p":"designation","r":"2 Timothy 4:13","v":"the cloak"},{"p":"owner","r":"2 Timothy 4:13","e":"PER-03213","at":"EVT-03292","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"Paul bids Timothy bring the cloak Paul left with Carpus at Troas (2 Timothy 4:13); it is taken to be Paul's, though the text does not say so."},{"p":"placed at","r":"2 Timothy 4:13","e":"PLC-01571","at":"EVT-03292"}],"nm":{"EVT-03292":"Paul writes his second letter to Timothy","PER-03213":"Saul","PLC-01571":"Troas"}},"OBJ-00675":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to Titus","d":"The letter Paul wrote to Titus, a true son in their common faith, whom he had left in Crete, of appointing elders in every city, of what a bishop must be, of rebuking those who teach what they ought not, of the things proper for sound doctrine, of God's grace, which brings salvation, of maintaining good works, and of avoiding foolish disputes (Titus 1:1–3:15)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Titus 1:4; NKJV notes, note on Titus 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Titus 1:7; NKJV notes, note on Titus 1:16; Titus 1:1–16","v":"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ and a bondservant of God, greeted Titus, a true son in the faith they shared, wishing him peace, mercy and grace from the Lord Jesus Christ (NU Christ Jesus, by the NKJV note) our Savior and from God the Father. Paul was an apostle and a bondservant in keeping with the faith of God's elect and with the truth they acknowledge, a truth in keeping with a godly life, in the hope of eternal life, promised before time began by God, who cannot lie; and in due time God had manifested His word through preaching, a preaching committed to Paul at the command of God our Savior. Paul had left Titus in Crete for this reason: to set in order what was lacking, and in every city to appoint elders, as Paul had commanded him. A man was to be appointed an elder if he was the husband of one wife, and blameless, his children faithful and not accused of insubordination or dissipation (debauchery, lit. incorrigibility, by the NKJV note). For a bishop (Lit. overseer, by the NKJV note), as God's steward, was to be blameless: not greedy for money, not violent, not given to wine, not quick-tempered, not self-willed, but self-controlled, holy, just, sober-minded, loving what is good, and hospitable; and he was to hold fast the faithful word, as taught to him, so that by sound doctrine he might be able to convict those who contradict, and to exhort; for there were many insubordinate men, deceivers and idle talkers, most of all those of the circumcision, whose mouths had to be stopped, who subverted whole households, teaching what they ought not for dishonest gain; a prophet of their own, one of them, had called Cretans always liars, lazy gluttons, evil beasts, and this testimony is true. Titus was therefore to rebuke them sharply, so that they might be sound in the faith, paying no heed to the commandments of men, men turning from the truth, nor to Jewish fables. All things are pure to the pure, but nothing is pure to the defiled and the unbelieving; their conscience and mind alike are defiled. They claim to know God, yet they deny Him by their works, being disqualified for every good work, disobedient and abominable (detestable, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Titus 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Titus 2:8; NKJV notes, note on Titus 2:10; Titus 2:1–15","v":"Titus was to speak the things proper for sound doctrine: the older men were to be temperate, reverent and sober, sound in patience, in love and in faith; the older women likewise were to be teachers of good things, not given to much wine, not slanderers, and reverent in behavior, so that the young women might be admonished by them to love their children and their husbands, to be good, homemakers, chaste and discreet, obedient to their own husbands, so that the word of God might not be blasphemed. Titus was likewise to exhort the young men that they be sober-minded. In all things Titus was to show himself a pattern of good works, with incorruptibility (NU omits incorruptibility, by the NKJV note), reverence and integrity in doctrine, and speech that is sound and cannot be condemned, so that an opponent might be ashamed, with nothing evil to say of him (NU, M us, by the NKJV note). Titus was to exhort bondservants to obey their own masters with all good fidelity (honesty, by the NKJV note), not pilfering (thieving, by the NKJV note), not answering back, and being well pleasing in all things, so that they might adorn God our Savior's doctrine in all things. For God's grace, bringing salvation, has appeared to all men, and it teaches us to deny worldly lusts and ungodliness and to live godly, righteous and sober lives in this present age, as we look for the appearing in glory of Jesus Christ, our great God and Savior, and for the blessed hope; Jesus Christ gave Himself for us, that from every lawless deed He might redeem us, and purify for Himself a special people, His own, who are zealous for good works. Titus was to speak these things, and to rebuke and exhort with all authority; no one was to despise him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Titus 3:10; Titus 3:1–15","v":"Titus was to remind them to stand ready for every good work, to obey, to be subject to authorities and rulers, to be gentle and peaceable, showing all humility toward all men and speaking evil of no one. For we too were once foolish, deceived and disobedient, serving various pleasures and lusts, hateful and hating one another, living in envy and malice. But when God our Savior's kindness and love toward man appeared, He saved us, not by works of righteousness we had done, but by His mercy, through the Holy Spirit's renewing and the washing of regeneration; and through Jesus Christ our Savior He poured the Spirit out abundantly on us, so that, justified by His grace, we might become heirs in keeping with the hope of eternal life. The saying is faithful, and Paul desired that Titus affirm these things constantly, so that believers in God might take care to maintain good works; and these things are profitable and good for men. Titus was to avoid strivings about the law, contentions, genealogies and foolish disputes, for these are useless and unprofitable. After a first and a second admonition (warning, by the NKJV note), Titus was to reject a divisive man, knowing that such a man is warped, self-condemned and sinning. When Paul sent Tychicus or Artemas to Titus, Titus was to be diligent to come to Paul at Nicopolis, for Paul had decided to winter there. Titus was to send Apollos and Zenas the lawyer on their journey with haste, so that they might lack nothing. Our people too were to learn to maintain good works for meeting urgent needs, so that they might not be unfruitful. All those with Paul sent Titus greetings, and Titus was to greet those who, in the faith, love Paul and those with him. Paul closed by wishing grace to be with Titus and all those with him. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Titus 1:1","v":"Paul, a bondservant of God"},{"p":"made by","r":"Titus 1:1","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Titus 1:1–4","e":"EVT-03305"}],"nm":{"EVT-03305":"Paul writes his letter to Titus","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00676":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Paul the Apostle to Philemon","d":"The letter Paul and Timothy wrote to Philemon, Paul's beloved friend and fellow laborer, to Apphia, to Archippus and to the church in Philemon's house, of Philemon's love and faith, and of Onesimus, whom Paul was sending back to him as a beloved brother, more than a slave (Philemon 1:1–25)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Philemon 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Philemon 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Philemon 1:7; NKJV notes, note on Philemon 1:12; Philemon 1:1–25","v":"With Timothy our brother, Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus, greeted Philemon, their beloved friend and fellow laborer, Archippus their fellow soldier, the church in Philemon's house, and the beloved Apphia (NU our sister Apphia, by the NKJV note), wishing them peace and grace from the Lord Jesus Christ and from God our Father. Paul thanked his God, always mentioning Philemon in his prayers, hearing of Philemon's faith and love toward all the saints and toward the Lord Jesus; he prayed that the sharing of Philemon's faith might become effective by every good thing in him (NU, M us, by the NKJV note) in Christ Jesus being acknowledged. Paul wrote that we have (NU had, by the NKJV note) consolation (comfort, by the NKJV note) and great joy (M thanksgiving, by the NKJV note) in Philemon's love, since the saints' hearts (Lit. inward parts, heart, liver, and lungs, by the NKJV note) have been refreshed by Philemon. So, though in Christ Paul might be very bold and command him to do what was fitting, yet for love's sake he, Paul, the aged, appealed to him instead, being a prisoner of Jesus Christ now as well; he appealed to him for his son Onesimus, whom he had begotten in his chains, who was once unprofitable to Philemon but now was profitable both to Philemon and to Paul. Paul was sending Onesimus back (NU back to you in person, that is, my own heart, by the NKJV note); Philemon was therefore to receive him, Paul's own heart (See v. 7, by the NKJV note). Paul had wished to keep Onesimus with him, so that on Philemon's behalf he might minister to Paul in his chains for the gospel, but without Philemon's consent Paul had wanted to do nothing, so that Philemon's good deed might be voluntary and not by compulsion, as it were. Perhaps Onesimus had departed for a while so that Philemon might receive him forever, as a beloved brother, more than a slave and no longer as one, most of all to Paul, but how much more to Philemon, both in the Lord and in the flesh. If, then, Philemon counted Paul a partner, he was to receive Onesimus as he would Paul, and if Onesimus had wronged Philemon or owed him anything, Philemon was to put it on Paul's account. Paul wrote with his own hand that he would repay, not to mention that Philemon owed him even his own self besides. Paul asked to have joy from him in the Lord, and that he refresh Paul's heart in the Lord. Confident of Philemon's obedience, Paul wrote, knowing that he would do even more than Paul said. Meanwhile Philemon was also to prepare a guest room for Paul, for Paul trusted that through the prayers of those to whom he wrote he would be granted to them. Epaphras, Paul's fellow prisoner in Christ Jesus, sent greetings, as did Luke, Demas, Aristarchus and Mark, Paul's fellow laborers. Paul closed by wishing that our Lord Jesus Christ's grace be with the spirit of those to whom he wrote. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Philemon 1:1","v":"Paul, a prisoner of Christ Jesus"},{"p":"feature","r":"Philemon 1:19","v":"Paul writes in it with his own hand that he will repay"},{"p":"made by","r":"Philemon 1:1, 19","e":"PER-03213"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Philemon 1:1–2","e":"EVT-03307"}],"nm":{"EVT-03307":"Paul writes to Philemon","PER-03213":"Saul"}},"OBJ-00677":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle to the Hebrews","d":"The letter of a writer whom the text does not name, of God's speaking now, at the last, through His Son, of the Son's being better than the angels, of Jesus as High Priest and as Mediator of a covenant that is better, of faith, and of the closing appeal to the brethren to bear with its word of exhortation (Hebrews 1:1–13:25)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 1:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 1:3; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 1:8; Hebrews 1:1–14","v":"God spoke long ago to the fathers through the prophets, on many occasions (Or in many portions, by the NKJV note) and by varied means; now, at the last, He has spoken to us through His Son. He made the Son heir of all things, and through Him made the worlds (Or ages, Gr. aiones, aeons, by the NKJV note). The Son is the brightness of God's glory, and the express image of God's person, and holds up all things by His powerful word; having purged (cleansed, by the NKJV note) our (NU omits our, by the NKJV note) sins by Himself (NU omits by Himself, by the NKJV note), He took His seat at the Majesty's right hand on high, now become better than the angels by as much as the name He inherited is more excellent than theirs. God never called any of the angels His Son, begotten by Him today, nor said He would be a Father to one of them; and when He brings the firstborn again into the world, God bids all His angels worship Him. Of the angels God says that He makes them spirits, His servants a fiery flame; but God tells the Son, addressing Him as God, that His throne lasts forever, that the scepter (A ruler's staff, by the NKJV note) of His kingdom is one of righteousness, and that because He loved what is right and hated lawlessness, God, His God, anointed Him above His companions with oil, the oil of gladness; and, addressing Him as Lord, that in the beginning He laid the earth's foundation, and the heavens are His hands' work, that they will perish and wear out like a garment, and He will fold them up as one folds a cloak, and they will undergo change, while He remains the same, His years never failing. God never bade any angel sit at His right hand till He should make its enemies its footstool; the angels are all spirits in service, sent out to serve on behalf of those who are to inherit salvation"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:4; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:7; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:11; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:16; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 2:18; Hebrews 2:1–18","v":"So we must heed what we have heard more earnestly than ever (all the more careful attention, by the NKJV note), so as not to drift away. If the word that was spoken through angels stood firm, every transgression and act of disobedience being justly repaid (retribution or penalty, by the NKJV note), there is no escape for us if we neglect a salvation so great, which the Lord declared first and His hearers then confirmed to us, while God also bore witness by wonders, signs and miracles of many kinds and by the Holy Spirit's gifts (distributions, by the NKJV note), as He willed. He has not subjected to angels the world to come that we are speaking of. Someone testified somewhere, asking the One he addresses what man is, that He is mindful of him, or what the son of man is, that He takes care of him; He set him a little (Or for a little while, by the NKJV note) below the angels, gave him a crown of glory and honor, gave him charge of what His hands had made (NU, M omit the rest of v. 7, by the NKJV note), and made all things subject to him, beneath his feet. In subjecting all to him, He left nothing not subject to him; yet we do not see everything subject to him as yet. But we do see Jesus, set a little (Or for a little while, by the NKJV note) below the angels, and given a crown of glory and honor for His suffering of death, so that by God's grace He might taste death on behalf of everyone. It befitted Him by whom and for whom all things are, who brings many sons to glory, that He should perfect their salvation's captain through sufferings. The One who sanctifies (sets apart, by the NKJV note) and the ones being sanctified: all are of one, and so He calls them brethren without shame, saying that He will declare the name of the One He addresses to His brethren and sing praise to Him in the assembly, that He will trust in Him, and that He is here with the children God has given Him. Since the children share in flesh and blood, He too took part in the same, so that by dying He might destroy the one holding the power of death, the devil, and free those whom the fear of death kept in bondage their whole life long. It is not angels whom He aids (Or take on the nature of, by the NKJV note), but the seed of Abraham whom He aids (Or take on, by the NKJV note). So He had to be fashioned like His brethren in every way, to be a High Priest, merciful and faithful, in the things of God, making propitiation for the people's sins; for since He Himself suffered when tempted (tested, by the NKJV note), He can help those who are tempted"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 3:6; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 3:13; Hebrews 3:1–19","v":"So the holy brethren who share the heavenly calling are to consider Christ Jesus, the Apostle of our confession and its High Priest, faithful to the One who appointed Him, like Moses in the whole of His house. He has been judged to deserve greater glory than Moses, as a house's builder is honored above the house; every house has a builder, and the builder of all things is God. Moses, a servant, served faithfully in the whole of His house, bearing witness to what was to be spoken later, while Christ is over His own house as a Son, and we are His house if we keep, firm to the end (NU omits firm to the end, by the NKJV note), our confidence and our rejoicing in the hope. So the Holy Spirit's word is that, if they hear His voice today, they are not to harden their hearts like the fathers who rebelled in the wilderness, testing and trying Him while for forty years they saw what He did; He was angered by that generation, saying that their hearts always go astray and that His ways are unknown to them, and in His wrath He swore they would never come into His rest. They are to beware that none of them has an unbelieving, evil heart, turning from the living God, and, day by day, so long as the word Today still holds, are rather to exhort (encourage, by the NKJV note) one another, so that sin's deceit hardens none of them; for we have come to share in Christ, if we keep the confidence we began with steadfast to the end. Those who heard and rebelled were all whom Moses led out of Egypt; He was angry forty years with the sinners, who fell dead in the wilderness, and swore that the disobedient would never come into His rest; so unbelief kept them from entering"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 4:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 4:8; Hebrews 4:1–16","v":"A promise that we may enter His rest still stands, so we are to fear that any of the readers may seem to have missed it. We have had the good news as they did, but the message they heard was of no use to them, since its hearers did not join it with faith (NU, M since they were not united by faith with those who heeded it, by the NKJV note). Having believed, we are entering that rest, as in His wrath He swore they would never come into His rest, though the works stood complete since the world's foundation; for somewhere He has told of the seventh day, on which God rested from all His works, then again that they would never come into His rest. Since some are yet to enter it, and those who first heard the good news did not, for their disobedience, He again sets a day, Today, saying it in David so long afterward: if they hear His voice today, they are not to harden their hearts. Had Joshua (Gr. Jesus, same as Heb. Joshua, by the NKJV note) brought them to rest, He would have had no reason to speak later of a different day. So a rest remains for God's people, and anyone who has come into His rest has left off his own works, as God left off His. We are to strive diligently to enter that rest, so that no one falls through disobedience like theirs. God's word is living and powerful; it is keener than any sword with two edges, cutting so deep that it divides soul and spirit, joints and marrow, and judging what the heart thinks and intends; no creature can hide from His sight; everything lies bare and exposed before the eyes of the One who will call us to account. Since our great High Priest, Jesus, God's Son, has made His way through the heavens, we are to hold fast our confession; He is able to feel for our weaknesses, tempted in every way as we are, but He never sinned. So we are to draw near the throne of grace boldly, to receive mercy and to find grace that helps us when we need it"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 5:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 5:12; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 5:14; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 5:14; Hebrews 5:1–14","v":"Every high priest is a man chosen from among men, appointed on their behalf in the things of God, to present gifts and sacrifices on account of sins; he is able to have compassion on (deal gently with, by the NKJV note) the ignorant and those who stray, being subject to weakness himself, and so must offer sacrifices for sins for himself as for the people. No one claims this honor for himself; God calls him to it, as He called Aaron. In the same way Christ did not take for Himself the glory of becoming High Priest; it was the One who told Him that He is His Son, begotten today, and who says elsewhere that He is a priest forever in Melchizedek's order. While He lived in the flesh He offered prayers and pleas, crying vehemently with tears, to the One who could rescue Him from death; His godly fear won Him a hearing; though He was the Son, His sufferings taught Him obedience, and, made perfect, He became author of salvation that is eternal for all who obey Him, and God called Him High Priest in Melchizedek's order. On this there is much we could say, hard as it is to explain, since the hearers have grown dull of hearing; when by now they should be teaching others, they need to be taught once more the elementary truths of God's oracles (sayings, Scriptures, by the NKJV note), and now need milk rather than solid food. One who takes only milk is a babe, and the word of righteousness is beyond his skill; solid food is for the full-grown (mature, by the NKJV note), whose senses are trained by use (practice, by the NKJV note) to tell good from evil"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:3; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:6; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:10; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:12; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:17; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:17; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:18; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 6:18; Hebrews 6:1–20","v":"So, leaving the first teaching about Christ, we are to press on to perfection (maturity, by the NKJV note) and not lay a foundation again: faith toward God and repentance from dead works, teaching on eternal judgment and the resurrection of the dead, the laying on of hands and baptisms; and this, if God permits, we will do (M let us do, by the NKJV note). Those once enlightened, if they fall away (Or and have fallen away, by the NKJV note), cannot be brought back to repentance, though they have had a taste of heaven's gift, of God's good word and of the coming age's powers, and have come to share in the Holy Spirit, since for themselves they crucify God's Son again and hold Him up to open shame. God blesses land that soaks up the rain falling often on it and produces plants useful to those who farm it; but land bearing thorns and briers is worthless, close to a curse, and ends in burning. Yet we are sure of better things for the beloved, things that go with salvation, though we speak so; God is too just to overlook what they have done and the labor of love (NU omits labor of, by the NKJV note) they have given for His name's sake by serving the saints, as they still do. The letter wants each of them to be just as diligent in reaching a fully assured hope to the end, not growing sluggish (lazy, by the NKJV note) but following the example of those who inherit the promises through faith and patience. When God promised Abraham, having no one greater to swear by, He swore by Himself that He would surely bless and multiply him; and Abraham, after waiting patiently, received the promise. People swear by someone above themselves, and with them an oath settles every dispute as confirmation; so God, wishing to make it all the plainer to the promise's heirs that His counsel cannot change (unchangeableness of His purpose, by the NKJV note), added an oath to confirm (guaranteed, by the NKJV note) it, so that through two things that cannot change (unchangeable, by the NKJV note), in which God cannot lie, we who have run for safety, to seize the hope held out before us, might have (M omits might, by the NKJV note) strong consolation. This hope is our soul's anchor, firm and secure, reaching behind the veil, into the Presence, which Jesus has entered on our behalf as forerunner, now High Priest forever in Melchizedek's order"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:4; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:13; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:14; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:17; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:19; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:21; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:22; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:25; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 7:26; Hebrews 7:1–28","v":"Melchizedek, Salem's king and a priest serving the Most High God, came to meet Abraham as he returned from striking down the kings, blessed him, and was given by him a tenth of everything; his name means king of righteousness, and as Salem's king he is king of peace; with no genealogy, no father or mother, with no beginning to his days and no end to his life, and resembling God's Son, he remains a priest for all time. They are to consider how great he was, for he was given the tenth part of the spoils (plunder, by the NKJV note) by the patriarch Abraham himself. Those of Levi's sons who hold the priesthood are commanded by the law to take tithes from the people, their brethren, though these too came from Abraham's loins; but with no descent from Levi, he still collected a tenth from Abraham, and it was he who blessed the holder of the promises, and beyond dispute the lesser receives blessing from the greater. On earth tithes go to mortal men; in his case, to one attested as living; and Levi, the tithe-receiver, in a manner of speaking gave tithes himself by way of Abraham, as he was yet unborn, within his forefather, when Melchizedek met Abraham. Had perfection come by way of the Levitical priesthood, under which the law was given to the people, there would be no need for another priest to arise in Melchizedek's order rather than Aaron's; for a change of priesthood means a change of law too. The One these things speak of comes from another tribe, a tribe whose men never officiated (served, by the NKJV note) at its altar; our Lord, plainly, sprang from Judah, a tribe Moses never linked with priesthood (NU priests, by the NKJV note). It is plainer still when another priest arises like Melchizedek, come not by a law tied to fleshly descent but by the power of a life without end; for He testifies (NU it is testified, by the NKJV note) that He is a priest forever in Melchizedek's order. The former commandment is set aside as weak and unprofitable, for the law brought nothing to perfection (complete, by the NKJV note), and a better hope is brought in, by which we approach God. He became priest by an oath, while they became priests without any; He was told that the LORD, who will not relent, has sworn that He is a priest forever (NU ends the quotation after forever., by the NKJV note) in Melchizedek's order; to that degree Jesus has been made surety (guarantee, by the NKJV note) for a better covenant. Those priests were many, since death cut their service short; but He continues forever and holds a priesthood that cannot change, and so He can bring salvation to the uttermost (completely or forever, by the NKJV note) to all who draw near to God through Him, ever living to intercede for them. This is the High Priest we needed: one raised above the heavens, set apart from sinners, undefiled, harmless (innocent, by the NKJV note) and holy. Unlike those high priests, He does not need to bring sacrifices day by day, first on His own behalf, then on the people's; He did this one time for all by giving Himself up as the offering. The law makes weak men high priests, but the Son, perfected forever, is appointed by the oath's word, which came later than the law"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 8:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 8:12; Hebrews 8:1–13","v":"The main point is this: ours is a High Priest of that kind, enthroned at the Majesty's right in the heavens, ministering in the true tabernacle, the sanctuary (Lit. holies, by the NKJV note), set up by the Lord rather than by man. As each high priest is put in office to present gifts and sacrifices, this One too must have an offering to make; on earth He would have no place as a priest, for priests already offer the gifts the law prescribes, their service a copy and shadow of what is in heaven, as Moses was divinely told when the tabernacle was to be made: he was to make everything by the pattern he saw on the mountain. Yet His ministry now is far more excellent, as Mediator of a covenant that is better, resting on better promises. Had the first covenant been without fault, no second would have been called for; but He finds fault with them and declares that, as the LORD says, days are coming when He will make with Israel's house and Judah's house a new covenant, not like the one He made with their fathers when He took their hand and led them from Egypt, for they broke faith with His covenant, so He turned from them; He will inscribe His laws on their minds and hearts; He will be God to them and they will belong to Him as His people; no one will need to urge neighbor or brother to know the LORD, for great and small alike will know Him; and He will show mercy to their unrighteousness, and never again will He call to mind their sins and lawless deeds (NU omits and their lawless deeds, by the NKJV note). By calling this covenant new, He has declared the first outworn, and what ages and wears out will soon disappear"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:10; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:11; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:13; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:13; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:14; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:19; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:22; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:22; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:23; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 9:24; Hebrews 9:1–28","v":"The first covenant too had its rules of worship and its sanctuary on earth. A tabernacle was set up: its first part, called the sanctuary (holy place, lit. holies, by the NKJV note), held the table with the showbread, and the lampstand; beyond the second veil lay the Holiest of All, holding the covenant's ark, covered all over with gold, and a censer of gold; in the ark were the covenant's tablets, Aaron's budded rod and the golden pot of manna, and over it the glorious cherubim spread their shadow across the mercy seat; a detailed account of these must wait. With all so prepared, the priests entered the first part continually to perform the services, while the high priest by himself entered the inner part, a single time each year, and always with blood, brought as an offering on his own behalf and for sins the people had done in ignorance; by this the Holy Spirit showed that, while the first tabernacle still stood, the path to the Holiest of All had yet to be revealed. This was a figure for the present time: the gifts and sacrifices offered under it cannot perfect the worshiper's conscience, being only rules for the flesh, about washings of various kinds (Lit. baptisms, by the NKJV note) and food and drink, laid on them until a time of reform. But Christ, High Priest of blessings yet to come (NU that have come, by the NKJV note), came with a tabernacle greater and more complete, made by no hands and belonging to no part of this creation, and He went once for all into the Most Holy Place by His own blood, not by that of goats and calves, having obtained redemption that is eternal. If sprinkling the unclean with a heifer's ashes and goats' and bulls' blood sanctifies (sets apart, by the NKJV note) them so that the flesh is purified (cleansing, by the NKJV note), how much more will Christ's blood cleanse their conscience of dead works, so they may serve the living God, He who offered Himself to God without spot (blemish, by the NKJV note) through the eternal Spirit. For this He mediates the new covenant, so that, by a death that redeems the first covenant's transgressions, the called may receive the promised eternal inheritance. A testament needs the testator's death; it comes into force once men are dead, and has none while its maker lives. So the first covenant too was put in force only with blood. Moses, having told the whole people each precept (command, by the NKJV note) the law laid down, took calves' and goats' blood and sprinkled it, with water, hyssop and scarlet wool, on the book and on every one of the people, telling them that this blood is the covenant's, the covenant God has laid on them; and he sprinkled blood over the tabernacle as well, and all the utensils used in its service. The law requires blood to purify (cleansed, by the NKJV note) almost everything, and without bloodshed nothing is remitted (forgiveness, by the NKJV note). So the copies of what is in the heavens had to be purified (cleansed, by the NKJV note) by such means, while what is heavenly needs sacrifices better than these. Christ has gone, not into a sanctuary men built, a mere copy (representations, by the NKJV note) of the real one, but into heaven itself, where He now stands before God on our behalf. Nor was He to offer Himself again and again, as the high priest goes yearly into the Most Holy Place with blood not his own; else His suffering would have had to be repeated ever since the world was founded. But now, as the ages reach their end, He has appeared a single time to do away with sin by sacrificing Himself. Just as death comes once to every man, with judgment after it, so Christ, having been offered one time to carry many people's sins, is to appear a second time to all who await Him eagerly, apart from sin, to save them"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:9; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:10; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:14; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:18; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:19; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:30; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:32; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:34; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:34; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:34; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:37; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:37; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:38; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:39; Hebrews 10:1–39","v":"The law holds only a shadow of the coming blessings, not their very image, and can never by the same sacrifices, offered without end year after year, perfect those who come to it; else the offering of them would have stopped, since worshipers, once purified (cleansed, by the NKJV note), would be conscious of sins no longer. Instead, those sacrifices bring sins to mind each year, for bulls' and goats' blood cannot take sins away. So, at His coming into the world, He told God that God had not desired sacrifice and offering but had prepared a body for Him, and had taken no pleasure in sin offerings or burnt offerings; then He said that He has come, as the book's scroll writes of Him, to do God's will. Having first said that God neither desired nor took pleasure in any of these, offered though they are by the law, He then says He has come to do God's will (NU, M omit O God, by the NKJV note); He removes the first to set up the second. That will is how we have been sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note), by Jesus Christ's body offered once for all. The priests serve daily, presenting the same sacrifices again and again, never able to take sins away; but this Man offered for sins a single sacrifice for all time and took His seat at God's right hand, there to wait until His enemies are put beneath His feet as a footstool; for His one offering has brought those being sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) to perfection for all time. The Holy Spirit too bears witness to us: having said first that this is the covenant the LORD is to make with them once that time comes, when He will inscribe His laws on their hearts and minds, He adds that never again will their sins and lawless acts be called to mind; and with these remitted (forgiveness, by the NKJV note), no offering for sin remains. So, brethren, having boldness (confidence, by the NKJV note) through Jesus' blood to go into the Holiest, by a way new and living, consecrated for us by Him through the veil of His flesh, and since God's house has a High Priest set over it, we are to come near sincerely, wholly confident in faith, our hearts cleansed by sprinkling from any evil conscience, our bodies, too, bathed clean in pure water; to keep firm hold, unwavering, of the hope we confess, since the One who made the promise is faithful; and to give thought to one another, to rouse each other to love and good works and not to give up meeting together, as some are in the habit of doing, but to urge one another on, all the more as the Day is seen drawing near. If, having come to know the truth, we sin willfully, no sacrifice for sins is left, only dread of coming judgment and of fierce fire that will destroy the adversaries. One who has set aside the law of Moses dies without mercy when two or three witnesses bear testimony against him; how far heavier, then, is the punishment that will be thought due to one who has trodden underfoot the Son of God, treated as common the covenant's blood that had sanctified him, and done outrage to the Spirit of grace. After all, we know the One who said vengeance belongs to Him and He will repay; the Lord says so (NU omits says the Lord, by the NKJV note); and He said again that the LORD is to judge His people. Coming into the living God's grip is terrifying. They are to look back to the days after they were illuminated (enlightened, by the NKJV note), when they held out through a painful struggle, sometimes made a public show through insults and afflictions, sometimes sharing the lot of others treated so; they had felt for the writer in his chains (NU the prisoners instead of me in my chains, by the NKJV note) and cheerfully let their goods (possessions, by the NKJV note) be taken, knowing they have for themselves in heaven (NU omits in heaven, by the NKJV note) a better possession, one that lasts. So they are to keep their confidence, which will be richly rewarded, and they need to persevere, so that once they have done what God wills they obtain what He promised: before long He who is coming (Or that which, by the NKJV note) is to arrive without lingering (delay, by the NKJV note); by faith the just (NU My just one, by the NKJV note) will live, but the speaker's soul takes no delight in one who shrinks back. We, though, are not ones who shrink back to perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note); we believe, and so our souls are saved"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:1; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:1; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:3; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:11; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:13; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:18; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:25; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:26; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:26; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:31; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 11:37; Hebrews 11:1–40","v":"Faith is the very substance (realization, by the NKJV note) of what is hoped for and the evidence (Or confidence, by the NKJV note) of what is unseen; the elders gained a good report through it. Through faith we grasp that God's word set the worlds (Or ages, Gr. aiones, aeons, by the NKJV note) in order, so that what is seen was not made out of what can be seen. His faith made Abel's sacrifice to God better than Cain's; through it he was attested righteous, God bearing witness to his gifts; through it too, though dead, he speaks still. Enoch, in faith, was taken away and did not see death; no one could find him, for God had taken him away, and before then he was known as one who pleased God. Without faith no one can please Him, for one who draws near to God has to believe in His existence and that He rewards those who seek Him with diligence. Noah, divinely warned of things still unseen, in faith and in reverent fear built an ark that saved his household; by it the world stood condemned, and the righteousness that comes by faith became his inheritance. In faith Abraham obeyed the call to set out for a place he was to receive as his inheritance, setting out without knowing his destination; in faith he made his home in the promised land as a stranger in a land not his own, living in tents, as did Isaac and Jacob, his fellow heirs of that promise, since he awaited a city with foundations, made and built by God. Through faith Sarah too, though beyond the age for it, was given power to conceive, and she gave birth to a child (NU omits she bore a child, by the NKJV note), because she counted faithful the One who had made the promise; so from one man whose body was all but dead came offspring like the sky's stars in number, countless as the sand along the seashore. Without receiving what was promised, all these died in faith; they had seen the promises from a distance and, persuaded of them (NU, M omit were assured of them, by the NKJV note), had welcomed them, owning that on the earth they were strangers and sojourners. Those who talk this way show plainly that a homeland is what they are looking for; had the country they came from been in their minds, they could have gone back to it. Instead they long for a better homeland, a heavenly one, and so God feels no shame in being called their God, having made a city ready for them. Abraham, put to the test, in faith offered Isaac; the man who held the promises was ready to give his only begotten son, the one of (to, by the NKJV note) whom it had been said that his seed would be named through Isaac; he reasoned that God could raise him even from death, and in a figure he did receive him back from it. Isaac's blessing on Jacob and Esau, looking to things ahead, was given in faith. In faith Jacob, near his death, blessed each of Joseph's sons and, propped on the head of his staff, bowed in worship. In faith Joseph, near his death, spoke of Israel's coming departure and gave orders about his bones. In faith the newborn Moses was hidden by his parents for three months, for they saw that the child was beautiful, and they did not fear the king's command. When grown, Moses in faith refused the name of son of Pharaoh's daughter; he preferred hardship alongside God's people to sin's brief (temporary, by the NKJV note) delights, and counted the reproach (reviling because of, by the NKJV note) of Christ worth more than whatever treasures were in Egypt (NU, M of, by the NKJV note), looking ahead to the reward. It was in faith that he left Egypt behind, not fearing the king's wrath, and held firm, as one who sees the unseen One; and faith led him to observe the Passover and the blood-sprinkling, so that the destroyer of the firstborn would leave them untouched. In faith they went over the Red Sea on what seemed dry ground, while the Egyptians who tried the same were drowned. Faith brought Jericho's walls down once they had been circled for seven days, and in faith Rahab the harlot, having welcomed the spies in peace, was not destroyed with the unbelieving (were disobedient, by the NKJV note). What more is there to say? There would not be time enough to tell of Barak and Gideon, Jephthah and Samson, Samuel and David, and the prophets. It was through faith that these routed foreign armies, did what was right, shut lions' mouths, conquered kingdoms, found strength in weakness, escaped the sword, gained what was promised, quenched raging fire and grew mighty in war. Women had their dead given back to them, raised to life; others, under torture, refused to be set free, so as to win a resurrection that is better; still others went through chains and prison, flogging and mockery. Some were sawn apart, some stoned, some killed by the sword, some tempted (NU omits were tempted, by the NKJV note); they roamed in the skins of sheep and goats, mistreated, oppressed and in want, people the world did not deserve, wandering over mountains and deserts, in caves and holes in the ground. All these, attested through their faith, did not obtain what was promised, for God had in view something better for us: they were to reach perfection only together with us"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:2; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:5; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:7; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:11; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:16; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:18; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:18; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:20; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:23; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:26; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 12:28; Hebrews 12:1–29","v":"So then, with so many witnesses around us like a cloud, we too are to put off every weight, together with the sin that trips us so easily, and run the race laid out before us with endurance, our eyes fixed on Jesus, our faith's author (originator, by the NKJV note) and finisher (perfecter, by the NKJV note), who endured the cross for the sake of the joy ahead of Him, scorning its shame, and has taken His seat at the right of God's throne. They are to consider Him who held out against such hostility from sinners, so that they do not grow weary and lose heart. In striving against sin they have not yet resisted so far as shedding blood; and they have forgotten the encouragement that addresses them as sons: a son is not to make light of the LORD's chastening (discipline, by the NKJV note) nor lose heart when He rebukes him, since the LORD disciplines those He loves, and He whips every son He takes as His own. If (NU, M It is for discipline that you endure; God, by the NKJV note) they bear up under chastening, it is as sons that God is dealing with them; for what father leaves his son undisciplined? Were they left without the discipline all receive, they would be illegitimate children, not true sons. We gave respect to our human fathers when they corrected us; how much more readily should the Father of spirits have our submission, that we may live? Their discipline lasted a short time and followed their own judgment, but His is for our good, to give us a share in His holiness. No chastening (discipline, by the NKJV note) feels joyful at the time; it is painful; later, though, it yields a peaceful harvest of righteousness for those trained by it. So they are to strengthen drooping hands and weak knees, and straighten the tracks their feet will follow, so that the lame limb is not put out of joint but healed instead. They are to seek peace with everyone, and holiness, since without holiness none will see the Lord, watching with care that no one falls short of God's grace, that no bitter root springs up to trouble them and defile many, and that there is no fornicator among them, nor anyone profane (godless, by the NKJV note) as Esau was, who gave up his birthright for one meal; afterward, they know, when he wanted the blessing as his inheritance, he was turned away; no room for repentance was open to him, though he searched for it with tears and diligence. What they have come to is not a voice speaking words, nor a trumpet's blast, nor storm, darkness (NU gloom, by the NKJV note) and blackness, nor a mountain (NU to that which, by the NKJV note) that can be touched and was ablaze with fire; those who heard that voice begged to be spoken to no further, unable as they were to bear the command that even a beast touching the mountain be stoned or pierced by an arrow (NU, M omit the rest of v. 20., by the NKJV note), and the sight was so fearful that Moses said he was greatly afraid and trembling. Instead they have come to Jesus, the new covenant's Mediator, and to the sprinkled blood that speaks a better word than Abel's; to God, who is Judge of all; to Mount Zion, the living God's city, the heavenly Jerusalem; to righteous men's spirits, now perfected; to the church of the firstborn, gathered in general assembly (festal gathering, by the NKJV note) and enrolled in heaven; and to angels past counting. They are to beware of refusing the One who speaks; those who refused Him when He spoke on earth did not escape, and much less will we if we turn from the One speaking from heaven. Back then the earth was shaken by His voice, and He has now promised, once more, to shake (NU will shake, by the NKJV note) heaven as well as the earth. The words once more show that the things shaken, as things that were made, are to be removed, so that the unshakable may remain. Since the kingdom we receive cannot be shaken, we are to have grace; through it we may (M omits may, by the NKJV note) offer God service that pleases Him, in godly fear and reverence; for a consuming fire is what our God is"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:9; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:12; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:15; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:17; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:21; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:21; NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 13:24; Hebrews 13:1–25","v":"Brotherly love is to go on. They are to be sure to welcome strangers as guests, since some who did this have had angels as their guests without realizing it. They are to keep prisoners in mind as if sharing their chains, and the ill-treated as well, since they too live in a body. Marriage is a thing of honor among all, and the marriage bed free of defilement; God will judge those who commit adultery or fornication. Covetousness is to have no place in how they live, and they are to count what they have as enough, for He has given His own promise never to abandon them in any way; so we can declare with confidence that the LORD helps us and we need not be afraid of what any mortal may do to us. They are to keep in mind those who rule over (lead, by the NKJV note) them, the ones who spoke God's word to them, and, weighing how their way of life turned out, imitate their faith. Jesus Christ does not change: He is today what He was yesterday, and He will remain so forever. They are not to be swept about (NU, M away, by the NKJV note) by teachings of every strange kind, for grace, not foods, should be what makes the heart firm; foods have done no good to those taken up with them. Ours is an altar the tabernacle's servants may not eat from; for when the high priest brings the blood of animals into the sanctuary for sin, their bodies are burned beyond the camp. So Jesus too endured His suffering beyond the gate, so that His own blood would sanctify (set apart, by the NKJV note) the people. So we are to leave the camp and go out to Him, sharing the reproach He bore, for no city here is lasting for us; the city we look for is still to come. Through Him, then, we are to bring God praise without ceasing as our offering, the thanks (Lit. confessing, by the NKJV note) our lips give to His name; and they are to keep sharing what they have and doing good, since sacrifices like these are what please God. They are to obey and submit to those who rule over (lead, by the NKJV note) them, for these keep watch over their souls and will be called to account; and they are to let their leaders do this gladly and not with grief, since grief would do the hearers no good. They are to pray for us, who are certain our conscience is clear and want to act honorably in everything; and the writer presses them all the more to do it, so that his return to them may come more quickly. The letter prays that the God of peace, by whom our Lord Jesus, the sheep's great Shepherd, was brought up from the dead through the everlasting covenant, by its blood, make them complete (perfect, by the NKJV note) in every good deed, so that they carry out His will, while He brings about in them (NU, M us, by the NKJV note) whatever pleases Him, through Jesus Christ; and it gives praise, that glory be to Him for all ages to come. Amen. The writer appeals to the brethren to put up with his word of exhortation, since he has written to them only briefly. They are to know of the release of Timothy, our brother; if he comes soon, the writer will see them together with him. They are to greet all the saints, and all who rule over (lead, by the NKJV note) them; those from Italy send greetings. The letter ends by asking grace for every one of them. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 1:1; Hebrews 1:1","v":"God, who at various times"},{"p":"designation","r":"Hebrews 13:22","v":"the word of exhortation"},{"p":"feature","r":"Hebrews 2:3","v":"Its writer counts himself among those to whom the salvation the Lord first declared was confirmed by His hearers"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Hebrews 10:34; Hebrews 10:34","v":"Its writer had been in chains (NU the prisoners instead of me in my chains, by the NKJV note), and the hearers had compassion on him"},{"p":"feature","r":"Hebrews 13:18–19, 23","v":"It asks the hearers' prayers for us, who are certain our conscience is clear, wanting to act honorably in everything; its writer especially asks those prayers so that his return to them may come more quickly, and he will see them with Timothy if Timothy comes soon"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Hebrews 1:1–2; 13:22","e":"EVT-03311"}],"nm":{"EVT-03311":"The Epistle to the Hebrews is written"}},"OBJ-00678":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of James","d":"The letter James, God's bondservant and the Lord Jesus Christ's, wrote to the twelve tribes scattered far and wide, of joy in trials, of asking God for wisdom, of hearing the word and doing it, of faith without partiality and faith shown by works, of the tongue, of the wisdom from above, of humbling oneself before God, and of patience until the Lord's coming (James 1:1–5:20)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on James 1:3; NKJV notes, note on James 1:4; NKJV notes, note on James 1:19; NKJV notes, note on James 1:21; NKJV notes, note on James 1:26; James 1:1–27","v":"James, God's bondservant and the Lord Jesus Christ's, sent greetings to the twelve tribes scattered far and wide. His brethren were to count it nothing but joy when they met with trials of many kinds, since their faith, when it is tested, yields patience (endurance or perseverance, by the NKJV note); and patience was to be allowed to do its full work, so that they would be perfect (mature, by the NKJV note) and complete, short of nothing. Any of them short of wisdom was to ask God, who gives freely to everyone and holds it against no one, and wisdom would be given him; but he was to ask in faith, never doubting, for a doubter is like a wave the wind throws this way and that across the sea. Such a man is not to think the Lord will give him anything; there is no steadiness in anything he does, divided as his mind is. The lowly brother was to make his raising up his boast, and the rich man his lowering, for like a wildflower the rich man will be gone: grass dries up under the sun's burning heat once it has risen, losing its flower and the beauty it had, and amid his dealings the rich man will waste away just so. The crown of life, promised by the Lord to all that love Him, will be given to the man who holds firm under temptation once he has stood the test, and he is blessed. No one under temptation was to say that God was tempting him, for evil cannot tempt God, and He tempts no one; each person is tempted by his own desire, which pulls him away and entices him; that desire, once it has taken hold, gives rise to sin, and sin, grown to its full, ends in death. His beloved brethren were not to be deceived: every gift that is good and perfect is from above, sent down by the Father of lights; in Him no change takes place, nor any turning that casts a shadow. He gave us birth through the word of truth because He willed it, meaning us to be, as it were, firstfruits of all He made. So then (NU Know this or This you know, by the NKJV note), each was to be slow to anger, in no hurry to speak, and quick to listen, for God's righteousness is not worked by a man's anger. Therefore they were humbly to welcome the word set within them, through which their souls can be brought to safety, and to strip off everything foul and the wickedness spilling over (abundance, by the NKJV note). They were to put the word into practice, not just listen to it, which would be to fool themselves; for anyone who hears the word but fails to do it is like a man who, as soon as he turns from the mirror in which he studied the face he was born with, can no longer recall the man he saw in it. But the one who keeps his eyes on the law of liberty, which is flawless, and stays with it, acting on what he hears rather than forgetting it, will find his doing of it blessed. Anyone among them (NU omits among you, by the NKJV note) who counts himself devout yet gives his tongue free rein is leading his own heart astray, and his religion is worth nothing. Visiting widows and orphans in their distress, and staying clear of the world's taint: that is the religion God and the Father counts clean and without blemish"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on James 2:2; NKJV notes, note on James 2:2; NKJV notes, note on James 2:3; NKJV notes, note on James 2:4; NKJV notes, note on James 2:9; NKJV notes, note on James 2:18; NKJV notes, note on James 2:18; NKJV notes, note on James 2:20; NKJV notes, note on James 2:22; NKJV notes, note on James 2:23; James 2:1–26","v":"His brethren, holding the faith of Jesus Christ, our Lord and the Lord of glory, were not to show partiality. Suppose a man came into their assembly wearing gold rings and fine (bright, by the NKJV note) clothes, and a poor man came in too, in filthy (vile, by the NKJV note) clothes, and they paid attention (Lit. look upon, by the NKJV note) to the finely dressed one, offering him a good seat, but told the poor man to stand over there or sit down by their footstool: in doing so they would have shown partiality (differentiated, by the NKJV note) among themselves, and set themselves up as judges whose reasoning was wicked. He bade his beloved brethren listen: those the world counts poor God had chosen to inherit the kingdom He pledged to people who love Him, and to have riches of faith. Yet they had shamed the poor man, though it was the wealthy who bore down hard on them, who dragged them before judges, and who spoke evil of the honorable name given them. To carry out the royal law of the Scripture, that each love his neighbor as himself, was to do well; but to show partiality (Lit. receive the face, by the NKJV note) was sin, and the law would find them guilty as transgressors. For to fail at one point while observing all the rest of the law is to be guilty of breaking the whole of it; the One who forbade adultery forbade murder too, so that one who avoids adultery yet murders has broken the law. As people whom the law of liberty is to judge, they were to govern both their words and their deeds, for those who showed no mercy will be judged without it, while judgment gives way before mercy. A man may say he has faith, yet if he lacks works, what is it worth, and can such faith bring him salvation? Take the case of a sister or a brother who has no clothes and lacks daily food, and someone among them wishes them well, bidding them keep warm, eat their fill and go in peace, without giving them what the body needs: what good is that? Faith left to itself, with no works, is likewise dead. Yet someone will object that one man has faith and another works; James bids such a one show his faith apart from his (NU omits your, by the NKJV note) works, and James for his part will let his (NU omits my, by the NKJV note) works display his faith. It is well to believe that God is one; the demons too believe that, and shudder. A foolish man should know that faith that has no works is dead (NU useless, by the NKJV note). It was by works that Abraham our father was justified, when he laid his son Isaac on the altar to offer him: his faith and his works were at work together, and his works made his faith perfect (complete, by the NKJV note); and so what the Scripture says came to pass: Abraham's belief in God was accounted (credited, by the NKJV note) as righteousness for him, and he was named God's friend. So works, not faith alone, justify a man. So too Rahab the harlot was justified by what she did, since she gave the messengers shelter and saw them away by a different road. Faith with no works is as dead as a body with no spirit"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on James 3:2; NKJV notes, note on James 3:3; NKJV notes, note on James 3:6; NKJV notes, note on James 3:6; NKJV notes, note on James 3:6; NKJV notes, note on James 3:9; NKJV notes, note on James 3:12; NKJV notes, note on James 3:14; James 3:1–18","v":"Not many of his brethren were to become teachers, since we who teach will be judged more strictly; and each of us goes wrong often. A man who makes no slip in what he says is perfect (mature, by the NKJV note), able to master the whole of himself as well. Indeed (NU Now if, by the NKJV note), we put bits into the mouths of horses to make them obey, and so guide all the rest of the animal; and ships, huge as they are and driven by strong winds, go wherever the helmsman means them to by a tiny rudder. So it is with the tongue: it is a small part of the body, yet its boasts are large. A small flame can set a vast woodland burning! Fire is what the tongue is, a world of iniquity (unrighteousness, by the NKJV note) among the parts of our body: it stains the body through and through, it kindles the course of nature (existence, by the NKJV note) into flame, and its own flame comes from hell (Gr. Gehenna, by the NKJV note). Mankind tames, and has tamed, creatures of every kind, sea creatures and reptiles, birds and beasts; but the tongue is beyond any man's taming: an evil that will not submit to rule, and laden with poison that kills. Our God and Father is blessed by it, and men are cursed by it, men made in God's similitude (likeness, by the NKJV note); blessing and cursing come out of one mouth, and his brethren were not to let it be so. A spring does not pour out both fresh and bitter water from one opening; olives do not grow on a fig tree, nor figs on a grapevine; so (NU Neither can a salty spring produce fresh water, by the NKJV note) no spring gives out fresh water and salt alike. Anyone among them who was wise and understanding was to let his good conduct show that wisdom's meekness marks his works. If self-seeking (selfish ambition, by the NKJV note) and bitter envy were in their hearts, they were not to boast, nor to deny the truth with lies. Such wisdom is not from above; it is demonic, sensual and earthly; for disorder and every kind of evil are found where self-seeking and envy are. The wisdom from above is gentle and yielding, rich in good fruits and in mercy, and free of hypocrisy and partiality; but first of all it is pure, and then it is peaceable; and those who make peace sow the fruit of righteousness in peace"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on James 4:1; NKJV notes, note on James 4:2; NKJV notes, note on James 4:2; NKJV notes, note on James 4:4; NKJV notes, note on James 4:12; NKJV notes, note on James 4:12; NKJV notes, note on James 4:12; NKJV notes, note on James 4:13; James 4:1–17","v":"The wars (battles, by the NKJV note) and fights among them came from the cravings for pleasure that are at war within them. Their desires went unmet; killing and envying won them nothing; they quarreled and made war (battle, by the NKJV note). Yet (NU, M omit Yet, by the NKJV note) the reason they lacked was that they did not ask; and when they did ask, nothing came, for their motive was wrong: they meant to use what they got on their own enjoyment. He called them adulterers (NU omits Adulterers and, by the NKJV note) and adulteresses: did they not know that to be friends with the world is to be at enmity with God? So anyone who wants the world's friendship makes himself God's enemy. Or did they suppose that the Scripture's word about the Spirit, whom we have living in us, that He longs jealously, is empty talk? But He gives grace all the more; and it is said that God sets Himself against the proud, while to the humble He gives grace. They were therefore to submit to God; as for the devil, they were to resist him, and he would take flight from them. They were to approach God, who would approach them in return; sinners were to wash their hands clean, and the double-minded to purify their hearts; they were to weep, mourn and lament, letting grief replace their laughter and heaviness their joy; and they were to bring themselves low before the Lord, who would then raise them high. They were not to run one another down, brethren; to run down a brother and pass judgment on him is to run down the law and pass judgment on it, and to pass judgment on the law is to stand over it as its judge rather than keep it. The Lawgiver is one (NU adds and Judge, by the NKJV note), who can save, and can destroy; who (NU, M But who, by the NKJV note) were they, then, to judge another (NU a neighbor, by the NKJV note)? Those who said that today or tomorrow they (M let us, by the NKJV note) were off to a certain town, where a year of trading would leave them richer, did not know what tomorrow would bring; for their life is a vapor, seen for a short time before it vanishes. They should instead be saying, 'If the Lord wills', and only then speak of living and doing one thing or another; but in fact their arrogance led them to boast, and every boast of that sort is evil. So one who knows the good he should do and fails to do it sins"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on James 5:2; NKJV notes, note on James 5:4; NKJV notes, note on James 5:5; NKJV notes, note on James 5:5; NKJV notes, note on James 5:5; NKJV notes, note on James 5:8; NKJV notes, note on James 5:9; NKJV notes, note on James 5:9; NKJV notes, note on James 5:12; NKJV notes, note on James 5:16; NKJV notes, note on James 5:16; NKJV notes, note on James 5:20; James 5:1–20","v":"The rich were to weep aloud and howl over the miseries on their way to them. Their wealth has spoiled (have rotted, by the NKJV note), moths have got into their clothing, and tarnish has eaten their silver and gold; that tarnish will bear witness against them and devour their flesh as fire would. And it is in the last days that they have stored up treasure. The pay they withheld by fraud from the workers who mowed their fields cries out, and the Lord of Sabaoth (Lit., in Heb., Hosts, by the NKJV note) has heard the reapers crying out. Their life on earth has been one of pleasure and luxury (indulgence, by the NKJV note), and they have made their hearts fat (Lit. nourished, by the NKJV note) as (NU omits as, by the NKJV note) though for a day of slaughter. The just they have condemned and put to death, though he offers them no resistance. So the brethren were to wait patiently for the Lord's coming, as a farmer waits on the earth's precious fruit, patient over it until both the early rain and the latter have fallen on it; they too were to be patient and make their hearts firm, for the Lord's coming is at hand (has drawn near, by the NKJV note). They were not to grumble (Lit. groan, by the NKJV note) at one another, so as not to be condemned (NU, M judged, by the NKJV note); the Judge stands even now at the door. They were to take as their pattern of patience in suffering the prophets, who had spoken in the Lord's name; those who endure are the ones we call blessed; they have heard how Job persevered, and have seen what the Lord had in view at the end, how deep His compassion and mercy are. Above all, they were not to swear by earth or by heaven or by any oath at all; their yes was to mean yes, and their no, no, so that they would not fall under judgment (M hypocrisy, by the NKJV note). Anyone among them suffering was to pray, and anyone cheerful to sing psalms. Anyone sick was to call the church's elders, who were to pray over him and anoint him in the Lord's name with oil; the sick man will be saved by the prayer of faith, and he will be raised up by the Lord, and any sins he has committed will be forgiven him. They were to confess (NU Therefore confess your sins, by the NKJV note) their trespasses to each other and pray each for the other, so as to be healed; a righteous man's prayer (supplication, by the NKJV note), made in earnest and with power, achieves a great deal. Elijah, a man of the same nature as ours, prayed earnestly that no rain would fall, and the land had none for three and a half years; then he prayed again, and rain came from the sky and the earth bore fruit once more. Should one of them stray from the truth and another bring him back, the one who did so could be sure of this: turning a sinner from the wrong road he was on rescues a soul (NU his soul, by the NKJV note) from dying and covers over a great many sins"},{"p":"designation","r":"James 1:1","v":"James, a bondservant of God"},{"p":"made by","r":"James 1:1","e":"PER-03374"},{"p":"occasion","r":"James 1:1","e":"EVT-03312"}],"nm":{"EVT-03312":"James writes to the twelve tribes","PER-03374":"James, who wrote to the twelve tribes"}},"OBJ-00679":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of Peter","d":"The letter Peter, Jesus Christ's apostle, wrote to the chosen pilgrims of the Dispersion in Bithynia, Asia, Galatia, Pontus and Cappadocia, of the hope that lives and the inheritance kept in heaven, of holy conduct, of Christ the living stone, of submitting for the Lord's sake, of suffering for doing good as Christ suffered, of the elders' care for the flock, and of standing firm in God's true grace (1 Peter 1:1–5:14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:6; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:17; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:20; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:22; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:22; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:23; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:23; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:23; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:24; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 1:25; 1 Peter 1:1–25","v":"Peter, writing as Jesus Christ's apostle, sent greetings to the chosen of the Dispersion who lived as pilgrims (sojourners, temporary residents, by the NKJV note) in Bithynia, Asia, Galatia, Pontus and Cappadocia, and asked that grace and peace abound to them. They had been chosen in keeping with what God the Father foreknew, and it was by the Spirit that they were set apart, for obedience and for sprinkling with Jesus Christ's blood. Peter blessed God, our Lord Jesus Christ's Father, whose great mercy, through Jesus Christ's rising from death, has brought us to a second birth: we now have a hope that lives, and they an inheritance that heaven keeps for them, unfading, unstained and incorruptible (imperishable, by the NKJV note). Through faith they are under the guard of God's power, kept for a salvation already prepared, whose revealing awaits the last time. They greatly rejoice in this, though trials of many kinds, if need be, have for a little while now grieved (distressed, by the NKJV note) them, so that the genuineness of their faith, worth far more than gold, which perishes though fire tests it, may be found, at Jesus Christ's revelation, to result in glory, praise and honor. They love Him without ever having seen (M known, by the NKJV note) Him; and their faith in Him, though He is out of their sight now, fills them with a gladness too great for words, full of glory, and the salvation of their souls is the end their faith is now receiving. The grace meant for them was foretold by prophets who studied and searched out this salvation with care. Christ's Spirit was in those prophets, bearing witness beforehand to Christ's sufferings and the glories coming after them, and they sought to learn what time He meant, or what kind of time. It was revealed to them that their service was for us (NU, M you, by the NKJV note), not for themselves, in the things now announced to them by those who brought them the gospel by the Holy Spirit, whom heaven sent; and angels long to look into these things. When Jesus Christ is revealed, grace will be brought to them, and on that grace they were to set their whole hope, their minds girded for action and sober. Scripture has it written that, because He is holy, they are to be holy; so, since the One who called them is holy, they too were to be holy in every part of their conduct, no longer letting the cravings they had in their old ignorance mold them, but living as children who obey. If they call on the Father, the One who judges each one's work without favoring anyone, then fear was to mark how they lived for the whole time of their stay (sojourning, dwelling as resident aliens, by the NKJV note), since they knew the price of their redemption from the aimless way of life their fathers handed down: Christ's precious blood, like that of a lamb with no blemish or spot, and no corruptible (perishable, by the NKJV note) thing, silver or gold. These last times have seen Christ made manifest (revealed, by the NKJV note) for their sake, though He was foreordained before the world was founded; it is through Him that they believe in God, by whom He was raised from the dead and given glory, so their faith and hope rest in God. Through the Spirit (NU omits through the Spirit, by the NKJV note), their obedience to the truth had purified their souls for a love of the brethren that is sincere (Lit. unhypocritical, by the NKJV note); so with a pure heart they were to love one another fervently. Their new birth came through God's word, living and remaining forever (NU omits forever, by the NKJV note), and from a seed that is incorruptible (imperishable, by the NKJV note), not corruptible (perishable, by the NKJV note); for all flesh is like grass, and man's glory, all of it (NU its glory as, by the NKJV note), like the grass's flower; the grass dries up and its flower drops off, but the LORD's word (spoken word, by the NKJV note) lasts for all time. And this is the word that was preached to them by the gospel"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:13; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:16; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:21; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:21; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:24; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 2:25; 1 Peter 2:1–25","v":"To grow by it (NU adds up to salvation, by the NKJV note), they were to crave the word's pure milk as newborn babies crave milk, if they have truly tasted the Lord's kindness; and they were to put away all evil speaking, envy, hypocrisy, deceit and every kind of malice. The stone they come to is living, rejected by men but precious, and chosen by God; and of them too, as living stones, a spiritual house is being built, so that, as a holy priesthood, they may offer God spiritual sacrifices that He accepts through Jesus Christ. So Scripture also holds these words: a chief cornerstone, chosen and precious, is laid in Zion, and no one believing on Him will be put to shame. To believers, then, He is precious; for the disobedient (NU disbelieve, by the NKJV note), the stone rejected by the builders is the one that has become chief cornerstone, a rock that offends and a stone they stumble on: they stumble, disobeying the word, and to that they were also appointed. They are His own special people, a royal priesthood, a chosen generation and a holy nation, so that the praises of the One who called them into His marvelous light out of darkness may be proclaimed by them. Now they are God's people, who once were no people at all; mercy, once not theirs, they have now obtained. Peter's plea to them, the beloved, as sojourners and pilgrims, was to abstain from fleshly lusts, since these war against the soul. Their conduct among the Gentiles was to be honorable, so that, when they are spoken against by the Gentiles as evildoers, the Gentiles may see their good works and on the day of visitation give God glory. Every ordinance (institution, by the NKJV note) of man was to have their submission, for the Lord's sake: the king's, as supreme, and that of governors, whom the king sends to praise those who do good and punish evildoers; for God's will is that by doing good they may silence foolish men's ignorance. Free as they are, they were to live as God's bondservants, not making their liberty a cloak for vice (wickedness, by the NKJV note); they were to fear God, to love the brotherhood, to honor the king and to honor everyone. Servants were to submit in all fear to their masters, the harsh as well as the gentle and good; for it is commendable when someone, mindful of God, bears pain and suffers unjustly. To be beaten for one's own faults and endure it earns no credit; but when they suffer for doing good and bear it patiently, such patience is commendable before God. They were called to this, because Christ too suffered for us (NU you, by the NKJV note), and in doing so left an example to us (NU, M you, by the NKJV note), so that they would walk in His steps. He committed no sin, and His mouth was found free of deceit. Reviled, He gave back no reviling; suffering, He made no threats, entrusting Himself instead to the One whose judging is righteous. His purpose in bearing our sins Himself, on the tree, in His own body, was that we might live for righteousness, having died to sins; His stripes (wounds, by the NKJV note) brought them healing. It is to their souls' Shepherd and Overseer (Gr. Episkopos, by the NKJV note) that they have now returned, after going astray like sheep"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:10; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:15; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:15; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 3:20; 1 Peter 3:1–22","v":"Wives, likewise, were to submit to their own husbands, with this aim: should some of the husbands disobey the word, they may be won over, without a word, by their wives' conduct, as they observe that it is chaste and joined with fear. Their beauty was to be that of the heart's hidden person, a quiet and gentle spirit, whose beauty is incorruptible (imperishable, by the NKJV note) and of great worth in God's sight, not merely the outward show of fine clothes, gold worn, or hair arranged. Sarah, calling Abraham lord, obeyed him; and in the same manner the holy women of former times who trusted in God made submission to their own husbands their adornment; and the wives are Sarah's daughters provided they do good and no terror frightens them. Likewise the husbands were to live with understanding alongside their wives, honoring the wife as to a vessel that is weaker, and as fellow heirs with them of life's grace, so that nothing may hinder the husbands' prayers. Last of all, they were all to be courteous (NU humble, by the NKJV note), tenderhearted, loving as brothers, compassionate toward one another and of one mind; they were not to repay evil with evil, nor reviling with reviling; on the contrary, they were to bless, aware that their calling was to this, with the aim that a blessing may be theirs to inherit. For a man who would see good days and love life, the commands are these: he is to keep his tongue back (restrain, by the NKJV note) from evil and deceit from his lips; he is to shun evil and do good; he is to seek peace and follow after it; for the LORD's eyes are on the righteous, whose prayers He listens to, but He sets His face against evildoers. If what is good is what they follow, who is there to harm them? They are blessed even if suffering comes to them for righteousness' sake. The threats made against them were not to frighten or trouble them; instead, the Lord God (NU Christ as Lord, by the NKJV note) was to be sanctified (set apart, by the NKJV note) in their hearts. Anyone who asks them the reason for the hope in them was to receive a defense from them, given with fear and meekness, and for this they were to be ready always. A good conscience was to be theirs, so that, when they are defamed as evildoers, shame may fall on the revilers of their good conduct in Christ. If God wills that they suffer, doing good is a better cause for it than doing evil. Christ too suffered for sins, once, a just One for the unjust, with this aim: to bring us (NU, M you, by the NKJV note) to God. In the flesh He was put to death, but by the Spirit made alive, and by the Spirit He also went to the imprisoned spirits and preached to them. Their disobedience was long ago, in Noah's days, when the Divine longsuffering waited (NU, M when the longsuffering of God waited patiently, by the NKJV note) as Noah's ark was being made ready; only a few, eight souls, were saved in it through water. Baptism now saves us too, an antitype, through Jesus Christ's resurrection: not a removal of the flesh's filth, but a good conscience's answer toward God. He is at God's right hand, having gone into heaven, and has powers, angels and authorities made subject to Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:11; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:14; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:14; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:15; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 4:16; 1 Peter 4:1–19","v":"Since Christ's suffering for us (NU omits for us, by the NKJV note) was in the flesh, they too were to arm themselves with the same mind: one has ceased from sin when he has borne suffering in the flesh, so that what time in the flesh remains to him may be lived no longer for men's lusts but for God's will. Our past lifetime (NU time, by the NKJV note) has gone long enough in doing what the Gentiles will, when our walk was in drunkenness, abominable idolatries, lewdness, drinking parties, lusts and revelries; the Gentiles find it strange that those to whom Peter wrote do not join them in running into that flood of dissipation, and speak evil of those to whom he wrote. The Gentiles will answer for it to the One ready to judge the dead as well as the living. That is the reason the dead too had the gospel preached to them: so that they might undergo judgment in the flesh according to men, but live in the spirit according to God. The end of everything has drawn near, so in their prayers they were to be watchful and serious. Since a multitude of sins will be covered by love, their love for one another was, above all things, to be fervent; and they were to welcome one another as guests without grumbling. God's grace takes many forms, and of it each was to be a good steward, putting the gift he has received to the service of the others: if anyone speaks, he was to speak as God's oracles (utterances, by the NKJV note); if anyone ministers, his ministering was to be as one using the ability God supplies; so that through Jesus Christ God may be glorified in everything. The dominion (sovereignty, by the NKJV note) and the glory belong to Him forever and ever. Amen. The fiery trial coming to try them was nothing strange, and the beloved were not to think it so, as though something foreign were happening to them; rather, their rejoicing was to match their share in Christ's sufferings, so that at the revealing of His glory they too may be glad with a joy that exceeds. Because God's Spirit, the Spirit of glory, rests on them, they are blessed if reproached (insulted or reviled, by the NKJV note) for Christ's name; and (NU omits the rest of v. 14, by the NKJV note) He whom others blaspheme is glorified on the part of those to whom Peter wrote. None of them was to suffer as a thief, a busybody (meddler, by the NKJV note) in others' affairs, a murderer or an evildoer; but if one of them suffers as a Christian, he was not to be ashamed, but in this matter (NU name, by the NKJV note) to glorify God. The time has come: God's house is where judgment begins. Those who disobey God's gospel, then, what will their end be, if it is with us that judgment first begins? And the sinner and the ungodly, where will they appear, if it is with difficulty that the righteous one is saved? So those who suffer by God's will were to entrust their souls to Him, a faithful Creator, while doing good"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:2; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:8; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:10; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:10; NKJV notes, note on 1 Peter 5:10; 1 Peter 5:1–14","v":"As a fellow elder himself, one who witnessed Christ's sufferings and will have a share in the glory yet to be revealed, Peter urged the elders among them to tend as shepherds God's flock in their care, watching over it as overseers: eagerly, not for shameful profit; willingly (NU adds according to God, by the NKJV note), not because they must; and they were to be the flock's examples, and not lords (masters, by the NKJV note) over the ones put in their charge. Glory's crown, which never fades, will be theirs at the Chief Shepherd's appearing. Likewise, it was to their elders that the younger were to submit; and all of them, each submitting to the other, were to put on humility as clothing, since the proud find God opposed to them, while to the humble He gives grace. God's hand is mighty, and under it they were to humble themselves, so that in due time He may raise them up; all their care they were to cast on Him, He being One who cares for them. They were to be sober (self-controlled, by the NKJV note) and vigilant (watchful, by the NKJV note), because (NU, M omit because, by the NKJV note) the devil, their adversary, goes about in the manner of a roaring lion, looking for someone to devour; firm in the faith, they were to resist him, aware that their brotherhood in the world is going through those same sufferings. Peter's wish (NU the God of all grace, by the NKJV note) was that once they have suffered a while, He who is God of all grace would settle, strengthen, establish and perfect (NU will perfect, by the NKJV note) them; it was He who called us (NU, M you, by the NKJV note), through Christ Jesus, to His eternal glory. May glory and dominion be His forever and ever. Amen. It was by Silvanus, a brother faithful in Peter's view, that he wrote them this short letter, exhorting them and testifying that God's true grace is this, in which they stand. Greetings come to them from her in Babylon, chosen as they were, and from Mark, Peter's son as he calls him. They were to greet each other with love's kiss. To all of them that are in Christ Jesus, Peter wished peace. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 Peter 1:1","v":"Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"1 Peter 1:1","e":"PER-03015"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 Peter 1:1","e":"EVT-03313"},{"p":"purpose","r":"1 Peter 5:12","v":"Peter wrote it briefly, exhorting them and testifying that God's true grace is this, in which they stand"}],"nm":{"EVT-03313":"Peter writes to the pilgrims of the Dispersion","PER-03015":"Simon"}},"OBJ-00680":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of Peter","d":"The letter Simon Peter, Jesus Christ's bondservant and apostle, wrote to those who share a faith as precious as that of Peter and those with him, of the virtues to add to faith, of his witness of Christ's majesty, of the prophetic word, of the false teachers and their judgment, of the scoffers, and of the day of the Lord (2 Peter 1:1–3:18)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:6; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:8; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:13; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:14; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:15; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:19; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:20; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 1:21; 2 Peter 1:1–21","v":"Simon Peter, Jesus Christ's bondservant and apostle, greeted those who have obtained (received, by the NKJV note) a faith of like (faith of the same value, by the NKJV note) preciousness to ours by the righteousness of Jesus Christ, our God and Savior, and asked that they have grace and peace in growing measure as they come to know God and Jesus our Lord; for through our knowing the One who called us by virtue and glory, everything that life and godliness need has been supplied to us by His divine power; by these, too, promises precious and exceedingly great have been granted us, so that, the world's corruption (depravity, by the NKJV note) that comes through lust now escaped, they might through them come to share in the divine nature. For this very reason, with every effort, they were to add to their faith seven things, which the NKJV links one to the next in a chain running from virtue to love; in alphabetical order they are brotherly kindness, godliness, knowledge, love, perseverance (patience, by the NKJV note), self-control and virtue. If they have these in abundance, their knowing our Lord Jesus Christ will not leave them unfruitful or barren (useless, by the NKJV note); without them a man is shortsighted to the point of blindness, and the cleansing of his old sins he has forgotten. Their call and election the brethren were to make sure, with still greater diligence; they will never stumble, if they do these things; they will be given a rich entrance into the everlasting kingdom that belongs to Jesus Christ, our Lord and Savior. They already know these things, and the present truth has them established; even so, because of this, Peter will not be negligent in reminding them of these things always. Stirring them up with reminders is right, he thinks, for the whole time he is in this tent (Body, by the NKJV note), since he knows that he must soon put off his tent (Die and leave this body, by the NKJV note), as Jesus Christ our Lord has shown him; and he will take care that, after his decease (Lit. exodus, departure, by the NKJV note), they have, at all times, a reminder of them. Cunningly devised fables were not what we followed in making known to them our Lord Jesus Christ's power and coming; we had seen His majesty as eyewitnesses. Honor and glory were given Him by God the Father at the time a voice from the Excellent Glory reached Him, naming Him God's beloved Son, in whom He is well pleased; we ourselves heard that voice come from heaven, being in His company on the holy mountain. The prophetic word stands confirmed for us (Or We also have the more sure prophetic word, by the NKJV note); in a dark place it shines as a light, and they do well to give it heed till day breaks and in their hearts the morning star rises, knowing this before all else: no prophecy in Scripture arises from private interpretation (Or origin, by the NKJV note): man's will never brought prophecy about; rather, the Holy Spirit moving them, God's holy men (NU men spoke from God, by the NKJV note) spoke"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:4; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:10; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:13; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:13; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:14; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:17; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:17; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:18; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:19; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 2:19; 2 Peter 2:1–22","v":"False prophets arose among the people, and in the same way false teachers will be among those to whom Peter wrote; destructive heresies are what they will secretly bring in; they will go so far as to deny the Lord, the One who bought them; swift destruction is what they will bring on themselves. Many will take up their destructive ways, and on their account blasphemy will fall on the way of truth; covetous, they will use deceptive words to exploit them; but as for their destruction, it does not (M will not, by the NKJV note) slumber, and their judgment, long at work, has not been idle. The angels who sinned were not spared: God sent them down to hell (Lit. Tartarus, by the NKJV note); they are reserved for judgment in chains of darkness. When He brought the flood upon the ungodly world, the ancient world too went unspared; but Noah, a preacher of righteousness, He saved, with seven others. Sodom and Gomorrah, the two cities, He condemned to destruction, reducing them to ashes; they stand as an example for any who would live ungodly in later times. He also delivered Lot, a righteous man oppressed by the wicked and their filthy conduct; living among them, day after day Lot tormented his own righteous soul as he saw their lawless deeds and heard them. The Lord, then, is One who knows how to hold the unjust under punishment until judgment day, and how to rescue the godly from temptations; this holds most of all for those who despise authority and whose walk follows the flesh in unclean lust. Presumptuous and self-willed, they have no fear of speaking evil of dignitaries (glorious ones, lit. glories, by the NKJV note); before the Lord, angels make no reviling accusation against them, greater though they are in power and might. Like brute beasts, creatures of nature made for capture and destruction, these men speak evil, and what they speak evil of is beyond their understanding; in their own corruption they will utterly perish, and the wages of unrighteousness will come to them. Pleasure, to them, is carousing (revel, by the NKJV note) in the daytime; spots and blemishes at the feasts they share with them, they carouse (reveling, by the NKJV note) amid deceptions of their own. Adultery (Lit. an adulteress, by the NKJV note) fills their eyes, eyes unable to cease from sin; they entice souls that are unstable; covetous practices are what their heart has been trained in; and they are accursed children. The way they follow is Balaam's, Beor's son, whose love was for the wages of unrighteousness; they have gone astray, forsaking the right way. A donkey without speech rebuked Balaam for his iniquity, speaking in a man's voice and restraining the prophet's madness. Wells without water, clouds (NU and mists, by the NKJV note) a tempest carries: that is what these men are, and the blackness of darkness is reserved for them forever (NU omits forever, by the NKJV note). Speaking words of emptiness, great and swelling, they allure, through the flesh's lusts and through lewdness, those who have in fact escaped (NU are barely escaping, by the NKJV note) from those living in error. Slaves of corruption (depravity, by the NKJV note) themselves, they promise liberty to those they allure; for a person is brought into bondage (slavery, by the NKJV note) by the one who overcomes him. For if some, after escaping the world's pollutions by knowing Jesus Christ, the Lord and Savior, are overcome, entangled in them again, their latter end is worse than their beginning. For them, a better lot than knowing the way of righteousness and then turning from the holy commandment that was handed down to them would have been never to know it at all. In them the true proverb is fulfilled, the one about a washed sow returning to wallow in the mire and a dog going back to its own vomit"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 3:2; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 3:7; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 3:9; NKJV notes, note on 2 Peter 3:10; 2 Peter 3:1–18","v":"Beloved, this is now the second letter Peter writes to them; in both he stirs up their pure minds with reminders, so that they may be mindful both of what the holy prophets spoke before and of the commandment that came from us (NU, M the apostles of your Lord and Savior or your apostles of the Lord and Savior, by the NKJV note), the Lord and Savior's apostles, knowing this first: the last days will bring scoffers, who walk by their own lusts and, asking where His promised coming is, claim that all things have continued unchanged from creation's beginning since the time the fathers fell asleep. What they willfully forget is that long ago the heavens existed by God's word, and the earth stood both in the water and out of it; through these the world then existing was flooded with water and perished. And God's word keeps the present heavens and earth preserved, held in store for fire against the day that brings judgment and ungodly men's perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note). This one thing the beloved were not to forget: to the Lord, one day and a thousand years are each as the other. Some count it slackness, but in the Lord there is no slackness as to His promise; He is longsuffering toward us (NU you, by the NKJV note): it is repentance He wills for all, and for none to perish. The Lord's day will come the way a thief comes at night, bringing a great noise as the heavens pass away; the elements will be melted by fervent heat, and the earth will be burned up (NU laid bare, lit. found, by the NKJV note), and so will the works in it. Since all these things are to be dissolved, they ought to be persons marked by holy conduct and godliness, as they look for the day of God and hasten its coming; because of that day the heavens, on fire, will be dissolved, and the elements melted by fervent heat. But what we look for, as He promised, is a new earth and new heavens, where righteousness dwells. Since these things are what the beloved look forward to, they were to be diligent, so that they may be found by Him at peace, blameless and without spot. They were to regard our Lord's longsuffering as salvation, as Paul, our beloved brother, also wrote to them by the wisdom given him. That is how he writes in all his epistles when these things come up in them, and some things in them are hard to understand; these, people untaught and unstable twist, as they do the other Scriptures, to their own destruction. Since they know this beforehand, the beloved were to take care that they too do not fall from their own steadfastness by being swept away by the wicked's error; instead they were to grow in the grace of Jesus Christ, our Lord and Savior, and in knowledge of Him. May the glory be His, now and forever. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 Peter 1:1","v":"Simon Peter, a bondservant and apostle"},{"p":"feature","r":"2 Peter 3:1","v":"It is Peter's second letter to its readers"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 Peter 1:1","e":"PER-03015"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 Peter 1:1","e":"EVT-03314"},{"p":"purpose","r":"2 Peter 1:12–15","v":"Peter will keep reminding them of these things while he is in this tent, and will see to it that after his departure they have, at all times, a reminder of them"},{"p":"purpose","r":"2 Peter 3:1–2","v":"In this letter, as in the first, Peter stirs up their pure minds with reminders, so that they may be mindful both of what the holy prophets spoke before and of the apostles' commandment"}],"nm":{"EVT-03314":"Peter writes his second letter","PER-03015":"Simon"}},"OBJ-00681":{"k":"object","n":"The First Epistle of John","d":"The letter of a writer whom the text does not name, of the Word of life that its writer, with those who were with him, touched, saw and heard, of God who is light and is love, of keeping His commandments and loving one another, of the antichrists and the testing of the spirits, and of God's witness to His Son, written so that those whose faith rests on God's Son may know that eternal life is theirs (1 John 1:1–5:21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 1:4; 1 John 1:1–10","v":"The Word of life, there from the beginning, is what the letter's writer, with those who were with him, proclaims: their hands touched it, their eyes saw it and gazed on it, and their ears heard it. That life came into view; having seen it, the writer and those with him bear witness and proclaim to the hearers the life eternal: it was with the Father; in their sight it was made visible. They proclaim what reached their eyes and ears with one aim, that the hearers may be partners in their fellowship; and that fellowship of theirs is in truth with the Father, and also with Jesus Christ, His Son. They write so that the hearers' (NU, M our, by the NKJV note) joy may be complete. A message heard from Him is what the writer and those with him now pass on to the hearers: God is light, and no darkness whatever is found in Him. It is a lie, and the truth goes unpracticed, when we walk in darkness while claiming fellowship with Him. Fellowship with each other, and a cleansing from every sin by His Son Jesus Christ's blood, are ours when our walk is in the light, where He Himself is. A claim to be free of sin is self-deception, and the truth then has no place in us. If we make confession of our sins, they are pardoned by Him, faithful and just as He is, and we are made clean of every unrighteousness. If we deny ever having sinned, His word has no place in us, and we cast Him as a liar"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:5; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:20; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:26; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:27; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:28; 1 John 2:1–29","v":"His aim in writing to his little children is that they not sin; but if anyone does, Jesus Christ the righteous pleads our cause with the Father as our Advocate, and the propitiation for the sins of the whole world, not ours alone, is He Himself. By keeping His commandments we are sure that we know Him. The truth has no place in a man who says he knows Him yet ignores His commandments; such a man lies. But God's love has truly been made perfect (has been completed, by the NKJV note) in anyone who keeps His word; that is our assurance of being in Him. A claim to abide in Him carries a duty: to walk in the way He walked. Since the true light already shines and the darkness is passing, the commandment he writes them is new, true in them and in Him; and yet what he writes to the brethren (NU Beloved, by the NKJV note) is no new commandment but an old one, the message they heard at the beginning (NU omits from the beginning, by the NKJV note), theirs since the start. Darkness, not light, is still where a man is if he claims the light but hates his brother; the darkness has blinded that man's eyes, so that he walks in it with no idea where he is heading. Abiding in the light belongs to the one who loves his brother, and nothing in him gives cause for stumbling. To the young men and to the fathers he writes, and has written: to the young men, since they are strong, God's word remaining in them, and the wicked one has been overcome by them; to the fathers, since they have known the One who has been from the beginning. To the little children he writes because the Father is known to them, and for His name's sake their sins have been forgiven. They are not to love the world or anything in it; the Father's love is absent from anyone who loves the world. For everything in the world comes not from the Father but from the world: the pride of life, the flesh's lust and the eyes' lust. The one who does God's will remains forever; the world, by contrast, and its lust with it, are passing away. The hour is the last, the little children are told: as they had heard, the (NU omits the, by the NKJV note) Antichrist is on his way, and now many antichrists have already appeared, from which we know the hour to be the last. Those who went out from among us did not belong to us; had they belonged, they would have remained in our company. Their going out was to make it plain that none of them was ours. From the Holy One the hearers have an anointing, and all things are known to them (NU you all know, by the NKJV note); it is their knowing the truth, not any ignorance of it, that has made him write to them, and their knowing that no lie comes from the truth. Anyone who says Jesus is not the Christ is the liar, and anyone who denies the Son and the Father is antichrist. Denial of the Son leaves a man without the Father as well; to acknowledge the Son is to have the Father too. The message that reached their ears at the start, then, is to remain with them; and if it does remain, the Son and the Father alike will be where they in turn abide. Eternal life is what He has promised us. He has written these things about those who are trying to deceive (lead you astray, by the NKJV note) them. The anointing they received from Him remains in them, and they have no need of a teacher: that same anointing instructs them in all things, and it is true, no lie; and they will (NU omits will, by the NKJV note) abide in Him, in keeping with its teaching. So that we may be confident, when (NU if, by the NKJV note) He appears, and not shrink from Him in shame at His coming, the little children are now to abide in Him. They know He is righteous, and so they know that anyone who does what is right has been born of Him"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:1; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:14; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:19; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:19; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 3:23; 1 John 3:1–24","v":"That we are called God's children (NU adds And we are., by the NKJV note) shows how great is the love the Father has given us. Since the world did not know Him, it does not know us (M you, by the NKJV note) either. God's children is what we are already, beloved, though what we will be is still to be revealed; but because we will see Him, and see Him as He truly is, we know we will be like Him when He is revealed. All who rest this hope on Him make themselves pure, to match the purity that is His. Sin is lawlessness, and a sinner is also a doer of lawlessness. Sinless Himself, He was revealed, as they know, to remove our sins. No one who abides in Him sins; as for the one who sins, he has not seen Him, nor has he known Him. The little children are not to be led astray by anyone: righteous, as He is righteous, is the man who practices righteousness; a man who sins belongs to the devil, whose sinning goes back to the start; and God's Son was revealed with this aim, to undo the devil's works. One born of God, with God's seed abiding within him, does not sin; and sin is impossible for him, his birth being from God. By this God's children are told apart from the devil's: not of God is the man who fails to practice righteousness, nor is the man without love for his brother. Loving one another is the message that reached them from the beginning; we are not to be like Cain, who killed his brother, being himself of the wicked one. His brother's works were righteous, while his own were evil, and for that he killed him. The world's hatred, should it come, is to cause the brethren no surprise. Our love for the brethren tells us that we have left death behind and passed into life; a man without love for his brother (NU omits his brother, by the NKJV note) remains in death. A murderer: such is anyone who hates his brother; and eternal life, as they know, remains in no murderer. What love is we know from His giving His life for us; it is our duty in turn to give up our lives on the brethren's behalf. When a man who has this world's goods closes his heart to a brother he sees in need, the letter asks how God's love can abide in him. Deeds and truth, rather than word or tongue, are to carry the little children's love. That we belong to the truth we know (NU shall know, by the NKJV note) by such love, and by it, in His presence, our hearts will be assured (persuade, set at rest, by the NKJV note): God, who knows all things, is the greater, even should our heart condemn us. And when we stand uncondemned by our heart, beloved, we can face God with confidence; every request we make is granted us by Him, since we are keepers of His commandments and doers of what He finds pleasing. Faith in His Son Jesus Christ's name, and mutual love, as He commanded us (M omits us, by the NKJV note): these make up His commandment. Abiding in Him is the lot of one who keeps His commandments, and He in turn abides in that one; it is through the Spirit He gave us that we know of His abiding in us"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 4:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 4:3; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 4:19; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 4:20; 1 John 4:1–21","v":"Since the world has many false prophets out in it now, the beloved are to put the spirits to the test, to see whether they come from God, rather than believe every spirit. Here is how God's Spirit is recognized: from God is each spirit that acknowledges Jesus Christ's coming in the flesh; and not from God is a spirit that does not acknowledge (NU omits that, by the NKJV note) Jesus Christ's coming in the flesh (NU omits Christ has come in the flesh, by the NKJV note); already in the world now is that spirit, the Antichrist's, whose coming they had heard of. The little children belong to God, and they have overcome those spirits and false prophets, for the one in the world is less than the One in them. The world listens to those others, who belong to it and speak in the world's way. We are of God; anyone not of God gives us no hearing, while one who knows God listens to us; that is how the spirit of error is told from the spirit of truth. Love comes from God; so the beloved are to love one another. Birth from God and knowledge of Him belong to each one who loves; God being love, a man without love does not know Him. So that we might have life through Him, God sent into the world His only begotten Son; in this His love toward us was made plain. Love, in its true sense, lies not in our having loved God; it lies in His love for us, and in His sending His Son as propitiation for our sins. Since God's love for us was such, the beloved in turn ought to show love to one another. God is One whom no one has ever seen; if love for one another is ours, His love has been made perfect in us, and God abides in us. Our abiding in Him, and His in us, is known to us from the share of His Spirit He has given us. As eyewitnesses we testify that the world's Savior is the Son whom the Father sent. For anyone who confesses Jesus to be God's Son, the abiding is mutual, his in God and God's in that man. That God loves us is something we have come to know and to believe. Love is what God is; and God is in the one who abides in love, and that one is in God. Love is brought to its perfection among us with this end, that on judgment day we may stand with boldness; for we, in this world, are as He is. Love has no room for fear; since fear brings torment, perfect love drives it out, and anyone afraid falls short as yet of being perfected in love. He loved us first, and so we love Him (NU omits Him, by the NKJV note). If a man says he loves God while hating his brother, he lies; for if he has no love for the brother before his eyes, how can (NU he cannot, by the NKJV note) his love reach God, unseen by him? Love for one's brother is to go with love for God: that is the commandment He gave us"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:4; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:7; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:9; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:12; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:13; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:18; NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:18; 1 John 5:1–21","v":"Everyone who believes Jesus to be the Christ owes his birth to God; and love for the One who begot carries with it love for the one He begot. Our love for God's children is known to us when we keep God's commandments and love Him; indeed keeping His commandments is what loving God means, and they weigh on no one. Our (M your, by the NKJV note) faith is the victory: by it the world has been conquered, as it is by everything born of God; the world's conqueror is none other than the one who believes Jesus to be the Son of God. Jesus Christ came through water and blood; not water only, but water and blood together. The Spirit, who is truth, is the One bearing witness. The Holy Spirit, the Word and the Father are three witnesses in heaven (NU, M omit the words from in heaven in 5:7 through on earth in 5:8, and only four or five very late manuscripts contain them in Greek, by the NKJV note), and they are one; the blood, the water and the Spirit are three witnesses on earth, and they agree as one. As for men's witness, if we accept it, God's is greater; for God's (NU God, that, by the NKJV note) witness lies in His testimony concerning His Son. The witness lies within anyone who believes in God's Son. To make God a liar is what one does who does not believe Him, for he has not believed God's testimony about His Son. Eternal life, given us by God and found in His Son, is what the testimony declares. Life (Or the life, by the NKJV note) belongs to the one who has the Son; without the Son of God, a man is without life. So that those whose faith rests on the Son of God's name may be sure that eternal life is theirs, and (NU omits the rest of v. 13, by the NKJV note) may go on believing in that name, the writer has written these things. Our confidence in Him rests on His hearing any request we make in keeping with His will; and if we are sure of His hearing us in whatever we ask, the requests we made of Him, we know, are ours. One who sees his own brother committing a sin not of the kind that leads to death will make request for him, and that brother will be given life by Him; so it is for all whose sin is not of that kind. Some sin does lead to death, and the writer does not bid him pray about that sin. Unrighteousness of every kind is sin, yet not all sin leads to death. No one born of God sins, as we know. The one God has begotten keeps (guards, by the NKJV note) himself (NU him, by the NKJV note), and he is untouched by the wicked one. The whole world, we know, lies in the wicked one's power, while we belong to God. God's Son has come, as we know, and given us understanding, so that the True One may be known to us; and we are in the True One, in Jesus Christ, His Son. This One is both eternal life and the true God. The little children are to guard themselves against idols. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"1 John 1:1","v":"That which was from the beginning"},{"p":"feature","r":"1 John 1:1–4","v":"Its writer speaks as 'we', as one of those whose hands touched the Word of life and whose eyes and ears saw and heard it, and he writes so that his hearers may share the fellowship that he and those with him have"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 2:26; 1 John 2:12–14, 21, 26","v":"Its writer says he writes, and has written, to the young men and the fathers, and writes to the little children, giving each a reason; that it is their knowing the truth, not any ignorance of it, that has made him write; and that he has written about those who are trying to deceive (lead you astray, by the NKJV note) them"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:7; 1 John 5:7–8","v":"Its passage running from in heaven (5:7) to on earth (5:8) is lacking in the NU and M texts, and in Greek it is found in no more than four or five manuscripts, all of them very late","b":"NKJV note"},{"p":"occasion","r":"1 John 1:4; 5:13","e":"EVT-03315"},{"p":"purpose","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 1:4; 1 John 1:4","v":"That the hearers' (NU, M our, by the NKJV note) joy may be complete"},{"p":"purpose","r":"1 John 2:1","v":"That its hearers may keep from sinning"},{"p":"purpose","r":"NKJV notes, note on 1 John 5:13; 1 John 5:13","v":"Assurance of eternal life for those whose faith rests on the Son of God's name, and (NU omits the rest of v. 13, by the NKJV note) their going on believing in that name"}],"nm":{"EVT-03315":"The First Epistle of John is written"}},"OBJ-00682":{"k":"object","n":"The Second Epistle of John","d":"The Elder's letter to the elect lady, with her children, of living in truth and loving each other, of the deceivers who do not acknowledge Jesus Christ's coming in the flesh, gone abroad in the world, and of giving no welcome to one who comes without Christ's doctrine (2 John 1:1–13)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 2 John 1:1; NKJV notes, note on 2 John 1:3; NKJV notes, note on 2 John 1:8; NKJV notes, note on 2 John 1:9; 2 John 1:1–13","v":"The Elder writes to her children and to the elect (chosen, by the NKJV note) lady herself. He loves them in truth, and not he alone: all who know the truth love them too, for the truth's sake, the truth that dwells in us and is to remain ours for ever. With you (NU, M us, by the NKJV note) will be peace, mercy and grace, in truth and love, from God the Father, together with the Father's Son, the Lord Jesus Christ. Great was the Elder's joy at finding certain of her children living in truth, which is what the Father commanded us. His plea to the lady now was for love of one another; in this he was writing her no new commandment, only the one we have held since the start. Love means a life lived by His commandments; and the commandment, heard by them from the start, is that they live in it. Many deceivers, men who do not acknowledge Jesus Christ's coming in the flesh, have gone abroad in the world; any such man is an antichrist and a deceiver. They were to keep watch over themselves, so that we (NU you, by the NKJV note) might not lose the fruit of our work, but be given a full reward. Without God is anyone who transgresses (NU goes ahead, by the NKJV note) and fails to remain in Christ's doctrine; one who remains in it has the Son and the Father both. Since to greet such a man is to take part in his evil works, anyone who comes to them without Christ's doctrine is to receive from them neither a greeting nor a welcome into their house. To make our joy complete, the Elder hoped to come and talk with them face to face, rather than set down in ink on paper the much he had to write. Her elect sister's children sent her greetings. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"2 John 1:1","v":"The Elder, To the elect lady"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 John 1:1","e":"PER-03376"},{"p":"occasion","r":"2 John 1:1, 12","e":"EVT-03316"}],"nm":{"EVT-03316":"The Elder writes to the elect lady","PER-03376":"The Elder, who wrote to the elect lady"}},"OBJ-00683":{"k":"object","n":"The Third Epistle of John","d":"The Elder's letter to the beloved Gaius, of living in truth, of sending on in a way that befits God the brethren who set out for His name, of Diotrephes, who would not receive them, and of Demetrius, of whom all spoke well (3 John 1:1–14)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:4; NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:5; NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:8; NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:10; NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:11; NKJV notes, note on 3 John 1:12; 3 John 1:1–14","v":"The Elder wrote to Gaius, the beloved, a man he loved in truth. The Elder prayed that Gaius might fare well in every way and enjoy good health, to match the well-being of his soul. The Elder's joy was great at the arrival of brethren who spoke of the truth found in Gaius, and of the way he walks in it; news that the Elder's own children walk in truth (NU the truth, by the NKJV note) gives him joy beyond any other. In all his work for the brethren, strangers included (NU and especially for strangers, by the NKJV note), Gaius acts faithfully, and they have told the church of his love. If he speeds them on their way as befits God, he will do well. The brethren took nothing from the Gentiles, having set out for the sake of His name. It is our duty, then, to welcome (NU support, by the NKJV note) men like these, and so become their partners in work for the truth. The Elder had sent the church a letter, but Diotrephes, eager to be first among them, gives us no welcome. So, if he comes, the Elder will bring up Diotrephes' doings: the spiteful prating (talking nonsense, by the NKJV note) aimed at us. And there is more: Diotrephes gives the brethren no welcome of his own, and those who want to welcome them he forbids, casting them from the church. Gaius was to model himself on the good and not on the evil. Of God is the doer of good; but (NU, M omit but, by the NKJV note) God has not been seen by the doer of evil. Everyone speaks well of Demetrius, and so does the truth itself; our own witness (testify, by the NKJV note) is given for him too, and Gaius knows that what we testify is true. Rather than put the much he had to write into pen and ink, the Elder hopes to see Gaius before long and talk with him face to face. Peace he wished him; greetings came from our friends; and Gaius was to greet the friends there, each by name"},{"p":"designation","r":"3 John 1:1","v":"The Elder, To the beloved Gaius"},{"p":"made by","r":"2 John 1:1, 4, 12; 3 John 1:1, 3–4, 13–14","e":"PER-03376","b":"inferred","c":"probable","w":"3 John 1:1 calls its writer the Elder, as 2 John 1:1 calls its writer. Each letter greets one whom the writer loves in truth (2 John 1:1; 3 John 1:1); each tells of his great joy at children walking in truth (2 John 1:4; 3 John 1:3–4); and each closes with much left to write, not in ink, and a hope of talking face to face (2 John 1:12; 3 John 1:13–14); so the writer is taken to be the Elder who wrote to the elect lady."},{"p":"occasion","r":"3 John 1:1, 13","e":"EVT-03317"}],"nm":{"EVT-03317":"The Elder writes to Gaius","PER-03376":"The Elder, who wrote to the elect lady"}},"OBJ-00684":{"k":"object","n":"The Epistle of Jude","d":"The letter Jude, Jesus Christ's bondservant and James's brother, wrote to the called, urging them to fight hard for the faith against ungodly men who had come in among them unnoticed, of judgments of old, of Enoch's prophecy and the apostles' warning, and of keeping themselves in God's love (Jude 1:1–25)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:4; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:7; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:9; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:12; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:12; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:19; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:22; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:23; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:23; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:24; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:25; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:25; NKJV notes, note on Jude 1:25; Jude 1:1–25","v":"Jude, Jesus Christ's bondservant and James's brother, wrote to the called, who are kept safe in Jesus Christ and made holy (NU beloved, by the NKJV note) by God the Father, wishing them love, mercy and peace in full measure. He had been earnestly set on writing to them of the salvation they share, yet found he must write to urge them to fight hard for the faith the saints received once for all time. For some men have entered among them without being noticed, men on whom this condemnation was set down long ago: ungodly men, turning our God's grace into lewdness, and denying Jesus Christ our Lord and the only Lord God (NU omits God, by the NKJV note). He wished to remind them of what they once knew: that the Lord rescued the people from Egypt, and later destroyed the unbelieving. The angels that gave up their proper (own, by the NKJV note) sphere and quit the dwelling that was theirs are held by Him in unending chains and in darkness, until the great day comes for their judgment. So too Sodom, Gomorrah and the towns around, which in like fashion pursued strange flesh and gave way to sexual immorality, stand as an example, suffering eternal fire's vengeance (punishment, by the NKJV note). Dignitaries (glorious ones, lit. glories, by the NKJV note) these dreamers slander in the same way; authority they cast off; the flesh they foul. Yet the archangel Michael, in his contest (arguing, by the NKJV note) with the devil over Moses' body, would not venture a slanderous accusation against the devil, but called on the Lord to rebuke him. These men, though, slander whatever lies beyond their knowledge, and what they do grasp by instinct, as brute beasts do, is where they bring ruin on themselves. They have perished in Korah's rebellion, rushed greedily for profit into Balaam's error, and taken Cain's way: woe to them! At the love feasts of the called these men are spots (stains, or hidden reefs, by the NKJV note), feasting with the called without fear and looking after only themselves: waterless clouds that the winds carry about (NU, M along, by the NKJV note); fruitless trees of late autumn, twice dead, uprooted; wandering stars, the blackness of darkness kept for them forever; violent sea waves that throw up the foam of their own shame. Of these men, too, Enoch, seventh from Adam, prophesied: the Lord is coming, accompanied by His saints in their tens of thousands, to judge everyone, and to convict the ungodly among them for each ungodly act done and each harsh word spoken against Him by such sinners. They grumble and complain, following their own lusts; their mouths speak great swelling words, and they flatter people for their own gain. The beloved were to bear in mind what our Lord Jesus Christ's apostles had foretold: that in the last time mockers would come, living by their own ungodly lusts. Such men cause divisions; they are sensual (soulish or worldly, by the NKJV note), without the Spirit. The beloved were to keep themselves in God's love while they await our Lord Jesus Christ's mercy, which leads to eternal life, building themselves up with their most holy faith as their footing, and with prayer in the Holy Spirit. Some they were to treat with compassion, telling one case from another (NU who are doubting, or making distinctions, by the NKJV note); others they were to save with fear (NU omits with fear, by the NKJV note), snatching them from the fire (NU adds and on some have mercy with fear, by the NKJV note), hating even clothing the flesh has stained. The letter closes in praise of the One able to guard you (M them, by the NKJV note) against a fall and to bring you, blameless and overjoyed, into His glorious presence: to God our Savior (NU the only God our, by the NKJV note), the One alone wise (NU Through Jesus Christ our Lord, Be glory, by the NKJV note), Jude ascribes glory, dominion, majesty and power (NU adds Before all time, by the NKJV note), today and forever. Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Jude 1:1","v":"Jude, a bondservant of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"made by","r":"Jude 1:1","e":"PER-03380"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Jude 1:1, 3","e":"EVT-03318"},{"p":"purpose","r":"Jude 1:3","v":"Jude wrote to urge them to fight hard for the faith the saints received once for all time, though he had been earnestly set on writing to them of the salvation they share"}],"nm":{"EVT-03318":"Jude writes to the called","PER-03380":"Jude, who wrote to the called"}},"OBJ-00685":{"k":"object","n":"The Revelation of Jesus Christ","d":"The book God gave Jesus Christ to show His servants what must soon take place, made known to John by an angel, which John wrote down for the seven churches in Asia: what he saw and heard in the Spirit, from Christ among the seven golden lampstands and His word to each church to the visions that followed and New Jerusalem, the holy city (Revelation 1:1–22:21)","f":[{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:17; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:20; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:20; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 1:20; Revelation 1:1–20","v":"God gave Jesus Christ the Revelation, so that His servants might be shown what must soon (quickly or swiftly, by the NKJV note) take place; it was sent and made known to John, His servant, by His angel, and John bore witness to God's word, to Jesus Christ's testimony, and to everything he saw. Blessed are the reader and the hearers of its prophecy who keep what it says, since the time is near. In his greeting to the seven churches in Asia, John wished them grace and peace from Jesus Christ, from the One who is, the One who was, and the One who is to come, as well as from the seven Spirits before His throne. Jesus Christ is firstborn from the dead, ruler over earth's kings and the faithful witness; He loved John and those he wrote to and, with His own blood, washed (NU loves us and freed; M loves us and washed, by the NKJV note) them clean of their sins, and for His God and Father He has made them priests and kings (NU, M a kingdom, by the NKJV note); John ascribes glory and dominion to Him for all ages. He is to come with the clouds, in the sight of every eye, even of those who pierced Him, and every tribe on earth will mourn on account of Him. The One who is, the One who was, and the One who is to come, who is the Lord (NU, M Lord God, by the NKJV note) and the Almighty, says that He is Alpha and Omega, Beginning and End (NU, M omit the Beginning and the End, by the NKJV note). John, both (NU, M omit both, by the NKJV note) brother and companion to those he wrote to in Jesus Christ's kingdom, patience and tribulation, was on the island called Patmos on account of God's word and Jesus Christ's testimony. On the Lord's Day, being in the Spirit, he heard a loud voice behind him like a trumpet saying (NU, M omit I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, and, by the NKJV note) that He is Alpha and Omega, First and Last, and bidding him write in a book what he saw, for the seven churches (NU, M omit which are in Asia, by the NKJV note), to be sent to Ephesus, Laodicea, Pergamos, Philadelphia, Sardis, Smyrna and Thyatira. He turned to see whose voice was speaking with him; there were seven golden lampstands, and among them One in the likeness of the Son of Man, in a garment reaching to His feet, with a golden band around His chest; white as wool and snow were His head and hair; His eyes were like flaming fire, His voice sounded like many waters, and like fine brass were His feet, as though refined in a furnace. Seven stars His right hand held, a sword, sharp and two-edged, came from His mouth, and His face looked like the sun when it shines in its strength. Seeing Him, John fell as though dead at His feet; but His right hand He laid on him, telling him (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) not to fear: He is First and Last; He lives; He was dead, and is alive forevermore; and He holds Death's keys and the keys of Hades (Lit. Unseen; the unseen realm, by the NKJV note). He bade John write (NU, M Therefore, write, by the NKJV note) what is to take place after this, what now is, and what he had seen; and He told him what the seven stars and the lampstands are, a mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note): the churches' angels (Or messengers, by the NKJV note) are the seven stars, and the lampstands (NU, M omit which you saw, by the NKJV note) are the seven churches themselves"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:15; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:20; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:20; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:20; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:21; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:22; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:23; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:24; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 2:24; Revelation 2:1–29","v":"John was to write to the Ephesus church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) the words of Him whose right hand holds the seven stars, who walks among the seven golden lampstands: He knows their patience (perseverance, by the NKJV note), their toil and their works, and that they cannot bear (endure, by the NKJV note) evil people; they have tested those who call themselves apostles, though they are not, and have proved them to be liars; they have persevered and been patient, laboring for His name's sake without growing weary. Yet He holds it against them that they have left the love they had at first; they are to remember where they fell from, repent, and do the works they did at first; unless they repent, He will come to them soon and take their lampstand out of its place. In their favor, they hate the Nicolaitans' deeds, which He hates too. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches: the one who overcomes will be given the tree of life, in the midst of God's Paradise, to eat from. To the Smyrna church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) John was to write the words of the First and Last, who died and came to life: He knows their works, their poverty and their tribulation, yet they are rich, and He knows of the blasphemy from those who call themselves Jews, though they are not, being Satan's synagogue (congregation, by the NKJV note). They are not to fear the suffering ahead: the devil will soon cast some of them into prison, for them to be tested; ten days of tribulation will be theirs; faithful until death they must be, and the crown of life will be His gift to them. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches: the second death will do the one who overcomes no harm. To the Pergamos church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) John was to write the words of Him whose sword is sharp and two-edged: He knows their works and where they live, the place of Satan's throne; they cling to His name, and His faith they did not deny even when Antipas, His faithful martyr, was put to death in their midst, where Satan has his dwelling. Yet He has a few things against them: some among them hold Balaam's doctrine, for Balaam taught Balak how Israel's children might be made to stumble, committing sexual immorality and eating food sacrificed to idols; some, too, hold the Nicolaitans' doctrine, a thing He hates (NU, M likewise, by the NKJV note). The church is to repent; unless it does, He will soon come and fight those who hold these doctrines with His mouth's sword. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches: the one who overcomes will be fed from the hidden manna, and will also be given a white stone bearing a new name, which only the one who receives it knows. To the Thyatira church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) John was to write the words of the Son of God, with feet like fine brass and eyes like flaming fire: He knows their love and faith, their service (NU, M faith, service, by the NKJV note), their patience (perseverance, by the NKJV note) and their works, and that their last works exceed their first. Yet He has a few things against them (NU, M against you that you tolerate, by the NKJV note): that they allow the woman Jezebel (M your wife Jezebel, by the NKJV note), a prophetess by her own word, to seduce and to teach (NU, M and teaches and seduces, by the NKJV note), leading His servants into the eating of food sacrificed to idols and into sexual immorality. Time to repent (NU, M repent, and she does not want to repent of her sexual immorality, by the NKJV note) was given her, yet she would not turn from her sexual immorality. He will throw her onto a bed of sickness, and bring great tribulation on her partners in adultery unless they repent of what they (NU, M her, by the NKJV note) have done; her children He will kill with death, so that every church will know Him as the searcher (examines, by the NKJV note) of minds and hearts; and each one in the church will receive from Him according to their works. He says to them, and (NU, M omit and, by the NKJV note) to the others in Thyatira, who neither hold this doctrine nor have known what is spoken of as Satan's depths, that He puts (NU, M omit will, by the NKJV note) no other burden on them; they must hold fast what they have until He comes. The one who overcomes, keeping His works to the end, will be given authority over the nations, ruling them with an iron rod and breaking them like a potter's pots, just as He too has received it from His Father; to him He will also give the morning star. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:16; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 3:19; Revelation 3:1–22","v":"To the Sardis church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) John was to write the words of Him who has God's seven Spirits and the seven stars: He knows their works; they have a name for being alive, but they are dead. They are to be watchful and strengthen what remains, which is near death, for their works He has not found complete in the sight of God (NU, M My God, by the NKJV note); they are to keep in mind how they received and heard, and hold to it and repent. If they do not watch, He will come on them as a thief, at an hour they will not know. Yet, even (NU, M omit even, by the NKJV note) in Sardis, there are (NU, M Nevertheless you, by the NKJV note) a few names who have kept their garments undefiled; these will walk with Him in white, being worthy. White garments will clothe the one who overcomes; He will not blot his name out of the Book of Life; rather, He will confess it before His Father and His Father's angels. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches. To the Philadelphia church's angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note) John was to write the words of the Holy and True One, who has David's key, whose opening and whose shutting no one can undo: He knows their works. Before them He has set an open door, a door (NU, M which no one can shut, by the NKJV note) no one is able to shut; for, small though their strength is, His word they have kept and His name they have not denied. He will make those of Satan's synagogue, who call themselves Jews, though they are not, but lie, come and worship before the church's feet, and know that He has loved it. Since His command to persevere (Lit. the word of My patience, by the NKJV note) has been kept by them, from that hour of trial He will keep them, an hour coming on the whole world, to put the earth's dwellers to the test. Behold (NU, M omit Behold, by the NKJV note), He is coming quickly; they must cling to what they have, so that their crown is taken by no one. The one who overcomes will be made a pillar in His God's temple, never to go out again; on him He will write His own new name, His God's name, and the name of His God's city, New Jerusalem, descending from heaven, from His God. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches. To the Laodiceans' church (NU, M in Laodicea, by the NKJV note), to its angel (Or messenger, by the NKJV note), John was to write the words of the Amen, who is also the Beginning of God's creation, a Witness faithful and true: He knows their works; they are neither hot nor cold, and He wishes they were either cold or hot. Since lukewarm is what they are, being neither cold (NU, M hot nor cold, by the NKJV note) nor hot, out of His mouth He will vomit them. They claim to be rich, grown wealthy, in need of nothing, not knowing that they are blind, naked, poor, wretched and miserable. He counsels them to anoint their eyes with salve, so as to see, and to buy from Him white garments, to be clothed, so that their nakedness is not exposed to shame, and gold refined in fire, to be rich. Those He loves He rebukes and chastens (discipline, by the NKJV note); so they are to be zealous (eager, by the NKJV note) and repent. He is standing at the door, knocking; to anyone who hears His voice and opens, He will come in and share a meal with him, each eating with the other. To the one who overcomes He will grant a seat with Him on His throne, just as He too, having overcome, took His seat with the Father on the Father's own throne. Anyone with an ear is to hear the Spirit's words to the churches"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:3; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 4:11; Revelation 4:1–11","v":"After these things John looked, and in heaven a door stood open; the trumpet-like voice he had first heard bade him come up, so that he would be shown what has to come about afterward. At once he was in the Spirit: a throne stood in heaven, with One seated on it; the One seated there (M omits And He who sat there was, making the following a description of the throne, by the NKJV note) looked like jasper and sardius stone, and an emerald-like rainbow encircled the throne. Twenty-four thrones stood around it, and on them sat twenty-four elders, robed in white (NU, M robes, with crowns, by the NKJV note), with golden crowns on their heads. Lightnings came out of the throne, with voices and with thunderings (NU, M voices, and thunderings, by the NKJV note); before it burned seven lamps of fire, and these are the (M omits the, by the NKJV note) seven Spirits belonging to God; and there lay (NU, M add something like, by the NKJV note) a sea of glass before the throne, resembling crystal. Four living creatures, covered with eyes before and behind, were in the throne's midst and on every side of it: the first resembled a lion, the second a calf; the fourth was like an eagle in flight, and the third had a man's face. Each of them had six wings and eyes all over, around it and inside, and day and night they never cease to cry that He is holy, holy, holy (M has holy nine times, by the NKJV note), He who was, who is and who is coming, the Lord God Almighty. Each time the living creatures give thanks, honor and glory to the One seated on the throne, the One living forever and ever, the elders, all twenty-four, fall before Him in worship, throwing down their crowns in front of the throne and saying that He, the Lord (NU, M our Lord and God, by the NKJV note), is worthy to receive power, honor and glory, since He created all things, and it is His will by which they came to be and exist (NU, M existed, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:13; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 5:14; Revelation 5:1–14","v":"John saw that the One seated on the throne held in His right hand a scroll sealed with seven seals, with writing on both its inside and its back. A strong angel proclaimed loudly, asking who was worthy to loose the scroll's seals and open it; but no one could open it or even look upon it, whether in heaven, beneath the earth or on it. John wept a great deal, since nobody was found worthy to unseal the scroll and read (NU, M omit and read, by the NKJV note) it, or even to look upon it; then one elder told him not to weep, for the Lion of Judah's tribe, David's Root, has prevailed, so as to loose (NU, M omit to loose, by the NKJV note) the seven seals of the scroll and open it. John looked, and there (NU, M I saw in the midst . . . a Lamb standing, by the NKJV note), in the throne's center, stood a Lamb, with the four living creatures about Him and the elders around Him, looking as though slain, with seven eyes and seven horns, the eyes being God's seven Spirits, sent out through the whole earth. He came forward and from the right hand of the One seated on the throne took the scroll. Once the Lamb had taken the scroll, down before Him the four living creatures threw themselves, and with them the elders, twenty-four in all; each had a harp, and they held golden bowls with incense in them, that is, the saints' prayers; and the song they sang was new: that the Lamb is worthy of receiving the scroll and of breaking its seals, since He was slain, and by His blood He has redeemed them to God from every nation, people, tongue and tribe, and has made them (NU, M them, by the NKJV note) priests and kings (NU a kingdom, by the NKJV note) for their God, and on the earth they (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) are to reign. John looked again; he heard many angels' voices, thousands of thousands, and ten thousand multiplied by ten thousand, who encircled the throne, and with it the elders and living creatures, proclaiming loudly that the slain Lamb is worthy to receive blessing, glory, honor, strength, wisdom, riches and power. And John heard every creature, whether in the sea, under the earth, on the earth or in heaven, with everything in them, saying: let glory, power, blessing and honor be given forever and ever (M adds Amen, by the NKJV note) to the Lamb and to the One seated on the throne. When this was said, the four living creatures answered Amen; bowing down, the elders, twenty-four (NU, M omit twenty-four, by the NKJV note) of them, worshiped the One living forever and ever (NU, M omit Him who lives forever and ever, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:3; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 6:15; Revelation 6:1–17","v":"John watched as one of the seals (NU, M seven seals, by the NKJV note) was opened by the Lamb, and heard a living creature, one of the four, call out like thunder to come and see; a white horse appeared, whose rider held a bow; he was given a crown, and went out as a conqueror, to conquer. At the second seal the second living creature called out to come and (NU, M omit and see, by the NKJV note) see, and out went another horse, a fiery red one, whose rider was given a great sword and leave to take away the earth's peace, and for people to kill one another. At the third seal the third living creature called out to come and see; John looked, and a black horse appeared, whose rider held a pair of scales (balances, by the NKJV note), and a voice was heard from among the four living creatures, pricing wheat at a quart (Gr. choinix, about 1 quart, by the NKJV note) and barley at three quarts, each for a denarius (About 1 day's wage for a worker, by the NKJV note), and forbidding harm to the wine and the oil. At the fourth seal the fourth living creature's voice called out to come and see; John looked, and a pale horse appeared, whose rider was named Death, with Hades following him, and power (authority, by the NKJV note) to kill over one quarter of the earth was given them, by its beasts, by hunger, by sword and by death. At the fifth seal John looked beneath the altar and saw there the souls of those slain because of God's word and the testimony they had held; they cried out loudly, asking the Lord, holy and true, how much longer before He would pass sentence and take vengeance for their blood on the people living on the earth; each was given a white robe, and they were told to rest a little longer, until their brethren and fellow servants, who were to be killed as they had been, reached their full number. At the sixth seal John looked, and (NU, M omit behold, by the NKJV note) a great earthquake came; the sun was darkened to the blackness of sackcloth of hair, the moon (NU, M whole moon, by the NKJV note) took on the look of blood, and heaven's stars fell to the earth, like late figs that fall from a tree shaken by a strong wind; like a scroll being rolled up, the sky receded (Or split apart, by the NKJV note), and from its place every island and mountain was shifted. Every man, slave or free, took cover in caves and among the mountains' rocks, and so did the earth's kings, with its great and its mighty men, its commanders and its rich men (NU, M the commanders, the rich men, by the NKJV note), calling on the rocks and mountains to fall on them and conceal them from the Lamb's wrath and the face of the One seated on the throne, for the day of that great wrath has arrived, and who can remain standing?"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 7:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 7:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 7:17; Revelation 7:1–17","v":"After these things, at the earth's four corners, John saw four angels holding back its four winds, so that no wind blew on any tree, nor on the earth or the sea. From the east John saw another angel come up, bearing the living God's seal, who cried out loudly to the four angels, permitted to do harm to the sea and the earth, that no harm was to be done to the trees, the sea or the earth until God's servants had been sealed on their foreheads. John heard the number of the sealed, a total of one hundred forty-four thousand, drawn from every tribe of Israel's children: twelve thousand were sealed (NU, M omit sealed in vv. 5b–8b, by the NKJV note) from each of the tribes, Asher, Benjamin, Gad, Issachar, Joseph, Judah, Levi, Manasseh, Naphtali, Reuben, Simeon and Zebulun. After these things John looked again: there was a great multitude, too many for anyone to number, from every people, tongue, nation and tribe, standing before the Lamb and the throne, robed in white and holding palm branches, and crying aloud that salvation lies with the Lamb and with their God, who is seated on the throne. Standing around the elders and living creatures and the throne, all the angels fell face down before it in worship of God, saying: Amen; let might, power, honor, thanksgiving, wisdom, glory and blessing be given forever and ever to God; Amen. John was then asked by one of the elders where those robed in white had come from, and who they were; John, calling him Sir (NU, M My lord, by the NKJV note), answered that the elder knew. The elder told him that these have come from the great tribulation, and in the Lamb's blood they washed their robes white; so they are before God's throne and serve Him in His temple day and night, and the One seated on the throne will have His dwelling among them; never again will they hunger or thirst, nor will the sun or any heat strike them, for the Lamb in the throne's midst will shepherd them, guiding them where there are living (NU, M fountains of the waters of life, by the NKJV note) fountains of water; every tear will be wiped from their eyes by God"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 8:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 8:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 8:13; Revelation 8:1–13","v":"At the opening of the seventh seal heaven fell silent; the silence lasted about half an hour. John saw seven trumpets given to the seven angels standing in God's presence. Another angel came with a golden censer and took his stand at the altar; much incense was given him, to offer, together with every saint's prayers, at the gold altar before the throne, and from his hand the incense smoke went up before God, together with the saints' prayers. Then he filled the censer from the altar's fire and hurled it down to the earth; there came an earthquake, thunderings, lightnings and noises. The seven angels made ready to sound the trumpets they held. At the first trumpet, hail and fire mixed with blood were hurled to the earth (NU, M add and a third of the earth was burned up, by the NKJV note); the trees were burned, a third of them, and all the green grass. At the second, a mountain, great and on fire, was hurled into the sea; one third of the sea turned to blood, one third of the sea's living things died, and of the ships one third were destroyed. At the third, a great star, aflame like a torch, dropped out of heaven onto the rivers, a third of them, and the water springs too; the star is named Wormwood, and the waters turned to wormwood, a third of them, and the water, made bitter, killed many men. At the fourth the stars, the moon and the sun were struck, each in a third part, and a third of each grew dark, and the day was without its shining (had no light, by the NKJV note) for a third part, as was the night. Then, looking, John heard an angel (NU, M eagle, by the NKJV note) flying in mid-heaven cry out loudly a threefold woe to the earth's inhabitants, for the blasts the last three angels were still to sound"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 9:21; Revelation 9:1–21","v":"At the fifth trumpet John saw a star fallen to the earth out of heaven; he received the bottomless pit's key (Lit. shaft of the abyss, by the NKJV note) and opened the pit; from it rose smoke such as a great furnace gives, darkening both air and sun. Locusts came out of the smoke onto the earth, and they were given power such as scorpions on the earth have, and were told to harm no tree, no green thing and none of the earth's grass, but only the men without God's seal on their foreheads. They (The locusts, by the NKJV note) had no leave to kill those men, but for five months were to torment them, with a pain like that of a scorpion's sting on a man. Men will look for death in those days without finding it, and wish to die, yet death will keep away from them. The locusts looked like warhorses armed for battle; their tails, like scorpions', bore stings; their breastplates were like iron; their teeth were like those of lions, their hair such as women have and their faces like men's, with crowns like gold upon their heads; their wings sounded like many horse-drawn chariots charging into battle, and for five months they had power to hurt men. As their king they had the bottomless pit's angel, named Abaddon in Hebrew (Lit. Destruction, by the NKJV note) and in Greek Apollyon (Lit. Destroyer, by the NKJV note). One woe is over; two more are yet to come after these things. At the sixth trumpet John heard a voice coming from the golden altar's four horns, the altar standing before God, telling the sixth angel, the one with the trumpet, to set free the four angels held bound by the Euphrates, the great river; and they were set free, made ready for the year, the month, the day and the hour, to slay one third of mankind. John heard how many were in the horsemen's army: two hundred million. This is how he saw the horses and their riders in the vision: the riders wore breastplates of sulfur yellow, hyacinth blue and fiery red, the horses had heads like lions' heads, and brimstone, smoke and fire issued from the horses' mouths, three plagues killing one third of mankind; it is in the horses' tails and mouths that their power (NU, M the power of the horses, by the NKJV note) lies, for the tails are snake-like and have heads, and with these they injure. Yet the others of mankind, whom these plagues did not kill, did not repent of what their hands had made, so as to stop worshiping demons and idols of wood, stone, brass, silver and gold, which cannot walk, hear or see; nor did they repent of their thefts, their sexual immorality, their sorceries (NU, M drugs, by the NKJV note) or their murders"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 10:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 10:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 10:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 10:11; Revelation 10:1–11","v":"John saw yet another mighty angel descending out of heaven, wrapped in a cloud, with a rainbow over his head; his feet were like fiery pillars, and his face shone like the sun. The angel held in his hand a little book, open; he planted one foot, the right, on the sea, and the other, the left, on the land, and gave a great cry like a lion's roar, at which seven thunders let their voices be heard. As the seven thunders spoke (NU, M sounded, by the NKJV note), John was on the point of writing, but a voice from heaven told him (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) to seal up what they had said and not to write it. The angel standing on sea and land lifted his hand (NU, M right hand, by the NKJV note) heavenward and took an oath by the One living forever and ever, Creator of the sea, the earth and heaven and all that is in them, that there would be no more delay, but that when the seventh angel is ready to sound his trumpet, in the days of its sounding, God's mystery would be completed, as He made it known to the prophets, His servants. Then the voice from heaven spoke to John again, bidding him go and take the open little book held by the angel standing on sea and earth. John went to the angel, asking for the little book, and the angel told him to take it and eat it: in his mouth it would taste sweet like honey, but it would turn his stomach bitter. John took it from the angel and ate it; in his mouth it tasted sweet like honey, but his stomach grew bitter once he had eaten it. And the one speaking (NU, M they, by the NKJV note) told John he must prophesy once more about many kings, tongues, nations and peoples"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:15; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:17; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 11:19; Revelation 11:1–19","v":"A reed resembling a rod for measuring was given to John, and the angel stood (NU, M omit And the angel stood, by the NKJV note) and bade him rise and measure the altar, those worshiping there and God's temple, but leave out and not measure the court outside the temple, since it is given over to the Gentiles, who for forty-two months will trample the holy city; and the speaker says that he will empower two witnesses of his, to prophesy in sackcloth for twelve hundred and sixty days. They are the two lampstands and the two olive trees that stand before the earth's God (NU, M Lord, by the NKJV note). Should any want to injure them, fire from the witnesses' mouths devours their enemies, and any who want to injure them must die in just that way. They can turn waters into blood; they can shut up heaven, and no rain falls while they prophesy; the earth too they can strike with every plague, as often as they please. Once their testimony is finished, they will be attacked in war, defeated and slain by the beast coming up from the bottomless pit, and their corpses will lie in the great city's street, the city that is spiritually called Egypt and Sodom, where the Lord too was crucified (NU, M their Lord, by the NKJV note). For three days and a half those from the nations, tongues, tribes and peoples will look on (NU, M see . . . and will not allow, by the NKJV note) their bodies and will not let them be laid in graves, and the earth's inhabitants will celebrate over them and exchange presents, since these two prophets had tormented them. But when the three days and a half were over, life-giving breath from God entered them; they rose to their feet, and those who watched were struck with great fear. The two witnesses heard (M I, by the NKJV note) a loud voice out of heaven calling them to come up, and in a cloud went up to heaven while their enemies watched. At that hour a great quake brought down a tenth part of the city and killed seven thousand; the survivors, afraid, glorified the God of heaven. Of the woes, the second has passed, and the third will soon come. At the seventh trumpet loud voices in heaven declared that this world's kingdoms (NU, M kingdom . . . has become the kingdom, by the NKJV note) now belong to the Lord and to His Christ; the reign is to last forever and ever. The elders, all twenty-four, seated on their thrones before God, bowed low and worshiped God, giving thanks to Him who was, who is and who is coming (NU, M omit and who is to come, by the NKJV note), Lord God Almighty, because He has laid hold of His great power and begun His reign: the nations raged, and His wrath (anger, by the NKJV note) has come, as has the time for the dead to be judged, for Him to reward all who revere His name, both great and small, as well as the prophets, His servants, and the saints, and for Him to destroy the earth's destroyers. Then in heaven God's temple was opened, and in His temple His covenant's ark (M the covenant of the Lord, by the NKJV note) was seen, and there came great hail, an earthquake, thunderings, noises and lightnings"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 12:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 12:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 12:17; Revelation 12:1–17","v":"A great sign was seen in heaven: a woman who wore twelve stars as a garland on her head, with the moon beneath her feet, robed in the sun; she was with child, crying out in the pain of labor as she was about to give birth. Another sign was seen in heaven: a dragon, great and fiery red, its horns ten and its heads seven, and on the heads seven diadems; with his tail he swept away a third of heaven's stars and flung them down to the earth. Before the woman, now about to give birth, the dragon took his stand, to devour her Child the moment it was born. The Child she bore was a male, one destined to rule every nation with an iron rod, and the Child was lifted to God, to the throne of God. As for the woman, she fled to the wilderness, to a place God has made ready for her, so that there she would be fed for 1,260 days. In heaven war broke out: Michael, with his angels, made war on the dragon; the dragon too fought, he and his angels; yet neither the dragon nor his angels prevailed (were not strong enough, by the NKJV note), and heaven no longer had any place for them (M him, by the NKJV note). The great dragon, who is that serpent of old, known as Satan and the Devil, the deceiver of the whole world, was thrown out and down to the earth, with his angels. Then a loud voice spoke in heaven, and John heard it say that the kingdom of God has come, and salvation, and strength, with the power of God's Christ, since the accuser of the brethren, who day and night brought charges against them before God, has been cast down. By the Lamb's blood and their testimony's word they had overcome him, not loving their lives even in the face of death. The heavens and all who live in them were to be glad; but woe to those who inhabit the sea and the earth, since the devil, in great wrath, has come down to them, knowing that his time is short. Finding himself cast down to the earth, the dragon went after the woman who had borne the male Child and persecuted her; but she was given a great eagle's two wings, to fly off to her place in the wilderness, where, away from the serpent's presence, she receives nourishment through a time and times, plus a half of a time. The serpent spewed from his mouth water like a flood, sending it after the woman so that the flood would carry her away; but the earth came to the woman's aid: it opened its mouth and drew down into itself the flood the dragon had poured from his mouth. In rage at the woman, the dragon went off to wage war on her other offspring, those who obey God's commands and bear the witness of Jesus Christ (NU, M omit Christ, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 13:17; Revelation 13:1–18","v":"Then John (NU he, by the NKJV note) stood by the sea, on its sand, and saw a beast coming up from the sea, its heads seven and its horns ten (NU, M ten horns and seven heads, by the NKJV note), with ten crowns on its horns and on its heads a name of blasphemy. The beast he saw had a lion's mouth and a bear's feet, and it resembled a leopard; and to it the dragon gave great authority, his throne and his power. One of its heads seemed to have been mortally wounded, but the deadly wound was healed; the whole world, in wonder, followed the beast. They gave worship to the dragon, who had given the beast authority, and to the beast too, asking who can match the beast, and who can fight it. It was given a mouth to speak blasphemies and great things, and authority, for forty-two months, to continue (M make war, by the NKJV note); against God it opened its mouth in blasphemy, to blaspheme His tabernacle and His name, and those dwelling in heaven. It was allowed to wage war against the saints and defeat them; and over every tongue, nation and tribe (NU, M add and people, by the NKJV note) authority was given it. Those on the earth not written by name in the Lamb's book, the Book of Life, will all worship it; the Lamb was slain from when the world was founded. Anyone with an ear is to hear. Those who take captives are themselves to be taken captive, and those who kill by the sword are themselves to die by it. The saints' patience (perseverance, by the NKJV note) and faith are here. Then John saw, coming up from the earth, another beast, with two horns like a lamb's, which spoke like a dragon. It exercises, in the first beast's presence, all of that beast's authority, and it makes the earth and those living there worship the first beast, the beast whose deadly wound had been healed. It performs great signs, even making fire descend to the earth from heaven while men look on, and by the signs it was allowed to perform before the beast it deceives the earth's inhabitants (M my own people, by the NKJV note), telling them to fashion an image of the beast, the one that, wounded by the sword, had lived. Further, power was given it to put breath into the beast's image, so that the image would speak, and would cause the death of all who refused to worship the image. It causes everyone, the rich and the poor, the slave and the free, the great and the small, to have a mark put on their foreheads or right hands, so that none can buy or sell unless he bears the number of the beast's name, or else its mark or its name (NU, M the mark, the name, by the NKJV note). Here, wisdom is called for: the one with understanding is to work out the beast's number, for the number is a man's; it is 666"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:13; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:15; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 14:20; Revelation 14:1–20","v":"Then John looked, and there stood on Mount Zion a Lamb (NU, M the, by the NKJV note), with 144,000 beside Him, who bore (NU, M add His name and, by the NKJV note) the name of His Father on their foreheads. From heaven John heard a voice like loud thunder and like many waters, and he heard harpists playing on their harps. Before the throne, the elders, and with them the four living creatures, they sang what seemed to be a new song, and none could learn it but the 144,000 redeemed from the earth. Being virgins, these were not defiled by women; wherever the Lamb goes, they follow Him; from among men they were redeemed (M adds by Jesus, by the NKJV note), as firstfruits for God and for the Lamb; no deceit (NU, M falsehood, by the NKJV note) was found in their mouth, for they are faultless (NU, M omit the rest of v. 5, by the NKJV note) before God's throne. Then John saw a further angel, flying in midheaven, carrying the everlasting gospel, to be preached to those living on the earth, of every people, tongue, tribe and nation; the angel said loudly that they are to give God glory and to fear Him, since the hour has come for His judgment, and to worship Him as the Maker of the sea, of heaven and earth, and of the springs of water. A second angel followed, saying that Babylon (NU Babylon the great is fallen, is fallen, which has made; M Babylon the great is fallen. She has made, by the NKJV note), that great city, has fallen, has fallen, for she made every nation drink the wine of her fornication's wrath. Then a third angel came after them, saying loudly that anyone who worships the beast and also its image, and bears its mark on the brow or the hand, will himself drink the wine of God's wrath, poured out unmixed to fill the cup of His anger, and fire and brimstone will torment him before the Lamb and the holy angels; their torment's smoke rises forever and ever; for those worshiping the beast and its image, or for anyone taking the mark of its name, there is no rest by day or night. The saints' endurance (steadfastness, perseverance, by the NKJV note) is seen in those (NU, M omit here are those, by the NKJV note) who obey God's commands and hold to the faith of Jesus. From heaven John then heard a voice telling him (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) to write that those who from now on die in the Lord are blessed; the Spirit says yes, that they may rest, their toil over, and that their works go with them. John looked again, and there was a white cloud, and seated on it One in the likeness of the Son of Man, who had a sharp sickle in His hand and a golden crown on His head. From the temple came another angel, crying loudly to the One seated on the cloud, bidding Him reap with His sickle, since it was time for Him (NU, M omit for You, by the NKJV note) to reap, the earth's harvest being ripe. He who was seated on the cloud then put His sickle to the earth, so that the earth was reaped. From the temple in heaven another angel then came out, he too with a sharp sickle, and from the altar came another angel, one with power over fire, who cried loudly to the one with the sharp sickle to thrust it in, gathering the clusters from the earth's vine, since its grapes are fully ripe. Putting his sickle to the earth, the angel gathered its vine, which he threw into God's great winepress, the winepress of His wrath; outside the city the winepress was trampled, and from it blood flowed, as high as the horses' bridles, for 1,600 furlongs (Lit. stadia, about 184 miles in all, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 15:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 15:3; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 15:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 15:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 15:6; Revelation 15:1–8","v":"Then John saw in heaven a further sign, a great and wonderful one: the seven last plagues, held by seven angels, since these plagues bring God's wrath to its end. John also saw what looked like a glass-like sea with fire blended in, and standing on it, holding God's harps, were those victorious over the beast, over its name's number, over its mark (NU, M omit over his mark, by the NKJV note) and over its image. They sing the Lamb's song and the song of Moses, God's servant, praising the Lord God Almighty, the King of the saints (NU, M nations, by the NKJV note), whose works are great and marvelous and whose ways are just and true: none will fail to fear Him, the Lord, and glorify His name, since He alone is holy; in His presence all nations will come and worship, since His judgments have been made plain. After these things John looked and beheld (NU, M omit behold, by the NKJV note) that in heaven the temple (sanctuary, the inner shrine, by the NKJV note) of the testimony's tabernacle was opened. From the temple (sanctuary, the inner shrine, by the NKJV note) the seven angels came forth with the seven plagues, robed in linen, pure and bright, with golden bands around their chests. Then a living creature, one of the four, gave seven golden bowls to the seven angels, filled with the wrath of God, the One living forever and ever; smoke from God's glory and from His power filled the temple, and nobody could enter it until the seven angels had finished their seven plagues"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 16:16; Revelation 16:1–21","v":"Then, from the temple, John heard a loud voice sending forth the seven angels, to empty on the earth the bowls (NU, M seven bowls, by the NKJV note) of God's wrath; the first angel went, and on the earth he poured out his bowl, and on those who worshiped the beast's image and the men bearing its mark came a sore, foul and loathsome (severe and malignant, lit. bad and evil, by the NKJV note). When the second angel's bowl was poured out on the sea, the sea became like a dead man's blood, and all that lived in it died; when the third angel's bowl was poured out on the springs and the rivers, they too became blood. John heard the waters' angel saying that the Lord (NU, M omit O Lord, by the NKJV note), He who is to be, who once was (NU, M was, the Holy One, by the NKJV note) and who now is, is righteous in judging so: since the prophets' and saints' blood was shed, He has given blood to drink to those who shed it, as (NU, M omit For, by the NKJV note) they deserve; and John heard another (NU, M omit another from, by the NKJV note) at the altar agreeing that the Lord God Almighty's judgments are righteous and true. When the fourth angel's bowl was poured out on the sun, the angel was given power to scorch men by fire; scorched by great heat, men blasphemed God's name, the name of the One with power over these plagues, nor did they repent and glorify Him. When the fifth angel's bowl was poured out on the beast's throne, darkness filled the beast's kingdom, and those in it gnawed their tongues for pain; for their sores and pains the God of heaven was blasphemed by them, and of their deeds they did not repent. The sixth angel's bowl was poured out on the Euphrates, the great river, whose water was dried up to make the way ready for the kings out of the east. John saw three unclean spirits that looked like frogs come out from the false prophet's mouth, and from the beast's and the dragon's: spirits of demons, they work signs, and they go out to the earth's kings and (NU, M omit of the earth and, by the NKJV note) those of the whole world, gathering them for the battle on God Almighty's great day. A saying breaks in, its speaker not named: the speaker is coming as a thief, and the one who keeps watch and keeps his garments is blessed; that way he does not walk naked and have his shame seen. The spirits gathered the kings to the place that in Hebrew is called Armageddon (Lit. Mount Megiddo; M Megiddo, by the NKJV note). The seventh angel's bowl was poured out into the air, and from the throne, out of heaven's temple, came a loud voice saying, It is done; there came lightnings, thunderings and noises, and an earthquake greater and mightier than any since men came to be on the earth. Into three parts the great city was split, the nations' cities collapsed, and great Babylon came to remembrance before God, so that she would be given the cup filled with the wine of His fierce wrath; the islands all fled away; nowhere were the mountains found; great hailstones, each about a talent in weight, fell from heaven on men, and men blasphemed God for the hail's plague, since it was exceedingly great"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 17:16; Revelation 17:1–18","v":"Then an angel, one of the seven bearing the bowls, spoke with John, telling him (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) to come and be shown how the great harlot is judged, she who is seated on many waters, and with her the earth's kings committed fornication, and those dwelling on the earth became drunk on her fornication's wine. In the Spirit the angel carried John away into the wilderness, where John saw, seated on a scarlet beast full of blasphemous names, its horns ten and its heads seven, a woman dressed in scarlet and purple and adorned with pearls, precious stones and gold, who held a golden cup in her hand, full of abominations and of her fornication's filth (M the fornication of the earth, by the NKJV note); her forehead bore a name in writing: Mystery, Babylon the Great, together with the title mother of the earth's abominations and of harlots; John saw the woman, and she was drunk with the saints' blood and that of Jesus' martyrs, and at the sight of her he was filled with amazement and marveled. The angel asked John why he marveled, and said that he would tell him the woman's mystery (hidden truth, by the NKJV note), and that of the beast carrying her, the beast with the ten horns and the seven heads: the beast John saw existed once, is not now, and will come up from the bottomless pit, to go on from there to perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note); those dwelling on the earth, whose names are not, from the world's foundation, written into the Book of Life, will marvel on seeing the beast which, having once been, now is not, though yet it is (NU, M shall be present, by the NKJV note). This needs a wise mind: the woman is seated on seven mountains, which are the seven heads; there are seven kings as well: five have fallen; one of them is now; the other is still to come, and once he has come, his stay must be short; as for the beast that, having been, now is not, it is itself an eighth, belongs to the seven, and is headed for perdition (destruction, by the NKJV note). The ten horns John saw are ten kings, not yet given a kingdom, who for one hour receive authority, reigning as kings along with the beast; all of one mind, they will hand over their authority and power to the beast; they will also wage war on the Lamb, but they will be overcome by the Lamb, since the Lamb is Lord of lords as well as King of kings; and faithful, chosen and called are those with Him. The angel told John that the waters he saw, where the harlot is seated, are tongues, nations, multitudes and peoples; the harlot will be hated by the ten horns John saw (NU, M saw, and the beast, by the NKJV note) on the beast; they will eat her flesh, leave her naked and desolate, and consume her with fire, since God moved their hearts to carry out His purpose: to agree as one, handing over to the beast their kingdom, until God's words are fulfilled; the woman John saw is the great city, the one reigning over the earth's kings"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:3; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:17; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 18:20; Revelation 18:1–24","v":"After these things John saw a further angel come down from heaven, one with great authority, whose glory lit up the earth; in a loud voice he cried out mightily (NU, M omit mightily, by the NKJV note) that fallen, fallen is Babylon the great, now a cage for every bird hated and unclean, a jail for foul spirits of every kind, and a home for demons; for every nation has drunk the wine of her fornication's wrath, with her the earth's kings have committed fornication, and the earth's merchants have grown rich by her luxury's abundance (Lit. strengths, by the NKJV note). From heaven John heard a further voice, which called the speaker's own people out of Babylon the great, so that they would have no part in her sins and none of her plagues would fall on them, since her sins reach (NU, M have been heaped up, by the NKJV note) as far as heaven, and her iniquities God has remembered. The voice bids those it addresses pay her back as she paid them (NU, M omit to you, by the NKJV note), repay her twofold for what she did, and fill her own cup twice over for her, giving her torment and sorrow equal to her self-glory and luxury (sensually, by the NKJV note); her plagues will all come within a single day, famine, death and mourning, because she tells herself in her heart that she sits as a queen, is no widow and will know no sorrow; and fire will burn her up completely, for the Lord God is strong, He who judges (NU, M has judged, by the NKJV note) her. The earth's kings, who lived in luxury with Babylon the great and committed fornication with her, will mourn her with weeping and lamentation at the sight of the smoke as she burns; standing far off, fearing her torment, they will cry alas, alas, for Babylon, that great and mighty city, since her judgment came within one hour. The earth's merchants will mourn and weep over Babylon the great, since none buys their goods any longer: marble, iron and bronze; silver and gold, pearls and precious stones; scarlet and silk, purple and fine linen; ware of every kind in ivory and in the costliest wood, and citron wood of every kind; frankincense, fragrant oil, incense and cinnamon; wheat and fine flour, oil and wine; sheep and cattle, chariots and horses; and the souls and bodies of men; the fruit her soul longed for has left her, the rich and splendid things have all departed (NU, M been lost to you, by the NKJV note) from her, and she will never find them again; those who traded in these things and grew rich through her will keep far off, weeping and wailing in fear of her torment, and will cry alas, alas, for the great city clothed in scarlet, purple and fine linen and adorned with pearls, precious stones and gold, since in one hour riches so great have come to nothing (have been laid waste, by the NKJV note). Sailors and every shipmaster, those who trade by sea and all who go by ship, stood far off, and on seeing the smoke as Babylon the great burned they cried out, asking what city could compare with this great city; with dust thrown on their heads they wept and wailed, crying alas, alas, for the great city by whose wealth all with ships at sea grew rich, since in one hour she has been made desolate (have been laid waste, by the NKJV note). Heaven is to rejoice over her, and the holy (NU, M saints and apostles, by the NKJV note) apostles and prophets with it, since God has taken vengeance on her for them. Then a mighty angel lifted a stone that was like a great millstone; into the sea he hurled it, saying that so violently will Babylon, the great city, be thrown down, never to be found again: in her no sound of trumpeters, flutists, musicians and harpists will be heard any more, no craftsman, whatever his craft, will be found, no millstone's sound will be heard, no lamp's light will shine, no voice of bride and bridegroom will be heard; since the earth's great men were her merchants, and she led every nation astray with sorcery; and the saints' and prophets' blood was found in her, and the blood of all slain on the earth"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:15; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:17; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 19:18; Revelation 19:1–21","v":"After these things John heard (NU, M add something like, by the NKJV note) in heaven a great multitude crying loudly Alleluia, and that to the Lord (NU, M omit the Lord, by the NKJV note), their God, belong honor, salvation, power and glory, since His judgments are true and righteous: He has passed judgment on the great harlot, whose fornication corrupted the earth, and on her He has avenged His servants' blood, which she shed. A second time they cried Alleluia, saying that her smoke ascends forever and ever. Saying Amen and Alleluia, all four living creatures bowed in worship before God, seated on the throne, as did the elders, all twenty-four. From the throne a voice spoke, bidding all who fear God and all His servants, the great and the small alike (NU, M omit both, by the NKJV note), to praise Him. John also heard what seemed to be a great multitude's voice, sounding like mighty thunderings and many waters, crying Alleluia over the reign of the (NU, M our, by the NKJV note) Lord God Omnipotent, and calling for glory to be given Him, and for rejoicing and gladness, since the Lamb's marriage has come, His wife having prepared herself; she was granted fine linen to wear, bright and clean, the saints' righteous acts being the fine linen. The one speaking with John then bade him write that those called to the Lamb's marriage supper are blessed, and told him that these are God's true sayings. Wanting to worship the speaker, John fell at the speaker's feet; but John was told not to do so, since the speaker was a fellow servant of John and of John's brethren who hold Jesus' testimony; John was to worship God, for Jesus' testimony is prophecy's spirit. John saw heaven standing open, and there was a white horse, whose rider, called Faithful and True, wages war and judges in righteousness. The rider had (M adds names written, and, by the NKJV note) a written name that only He Himself knew; many crowns were on His head, and His eyes were like flaming fire; His robe had been dipped in blood; He bears the name The Word of God. Riding after Him on white horses were the armies in heaven, robed in clean and white fine linen (NU, M pure white linen, by the NKJV note). A sword, sharp (M sharp two-edged, by the NKJV note), comes from His mouth, with which to strike the nations, whom He is to rule with an iron rod, and by Him is trodden the winepress of Almighty God's fierce wrath. A name is written on His thigh, as on His robe: of kings, the King; of lords, the Lord. John then saw an angel who stood in the sun and called out loudly to all the birds flying in midheaven to come and gather for the great God's supper (NU, M great supper of God, by the NKJV note), so as to eat the flesh of captains, of kings and of mighty men, of horses and of their riders, and of all people, slave and free (NU, M both free, by the NKJV note), great and small. John saw the earth's kings and the beast gathered with their armies to wage war on the horse's rider and on His army. Seized with the beast was the false prophet, the one who, by signs worked before it, had deceived those who worshiped its image and took the beast's mark; both were thrown alive into the fiery lake that burns with brimstone. The others were slain by the sword that came from the rider's mouth, and all the birds gorged themselves on their flesh"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 20:4; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 20:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 20:12; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 20:14; Revelation 20:1–15","v":"Then John saw an angel descending from heaven, holding in his hand a great chain and the bottomless pit's key. The angel seized the dragon, who is that serpent of old, and is both Satan and the Devil, and bound the dragon for a thousand years; the angel then threw the dragon into the bottomless pit, there shutting him in, with a seal set on him, in order that the dragon would not mislead the nations again before the thousand years were completed; after that, for a little while, he must be set free. John saw thrones, and those seated on them were entrusted with judgment; he saw too the souls of those beheaded on account of God's word and their witness to Jesus, who had given no worship to the beast's image or to the beast, nor taken its mark on hand or forehead; these lived, and with Christ they reigned for a (M the, by the NKJV note) thousand years. Not until the thousand years had ended did the other dead live again; that is the first resurrection. The one sharing in the first resurrection is holy and blessed: the second death holds no power over such, but they will be Christ's priests and God's, and for a thousand years they will reign. Satan, when the thousand years are over, will be let out of his prison, to go forth deceiving the nations at the earth's four corners, Gog and Magog, gathering them for battle, as many as the sea's sand. Across the earth's breadth those nations went up and encircled the beloved city and the saints' camp, but from God fire came down out of heaven and consumed them. Their deceiver, the devil, was thrown into the fiery lake of brimstone, where (NU, M where also, by the NKJV note) the false prophet and the beast are; forever and ever, day and night, they will be tormented. Then John saw the One seated on a great white throne, from whose face heaven and earth fled, and no place for them was found. Before God (NU, M the throne, by the NKJV note) John saw the dead standing, great and small; books were opened, and so was another book, the Book of Life; every one of the dead was judged by his own works, as the books recorded them. The sea gave back the dead in it, and so did Death and Hades the dead in them, and each was judged by what he had done. Then into the fiery lake were thrown Death and Hades; that is the second death (NU, M death, the lake of fire, by the NKJV note); into the fiery lake too was thrown each one not found recorded in that book, the Book of Life"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:2; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:5; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:7; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:10; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:16; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:23; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:23; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:24; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:24; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:26; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 21:27; Revelation 21:1–27","v":"Next John saw a heaven and an earth, both new, since the first heaven and earth were gone, and the sea was no more; and John (NU, M omit John, by the NKJV note) beheld New Jerusalem, the holy city, descending from heaven, from God, made ready, as a bride is dressed for her husband. From heaven a loud voice was heard, saying that God's tabernacle is among men, and God will make His dwelling with them; they will be a people belonging to Him, and God will Himself be present with them as their God; every tear will be wiped from their eyes by God; there will be no more pain, nor crying or sorrow, and death will be no more, since the former things are gone. The One seated on the throne said that He makes everything new, and told John (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) to write, since what is said is true and faithful; He said too: It is done (M omits It is done, by the NKJV note); He is Beginning and End, Alpha and Omega; to the one who thirsts He will freely give from the fountain of life's water; the one who overcomes will inherit (M I shall give him these things, by the NKJV note) everything, and He will be God to him, and he a son to Him; but murderers and sorcerers, idolaters, the sexually immoral and the abominable, the cowardly (M adds and sinners, by the NKJV note) and the unbelieving, and liars of every kind will have their portion in the lake burning with fire and brimstone, the second death. Then John was approached (NU, M omit to me, by the NKJV note) by an angel, one of the seven that had the bowls full of the seven last plagues, who talked with him and bade him come to be shown the Lamb's wife, the bride (M woman, the Lamb's bride, by the NKJV note); the angel carried him, in the Spirit, off to a mountain, high and great, where he showed him the great (NU, M omit great, by the NKJV note) city, which was the holy Jerusalem (NU, M holy city, Jerusalem, by the NKJV note), descending from heaven, from God, with God's glory; the city's light resembled a stone most precious, a jasper, clear as crystal. The city had a wall, high and great, with twelve gates, three facing each way, north, south, west and east, and at the gates stood twelve angels; on the gates were written names, those of Israel's twelve tribes; the city's wall had twelve foundations, on which the Lamb's twelve apostles had their names (NU, M twelve names, by the NKJV note). The angel who spoke with John had a reed of gold for measuring the city, with its wall and gates; the city lay foursquare, as broad as it was long, and, measured with the reed of gold, it came to twelve thousand furlongs (Lit. stadia, about 1,380 miles in all, by the NKJV note), equal in its height, length and breadth; its wall came to 144 cubits, by a man's measure, which is an angel's. The city itself was gold, pure and like clear glass, and its wall was built of jasper; precious stones of every kind adorned the wall's foundations: jasper the first and amethyst the twelfth, sapphire the second and jacinth the eleventh, chalcedony the third and chrysoprase the tenth, emerald the fourth and topaz the ninth, sardonyx the fifth and beryl the eighth, sardius the sixth and chrysolite the seventh; each of the twelve gates was a single pearl, and the city's street was gold, pure and like transparent glass. John saw no temple in the city, since its temple is the Lord God Almighty, together with the Lamb; the city needed neither sun nor moon shining in it (NU, M omit in it, by the NKJV note), for God's glory (M very glory, by the NKJV note) gave it light, and its light is the Lamb. The nations of the saved (NU, M omit of those who are saved, by the NKJV note) will walk by the city's light, and the earth's kings carry into it (M of the nations to Him, by the NKJV note) their honor and glory; its gates will never be shut by day, for night will not come there; the nations' honor and glory will be carried into it (M adds that they may enter in, by the NKJV note); but nothing that defiles (NU, M profane, nor one who causes, by the NKJV note) will ever enter it, nor anything causing a lie or an abomination, but only those enrolled in the Lamb's book, the Book of Life"},{"p":"contents","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:1; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:6; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:8; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:9; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:11; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:13; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:14; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:15; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:21; Revelation 22:1–21","v":"Then the angel showed John a river of life's water, pure (NU, M omit pure, by the NKJV note) and clear as crystal, flowing out from the throne that is God's and the Lamb's; the tree of life grew in the street's middle and on both sides of the river, bearing twelve fruits, with fruit from each tree every month, and the tree's leaves served to heal the nations. In time to come no curse will be there any longer; the throne that is God's and the Lamb's will be in the city, and the servants of the One on that throne, the verse not saying God or the Lamb, will serve Him there, will see His face and will bear His name on their foreheads; night will not come there, and they will need neither a lamp nor the sun's light, since they have their light from the Lord God; and forever and ever they will reign. Then the angel who had shown John all this told him that what was said is faithful and true; the holy prophets' (NU, M spirits of the prophets, by the NKJV note) Lord God sent His angel so that His servants would be shown what must soon take place. Next words follow, from a speaker the verse does not name, that the speaker is coming quickly, and that the one who keeps what the book's prophecy says is blessed. It was John who saw (NU, M am the one who heard and saw, by the NKJV note) and heard all this; on hearing and seeing it he bowed down in worship at the angel's feet, the angel who had shown it to him; but the angel told him not to do so, since (NU, M omit For, by the NKJV note) the angel serves alongside John, and alongside John's brethren the prophets and those keeping the book's words, and bade him worship God. The angel told John also to leave the book's prophetic words unsealed, since the time is near; the holy one is to remain holy, and the righteous one righteous (NU, M do right, by the NKJV note), and the filthy and the unjust are to remain so. Again words follow, from a speaker the verse does not name, that the speaker is coming quickly, bringing a reward, to repay each one as his work deserves, and that the speaker is First and Last, Beginning (NU, M First and the Last, the Beginning and the End, by the NKJV note) and End, Alpha and Omega. Those who carry out His commandments (NU wash their robes, by the NKJV note), the verse not saying whose, are blessed, so that the tree of life may be theirs by right and they may go into the city by its gates; but (NU, M omit But, by the NKJV note) outside are murderers and dogs, the sexually immoral, idolaters and sorcerers, and all who love a lie and practice it. Jesus says that He has sent His angel to bear witness of all this in the churches, and that He is David's Root and Offspring, the bright Morning Star. With the bride, the Spirit says Come, and so is the hearer to say; the thirsty one is to come, and anyone who desires is to receive the water of life as a free gift. Then (NU, M omit For, by the NKJV note) every hearer of the book's prophetic words is warned that God will add (M may God add, by the NKJV note) the plagues written in the book to anyone adding to these things, and that from anyone taking anything from its prophetic words God will take away (M may God take away, by the NKJV note) his share of the holy city, of what the book records and of the Book of Life (NU, M tree of life, by the NKJV note). The One who bears witness to all this says that He is surely coming quickly, and the answer is Amen, with a call to the Lord Jesus to come. The Lord Jesus Christ's grace is wished on all the book's readers (NU with all; M with all the saints, by the NKJV note); Amen"},{"p":"designation","r":"Revelation 1:1","v":"The Revelation of Jesus Christ"},{"p":"feature","r":"Revelation 1:3","v":"It pronounces blessed the reader and the hearers of its prophecy who keep what it says, since the time is near"},{"p":"feature","r":"Revelation 22:7","v":"It pronounces blessed the one who keeps what its prophecy says"},{"p":"feature","r":"NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:18; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:19; NKJV notes, note on Revelation 22:19; Revelation 22:18–19","v":"In its closing words (NU, M omit For, by the NKJV note) every hearer of its prophetic words is warned that God will add (M may God add, by the NKJV note) the plagues written in it to anyone adding to these things, and that from anyone taking anything from its prophetic words God will take away (M may God take away, by the NKJV note) his share of the holy city, of what it records and of the Book of Life (NU, M tree of life, by the NKJV note)"},{"p":"made by","r":"Revelation 1:4, 9–11","e":"PER-03381"},{"p":"occasion","r":"Revelation 1:4, 11, 19","e":"EVT-03321"}],"nm":{"EVT-03321":"John writes to the seven churches in Asia","PER-03381":"John, who wrote to the seven churches"}}});
